/* AQA GCSE Mathematics (8300) — room kits built by hand in a Claude Code session: written by
   Opus 5 subagents to the contract in src/kit-validator.js, every question re-solved and the lesson read against
   the specification map by a fresh Opus 5 subagent before shipping. See .claude/skills/course-builder/references/depth.md. */
module.exports = { ID: 'AQA-8300', KITS: {"number-structure":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"number-structure","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Number is 25% of the Foundation tier assessment, the largest single slice, and this room holds the machinery every other room runs on. Ordering, the four operations, priority of operations, primes, systematic listing, powers and roots, exact answers and standard form all turn up inside ratio, area, probability and algebra questions that are never labelled 'number' at all. Paper 1 is taken without a calculator, so the written method and the exact form of the answer are both credited and a bare answer can lose every method mark; Papers 2 and 3 still expect the calculator display to be interpreted and the answer written out properly. A slip here costs marks in topics that have nothing to do with number.","idea":[{"h":"Ordering numbers and the inequality symbols","t":"To compare numbers written in different forms, put them all into one form first — usually decimals, by dividing the top of each fraction by the bottom. On a number line the values increase from left to right, so every negative number is smaller than every positive one, and of two negatives the one further from zero is the smaller: −9 < −3. Write comparisons with =, ≠, <, >, ≤ or ≥, reading the symbol as pointing at the smaller value. Examiners ask for a list 'smallest first', or for the right symbol between two values; a list written in the wrong direction scores nothing.","code":"N1"},{"h":"The four operations, formal written methods and place value","t":"Apply the four operations to integers, decimals, proper and improper fractions and mixed numbers, positive and negative, using formal written methods on Paper 1. Multiplying decimals is a whole-number multiplication with the point put back: the answer carries as many decimal places as the two factors have between them. Subtracting a negative adds, and a negative multiplied by a negative is positive. Place value is what keeps very large and very small numbers under control. These questions are set in context, so know the household-finance words the specification names: cost price, selling price, profit, loss, debit, credit, balance, income tax, VAT and interest rate.","code":"N2"},{"h":"Inverse operations and priority of operations","t":"Every operation has an inverse — addition and subtraction, multiplication and division, powers and roots — and the inverse is what checks an answer or cancels to simplify a calculation. Priority of operations fixes the order: brackets first, then indices and roots, then division and multiplication from left to right, then addition and subtraction from left to right. Working straight through from left to right is the commonest mistake in this room. Questions often ask you to write a single calculation, with brackets where they are needed, and then work it out, so the bracket itself carries a mark.","code":"N3"},{"h":"Primes, factors, multiples, HCF and LCM","t":"A prime has exactly two factors, itself and 1, so 1 is not prime and 2 is the only even prime. The unique factorisation theorem says every whole number above 1 is a product of primes in exactly one way, and repeated division by the smallest prime that fits produces that product, written in index form such as 720 = 2⁴ × 3² × 5. From two factorisations, the highest common factor is the product of the lowest power of each shared prime and the lowest common multiple is the product of the highest power of every prime that appears in either. Context questions about equal rows, tiles or repeating events are HCF and LCM in disguise.","code":"N4"},{"h":"Systematic listing strategies","t":"A question asking how many outcomes, combinations or arrangements there are wants a complete list with no repeats, and the method mark is for the order in which you list them. Fix one feature — the first digit, the first item on the menu — and run through every possibility for the rest before changing it, then move the fixed feature on. Lists, two-way tables and simple diagrams all do the same job. An unordered scribble nearly always misses a case or counts one twice, and it cannot earn the method mark even when the total happens to come out right.","code":"N5"},{"h":"Positive integer powers and associated real roots","t":"A power is repeated multiplication and a root undoes it: √49 = 7 because 7² = 49, and the cube root of 125 is 5 because 5³ = 125. You are expected to recognise the square numbers up to 15 × 15 = 225 on sight, the cubes 2³ = 8, 3³ = 27, 4³ = 64 and 5³ = 125, and the powers of 2, 3, 4 and 5 — so 64 can be read as 2⁶, 4³ or 8² depending on what the question needs. Two results are named in the specification itself: 1000 = 10³ and 1 million = 10⁶, which is where standard form begins.","code":"N6"},{"h":"Calculating with roots and with integer indices","t":"Powers of the same base multiply by adding the indices and divide by subtracting them: 3⁵ × 3⁻² = 3³ and 5⁶ ÷ 5⁴ = 5². Any non-zero number to the power 0 is 1, and a negative index means a reciprocal, so 4⁻² = 1/4² = 1/16. Roots combine the same way: √45 × √5 = √225 = 15 and √50 ÷ √2 = √25 = 5, which is how a non-calculator answer stays exact and whole. Write down the index law you used — that line is where the method mark sits, and an answer alone can score nothing on Paper 1.","code":"N7"},{"h":"Calculating exactly with fractions and with multiples of π","t":"The word 'exactly' forbids a rounded decimal. A fraction answer is left in its lowest terms and a circle answer is left as a multiple of π: the area of a circle of radius 3 cm is 9π cm², not 28.3 cm². Add and subtract fractions over a common denominator, multiply tops and bottoms after cancelling, and divide by multiplying by the reciprocal; a mixed number is turned into an improper fraction before any of that. Multiplying a multiple of π by a fraction only touches the number in front, so 2/3 × 9π = 6π, and the π is carried through untouched.","code":"N8"},{"h":"Standard form","t":"Standard form writes a number as A × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ A < 10 and n an integer; n is positive for large numbers and negative for small ones, so 0.00045 = 4.5 × 10⁻⁴. To multiply, multiply the A values and add the indices of 10; to divide, divide the A values and subtract the indices; then adjust A back into range, so 13.5 × 10⁻³ is rewritten as 1.35 × 10⁻². On Papers 2 and 3 a calculator shows a display such as 4.5⁻⁰⁴, whose raised digits are the index of 10 — it must be written out as 4.5 × 10⁻⁴ and never copied down as it appears on the screen.","code":"N9"}],"examples":[{"title":"Putting mixed signed numbers in order","setup":"A number line runs from −4 to 4 in steps of 0.5. Five cards are to be placed on it: −3, −1/2, 0.35, 7/4 and 2.5. Write the five values in order, starting with the smallest, and then write the comparison between the smallest and the largest using <, and again using >.","steps":["Rewrite the two fractions as decimals so that all five cards are in one form: −1/2 = −0.5 and 7/4 = 1.75.","Every negative card sits to the left of zero, and of −3 and −0.5 the one further from zero is the smaller, so −3 comes first and −0.5 second.","The positive cards follow, in increasing size: 0.35, then 1.75, then 2.5.","Putting the two halves of the line together, the order starting with the smallest is −3, −1/2, 0.35, 7/4, 2.5.","A symbol points at the smaller value, so the smallest against the largest is −3 < 2.5, which written the other way round is 2.5 > −3."],"cues":["What has to be true of five numbers before they can be compared at a glance?","Of two negative numbers, which one is the smaller?","How do the positive cards compare with one another?","Which end of the number line does 'starting with the smallest' begin at?","Which value does the pointed end of < or > face?"]},{"title":"Profit on a market stall, written as one calculation","setup":"A market trader buys 24 boxes of fruit at a cost price of £3.75 a box. She sells 20 of the boxes at a selling price of £6.20 each and the last 4 boxes at £2.50 each. Write a single calculation, with brackets where they are needed, that gives her profit, and then work the profit out.","steps":["Money in is 20 × £6.20 together with 4 × £2.50, money out is 24 × £3.75, and profit is money in minus money out: (20 × 6.20 + 4 × 2.50) − 24 × 3.75.","Priority of operations takes the bracket first, and inside it the multiplications come before the addition: 20 × 6.20 = 124 and 4 × 2.50 = 10.","Adding inside the bracket gives 124 + 10 = 134, the total selling price of all the boxes.","The multiplication outside the bracket is next: 24 × 3.75 = 24 × 3 + 24 × 0.75 = 72 + 18 = 90, the total cost price.","The subtraction is left until last: 134 − 90 = 44, so she makes a profit of £44."],"cues":["Which parts of this are money coming in, and which is money going out?","Inside a bracket, which operation is carried out first?","What do the two amounts of money in add up to?","What is still to be worked out before anything can be subtracted?","What is left when the cost is taken away from the takings?"]},{"title":"Prime factors, HCF and LCM of 720 and 168","setup":"Two numbers are 720 and 168. Write each of them as a product of prime factors in index form, and then use those two factorisations to write down the highest common factor and the lowest common multiple of the pair.","steps":["Divide 720 by 2 as often as it will go: 720 = 2 × 360 = 2 × 2 × 180 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 90 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 45, and 45 = 3 × 3 × 5, so 720 = 2⁴ × 3² × 5.","Treat 168 the same way: 168 = 2 × 84 = 2 × 2 × 42 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 21, and 21 = 3 × 7, so 168 = 2³ × 3 × 7.","The highest common factor takes the lowest power of each prime that appears in both lists: 2 appears as 2⁴ and as 2³, 3 appears as 3² and as 3, and 5 and 7 appear in only one list each, so HCF = 2³ × 3 = 24.","The lowest common multiple takes the highest power of every prime that appears in either list, so LCM = 2⁴ × 3² × 5 × 7 = 5040."],"cues":["Which prime is worth trying as a divisor first?","Does the same repeated division work on the second number?","For a factor shared by both, which power can both numbers supply?","For a multiple of both, which power of each prime is needed?"]},{"title":"Listing every code without missing one","setup":"A padlock opens to a three-digit code that uses each of the digits 2, 5 and 8 exactly once. Working in a fixed order, list every possible code, then say how many codes there are altogether and how many of them end in an even digit.","steps":["Hold the first digit at the smallest, 2, and write the two ways the other digits can follow: 258 and 285.","Now hold the first digit at 5, giving 528 and 582, and then at 8, giving 825 and 852.","Each first digit has been used once and each pair is complete, so the full list is 258, 285, 528, 582, 825, 852 — six codes in all.","The even digits in the set are 2 and 8, so the codes finishing in one of them are 258, 528, 582 and 852: four of the six end in an even digit."],"cues":["Which digit is the sensible one to hold still first?","What changes in the list when the first digit moves on?","How can you be sure no code has been missed or written twice?","Which of the three digits are even?"]},{"title":"A cube, a power of 2 and an exact circle area","setup":"A solid cube has a volume of 512 cm³. Write 512 as a power of 2, find the length of one edge of the cube, and then write down, exactly as a multiple of π, the area of a circle whose radius is equal to that edge.","steps":["Halve 512 again and again: 512, 256, 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1 — nine halvings in all, so 512 = 2⁹.","The edge of a cube is the cube root of its volume, and 8 × 8 × 8 = 512, so each edge is 8 cm.","In index form that edge is 2³, which agrees with the volume because 2³ × 2³ × 2³ = 2⁹ once the indices are added.","A circle of radius 8 cm has area πr² = π × 8² = 64π cm², and leaving it as 64π is what makes the answer exact."],"cues":["What operation, applied to 512 over and over, would let you read off the index?","How is the edge of a cube found from its volume?","How many equal factors multiply together to give the volume of a cube?","Why should π be left in the answer rather than multiplied out?"]},{"title":"Distances in space in standard form","setup":"The Earth is about 1.5 × 10⁸ km from the Sun and Neptune is about 4.5 × 10⁹ km from the Sun. A comet is 6 × 10⁷ km from the Sun. Write the Earth's distance as an ordinary number, work out how many times further from the Sun Neptune is than the Earth, and give the difference between the Earth's distance and the comet's distance in standard form.","steps":["A positive index moves the digits up the place-value columns, eight columns in this case, so 1.5 × 10⁸ = 150 000 000 km.","'How many times further' is a division, and the front numbers and the powers of ten are handled separately: 4.5 ÷ 1.5 = 3.","For the powers, subtract the indices: 10⁹ ÷ 10⁸ = 10¹, so Neptune is 3 × 10¹ = 30 times further out than the Earth.","The comet's distance written out is 6 × 10⁷ = 60 000 000 km, against the Earth's 150 000 000 km.","The difference is 150 000 000 − 60 000 000 = 90 000 000 km, which as A × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ A < 10 is 9 × 10⁷ km."],"cues":["Which way do the digits move when the index of ten is positive?","What operation answers 'how many times further'?","What happens to the indices when powers of ten are divided?","What does the comet's distance look like written out in full?","What must the number in front be between for standard form?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which is smaller, −0.7 or −0.07?","a":"−0.7"},{"q":"Work out 20 − 4 × 3.","a":"8"},{"q":"Write 90 as a product of prime factors in index form.","a":"2 × 3² × 5"},{"q":"Work out 4⁻¹, giving your answer as a fraction.","a":"1/4"},{"q":"Write 6 300 000 in standard form.","a":"6.3 × 10⁶"}]},"room":{"facts":["Priority of operations: brackets, then indices and roots, then division and multiplication from left to right, then addition and subtraction from left to right.","Laws of indices: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ, aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, a⁰ = 1 and a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ. Roots combine the same way: √a × √b = √(ab).","HCF is the product of the lowest power of each prime the two factorisations share; LCM is the product of the highest power of every prime that appears in either.","Standard form is A × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ A < 10 and n an integer; a number smaller than 1 has a negative n.","The square numbers to 15 × 15: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100, 121, 144, 169, 196, 225. Also 1000 = 10³ and 1 million = 10⁶.","Subtracting a negative adds: a − (−b) = a + b; a negative multiplied or divided by a negative gives a positive.","'Exactly' means leaving the answer as a fraction in its lowest terms or as a multiple of π, never as a rounded decimal; multiplying a multiple of π by a fraction only changes the number in front, so 2/3 × 9π = 6π."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) (a) Write the correct symbol, < or >, between −6 and −9. (b) Write the correct symbol, < or >, between 3/8 and 0.4. (c) Write these three numbers in order, starting with the smallest: −0.8, −5/6, −0.75","a":"(a) −6 > −9   (b) 3/8 < 0.4   (c) −5/6, −0.8, −0.75","sol":"−6 lies to the right of −9 on the number line, so −6 > −9 (B1). Dividing to compare, 3 ÷ 8 = 0.375, and 0.375 is less than 0.4, so 3/8 < 0.4 (B1). Writing the last fraction as a decimal, −5/6 = −0.8333…, and the further a negative number lies from zero the smaller it is, so the order is −5/6, −0.8, −0.75 (B1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Two numbers can only be compared at a glance once they are written in the same form.","Turn each fraction into a decimal by dividing the top by the bottom, then compare the digits column by column; on a number line the values grow from left to right.","The first lines of working are 3 ÷ 8 written as a decimal, and then 5 ÷ 6 written as a decimal."],"codes":["N1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out (a) 4.7 × 0.6 and (b) −8 − (−15).","a":"(a) 2.82   (b) 7","sol":"Do the multiplication as whole numbers first: 47 × 6 = 282 (M1). The two factors have two decimal places between them, so 4.7 × 0.6 = 2.82 (A1). Subtracting a negative adds, so −8 − (−15) = −8 + 15 = 7 (B1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["A decimal multiplication can be turned into a whole-number one and put right afterwards.","Multiply without the points, then count how many decimal places the two factors have between them; and think what two minus signs side by side do.","The first lines are 47 × 6, and then −8 − (−15) rewritten with the double sign replaced by a single one."],"codes":["N2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 7 + 3 × 4² ÷ 8","a":"13","sol":"Indices come before the other operations, so replace 4² by 16: the calculation is 7 + 3 × 16 ÷ 8 (M1). Multiplication and division come next, left to right: 3 × 16 = 48 and 48 ÷ 8 = 6, so the value is 7 + 6 = 13 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["These operations are not carried out in the order they are written.","Deal with the index first, then the multiplication and division from left to right, and leave the addition until last.","The first line rewrites the calculation with 4² replaced by its value."],"codes":["N3","N6"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write down (a) the value of 14², (b) the cube root of 27, (c) 10³ as an ordinary number.","a":"(a) 196   (b) 3   (c) 1000","sol":"14 × 14 = 196 (B1). The cube root asks which number multiplied by itself three times gives 27, and 3 × 3 × 3 = 27, so the cube root is 3 (B1). 10³ = 10 × 10 × 10 = 1000 (B1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["All three of these are results you are expected to recall rather than work out at length.","A square is a number multiplied by itself; a cube root asks which number multiplied by itself three times gives the number; a power of 10 counts the zeros.","Each part wants one whole number recalled rather than worked out: match the square against your squares up to 15², match 27 against your list of cubes and read the root off it, and match the power of 10 against the place-value column it names."],"codes":["N6"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Write 84 as a product of its prime factors, giving your answer in index form.","a":"2² × 3 × 7","sol":"Divide repeatedly by the smallest prime that fits: 84 = 2 × 42 = 2 × 2 × 21, and 21 = 3 × 7 (M1). Collecting the primes gives 84 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 7 = 2² × 3 × 7 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Every whole number above 1 breaks into primes in exactly one way.","Divide by the smallest prime that goes in, then keep dividing the result until only primes are left, and gather any repeats into an index.","The first line is 84 = 2 × 42."],"codes":["N4"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Work out (4.8 × 10⁵) ÷ (1.6 × 10⁻²). Give your answer in standard form.","a":"3 × 10⁷","sol":"Divide the front numbers: 4.8 ÷ 1.6 = 3 (M1). Divide the powers of ten by subtracting the indices: 10⁵ ÷ 10⁻² = 10⁵⁻⁽⁻²⁾ = 10⁷ (M1). Putting them together gives 3 × 10⁷, and since 1 ≤ 3 < 10 this is already in standard form (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The front numbers and the powers of ten can be dealt with separately.","Divide the front numbers, and for the powers subtract the second index from the first, taking care with the negative index.","The first line is 4.8 ÷ 1.6 alongside 10⁵ ÷ 10⁻²."],"codes":["N9","N7"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out (a) 5⁶ ÷ 5⁴, (b) 2⁻³ as a fraction, (c) √45 × √5.","a":"(a) 25   (b) 1/8   (c) 15","sol":"Dividing powers of the same base subtracts the indices: 5⁶ ÷ 5⁴ = 5⁶⁻⁴ = 5² = 25 (B1). A negative index means a reciprocal: 2⁻³ = 1/2³ = 1/8 (B1). A product of two roots is the root of the product: √45 × √5 = √(45 × 5) = √225 = 15 (B1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each part is one law of indices or the matching law for roots.","Dividing powers of the same base subtracts the indices; a negative index means a reciprocal; the product of two roots is the root of the product.","The first lines are 5⁶⁻⁴, then 1 over 2³, then √(45 × 5)."],"codes":["N7","N6"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A café set lunch is one of three sandwiches (cheese, tuna, ham), one of two sides (salad, crisps) and one of two drinks (tea, juice). By listing the choices systematically, work out how many different set lunches are possible and how many of them include tuna.","a":"12 set lunches, 4 of which include tuna","sol":"Hold the sandwich at cheese and run through the sides and drinks in a fixed order: cheese-salad-tea, cheese-salad-juice, cheese-crisps-tea, cheese-crisps-juice — four lunches (M1). The same four pairings follow for tuna and again for ham, so there are 3 × 4 = 12 lunches in all (M1). Exactly one sandwich in three is tuna, and the tuna block of the list holds four of the twelve, so 12 set lunches are possible and 4 of them include tuna (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Nothing may be counted twice and nothing left out, so the order in which you list matters.","Hold the sandwich fixed and run through every side with every drink, then move on to the next sandwich.","The first line lists the lunches that begin with cheese."],"codes":["N5"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out exactly (a) 2/3 + 3/8 and (b) 5/6 × 2/5, giving each answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"(a) 25/24 (or 1 1/24)   (b) 1/3","sol":"The lowest common denominator of 3 and 8 is 24, and 2/3 = 16/24 while 3/8 = 9/24 (M1). Adding gives 16/24 + 9/24 = 25/24, which is 1 1/24 and cannot be cancelled further (A1). For the product, cancel the 5 on the top against the 5 on the bottom, leaving 1/6 × 2/1 = 2/6, which cancels to 1/3 (B1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Fractions can only be added once they are counted in pieces of the same size.","Find the lowest common denominator of the two bottoms and rewrite both fractions over it; for the product, cancel before multiplying the tops and the bottoms.","The first line rewrites 2/3 and 3/8 over their lowest common denominator."],"codes":["N8","N2"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Two lighthouses flash at regular intervals: one every 36 seconds, the other every 48 seconds. They flash together at 21:00. (a) After how many seconds do they next flash together? (b) How many times do they flash together in the 30 minutes after 21:00?","a":"(a) 144 seconds   (b) 12 times","sol":"Write each interval as a product of primes: 36 = 2² × 3² and 48 = 2⁴ × 3 (M1). The lowest common multiple takes the highest power of each prime, so LCM = 2⁴ × 3² = 144 and they next flash together after 144 seconds (A1). Thirty minutes is 30 × 60 = 1800 seconds, and 1800 ÷ 144 = 12.5 (M1). Only whole multiples of 144 count, so they flash together 12 times (A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The two flashes coincide at a time that is a multiple of both intervals.","Write each interval as a product of prime factors and build the lowest common multiple from the highest power of each prime; then turn the half hour into seconds.","The first lines are 36 and 48 written as products of prime factors, and 30 minutes written in seconds."],"codes":["N4","N2"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. On 1 March Priya's account balance is £248.60. During the month the account is credited with wages of £1245.00 and debited with rent of £362.40, bills of £58.75 and three equal payments of £29.50. Work out the balance at the end of March.","a":"£983.95","sol":"A credit is money in, so the balance after the wages is £248.60 + £1245.00 = £1493.60 (M1). The three equal payments come to 3 × £29.50 = £88.50 (M1). All the debits together are £362.40 + £58.75 + £88.50 = £509.65 (M1). Debits are money out, so the closing balance is £1493.60 − £509.65 = £983.95 (A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["A credit is money into the account and a debit is money out of it.","Add the opening balance to the credit, total all the debits, then subtract the one from the other.","The first lines are the opening balance added to the wages, and the three equal payments multiplied out."],"codes":["N2"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A gardener has 60 tulip bulbs and 84 daffodil bulbs. She plants them all in identical rows, every row holding the same number of tulips and the same number of daffodils. (a) What is the greatest number of rows she can make? (b) With that many rows, how many tulips and how many daffodils are in each row? (c) List every other number of rows she could have used.","a":"(a) 12 rows   (b) 5 tulips and 7 daffodils   (c) 1, 2, 3, 4 and 6 rows","sol":"Write each total as a product of primes: 60 = 2² × 3 × 5 and 84 = 2² × 3 × 7 (M1). The number of rows must divide both totals, and the greatest such number is the HCF, taking the lowest power of each shared prime: 2² × 3 (M1) = 12 rows (A1). Then 60 ÷ 12 = 5 tulips and 84 ÷ 12 = 7 daffodils in each row (A1). Any common factor of 60 and 84 would work, and the factors of 12 are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 and 12, so the other possibilities are 1, 2, 3, 4 and 6 rows (B1).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The rows are identical and every bulb is used, so the number of rows divides both totals exactly.","The greatest number that divides both is the highest common factor, built from the prime factorisations; the other possibilities are the remaining common factors.","The first lines are 60 and 84 written as products of prime factors."],"codes":["N4","N5"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. The mass of one oxygen atom is 2.7 × 10⁻²⁶ kg. (a) Work out the total mass of 5 × 10²³ oxygen atoms, giving your answer in standard form. (b) A student's calculator shows this answer as 1.35⁻⁰². Write that display as an ordinary number.","a":"(a) 1.35 × 10⁻² kg   (b) 0.0135","sol":"Multiply the front numbers: 2.7 × 5 = 13.5 (M1). Add the indices of ten: 10⁻²⁶ × 10²³ = 10⁻²⁶⁺²³ = 10⁻³ (M1). That gives 13.5 × 10⁻³, and since the front number must satisfy 1 ≤ A < 10 it is adjusted to 1.35 × 10⁻² kg (A1). The raised digits on the display are the index of ten, so 1.35⁻⁰² means 1.35 × 10⁻², which written out is 0.0135 (B1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The mass of one atom multiplied by the number of atoms gives the total mass.","Multiply the front numbers, add the indices of ten, then check whether the front number still lies between 1 and 10 and adjust the index if it does not.","The first line is 2.7 × 5 alongside 10⁻²⁶ × 10²³."],"codes":["N9","N7","N2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A recipe uses 3/4 kg of flour for one loaf. Ann has 5 1/2 kg of flour. (a) How many whole loaves can she make? (b) Exactly how much flour is left over? Give the answer as a fraction of a kilogram in its simplest form.","a":"(a) 7 loaves   (b) 1/4 kg","sol":"Write the mixed number as an improper fraction: 5 1/2 = 11/2 (M1). Dividing by a fraction means multiplying by its reciprocal: 11/2 ÷ 3/4 = 11/2 × 4/3 = 44/6 = 22/3 = 7 1/3 (M1). Only whole loaves count, so she can make 7 loaves (A1). Those loaves use 7 × 3/4 = 21/4 kg (M1), and 11/2 − 21/4 = 22/4 − 21/4 = 1/4, so 1/4 kg is left over (A1).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Only whole loaves can be baked, so this division will not come out exactly.","Turn the mixed number into an improper fraction and divide by multiplying by the reciprocal; then work out how much flour the whole loaves really use and take it away.","The first line is 5 1/2 rewritten as an improper fraction over 2."],"codes":["N8","N2","N3"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A number n has prime factorisation n = 2⁵ × 3² × 7. (a) Work out the value of n. (b) Explain, using the factorisation, why n is not a square number. (c) Find the smallest whole number that n must be multiplied by to give a square number.","a":"(a) 2016   (b) the indices 5 and 1 are odd   (c) 14","sol":"2⁵ = 32 and 3² = 9, so n = 32 × 9 × 7 = 2016 (B1). A square number is a product of two identical numbers, so every index in its prime factorisation must be even (M1). Here 2⁵ and 7¹ have odd indices, so n is not a square number (A1). Raising each odd index by one needs a factor of 2 and a factor of 7, so multiply by 2 × 7 = 14, giving 2⁶ × 3² × 7² = 28 224 = 168² (B1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The prime factorisation of a square number has a pattern to its indices.","Multiply the prime powers out for the value; then ask what must be true of each index if the number is to split into two identical halves.","The first line is 2⁵ × 3² × 7 rewritten as 32 × 9 × 7."],"codes":["N4","N6","N7"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write the correct symbol, < or >, between −4.5 and −4.05.","a":"−4.5 < −4.05"},{"q":"Write 156 as a product of prime factors in index form.","a":"2² × 3 × 13"},{"q":"Work out 3⁴ ÷ 3⁶, giving your answer as a fraction.","a":"1/9"},{"q":"Write 8.7 × 10⁻³ as an ordinary number.","a":"0.0087"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The six symbols =, ≠, <, >, ≤, ≥","back":"= equal to; ≠ not equal to; < less than; > greater than; ≤ less than or equal to; ≥ greater than or equal to. The symbol always points at the smaller value.","code":"N1","open":false},{"front":"Ordering negative numbers","back":"Every negative number is smaller than every positive one, and the further a negative number lies from zero the smaller it is: −9 < −3 < −0.5 < 0 < 0.5.","code":"N1","open":false},{"front":"Name two ways of showing that 5/8 is less than 0.65","back":"Write 5/8 as a decimal, since 0.625 < 0.65; put both over 1000, since 625/1000 < 650/1000; multiply out, since 0.65 × 8 = 5.2 and 5.2 > 5.","code":"N1","open":true},{"front":"Subtracting a negative number","back":"a − (−b) = a + b: two minus signs side by side become a plus, so −6 − (−10) = −6 + 10 = 4. A negative times or divided by a negative is positive.","code":"N2","open":false},{"front":"Multiplying two decimals","back":"Multiply as whole numbers, then give the answer as many decimal places as the two factors have between them: 0.3 × 0.04 has three, so 3 × 4 = 12 becomes 0.012.","code":"N2","open":false},{"front":"Priority of operations","back":"Brackets first, then indices and roots, then division and multiplication from left to right, then addition and subtraction from left to right.","code":"N3","open":false},{"front":"Give one way to check that 372 ÷ 12 = 31 without dividing again","back":"Multiply back, since 31 × 12 = 372; divide the answer into the starting number, since 372 ÷ 31 = 12; estimate, since 360 ÷ 12 = 30, which is close to 31.","code":"N3","open":true},{"front":"Prime number","back":"A whole number greater than 1 with exactly two factors, itself and 1. The primes begin 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19. 1 is not prime, and 2 is the only even prime.","code":"N4","open":false},{"front":"Prime factorisation in index form","back":"Every whole number above 1 is a product of primes in exactly one way. Repeated division gives 90 = 2 × 3 × 3 × 5, written in index form as 2 × 3² × 5.","code":"N4","open":false},{"front":"HCF and LCM from two prime factorisations","back":"HCF: the product of the lowest power of each prime that appears in both lists. LCM: the product of the highest power of every prime that appears in either list.","code":"N4","open":false},{"front":"A systematic listing strategy","back":"Fix one feature, run through the others in a set order, then move the fixed feature on. Listing in that order is what shows nothing is missed and nothing repeated.","code":"N5","open":false},{"front":"The square numbers to 15 × 15","back":"1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100, 121, 144, 169, 196, 225. Also 1000 = 10³ and 1 million = 10⁶.","code":"N6","open":false},{"front":"The laws of indices","back":"aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ; aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ; a⁰ = 1; a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ. Roots follow the same pattern: √a × √b = √(ab) and √a ÷ √b = √(a ÷ b).","code":"N7","open":false},{"front":"Calculating exactly","back":"Leave the answer as a fraction in its lowest terms or as a multiple of π, never as a rounded decimal: the area of a circle of radius 3 cm is 9π cm², not 28.3 cm².","code":"N8","open":false},{"front":"Standard form","back":"A × 10ⁿ, where 1 ≤ A < 10 and n is an integer. To multiply, multiply the A values and add the indices; to divide, divide and subtract. Then adjust A back into range.","code":"N9","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Given to you: area of a circle = πr², where r is the radius. The Foundation tier formulae sheet prints it, so it may be read off the insert rather than recalled; it is the result N8's exact answers in terms of π are built from. (Note that the specification's own Appendix, written before the sheet existed, puts it in list 1 as a formula that 'will not be given in the exam'; the later sheet governs what is given on the day.)","Given to you: circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d the diameter. The Foundation tier formulae sheet prints this too, and an exact circumference such as 14π cm is written straight from it.","Those two circle results are the only things this room uses that the Foundation tier formulae sheet prints. Everything below has to be remembered.","Remember: the first law of indices, aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ (N7). Not on the Foundation tier formulae sheet, and in none of the Appendix's three lists.","Remember: the second law of indices, aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ (N7). Not on the Foundation tier formulae sheet, and in none of the Appendix's three lists.","Remember: a⁰ = 1 (N7). Not on the Foundation tier formulae sheet, and in none of the Appendix's three lists.","Remember: a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ (N7). Not on the Foundation tier formulae sheet, and in none of the Appendix's three lists.","Remember: √a × √b = √(ab) (N7). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: √a ÷ √b = √(a ÷ b) (N7). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: standard form is A × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ A < 10 and n an integer (N9). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: to multiply or divide numbers in standard form, multiply or divide the A values, add or subtract the indices of 10, then adjust A back into range (N9). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: HCF = the product of the lowest power of each prime the two factorisations share (N4). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: LCM = the product of the highest power of every prime that appears in either factorisation (N4). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: priority of operations — brackets, then indices and roots, then division and multiplication from left to right, then addition and subtraction from left to right (N3). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: the square numbers to 15² = 225 (N6). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: the cubes 2³ = 8, 3³ = 27, 4³ = 64 and 5³ = 125 (N6). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: 1000 = 10³ and 1 million = 10⁶ (N6). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: a − (−b) = a + b (N2). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: a negative times a negative is positive (N2). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: a product of decimals carries as many decimal places as the two factors have between them (N2). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: to add or subtract fractions, write them over a common denominator (N8). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: to multiply fractions, cancel then multiply tops and bottoms (N8). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: to divide by a fraction, multiply by its reciprocal (N8). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: when a question says 'exactly', leave the answer as a fraction in lowest terms or as a multiple of π (N8). Not on the formulae sheet.","For reference, the rest of the Foundation tier formulae sheet — none of it needed in this room — is the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios in a right-angled triangle, compound interest, and the addition rule for the probability of one outcome or another. The sheet is reissued every series, so check the insert at the front of your paper before relying on this list."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:15:27Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Every answer key, solution line and worked example checks out and the Formulae sheet matches appendix.md section 4 exactly — it claims only area and circumference of a circle as printed on the Foundation insert, which is right, and its closing 'rest of the sheet' list reproduces the other eight Foundation results with nothing added or dropped; publish it, with the Formulae sheet split into one result per item at the next edit."}}},"number-fdp":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"number-fdp","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Fractions, decimals and percentages are three ways of writing the same numbers, and Foundation papers move between them constantly: Number is 25% of the Foundation assessment, and these three statements also sit underneath ratio, proportion, money, statistics and probability. Paper 1 asks for these conversions and for 'of' calculations with no calculator, where the written method is what carries the method marks; Papers 2 and 3 expect a multiplier keyed straight in and the display read back in context. Getting 0.375 = 3/8, the fraction hidden inside a ratio and the multiplier hidden inside a percentage into your fingers makes a large slice of every paper routine.","idea":[{"h":"Moving between terminating decimals and fractions","t":"A terminating decimal stops after a fixed number of digits, so it can always be written over a power of 10 — one zero for every decimal place. 0.375 has three decimal places, so it is 375/1000, which cancels to 3/8; 3.5 is 35/10 = 7/2. Going the other way, divide the numerator by the denominator (7 ÷ 8 = 0.875) or scale the denominator to 10, 100 or 1000 (13/20 = 65/100 = 0.65). The Notes add ordering: when a question mixes fractions, decimals and percentages, convert every number into one form — decimals are usually quickest — compare them there, then write the answer back in the forms the question used, because the accuracy mark is for the order of the original numbers.","code":"N10"},{"h":"The fraction hidden in a ratio","t":"A ratio a : b splits the whole into a + b equal shares, so the first quantity is a/(a + b) of the whole and the second is b/(a + b). Once you can read that fraction, a ratio question becomes a fraction question: multiply the whole by the fraction to find a part, or divide a known part by its number of shares to find what one share is worth and multiply up to reach the whole. The exam sets this with counters, beads, money and people, and the method mark usually falls on the total number of shares or on the value of one share, so write that line down even when a calculator is doing the arithmetic.","code":"N11"},{"h":"Fractions and percentages as operators","t":"'Of' is an instruction to multiply, so a fraction or a percentage acts on a quantity and scales it: 3/4 of 96 = 3/4 × 96 = 72, and 35% of 240 = 0.35 × 240 = 84. A percentage is a number of parts per hundred, so dividing by 100 turns it into a decimal multiplier. The Notes push this further into percentage problems done by multiplier: an increase of p% multiplies by 1 + p/100 and a decrease of p% multiplies by 1 − p/100, so a 6% rise uses 1.06 and an 18% fall uses 0.82. On Paper 1 build the percentage instead from 10%, 5% and 1%; that working is what earns the method mark.","code":"N12"}],"examples":[{"title":"Putting fractions and decimals in order","setup":"Four numbers are written in different forms: 0.6, 5/8, 0.62 and 13/20. Put them in order, smallest first, writing each number in the form it was given in.","steps":["Divide to change 5/8 into a decimal: 5 ÷ 8 = 0.625","Scale 13/20 so its denominator is 100: 13/20 = 65/100 = 0.65","Compare the four decimals: 0.6 < 0.62 < 0.625 < 0.65","Back in the forms given, smallest first: 0.6, 0.62, 5/8, 13/20"],"cues":["Which single form should every number be changed into before comparing?","What do you multiply 20 by to reach 100, and what must happen to the 13?","Which of the four decimals is the smallest?","Which original form does each decimal belong to?"]},{"title":"The fraction each part of a ratio represents","setup":"A bag holds only red counters and blue counters, in the ratio red : blue = 3 : 5. Altogether there are 48 counters. Work out the fraction of the counters that are red, and the number of counters that are blue.","steps":["The ratio has 3 + 5 = 8 equal shares in the whole bag","Red is 3 of those 8 shares, so red is 3/8 of the counters and blue is 5/8","One share is 48 ÷ 8 = 6 counters","Blue = 5/8 of 48 = 5 × 6 = 30 counters"],"cues":["How many equal shares does the whole bag split into?","How many of those shares are red, and how many are blue?","How many counters are there in one share?","How many shares are blue, and what is each one worth?"]},{"title":"A percentage as a multiplier","setup":"A jacket is priced at £240. In a sale every price is reduced by 35%. A calculator may be used. Work out the amount taken off the jacket and the sale price.","steps":["35% means 35 parts per hundred, so the multiplier is 35 ÷ 100 = 0.35","Amount taken off = 0.35 × 240 = £84","A 35% reduction leaves 100% − 35% = 65% of the price, multiplier 0.65","Sale price = 0.65 × 240 = £156"],"cues":["What decimal does 35% become?","Which operation does the word 'of' stand for?","What percentage of the price is left after the reduction?","Which multiplier takes the original price straight to the sale price?"]},{"title":"Finding the whole from a fraction of it","setup":"In a class survey, 3/7 of the students walk to school and the rest do not. 18 students walk to school. Work out how many students are in the class, and how many of them do not walk.","steps":["3/7 of the class is 18 students, so 1/7 of the class is 18 ÷ 3 = 6 students","The whole class is seven sevenths: 7 × 6 = 42 students","The fraction who do not walk is 1 − 3/7 = 4/7","Students who do not walk = 4/7 of 42 = 4 × 6 = 24"],"cues":["How many students does one seventh stand for?","How many sevenths make up the whole class?","What fraction is left when 3/7 is taken from the whole?","What is each seventh worth, and how many are needed?"]},{"title":"Comparing two offers without a calculator","setup":"(non-calculator) Shop A sells a game for £60 and takes 15% off it in a sale. Shop B sells the same game for £54 and takes 1/10 off it in a sale. Work out the sale price in each shop and decide which shop sells the game more cheaply.","steps":["Shop A: 10% of £60 is £6, and 5% is half of that, £3","15% of £60 = 6 + 3 = £9, so Shop A's sale price is 60 − 9 = £51","Shop B: 1/10 of £54 = 54 ÷ 10 = £5.40","Shop B's sale price is 54 − 5.40 = £48.60","£48.60 is less than £51, so Shop B sells the game more cheaply"],"cues":["What is 10% of £60, and how do you get 5% from it?","Which two of those percentages add to give 15%?","What does dividing by 10 do to £54?","What is left when that reduction is taken off £54?","Which of the two sale prices is smaller?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write 0.24 as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"0.24 = 24/100 = 6/25"},{"q":"Marbles are blue or yellow in the ratio blue : yellow = 5 : 4. What fraction of the marbles are yellow?","a":"4/9, because there are 5 + 4 = 9 shares"},{"q":"Write down the multiplier that decreases an amount by 12%.","a":"0.88, since 100% − 12% = 88%"},{"q":"Work out 30% of 70.","a":"21, from 0.3 × 70"}]},"room":{"facts":["A terminating decimal becomes a fraction by writing the decimal digits over 10, 100, 1000 … — one zero for each decimal place — then cancelling: 0.375 = 375/1000 = 3/8.","A fraction becomes a decimal by dividing the numerator by the denominator, or by scaling the denominator to 10, 100 or 1000: 7/2 = 7 ÷ 2 = 3.5 and 13/20 = 65/100 = 0.65.","To order numbers written in different forms, convert them all into one form first, compare there, then write the answer in the forms the question used.","A ratio a : b has a + b equal shares, so the first part is a/(a + b) of the whole and the second part is b/(a + b).","'Of' means multiply: p% of x = (p ÷ 100) × x, and a/b of x = (a ÷ b) × x.","An increase of p% has multiplier 1 + p/100; a decrease of p% has multiplier 1 − p/100. So +6% uses 1.06 and −18% uses 0.82."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Write 0.375 as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"3/8","sol":"0.375 has three decimal places, so 0.375 = 375/1000. Dividing the numerator and denominator by 125 gives 375/1000 = 3/8. B1 for 3/8 (total 1 mark).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The digits after the point can be written over a power of 10.","Three decimal places means a denominator of 1000, and the fraction then cancels down.","The first line is 0.375 = 375/1000 — now divide both parts by their highest common factor."],"codes":["N10"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write 3.5 as an improper fraction in its simplest form.","a":"7/2","sol":"3.5 = 3 + 0.5 = 3 + 1/2, and 3 = 6/2, so 3.5 = 6/2 + 1/2 = 7/2. (Or 3.5 = 35/10 = 7/2.) B1 for 7/2 (total 1 mark).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["One decimal place here is worth a half, so count in halves.","Write the whole number part as halves and add the half that is left over, or put 3.5 over 10 and cancel.","The first line is 3.5 = 35/10 — now cancel using the highest common factor of 35 and 10."],"codes":["N10"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Work out 24% of 150.","a":"36","sol":"24% as a multiplier is 24 ÷ 100 = 0.24. M1 for 0.24 × 150 (oe, for example 150 ÷ 100 × 24). 0.24 × 150 = 36. A1 for 36 (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A percentage is a number of parts per hundred, and 'of' is an instruction to multiply.","Turn the percentage into a decimal multiplier, then apply it to the amount.","The first line is 24 ÷ 100 = the multiplier — then multiply 150 by it."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 3/4 of 96.","a":"72","sol":"'Of' means multiply, so 3/4 of 96 = 3/4 × 96. M1 for 96 ÷ 4 = 24 (one quarter). Then 3 × 24 = 72. A1 for 72 (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The fraction is acting as an operator on 96.","Find one quarter of the amount first, then take three of them.","The first line is 96 ÷ 4 — then multiply that result by the numerator."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. The sweets in a jar are red or green in the ratio red : green = 2 : 7. What fraction of the sweets are green?","a":"7/9","sol":"The ratio splits the jar into 2 + 7 = 9 equal shares. B1 for 9 shares (or for a denominator of 9). Green takes 7 of those 9 shares. B1 for 7/9 (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A ratio splits the whole jar into equal shares.","Add the two numbers in the ratio to find how many shares the whole jar is split into.","The first line is total shares = 2 + 7 — then write the green shares over that total."],"codes":["N11"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A number line game uses cards. Four cards are already laid out in order, smallest first: 0.3, 7/20, 2/5, 0.45. Jo turns over a card showing 9/25. Write down which two of the four laid-out cards Jo's card must be placed between.","a":"7/20 and 2/5","sol":"Scale 9/25 so that its denominator is 100: 9/25 = 36/100 = 0.36. M1 for 9/25 written as 0.36 (oe 36/100). The laid-out cards as decimals are 0.3, 7/20 = 0.35, 2/5 = 0.4 and 0.45. M1 for both fractions in the line written in the same form as 9/25. Since 0.35 < 0.36 < 0.4, the card goes between 7/20 and 2/5. A1 for 7/20 and 2/5 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A new number cannot be slotted into the line until it is written in the same form as the cards already there.","Change Jo's card and the fractions in the line into decimals, then see which gap the new value falls into.","The first line is 9/25 = …/100 — then write the two fractions in the line as decimals as well."],"codes":["N10"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A car is worth £12 400. During its first year the value falls by 18%. Work out the value of the car at the end of the first year.","a":"£10 168","sol":"A fall of 18% leaves 100% − 18% = 82% of the value, so the multiplier is 0.82. M1 for 0.82 (or for 0.18 × 12400 = 2232). M1 for 0.82 × 12400 (or for 12400 − 2232). 0.82 × 12400 = 10168. A1 for £10 168 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A percentage decrease can be done in a single multiplication.","Find the percentage of the value that is left, turn it into a decimal multiplier, then apply it to the starting value.","The first line is 100% − 18% = … % — write that as a decimal and multiply the value by it."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A club has 320 members. 3/8 of the members are under 18. Work out how many members are 18 or over.","a":"200","sol":"M1 for 320 ÷ 8 = 40 (one eighth). M1 for 3 × 40 = 120 members under 18, then 320 − 120 (oe 5/8 × 320). 320 − 120 = 200. A1 for 200 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The fraction given counts only part of the club.","Either find how many are under 18 and subtract, or find the fraction who are 18 or over first.","The first line is 320 ÷ 8 — then decide how many eighths you need."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A necklace is made only from gold beads and silver beads, in the ratio gold : silver = 4 : 9. The necklace has 36 silver beads. Work out the total number of beads in the necklace.","a":"52","sol":"The ratio has 4 + 9 = 13 equal shares, so the silver beads are 9/13 of the whole. M1 for 13 shares or for silver = 9/13 of the total. M1 for 36 ÷ 9 = 4 beads in one share (oe 36 ÷ 9 × 13). Then 13 × 4 = 52. A1 for 52 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The 36 beads are not the whole necklace, only the silver shares of it.","Work out what one share is worth, then how many shares the whole necklace has.","The first line is total shares = 4 + 9 — then find one share from the silver beads."],"codes":["N11"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A train ticket costs £48.50. The price increases by 6%. Work out the new price of the ticket.","a":"£51.41","sol":"An increase of 6% gives the multiplier 1 + 6/100 = 1.06. M1 for 1.06 × 48.50 (or for 0.06 × 48.50 = 2.91 followed by addition). 1.06 × 48.50 = 51.41. A1 for £51.41 (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["An increase keeps all of the original price and adds more on top of it.","Use a single multiplier greater than 1, or find the extra amount and add it on.","The first line is 1 + 6/100 = the multiplier — then multiply £48.50 by it."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 45% of £80.","a":"£36","sol":"10% of 80 = 8, so 40% = 4 × 8 = 32. M1 for 10% = 8 (oe 1% = 0.8). 5% is half of 10%, so 5% = 4. M1 for combining correct parts, for example 32 + 4. Then 32 + 4 = 36. A1 for £36 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["45% can be built out of percentages that are easy to find by hand.","Find 10% of the amount, then use it to build the larger and the smaller pieces you need.","The first line is 10% of 80 = 80 ÷ 10 — now build 45% from parts of that."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. In a car park the vehicles are cars and vans only, in the ratio cars : vans = 7 : 2. There are 126 cars. 25% of the vans are white. Work out how many vans are white.","a":"9","sol":"M1 for 126 ÷ 7 = 18 (one share). M1 for 2 × 18 = 36 vans (oe cars are 7/9 of the total, so the total is 162 and the vans are 162 − 126 = 36). M1 for 0.25 × 36 (oe 36 ÷ 4). 0.25 × 36 = 9. A1 for 9 (total 4 marks).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The 126 cars tell you what one share of the ratio is worth.","Find the number of vans first, then take the percentage of that number.","The first line is 126 ÷ 7 — that gives the value of one share."],"codes":["N11","N12"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A bottle holds 0.75 litres of juice. Priya drinks 2/5 of the juice in the bottle. Write the amount Priya drinks as a fraction of a litre, in its simplest form.","a":"3/10 litre","sol":"0.75 = 75/100 = 3/4. M1 for 0.75 written as 3/4. Then 2/5 of 3/4 = 2/5 × 3/4 = 6/20. M1 for 2/5 × 3/4 (oe). 6/20 = 3/10. A1 for 3/10 litre (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["The answer is wanted as a fraction, so the capacity is in the wrong form to start with.","Change the capacity into a fraction, then read 'of' as a multiplication of two fractions.","The first line is 0.75 = 75/100 — cancel that, then multiply by 2/5."],"codes":["N10","N12"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A factory made 2.4 × 10⁵ bolts in a week. 3/8 of the bolts were made on the night shift. 15% of the night-shift bolts failed a test. Work out how many night-shift bolts failed the test. Give your answer in standard form.","a":"1.35 × 10⁴","sol":"2.4 × 10⁵ = 240 000. M1 for 240000 ÷ 8 × 3 (oe 3/8 × 2.4 × 10⁵). That gives 90 000 night-shift bolts. A1 for 90 000. M1 for 0.15 × 90000. 0.15 × 90000 = 13500 = 1.35 × 10⁴. A1 for 1.35 × 10⁴ (total 4 marks).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Writing the standard form number out in full makes the fraction easy to apply.","Take the fraction of the total first, then the percentage of that result, and convert back at the end.","The first line is 2.4 × 10⁵ = … — then take 3/8 of it."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A box holds 60 chocolates. 2/5 of them are milk chocolates and 25% of them are dark chocolates. The rest are white chocolates. Work out how many white chocolates are in the box.","a":"21","sol":"M1 for 60 ÷ 5 × 2 = 24 milk chocolates. M1 for 25% of 60 = 60 ÷ 4 = 15 dark chocolates. M1 for 60 − (24 + 15). 60 − 39 = 21. A1 for 21 (total 4 marks).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Both operators act on the same 60 chocolates.","Work out the milk and the dark chocolates separately, then take both away from the total.","The first line is 60 ÷ 5 — then decide how many fifths are milk."],"codes":["N12"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 0.85 as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"17/20"},{"q":"Work out 2/5 of 150.","a":"60"},{"q":"Coins in a purse are 20p or 50p in the ratio 20p : 50p = 5 : 3. What fraction of the coins are 50p?","a":"3/8"},{"q":"Write down the single number you multiply by to increase an amount by 7%.","a":"1.07"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Terminating decimal","back":"A decimal that stops after a fixed number of digits, such as 0.375 or 3.5. It can always be written as a fraction with denominator 10, 100, 1000 …","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"Turning a terminating decimal into a fraction","back":"Put the decimal digits over a power of 10, one zero per decimal place, then cancel: 0.375 = 375/1000 = 3/8.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"Turning a fraction into a decimal","back":"Divide the numerator by the denominator, or scale the denominator to 10, 100 or 1000: 7/8 = 7 ÷ 8 = 0.875 and 13/20 = 65/100 = 0.65.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"Ordering numbers written in different forms","back":"Convert them all into one form first, usually decimals, compare there, then write the answer in the forms the question used.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"Decimal equivalents worth knowing by heart","back":"1/2 = 0.5, 1/4 = 0.25, 3/4 = 0.75, 1/5 = 0.2, 1/10 = 0.1, 1/8 = 0.125, 3/8 = 0.375.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"A mixed number written as an improper fraction","back":"Multiply the whole number by the denominator and add the numerator: 3.5 = 3 and 1/2 = (3 × 2 + 1)/2 = 7/2.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"The number of shares in the ratio a : b","back":"a + b. The ratio splits the whole into a + b equal shares, and each share has the same value.","code":"N11","open":false},{"front":"The fraction of the whole each part of the ratio a : b represents","back":"The first part is a/(a + b) of the whole and the second part is b/(a + b). For 3 : 5 these are 3/8 and 5/8.","code":"N11","open":false},{"front":"Finding the whole when one part of a ratio is known","back":"Divide the known part by its number of shares to get one share, then multiply by the total number of shares.","code":"N11","open":false},{"front":"Percentage","back":"A number of parts per hundred. 35% means 35/100, which is 0.35 as a decimal.","code":"N12","open":false},{"front":"The word 'of' in a fraction or percentage calculation","back":"It means multiply: 3/4 of 96 = 3/4 × 96 = 72, and 35% of 240 = 0.35 × 240 = 84.","code":"N12","open":false},{"front":"A percentage as a decimal multiplier","back":"Divide the percentage by 100: 35% becomes 0.35, 6% becomes 0.06 and 120% becomes 1.2.","code":"N12","open":false},{"front":"The multiplier for an increase of p%","back":"1 + p/100. An increase of 6% uses 1.06 and an increase of 20% uses 1.2.","code":"N12","open":false},{"front":"The multiplier for a decrease of p%","back":"1 − p/100, because 100% − p% of the amount is left. A fall of 18% uses 0.82 and a fall of 35% uses 0.65.","code":"N12","open":false},{"front":"Two ways to work out 15% of an amount without a calculator","back":"Find 10%, halve it for 5% and add the two; multiply the amount by 0.15 using written multiplication; take 15/100 of the amount and cancel to 3/20.","code":"N12","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Terminating decimal to fraction: decimal digits over 10ⁿ, where n is the number of decimal places, then cancel — 0.375 = 375/1000 = 3/8. Recall: not in the specification's Appendix, so nothing is printed for it.","Fraction to decimal: numerator ÷ denominator, or scale the denominator to 10, 100 or 1000 — 7/2 = 3.5, 13/20 = 0.65. Recall: not in the Appendix.","Fractions from the ratio a : b: total shares = a + b, first part = a/(a + b) of the whole, second part = b/(a + b). Recall: not in the Appendix.","One share of a ratio from a known part: one share = known part ÷ its number of shares; whole = one share × (a + b). Recall: not in the Appendix.","A fraction as an operator: a/b of x = (a ÷ b) × x. Recall: not in the Appendix.","A percentage as an operator: p% of x = (p ÷ 100) × x, so p% becomes the decimal multiplier p ÷ 100. Recall: not in the Appendix.","Multiplier for a percentage change: increase of p% → × (1 + p/100); decrease of p% → × (1 − p/100). Recall: not in the Appendix.","What the Appendix gives: its three lists hold the quadratic formula, the circle formulae, Pythagoras' theorem, the trigonometric ratios, the sine and cosine rules, Area = ½ab sin C, the trapezium and prism formulae, compound interest, the two probability formulae, the cone and sphere formulae and the kinematics formulae. None of them belongs to fractions, decimals and percentages, so every result above must be written from memory.","Nearest relative in the Appendix: compound interest, Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, is list 2 — 'expected to know or be able to derive' — and it is repeated multiplication by the same percentage multiplier used above. It belongs to the ratio room (R16), not to this one, and section 4 of the appendix shows that the Foundation formulae sheet does print it, so on the day it is given rather than recalled.","What governs on the day: since 2022 Ofqual has required a formulae sheet in every GCSE Mathematics paper, and AQA issues one per tier. The sheet issued with your paper is what decides which results are given to you — where it prints a formula the Appendix says must be known, the sheet governs. Section 4 of the course appendix records what the Foundation tier sheet prints: the trapezium and prism formulae, the circle formulae, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios, compound interest and the addition rule for probability. Not one conversion, ordering or operator result from this room appears on it, so every result above must still be written from memory."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:11:51Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Mathematically clean and squarely Foundation: all 15 bank answers, the 4 exit-ticket answers and the 4 lesson checks re-solve correctly, every sol's M/A/B marks add to its tariff, 8 questions are non-calculator with hand-workable numbers, difficulty splits 5/6/4, and the Formulae sheet's claims match appendix.md exactly (no FDP formula appears in any of the three lists, and compound interest is correctly placed in list 2 and assigned to R16) - publish it, but consider replacing the ordering question so it does not mirror the first worked example."}}},"number-measures":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"number-measures","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Almost every question on a Foundation paper ends with a number that has to carry a unit and an accuracy: a mass in grams, a cost to the nearest penny, a length rounded to the nearest centimetre. This room is where those habits are built. You learn the metric conversion factors by heart, you learn to work with compound measures such as speed and rate of pay, you learn to check a calculator display by rounding everything to one significant figure first, and you learn to say — in inequality notation — exactly how far a rounded or truncated measurement can be from the value written down. The marks are real: an unrounded answer, a missing unit or a silent conversion loses the accuracy mark even when the method is perfect, and estimation questions give the method mark for the rounded values you write down, not for the final figure.","idea":[{"h":"Standard units and compound measures","t":"Length, mass, capacity, time and money each have a ladder of metric units, and you are expected to know the factors: 1 km = 1000 m, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 cm = 10 mm; 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 kg = 1000 g; 1 litre = 1000 ml = 1000 cm³ and 1 m³ = 1000 litres. Area and volume factors are the length factor squared and cubed, so 1 m² = 10 000 cm² and 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³. A compound measure is built from two of these — speed = distance ÷ time, density = mass ÷ volume, pressure = force ÷ area, and everyday rates such as pounds per hour or litres per minute. The unit tells you the operation: m/s means metres divided by seconds, so the two quantities must be in metres and seconds before you divide. Imperial units appear only with the conversion printed in the question.","code":"N13"},{"h":"Estimating, and using an estimate to check","t":"To estimate, round every number in the calculation to one significant figure, write those rounded values down, and work out the simpler calculation — the written rounded values are what earns the method mark, so never do it in your head. Use the symbol ≈ for 'is approximately equal to'. Dividing by a number smaller than 1 makes the answer bigger, which is exactly where calculator slips show up: ÷ 0.2 is the same as × 5. The estimate is then compared with the real answer to judge whether it is sensible; if a display is ten times the estimate, a decimal point or a zero has gone astray. In context the estimate also tells you whether an answer is reasonable at all — a room 300 m long or a bill of £2 000 for a sandwich fails the sense check.","code":"N14"},{"h":"Rounding, and error intervals from rounding or truncation","t":"Rounding to a number of decimal places counts digits after the point; rounding to significant figures counts from the first non-zero digit, so 0.008 361 to 2 significant figures is 0.0084. Look at the next digit: 5 or more rounds up, otherwise the kept digit stays. Choose the accuracy the context needs — money to the nearest penny, people to a whole number — and never round part-way through a calculation, because rounded intermediate values push the final answer off. An error interval says, with inequality notation, every value that would have produced the number written down: rounding to the nearest unit gives value − half a unit ≤ x < value + half a unit, while truncating (cutting the extra digits off) gives value ≤ x < value + 1 in the last place kept. The lower limit is included, the upper limit is not.","code":"N15"},{"h":"Limits of accuracy, and what they mean in context","t":"A measurement given to a stated accuracy is not exact, and questions ask you to apply that. '24 cm to the nearest centimetre' means the true length is anywhere from 23.5 cm up to, but never reaching, 24.5 cm; 'to the nearest 10 g' widens the gap to 5 g either side. Interpreting the limits is what earns the reasoning mark: whether a box is certain to fit a gap, whether a load is definitely under a weight limit, whether a stated value could really be exactly the figure at the top of the interval. Say what the limits are, then say what they mean for the situation — a value at the very top of an interval would round to the next figure up, so it is never included, and a measurement whose whole interval lies below a stated limit is certain to be under it. Combining the bounds of two different measurements — adding, subtracting, multiplying or dividing them to find the extremes of a calculated value — is Higher tier work and is not needed here.","code":"N16"}],"examples":[{"title":"Converting units and working with a rate of pay","setup":"A kitchen worktop is 2.4 m long. A watering can holds 3500 cm³ of water. Kofi is paid £9.40 for each hour he works and he works 2.5 hours. Write the worktop's length in centimetres, write the can's capacity in litres, and work out Kofi's pay.","steps":["Length: 1 m = 100 cm, so 2.4 m = 2.4 × 100 = 240 cm.","Capacity: 1 litre = 1000 cm³, so 3500 cm³ = 3500 ÷ 1000 = 3.5 litres.","Pay: pay = rate × time = 9.40 × 2.5.","9.40 × 2.5 = 23.50, so Kofi is paid £23.50; the worktop is 240 cm long and the can holds 3.5 litres."],"cues":["How many centimetres are there in one metre?","How many cm³ make one litre?","Which calculation turns an hourly rate into a total pay?","What does that multiplication come to, in pounds and pence?"]},{"title":"Estimating to check a calculator display","setup":"A student works out 29.3 × 4.87 ÷ 0.51 on a calculator and the display reads 27.98. Estimate the answer by rounding each number to one significant figure, then use the estimate to decide whether the display is sensible.","steps":["Round each value to 1 significant figure: 29.3 ≈ 30, 4.87 ≈ 5, 0.51 ≈ 0.5.","Estimate = 30 × 5 ÷ 0.5 = 150 ÷ 0.5.","Dividing by 0.5 doubles a number, so 150 ÷ 0.5 = 300: the estimate is 300, while the display reads 27.98.","300 is about 10 times 27.98, so the display is not sensible; 0.51 was most likely keyed in as 5.1."],"cues":["What is each number to one significant figure?","Which simpler calculation replaces the original one?","What does dividing by 0.5 do to a number?","How do the two figures compare, and what keying slip would explain it?"]},{"title":"Rounding to decimal places, to significant figures and to fit the context","setup":"π is 3.14159 to five decimal places. A town's population is 48 620. Seven identical notebooks cost £2.35 in total. Round π to 3 decimal places, round the population to 2 significant figures, and work out the cost of one notebook to an appropriate degree of accuracy.","steps":["Three decimal places keeps three digits after the point; the digit after them is 5, so the third one rounds up: 3.142.","The first two significant digits of 48 620 are 4 and 8; the next digit is 6, so round up to 49 000.","One notebook costs 2.35 ÷ 7 = 0.3357… pounds.","Money is given to the nearest penny, so 0.3357… rounds to £0.34 (seven at 34p is £2.38, not £2.35: that gap is what rounding to the nearest penny means); π ≈ 3.142 and the population ≈ 49 000."],"cues":["Which digit decides whether the third decimal place changes?","Which digits of the population are significant here, and which digit decides?","Which calculation gives the cost of a single notebook?","To what accuracy should an amount of money be given?"]},{"title":"The error interval of a length measured to the nearest centimetre","setup":"A pencil case is measured as 24 cm long, correct to the nearest centimetre. Using inequality notation, write down the error interval for the true length, L cm, and state the upper limit of the interval.","steps":["'To the nearest centimetre' means the true length was rounded to the nearest 1 cm, so it lies within half a centimetre either side: half of 1 cm is 0.5 cm.","Smallest possible length = 24 − 0.5 = 23.5 cm; largest = 24 + 0.5 = 24.5 cm.","Every value from 23.5 cm up to but not including 24.5 cm rounds to 24 cm, so 23.5 ≤ L < 24.5.","The interval stops at 24.5, so the upper limit is 24.5 cm, which the length can approach but never reach."],"cues":["How far either side of the stated length could the truth lie?","What are the two limits of that interval?","Which limit is included and which is not, and why?","Which of the two limits answers the second part?"]},{"title":"Changing a compound unit and using it","setup":"A cyclist rides at a steady speed of 8 m/s. Convert this speed into kilometres per hour, then work out how far the cyclist travels in 45 minutes, giving the distance in kilometres.","steps":["In one hour there are 3600 seconds, so the cyclist covers 8 × 3600 = 28 800 m in an hour.","1 km = 1000 m, so 28 800 m = 28 800 ÷ 1000 = 28.8 km, that is 28.8 km/h.","45 minutes = 45 ÷ 60 = 0.75 hours.","Distance = speed × time = 28.8 × 0.75 = 21.6 km."],"cues":["How many seconds are there in one hour?","How many metres make one kilometre?","What is 45 minutes as a decimal part of an hour?","Which calculation gives the distance travelled?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Change 0.85 litres into cm³.","a":"850 cm³"},{"q":"Round 0.008 361 to 2 significant figures.","a":"0.0084"},{"q":"Work out an estimate for 0.48 × 612 by rounding each number to one significant figure.","a":"0.5 × 600 = 300"},{"q":"A bag of sugar has mass 500 g, correct to the nearest 10 g. Write down the error interval for the mass, m grams.","a":"495 ≤ m < 505"}]},"room":{"facts":["Length, mass and capacity: 1 km = 1000 m, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 cm = 10 mm; 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 g = 1000 mg; 1 litre = 1000 ml, 1 ml = 1 cm³, 1 litre = 1000 cm³, 1 m³ = 1000 litres.","Area and volume factors are the length factor squared and cubed: 1 cm² = 100 mm², 1 m² = 10 000 cm²; 1 cm³ = 1000 mm³, 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³.","Time: 1 minute = 60 seconds, 1 hour = 60 minutes = 3600 seconds; a decimal part of an hour is minutes ÷ 60, so 45 minutes = 0.75 hours.","Compound measures: speed = distance ÷ time, density = mass ÷ volume, pressure = force ÷ area; amount = rate × time, and the two quantities must be in the units named by the compound unit before you divide.","To estimate, round every number to 1 significant figure, write the rounded values down and work the simpler calculation out; ÷ 0.5 is × 2 and ÷ 0.2 is × 5.","Rounded to the nearest u: value − u/2 ≤ x < value + u/2. Truncated at a given place: value ≤ x < value + 1 in the last place kept. The lower limit is included, the upper limit is not."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Round 7.0649 to 2 decimal places.","a":"7.06","sol":"Two decimal places keeps the digits 0 and 6 after the point. The next digit is 4, which is less than 5, so the second decimal place does not change: 7.0649 → 7.06 B1. (1 mark: B1)","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Decimal places are counted after the decimal point, not from the start of the number.","Keep two digits after the point, then look at the digit immediately after them to decide what happens.","Mark the cut after the second decimal digit and read the deciding digit just past the cut."],"codes":["N15"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Round 30 748 to 2 significant figures.","a":"31 000","sol":"Significant figures are counted from the first non-zero digit, so the first two are 3 and 0. The next digit is 7, which is 5 or more, so the second significant figure rounds up from 0 to 1, and the remaining digits become zeros to hold the place value: 31 000 B1. (1 mark: B1)","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Significant figures start at the first non-zero digit of the number.","Keep two significant digits, then use the next digit to decide whether the second one goes up.","Write the two significant digits down and replace every digit after them with a zero of the right place value."],"codes":["N15"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Change 4.6 kg into grams.","a":"4600 g","sol":"1 kg = 1000 g, and grams are smaller than kilograms so the number gets bigger: 4.6 × 1000 = 4600 g B1. (1 mark: B1)","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Grams are smaller than kilograms, so the number of them must be larger.","Use the conversion factor between kilograms and grams and multiply by it.","Write the mass in kilograms multiplied by the number of grams in one kilogram."],"codes":["N13"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out an estimate for 6.1 × 39.6. You must write down the rounded values you use.","a":"6 × 40 = 240","sol":"Round each number to 1 significant figure: 6.1 ≈ 6 and 39.6 ≈ 40, giving 6 × 40 M1. 6 × 40 = 240, so the estimate is 240 A1. (2 marks: M1 A1)","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["An estimate swaps awkward numbers for easy ones of roughly the same size.","Round each number to one significant figure before you multiply anything.","Write the two rounded values side by side as a multiplication, then work it out."],"codes":["N14"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A number z is truncated to 1 decimal place and the result is 5.3. Write down the error interval for z.","a":"5.3 ≤ z < 5.4","sol":"Truncating cuts off the digits after the first decimal place rather than rounding, so nothing is ever rounded up and z is at least 5.3: 5.3 ≤ z B1. Every value below 5.4 still truncates to 5.3, while 5.4 itself truncates to 5.4, so z < 5.4, giving 5.3 ≤ z < 5.4 B1. (2 marks: B1 B1)","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Truncating means chopping the later digits off, not rounding them.","Decide the smallest value that would be chopped down to this one, then the first value that would not.","Write an inequality holding z between two values one tenth apart, and decide which end chopping the digits off puts it at."],"codes":["N15"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out an estimate for (812 − 396) ÷ 19.4. You must write down the rounded values you use.","a":"(800 − 400) ÷ 20 = 20","sol":"Round each number to 1 significant figure: 812 ≈ 800, 396 ≈ 400 and 19.4 ≈ 20 M1. The bracket is worked out first: 800 − 400 = 400, so the estimate becomes 400 ÷ 20 M1. 400 ÷ 20 = 20, so the estimate is 20 A1. (3 marks: M1 M1 A1)","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["An estimate replaces every awkward number with a one-digit number of about the same size.","Round all three numbers to one significant figure, work the bracket out first, then divide.","Write the two rounded values inside the bracket as a subtraction, then divide by the rounded divisor."],"codes":["N14"]},{"q":"A car travels 14.5 km on each litre of fuel. Fuel costs £1.48 per litre. Work out the cost of the fuel for a journey of 232 km. A calculator may be used.","a":"£23.68","sol":"Fuel needed = 232 ÷ 14.5 M1 = 16 litres. Cost = 16 × 1.48 M1 = 23.68, so the fuel costs £23.68 A1. (3 marks: M1 M1 A1)","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The journey has to be turned into litres before it can be turned into money.","Divide the distance by the distance covered per litre, then multiply by the price of a litre.","Write the journey length divided by the kilometres per litre as the first line."],"codes":["N13"]},{"q":"A 2.5 kg bag of rice costs £3.79. Work out the cost per kilogram, correct to the nearest penny. A calculator may be used.","a":"£1.52","sol":"Cost per kilogram = 3.79 ÷ 2.5 M1 = 1.516. The nearest penny is two decimal places, and the next digit is 6, so 1.516 rounds up to £1.52 A1. (2 marks: M1 A1)","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Cost per kilogram means the cost of one kilogram.","Divide the price by the mass, then round the result to the accuracy money is written in.","Write the price divided by the number of kilograms, and keep all the decimals until the end."],"codes":["N15","N13"]},{"q":"A length of ribbon is 3.6 m, correct to the nearest 0.1 m. (a) Write down the error interval for the length, L metres. (b) Write down the shortest possible length of the ribbon, in centimetres. A calculator may be used.","a":"(a) 3.55 ≤ L < 3.65 (b) 355 cm","sol":"(a) 'To the nearest 0.1 m' puts the true length within half of 0.1 m, that is 0.05 m, of 3.6 m, so the smallest it can be is 3.6 − 0.05 = 3.55 m B1. A length of 3.65 m or more would round to 3.7 m, so the interval is 3.55 ≤ L < 3.65 B1. (b) The shortest possible length is the lower limit, 3.55 m, and 1 m = 100 cm, so 3.55 × 100 = 355 cm B1. (3 marks: B1 B1 B1)","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A length given to the nearest 0.1 m is not exact, and the answer to the second part is not in metres.","Halve the accuracy the length is given to and take that either side, decide which limit is included, then convert the lower one.","Write the stated length minus half of 0.1 m as the lower limit of the interval."],"codes":["N16","N15","N13"]},{"q":"Water flows into a paddling pool at a rate of 0.25 litres per second. Work out how many minutes it takes to put 90 litres of water into the pool. A calculator may be used.","a":"6 minutes","sol":"Time in seconds = 90 ÷ 0.25 M1 = 360 seconds. 1 minute = 60 seconds, so the time = 360 ÷ 60 M1 = 6 minutes A1. (3 marks: M1 M1 A1)","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The rate is given per second, so the first time you find will be in seconds.","Divide the volume by the rate, then convert the time into minutes.","Write the volume divided by the litres per second as the first line."],"codes":["N13"]},{"q":"A water tank is a cuboid measuring 1.2 m by 0.8 m by 0.5 m. A tap fills the tank at 12 litres per minute. Work out how long it takes to fill the empty tank, in minutes. A calculator may be used.","a":"40 minutes","sol":"Volume = 1.2 × 0.8 × 0.5 M1 = 0.48 m³ A1. 1 m³ = 1000 litres, so the tank holds 0.48 × 1000 = 480 litres M1. Time = 480 ÷ 12 = 40 minutes A1. (4 marks: M1 A1 M1 A1)","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The tap is measured in litres but the tank is measured in metres.","Find the volume of the cuboid, change it into litres, then divide by the rate of filling.","Write the three lengths multiplied together as the first line, keeping the units in metres."],"codes":["N13"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A hall floor is a rectangle measuring 19.2 m by 8.7 m. Each floor tile covers 0.48 m². Work out an estimate for the number of tiles needed to cover the floor. You must write down the rounded values you use.","a":"360 tiles","sol":"Round each number to 1 significant figure: 19.2 ≈ 20, 8.7 ≈ 9, 0.48 ≈ 0.5 M1. Estimated floor area = 20 × 9 = 180 m² M1. Number of tiles = 180 ÷ 0.5 M1, and dividing by 0.5 doubles, so the estimate is 360 tiles A1. (4 marks: M1 M1 M1 A1)","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Estimate the area of the floor before you think about the tiles.","Round all three numbers to one significant figure, find the area, then divide by the area of one tile.","Write the two rounded side lengths multiplied together as the first line."],"codes":["N14","N13"]},{"q":"A gap in a bookcase measures 90 cm, correct to the nearest centimetre. A box is exactly 89.6 cm wide. Sam says the box is certain to fit in the gap. Is Sam right? You must show your working. A calculator may be used.","a":"No — the gap could be as small as 89.5 cm, which is less than 89.6 cm","sol":"The gap g is given to the nearest centimetre, so the truth lies within half a centimetre of 90 cm and the smallest it can be is 89.5 cm B1. A gap of 90.5 cm or more would round to 91 cm, so 89.5 ≤ g < 90.5 B1. The box is 89.6 cm wide, which is wider than the smallest gap the measurement allows M1. So the gap may be too narrow and the box is not certain to fit — Sam is not right A1. (4 marks: B1 B1 M1 A1)","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The width of the box is exact, but the gap is not.","Write an error interval for the gap, then test the box against the least room that interval allows.","Write the stated gap minus half a centimetre as the lower limit of the interval."],"codes":["N16","N15"]},{"q":"Jo uses a calculator to work out 8.6% of £2480 and the display reads 2132.8. (a) By rounding, show that the display cannot be right. (b) Work out 8.6% of £2480, correct to the nearest penny. A calculator may be used.","a":"(a) An estimate of about £180 shows £2132.80 is far too big (b) £213.28","sol":"(a) To 1 significant figure, 8.6% ≈ 9% and £2480 ≈ £2000 M1. 9% of £2000 = 0.09 × 2000 = £180, and the display of 2132.8 is more than ten times that, so it cannot be right A1. (b) 8.6% of 2480 = 0.086 × 2480 M1 = 213.28, so the answer is £213.28 A1. (4 marks: M1 A1 M1 A1)","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A percentage of a sum of money can be estimated the same way as any other calculation.","Round the percentage and the amount to one significant figure to get a rough value, then compare it with the display.","Write the rounded percentage of the rounded amount as the first line of part (a)."],"codes":["N14","N15"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Round 0.04728 to 2 significant figures.","a":"0.047"},{"q":"Change 2500 cm³ into litres.","a":"2.5 litres"},{"q":"Work out an estimate for 41.6 ÷ 7.8 by rounding each number to 1 significant figure.","a":"40 ÷ 8 = 5"},{"q":"A time is 15 seconds, correct to the nearest second. Write down the error interval for the time, t seconds.","a":"14.5 ≤ t < 15.5"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Metric length conversions","back":"1 km = 1000 m; 1 m = 100 cm; 1 cm = 10 mm.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Metric mass conversions","back":"1 tonne = 1000 kg; 1 kg = 1000 g; 1 g = 1000 mg.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Metric capacity and volume conversions","back":"1 litre = 1000 ml; 1 ml = 1 cm³; 1 litre = 1000 cm³; 1 m³ = 1000 litres.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Area conversion factors","back":"1 cm² = 100 mm²; 1 m² = 10 000 cm². The area factor is the length factor squared.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Volume conversion factors","back":"1 cm³ = 1000 mm³; 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³. The volume factor is the length factor cubed.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Compound measure","back":"A measure made from two others, its unit written as one divided by another: speed in m/s, density in g/cm³, pressure in N/m², pay in £ per hour.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"The speed, distance and time formulae","back":"speed = distance ÷ time; distance = speed × time; time = distance ÷ speed. The units must match the compound unit, such as metres with seconds for m/s.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Time conversions","back":"1 minute = 60 seconds; 1 hour = 60 minutes = 3600 seconds. A decimal part of an hour is minutes ÷ 60.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"How to estimate the answer to a calculation","back":"Round every number to 1 significant figure, write the rounded values down, then work out the simpler calculation. Use ≈ for 'approximately equal to'.","code":"N14","open":false},{"front":"Significant figures","back":"Counting starts at the first non-zero digit; every digit after it counts, including zeros. So 0.008 361 to 2 significant figures is 0.0084.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"Rounding to a given number of decimal places","back":"Keep that many digits after the point. If the next digit is 5 or more, add 1 to the last digit kept; otherwise leave it as it is.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"Why intermediate values are not rounded","back":"Rounding part-way through pushes the final answer away from the true value. Keep the full accuracy in the calculator and round only the final answer.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"Error interval","back":"The inequality holding every value that would produce the number written down, in the form smallest ≤ x < largest. The lower limit is included, the upper one is not.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"Error interval after truncation","back":"Truncating chops digits off instead of rounding, so the stated value is the smallest possible: value ≤ x < value + 1 in the last place kept.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"The limits of a measurement given to the nearest u","back":"The true value lies within half of u of the stated value: value − u/2 ≤ x < value + u/2. A mass of 500 g to the nearest 10 g gives 495 ≤ m < 505.","code":"N16","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Recall: 1 km = 1000 m, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 cm = 10 mm.","Recall: 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 g = 1000 mg.","Recall: 1 litre = 1000 ml, 1 ml = 1 cm³, 1 litre = 1000 cm³, 1 m³ = 1000 litres.","Recall: 1 cm² = 100 mm², 1 m² = 10 000 cm², 1 cm³ = 1000 mm³, 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³.","Recall: 1 minute = 60 seconds, 1 hour = 60 minutes = 3600 seconds.","Recall: speed = distance ÷ time, and so distance = speed × time and time = distance ÷ speed.","Recall: density = mass ÷ volume, pressure = force ÷ area, amount = rate × time.","Recall: rounded to the nearest u, value − u/2 ≤ x < value + u/2.","Recall: truncated at a given place, value ≤ x < value + 1 in the last place kept.","Given in the question: imperial/metric conversions — N13's Notes say 'Imperial/metric conversions will be given in the question', so no imperial factor has to be memorised.","Not on the formulae sheet: nothing in this room is printed for you. Section 4 of the course appendix records the results named on AQA's Foundation tier insert for exams from 2026, and not one of the ten is a unit conversion or an accuracy rule, so every conversion factor and every rounding rule above has to be written from memory. The contents of that sheet are not claimed here: the insert is reissued for each series, and it is the formulae sheet issued with your own paper that governs what is given on the day, so read the one in front of you."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:18:11Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.95,"notes":"Re-judged after the fix pass: all 14 bank questions, 4 exit items, 4 lesson checks and 5 worked examples re-solved in python from the question text alone, and every answer, unit, inequality and mark tally is correct, including the three changed items — (812 - 396) / 19.4 estimating to (800 - 400) / 20 = 20 against a true 21.44, the ribbon at 3.55 <= L < 3.65 with a lower limit of 355 cm, and the exact 89.6 cm box against a gap that could be only 89.5 cm, so 'No'. The tier risk is gone: questions[12] now forms one interval instead of comparing two, and lesson.idea[3] names bound-combining as Higher tier work; the Formulae sheet now cites appendix.md section 4 for the Foundation insert and carries the 'contents not claimed here' caveat, with the off-topic kinematics item removed. Publish it."}}},"algebra-notation":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"algebra-notation","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Almost every algebra mark on Papers 1, 2 and 3 is written in the notation this room sets up, and Algebra is 20% of the Foundation tier. Before you can solve an equation, use a formula in a mensuration or ratio question, or read a straight-line graph, you have to be able to write a × b as ab, put a negative value in brackets before squaring it, tell an expression from an equation, an identity from a formula, collect like terms, expand brackets, take out a common factor and turn a formula round to give a different letter. A1's Notes make the standard higher than it looks: answers are expected in their simplest form even when the question does not say so, so an unsimplified line can lose the accuracy mark. Most of the marks lost here go to careless notation — 0.25x written where x/4 is wanted, −4² treated as (−4)², a sign dropped after a bracket — and much of it is examined on Paper 1, where the written method is what earns the method mark.","idea":[{"h":"The conventions of algebraic notation","t":"Algebra has a shorthand and AQA marks answers in it. The multiplication sign is left out, so a × b is written ab and 3 × y is written 3y; repeated addition of the same letter is written as a multiple, so y + y + y is 3y; repeated multiplication is written as a power, so a × a is a², a × a × a is a³ and a × a × b is a²b. A division is written as a fraction, so a ÷ b is a/b. The number a term is multiplied by is its coefficient, and A1 says a coefficient is written as a fraction rather than a decimal, so x/4 and never 0.25x. Brackets hold a whole quantity together: 3(x + 2) means three lots of the whole of x + 2. A1's Notes add that answers are expected in their simplest form without being asked, so an expression left as 2x + 3x loses the accuracy mark even when the working is right.","code":"A1"},{"h":"Substituting numerical values into formulae and expressions","t":"To find a value, replace every letter by the number it stands for and then work the arithmetic out in the usual priority: brackets first, then powers, then × and ÷, then + and −. Write the substitution line down before you evaluate anything, because that line is where the method mark sits, and on Paper 1 an answer with no working can lose it. The trap is a negative value: put it in brackets as you substitute, since (−4)² = 16 while −4² means −(4²) = −16. A2 covers scientific formulae too, and its Notes say unfamiliar formulae will be given in the question, so a formula such as the volume of a cone is printed for you — the marks are for using it, not for recalling it. Give the answer to the accuracy asked for and with its unit.","code":"A2"},{"h":"Expression, equation, formula, identity, inequality, term, factor","t":"A3 says this vocabulary is assessed both implicitly and explicitly, so the words themselves are worth marks. An expression, such as 5y − 2, is a collection of terms with no equals sign: it can be simplified or evaluated, but it cannot be solved. An equation, such as 5y − 2 = 13, has an equals sign and is true only for particular values. An identity is true for every value and is written with ≡, as in 2(x + 3) ≡ 2x + 6. A formula, such as v = u + at or A = πr², is a rule for working out one quantity — its subject — from others. An inequality compares two expressions with <, ≤, > or ≥. Inside an expression, a term is a part separated by + or −, and a factor is something that divides it exactly: the factors of 6x²y include 2, 3, x and y.","code":"A3"},{"h":"Simplifying and manipulating expressions","t":"Five techniques do the work at Foundation tier. Collect like terms: only terms with exactly the same letters and the same powers can be joined, and each term carries the sign in front of it, so 7m − 4k + 2m + 9k is 9m + 5k. Multiply a single term over a bracket: every term inside is multiplied, signs included. Take out common factors: divide each term by the highest common factor and write it outside the bracket, then check by expanding — 'factorise fully' means a partly finished answer loses the accuracy mark. Use the laws of indices on products and powers: xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ, xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ, (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ and x⁰ = 1, with the coefficients multiplied separately. Expand products of two binomials: every term in the first bracket multiplies every term in the second, giving four products to collect. The same statement covers factorising x² + bx + c, where two numbers multiply to c and add to b, and the difference of two squares, x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a).","code":"A4"},{"h":"Standard formulae and changing the subject","t":"A formula has a subject, the letter standing alone on one side, and questions often want a different one. Work out what has been done to the letter you want, then undo those operations in reverse order, doing the same thing to both sides each time, until that letter stands alone and appears only once. To make m the subject of E = mgh, divide both sides by gh; to make a the subject of v = u + at, take u from both sides and then divide by t. A5's Notes include formulae from other subjects, given in words or in symbols, so a science formula may arrive with the letters explained. The Appendix lists the prescribed formulae and says which of them you are expected to know; the rearranging is what earns the method mark either way.","code":"A5"},{"h":"Equations, identities and algebraic argument","t":"An equation is true for particular values of the letter; an identity is true for all of them. To argue that two expressions are equivalent, take one side, expand every bracket and collect every like term, show the result is exactly the other side, and then write a sentence saying so — that sentence is what turns working into an argument and is where the reasoning mark falls. Checking one value is not enough: it shows the two sides agree there, not everywhere. Once shown, the statement may be written with ≡ rather than =. The same expand-and-collect chain proves results about numbers: writing 3(2n + 1) − 3(n − 2) as 3(n + 3) shows the value is always a multiple of 3, because it has been written as 3 × a whole number. 'Show that' means the answer is already printed, so nothing may be quoted back — every line must be seen.","code":"A6"},{"h":"An expression as a function: inputs and outputs","t":"An expression such as 3x − 5 can be read as a machine: an input goes in, it is multiplied by 3 and then 5 is taken away, and one output comes out. Substituting an input gives the output, so an input of 4 gives 3 × 4 − 5 = 7. Running the machine backwards — undoing each operation in the reverse order — takes an output back to the input that produced it, which is the same inverse-operation thinking used to change the subject of a formula. Questions give a function machine drawn as boxes, a mapping written x → 3x − 5, or a plain expression, and ask for the output from a given input or the input that gives a stated output. Each input has exactly one output, which is what makes the expression a function.","code":"A7"}],"examples":[{"title":"Rewriting an expression in the conventional notation","setup":"Rewrite the expression 0.75m + n ÷ 4 + k × k × 3 in the conventional algebraic notation, with every coefficient written as a fraction rather than as a decimal.","steps":["k × k × 3 is repeated multiplication of one letter, so it is written as a power with its multiplier in front: 3k²","n ÷ 4 is a division, and a division is written as a fraction: n/4","0.75 as a fraction is three quarters, so 0.75m is written with a fractional coefficient: 3m/4","Putting the three terms back together, the expression is 3m/4 + n/4 + 3k²"],"cues":["Which of the three terms is a repeated multiplication?","How is a division written in algebra?","What is 0.75 written as a fraction in its simplest form?","In what order should the rewritten terms be listed?"]},{"title":"Substituting a negative value into a scientific formula","setup":"A formula met in science is s = ut + ½at², where s is measured in metres. Use it to work out the value of s when u = −7, a = 10 and t = 3.","steps":["Replace each letter by its value, writing the negative one inside brackets: s = (−7) × 3 + ½ × 10 × 3²","Powers come before multiplication, and 3² = 9, so the second term is ½ × 10 × 9","Working each term out: ½ × 10 = 5 and 5 × 9 = 45, while (−7) × 3 = −21","s = −21 + 45 = 24, so s = 24 m"],"cues":["Why must the value of u be written inside brackets?","Which part of the second term is worked out first?","Which of the two terms will come out negative?","What unit must be written with the value?"]},{"title":"Multiplying out two brackets and collecting like terms","setup":"Multiply out the brackets in 4(3x − 2) − 3(x − 5) and simplify your answer by collecting like terms.","steps":["Every term inside the first bracket is multiplied by 4: 4(3x − 2) = 12x − 8","The second bracket is multiplied by −3, taking the sign with it: −3(x − 5) = −3x + 15","Collect the x terms: 12x − 3x = 9x","Collect the number terms: −8 + 15 = 7, so the simplified expression is 9x + 7"],"cues":["What multiplies the contents of the first bracket?","What sign is attached to the 3 in front of the second bracket?","Which two terms contain a letter?","Will the number term end up positive or negative?"]},{"title":"Factorising a quadratic expression into two brackets","setup":"Factorise the quadratic expression x² + 2x − 15, writing it as a product of two brackets.","steps":["This has the form x² + bx + c with b = 2 and c = −15, so two numbers are needed whose product is −15 and whose sum is 2","The factor pairs of 15 are 1 × 15 and 3 × 5; one number must be negative, and 5 with −3 gives a product of −15","Check the sum: 5 + (−3) = 2, which matches the coefficient of x","So the factorised form is x² + 2x − 15 = (x + 5)(x − 3)"],"cues":["What must the two numbers multiply to, and what must they add to?","Why must one of the two numbers be negative?","Which pair still has to be tested against the middle term?","Which number goes in the bracket with the minus sign?"]},{"title":"Changing the subject of a conversion formula","setup":"The formula C = 5(F − 32)/9 converts a temperature F in degrees Fahrenheit into the temperature C in degrees Celsius. Rearrange this formula to make F the subject.","steps":["The right-hand side is divided by 9 last, so undo that first: multiply both sides by 9 to give 9C = 5(F − 32)","The bracket is multiplied by 5, so divide both sides by 5: 9C/5 = F − 32","Only the subtraction is left, so add 32 to both sides: 9C/5 + 32 = F","Written with the new subject first, F = 9C/5 + 32"],"cues":["Which operation is applied to the bracket last?","What is still multiplying the bracket after the first move?","What is left attached to F at this point?","On which side of the formula should the new subject be written?"]},{"title":"Showing that two expressions are equivalent","setup":"Show that 4(2x − 3) + 6 is equivalent to 2(4x − 3) for every value of x, and say what kind of statement this makes it.","steps":["Expand the bracket on the left-hand side: 4(2x − 3) = 8x − 12","Now include the 6 that follows it: 8x − 12 + 6 = 8x − 6","Expand the bracket on the right-hand side: 2(4x − 3) = 8x − 6","Both sides simplify to 8x − 6, so they are equal whatever x is: the statement is an identity"],"cues":["Which side has more than one step of work to do?","What is left to combine on the left-hand side?","What has to be done to the other side before the two can be compared?","How do the two simplified sides compare, and what does that make this statement?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write 5 × p × p × q using the conventions of algebraic notation.","a":"5p²q"},{"q":"Work out the value of 2y² + 3 when y = −5.","a":"53"},{"q":"Show that (n + 1)² − n² is equivalent to 2n + 1, and say whether it is an equation or an identity.","a":"(n + 1)² = n² + 2n + 1, so (n + 1)² − n² = n² + 2n + 1 − n² = 2n + 1. It is an identity."},{"q":"Make t the subject of the formula d = st.","a":"t = d/s"},{"q":"Which of these is a formula, which an expression and which an equation: 4h − 1, A = bh, 4h − 1 = 11?","a":"A = bh is the formula, 4h − 1 is the expression and 4h − 1 = 11 is the equation."}]},"room":{"facts":["The notation conventions: ab means a × b; 3y means y + y + y and 3 × y; a² means a × a; a³ means a × a × a; a²b means a × a × b; a/b means a ÷ b; brackets hold a whole quantity together.","A coefficient is written as a fraction and not as a decimal: x/4, never 0.25x. Answers are expected in their simplest form even when the question does not say so.","Substitution order: brackets, then powers, then × and ÷, then + and −, with every negative value written in brackets, so (−4)² = 16 but −4² = −16.","An expression has no equals sign; an equation is true for particular values; an identity (≡) is true for every value; a formula gives a rule for its subject; an inequality compares with <, ≤, > or ≥.","Laws of indices: xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ; xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ; (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ; x⁰ = 1, with the coefficients multiplied or divided separately.","x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q) where p × q = c and p + q = b; the difference of two squares is x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a).","To change the subject, apply the inverse operation to both sides in turn, undoing the last operation first, until the wanted letter stands alone and appears only once."],"questions":[{"q":"Write each of these using the conventions of algebraic notation: (a) 7 × t   (b) m × m × m × n. (non-calculator)","a":"(a) 7t   (b) m³n","sol":"(a) The multiplication sign between a number and a letter is left out, so 7 × t = 7t. B1 for 7t. (b) m × m × m is repeated multiplication of the same letter, so it is written as the power m³, and the n is written straight after it: m³n. B1 for m³n. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Algebra leaves multiplication signs out; repeated multiplication of the same letter has its own shorthand.","In part (a) a number in front of a letter needs no sign between them; in part (b) count how many times the same letter is multiplied by itself.","The first line of part (b) rewrites the repeated letter as a single power, and the remaining letter is written after it."],"codes":["A1"]},{"q":"The expression 0.2y has a decimal coefficient. Rewrite it with its coefficient written as a fraction instead. (non-calculator)","a":"y/5","sol":"0.2 = 2/10 = 1/5, so 0.2y = y/5. B1 for y/5 oe. Total 1 mark.","hints":["A1 expects a coefficient to be written as a fraction rather than as a decimal.","Change the decimal 0.2 into a fraction and cancel it down before writing the term.","The first line is 0.2 written as a fraction over 10, ready to be cancelled."],"m":1,"d":1,"codes":["A1"]},{"q":"Work out the value of 3x² − 4x when x = −2. (non-calculator)","a":"20","sol":"Substitute, keeping the negative value in brackets: 3 × (−2)² − 4 × (−2). M1 for this substitution line. (−2)² = 4, so the first term is 3 × 4 = 12, and the second term is −4 × (−2) = +8. 12 + 8 = 20. A1 for 20. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A power is worked out before a multiplication, and a negative value needs care when it is squared.","Replace x by its value everywhere it appears, writing that value inside brackets, then follow the priority of operations.","The first line is 3 × (   )² − 4 × (   ), with the value of x written inside each pair of brackets."],"codes":["A2"]},{"q":"Here are four statements. A: 4x − 9    B: 4x − 9 = 3    C: P = 4x − 9    D: 4(x − 2) ≡ 4x − 8. Write down the letter of the expression and the letter of the identity. (non-calculator)","a":"Expression A; identity D","sol":"An expression has no equals sign, so A is the expression. B1 for A. An identity is true for every value of the letter and is written with ≡; expanding 4(x − 2) gives 4x − 8, which is the right-hand side, so D is the identity. B1 for D. (B is an equation, true only when x = 3; C is a formula with subject P.) Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Look first at which statements have an equals sign at all, and at the shape of the sign used.","One statement can only be simplified, not solved; another stays true no matter what number the letter stands for.","The first move is to expand any bracket that appears, so that the two sides of that statement can be compared."],"codes":["A3"]},{"q":"Simplify 8p + 5q − 3p − 9q. (non-calculator)","a":"5p − 4q","sol":"Collect the p terms, each keeping the sign in front of it: 8p − 3p = 5p. B1 for 5p. Collect the q terms: 5q − 9q = −4q. B1 for −4q. The simplified expression is 5p − 4q. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Only terms with exactly the same letter can be joined together.","Deal with the p terms and the q terms separately, taking the sign in front of each term with it.","The first line groups the terms as (8p − 3p) + (5q − 9q)."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"Simplify (a) 5c⁴ × 3c²   (b) 12d⁷ ÷ 4d³. (a calculator may be used)","a":"(a) 15c⁶   (b) 3d⁴","sol":"(a) Multiply the coefficients and add the indices: 5 × 3 = 15 and c⁴ × c² = c⁴⁺² = c⁶, so the answer is 15c⁶. B1 for 15c⁶. (b) Divide the coefficients and subtract the indices: 12 ÷ 4 = 3 and d⁷ ÷ d³ = d⁷⁻³ = d⁴, so the answer is 3d⁴. B1 for 3d⁴. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Each part has numbers and letters that are dealt with separately.","Work on the coefficients with ordinary arithmetic, and use the law of indices for multiplying or dividing powers of the same letter.","Part (a) begins as (5 × 3) × (c⁴ × c²), with the indices still to be combined."],"codes":["A4","A1"]},{"q":"Multiply out and simplify 2x(3x − 4) + 5x. (a calculator may be used)","a":"6x² − 3x","sol":"Multiply each term in the bracket by 2x: 2x × 3x = 6x² and 2x × (−4) = −8x, giving 6x² − 8x. M1 for 6x² − 8x. Now collect the like terms −8x and +5x: −8x + 5x = −3x, so the expression is 6x² − 3x. A1 for 6x² − 3x. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The term in front of the bracket multiplies everything inside it, not just the first term.","Expand first and only then look for terms that can be collected; x² terms and x terms are not alike.","The first line is 2x × 3x + 2x × (−4) + 5x, with the products still to be worked out."],"codes":["A4","A1"]},{"q":"Factorise fully 18a²b − 12ab². (a calculator may be used)","a":"6ab(3a − 2b)","sol":"The highest common factor of 18 and 12 is 6, and both terms contain a and b, so the highest common factor of the two terms is 6ab. M1 for any correct partial factorisation, such as 2ab(9a − 6b) or 6a(3ab − 2b²), or for 6ab seen as the common factor. Dividing: 18a²b ÷ 6ab = 3a and 12ab² ÷ 6ab = 2b, so 18a²b − 12ab² = 6ab(3a − 2b). A1 for 6ab(3a − 2b). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["'Fully' means nothing may be left that both terms inside the bracket still share.","Find the highest common factor of the two numbers and then ask which letters appear in both terms.","The first line writes the highest common factor outside a bracket, with the two terms it leaves behind still to be found inside."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"Expand and simplify (x + 6)(x − 4). (a calculator may be used)","a":"x² + 2x − 24","sol":"Every term in the first bracket multiplies every term in the second: x × x = x², x × (−4) = −4x, 6 × x = 6x and 6 × (−4) = −24. M1 for at least three of these four products correct. Collecting the like terms −4x + 6x = 2x gives x² + 2x − 24. A1 for x² + 2x − 24. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Each bracket has two terms, so multiplying them out gives four separate products.","Multiply every term in the first bracket by every term in the second, then collect the two terms in x.","The first line lists the four products x × x, x × (−4), 6 × x and 6 × (−4), still to be worked out."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"The perimeter P of a rectangle of length l and width w is given by P = 2l + 2w. Rearrange this formula to make w the subject. (a calculator may be used)","a":"w = (P − 2l)/2","sol":"The term 2l is added last on the right, so subtract it from both sides: P − 2l = 2w. M1 for P − 2l = 2w. Now w is multiplied by 2, so divide both sides by 2: (P − 2l)/2 = w, that is w = (P − 2l)/2. A1 for w = (P − 2l)/2 oe, for example w = P/2 − l. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The subject is the letter standing alone on one side, and here that has to become w.","Undo the operations that have been applied to w, in reverse order, doing the same to both sides each time.","The first line takes the whole of the other term on the right away from both sides."],"codes":["A5"]},{"q":"The surface area A of a sphere of radius r is given by the formula A = 4πr². Work out the surface area of a sphere of radius 3.5 cm. Give your answer in cm² to 3 significant figures. (a calculator may be used)","a":"154 cm²","sol":"Substitute r = 3.5 into the formula given: A = 4 × π × 3.5². M1 for this substitution. 3.5² = 12.25, so A = 4 × π × 12.25 = 153.938... cm², which is 154 cm² to 3 significant figures. A1 for 154 (cm²). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The formula is printed in the question, so nothing has to be recalled here — only used.","Square the radius first, then multiply by 4 and by π, and round only at the very end.","The first line is A = 4 × π × (radius)², with the radius still to be put in place of r."],"codes":["A2"]},{"q":"Show that (x + 4)(x − 4) + 16 is equivalent to x², and state whether the statement is an equation or an identity. (non-calculator)","a":"Shown: it simplifies to x²; it is an identity.","sol":"Expand the product: x × x = x², x × (−4) = −4x, 4 × x = 4x and 4 × (−4) = −16, so (x + 4)(x − 4) = x² − 4x + 4x − 16 = x² − 16 (the difference of two squares). M1 for the expansion or for x² − 16. Adding 16: x² − 16 + 16 = x², which is the right-hand side. A1 for reaching x² with the working seen. Because the two sides are equal for every value of x, the statement is an identity. B1 for identity. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The two brackets contain the same two terms, one added and one subtracted — a pattern worth recognising.","Expand the product fully, collect the like terms, and only then deal with the number that is added on.","The first line writes out the four products x × x, x × (−4), 4 × x and 4 × (−4), ready to be collected."],"codes":["A4","A6","A3"]},{"q":"The volume V of a cone of base radius r and perpendicular height h is given by V = ⅓πr²h. (a) Rearrange the formula to make h the subject. (b) A cone has volume 480 cm³ and base radius 6 cm. Work out its perpendicular height in cm, to 1 decimal place. (a calculator may be used)","a":"(a) h = 3V/(πr²)   (b) 12.7 cm","sol":"(a) Multiply both sides by 3 to clear the fraction: 3V = πr²h. M1 for 3V = πr²h. Then divide both sides by πr²: h = 3V/(πr²). A1 for h = 3V/(πr²) oe. (b) Substitute V = 480 and r = 6: h = (3 × 480)/(π × 6²) = 1440/(36π). M1 for this substitution into a correct rearrangement (ft their part (a)). 36π = 113.097..., so h = 12.7323... = 12.7 cm to 1 decimal place. A1 for 12.7 (cm). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["In part (a) the letter you want is buried under a fraction, a π and a squared letter; part (b) then only needs numbers put into your new formula.","Clear the fraction first by multiplying both sides, then divide by everything that is still multiplying h.","Part (a) begins by multiplying both sides of the formula by 3 to remove the third."],"codes":["A5","A2"]},{"q":"A rectangle has width (x + 4) cm and length (2x − 1) cm. Write an expression, in its simplest form, for (a) the perimeter of the rectangle in cm and (b) the area of the rectangle in cm². (non-calculator)","a":"(a) 6x + 6   (b) 2x² + 7x − 4","sol":"(a) The perimeter is twice the width plus twice the length: 2(x + 4) + 2(2x − 1) = 2x + 8 + 4x − 2. M1 for a correct perimeter expression before simplifying. Collecting: 2x + 4x = 6x and 8 − 2 = 6, so the perimeter is 6x + 6. A1 for 6x + 6. (b) The area is width × length: (x + 4)(2x − 1) = 2x² − x + 8x − 4. M1 for at least three of the four products correct. Collecting −x + 8x = 7x gives 2x² + 7x − 4. A1 for 2x² + 7x − 4. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A rectangle has two widths and two lengths; its area is one width multiplied by one length.","For the perimeter, double each side expression and then collect like terms; for the area, multiply the two brackets out and collect.","Part (a) begins by writing the perimeter as 2(width) + 2(length) with the two bracketed expressions in place."],"codes":["A4","A1"]},{"q":"A number machine multiplies its input by 4 and then subtracts 9. (a) Work out the output when the input is −3. (b) Work out the input that gives an output of 31. (non-calculator)","a":"(a) −21   (b) 10","sol":"(a) The rule as an expression is 4x − 9. Substituting x = −3 gives 4 × (−3) − 9 = −12 − 9 = −21. B1 for −21. (b) Run the machine backwards, undoing each step in reverse order: add 9, then divide by 4, so the input is (31 + 9) ÷ 4. M1 for (31 + 9) ÷ 4, or for both steps reversed in the correct order. 40 ÷ 4 = 10. A1 for 10. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["The machine is just the expression 4x − 9 read as a rule that turns an input into one output.","Part (a) puts the input into the rule; part (b) needs the machine run backwards, undoing each operation in the reverse order.","For part (b), the first move undoes the subtraction by adding 9 to the output."],"codes":["A7","A2"]},{"q":"Jamal says that, for every whole number n, the value of 3(n + 2) − (n − 4) is always even. Show that Jamal is right. (non-calculator)","a":"3(n + 2) − (n − 4) = 2n + 10 = 2(n + 5), which is 2 × a whole number, so it is always even.","sol":"Expand both brackets, taking the minus sign in front of the second one with it: 3(n + 2) = 3n + 6 and −(n − 4) = −n + 4. M1 for both expansions correct. Collecting like terms: 3n − n = 2n and 6 + 4 = 10, so the expression is 2n + 10. A1 for 2n + 10. Taking out the common factor 2 gives 2(n + 5); since n is a whole number, n + 5 is a whole number, so the value is 2 × a whole number and therefore even. B1 for the factorised form with a conclusion in words. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["A number is even exactly when it can be written as 2 multiplied by a whole number.","Expand both brackets and collect like terms, then look for a factor that can be taken out of the result.","The first line expands 3(n + 2) and then −(n − 4), keeping the minus sign with the second bracket."],"codes":["A4","A6"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write y × y × 5 using the conventions of algebraic notation.","a":"5y²"},{"q":"Work out the value of 5 − 2x when x = −4.","a":"13"},{"q":"Factorise fully 7x + 21.","a":"7(x + 3)"},{"q":"Make x the subject of the formula y = 3x + 7.","a":"x = (y − 7)/3"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The conventions of algebraic notation","back":"ab means a × b; 3y means y + y + y and 3 × y; a² means a × a; a³ means a × a × a; a²b means a × a × b; a/b means a ÷ b.","code":"A1","open":false},{"front":"Coefficient","back":"The number a term is multiplied by: in 7x the coefficient is 7. AQA expects a coefficient written as a fraction, not a decimal, so x/4 and never 0.25x.","code":"A1","open":false},{"front":"The order of operations when substituting","back":"Brackets, then powers, then × and ÷, then + and −. Write the substitution line down before evaluating: that line is where the method mark falls.","code":"A2","open":false},{"front":"Squaring a negative value","back":"Put the value in brackets as you substitute: (−4)² = −4 × −4 = 16, while −4² means −(4²) = −16. The brackets change the answer.","code":"A2","open":false},{"front":"Expression, term and factor","back":"An expression is a collection of terms with no equals sign, like 5y − 2. A term is a part separated by + or −; a factor divides the expression exactly.","code":"A3","open":false},{"front":"Equation","back":"Two expressions joined by an equals sign, true only for particular values of the letter: 5y − 2 = 13 is true only when y = 3.","code":"A3","open":false},{"front":"Formula","back":"A rule connecting quantities, with one letter as its subject: v = u + at, A = πr². Substituting values for the other letters gives the subject's value.","code":"A3","open":false},{"front":"Identity","back":"A statement true for every value of the letter, written with ≡: 2(x + 3) ≡ 2x + 6. Expanding and collecting one side gives the other exactly.","code":"A3","open":false},{"front":"Like terms","back":"Terms with exactly the same letters raised to the same powers, so 5a and −2a are like terms but 5a and 5a² are not. Only like terms can be collected.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"The laws of indices","back":"xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ; xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ; (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ; x⁰ = 1. Coefficients are multiplied separately: 7y⁵ × 2y³ = 14y⁸.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Factorising x² + bx + c","back":"Find two numbers with product c and sum b; they are the numbers in the brackets. x² + 7x + 12 = (x + 3)(x + 4).","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"The difference of two squares","back":"x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a). The brackets hold the same two terms, one added and one subtracted: x² − 9 = (x + 3)(x − 3).","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Changing the subject of a formula","back":"Apply the inverse operation to both sides in turn, undoing the last operation first, until the wanted letter stands alone on one side and appears only once.","code":"A5","open":false},{"front":"Showing two expressions are equivalent","back":"Expand every bracket and collect every like term on one side until it is exactly the other side, then say so. Testing one value is not enough — it shows they agree there, not everywhere.","code":"A6","open":false},{"front":"An expression read as a function","back":"A rule turning each input into exactly one output. 3x − 5 multiplies the input by 3 then subtracts 5; undoing those steps in reverse order takes an output back to its input.","code":"A7","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["How to read this list: the specification's Appendix sorts every prescribed formula into three lists — lists 1 and 2 are marked 'they will not be given in the exam', list 3 'they will be given in the exam in the relevant question'. Since 2022 every AQA 8300 paper also carries a formulae sheet, one for each tier, and section 4 of appendix.md records the results the 2026 Foundation insert prints. This is a Foundation room, so that insert is the one that applies, and where the Appendix says a result must be known but section 4 shows the Foundation sheet prints it, the sheet governs on the day. Remember below means you must write it from memory; Given on the Foundation sheet means it is printed on the insert in front of you; Given in the question means it is printed with the question that needs it.","Remember — not in the Appendix at all: ab means a × b, 3y means y + y + y and 3 × y, a² means a × a, a³ means a × a × a, a²b means a × a × b, and a/b means a ÷ b. These are notation conventions, not prescribed formulae, so no sheet carries them.","Remember — not in the Appendix at all: a coefficient is written as a fraction and not as a decimal, x/4 rather than 0.25x.","Remember — not in the Appendix at all: the priority of operations when substituting — brackets, then powers, then × and ÷, then + and − — and the rule that a negative value is put in brackets, so (−4)² = 16 but −4² = −16.","Remember — not in the Appendix at all: the laws of indices, xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ, xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ, (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ and x⁰ = 1.","Remember — not in the Appendix at all: x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q) where p × q = c and p + q = b, and the difference of two squares, x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a).","Remember — not in the Appendix at all: to change the subject, apply the inverse operation to both sides in turn until the wanted letter stands alone and appears only once.","Given on the Foundation sheet, not memorised — section 4 of appendix.md records that the 2026 Foundation insert prints the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, the circumference of a circle, the area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios in a right-angled triangle, compound interest (the total accrued) and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). The Appendix puts all nine in list 1 or list 2, 'they will not be given in the exam', but the insert is later than the Appendix and governs on the day, so none of the nine has to be recalled. None of them is needed in this room either — A = πr² appears only as an example of what a formula is — and section 4 warns that the sheets are reissued each series, so check the current insert.","Given in the exam in the relevant question — Appendix list 3, 'students are not expected to memorise the following formulae': surface area of a sphere = 4πr², as printed in this room's calculator question on substitution.","Given in the exam in the relevant question — Appendix list 3: volume of a sphere = 4/3 πr³.","Given in the exam in the relevant question — Appendix list 3: volume of a cone = ⅓πr²h, as printed in this room's stretch question on changing the subject.","Given in the exam in the relevant question — Appendix list 3: curved surface area of a cone = πrl, where l is the slant height.","Given in the exam in the relevant question — Appendix list 3: the kinematics formulae v = u + at, s = ut + ½at² and v² = u² + 2as, which is why a formula like s = ut + ½at² is printed for you in a substitution question. A2's Notes agree: 'unfamiliar formulae will be given in the question'.","Not assessed at this tier — Appendix list 1: the quadratic formula x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/2a, the sine rule a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C, the cosine rule a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A and Area = ½ab sin C. The Appendix is not divided by tier, but the content references that carry them (A18, G22, G23) are printed in the higher content only column, so a Foundation paper does not assess them; Appendix list 2's P(A and B) = P(A given B) P(B) carries the higher-only reference P9 in the same way."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:19:21.342477Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Both substantive faults are properly fixed: the Formulae sheet now matches appendix.md section 4 result by result for the Foundation insert, and the four pre-answered questions plus worked example 4 have fresh, correct teaching instances, leaving only two wording nits that do not affect a single mark."}}},"algebra-graphs":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"algebra-graphs","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Algebra is 20% of the Foundation tier and graphs carry a large share of it, on all three papers. Every stage of the exam asks you to move between an equation, a table of values, a drawn line or curve and a real situation: plotting a point in any of the four quadrants, reading a gradient and a y-intercept, finding roots and a turning point on a parabola, recognising a cubic or a reciprocal curve on sight, and reading a speed off a distance-time graph. These skills are the ones AO2 and AO3 questions are built from, because a graph is where a story, a table and an equation meet, and a sign slip in a coordinate loses the accuracy mark however good the rest of the method is.","idea":[{"h":"Points on the plane: coordinates in all four quadrants","t":"A point is fixed by an ordered pair (x, y). The x-coordinate is read first, along the horizontal axis from the origin (0, 0); the y-coordinate is read second, up or down the vertical axis. To the left of the y-axis x is negative; below the x-axis y is negative, so the four quadrants carry the sign pairs (+, +) top right, (−, +) top left, (−, −) bottom left and (+, −) bottom right. The exam asks you to plot points in all four quadrants, to write down the coordinates of a point already marked, and to complete a table of values before a line or curve is drawn. Write the pair in the right order and with the right signs: a point misplotted by more than half a square, or with x and y swapped, loses the accuracy mark even when the arithmetic is correct.","code":"A8"},{"h":"Straight lines: plotting them and finding their equations","t":"To plot a straight line, build a table of values: substitute each x into the equation, work out y, plot the pairs and join them with a ruler. The table is where the method mark sits, so show it. Read off an equation written as y = mx + c: m is the gradient and c the y-intercept. Two lines are parallel when their values of m are equal, whatever c is — so y = 2x − 3 and y = 2x + 5 are parallel. Given two points, the gradient is the change in y divided by the change in x; substitute that m and either point back into y = mx + c to find c. Given one point and a gradient, the same substitution finds c in one step. Always write the finished equation in the form the question asks for.","code":"A9"},{"h":"Reading a gradient and an intercept, and saying what they mean","t":"The gradient m measures steepness: rise over run, the change in y divided by the change in x, taken between two clear points on the line. A positive gradient rises from left to right, a negative gradient falls, and a gradient of 0 is a horizontal line. The y-intercept c is the y value where the line crosses the y-axis, the point (0, c). Algebraically both are read straight off y = mx + c once the x term is written first: for y = 5 − 3x, m = −3 and c = 5. Questions also ask what they mean in context — the gradient is a rate of change (pounds per hour, litres per minute, kilometres per hour) and the intercept is the starting value when the other quantity is zero. The mark is for the sentence with its unit, not for the number alone.","code":"A10"},{"h":"Quadratic graphs: roots, intercepts and the turning point","t":"The graph of a quadratic is a parabola. Its roots are the x values where it crosses the x-axis, so where y = 0: put y = 0 and factorise, then set each bracket to zero. Its y-intercept is found by putting x = 0, which for y = x² + bx + c is simply (0, c). Its turning point is the minimum (or, when the x² term is negative, the maximum), and the parabola is symmetrical about the vertical line through it — so the turning point lies midway between the roots. That symmetry is the quickest route in the exam: average the two roots to get the x-coordinate, then substitute that x back into the equation to get the y-coordinate. A question may give you the graph and ask you to read these off, or give the equation and ask you to deduce the roots algebraically.","code":"A11"},{"h":"Graph shapes you must recognise on sight","t":"The highest power of x decides the shape. A linear function (highest power x) is a straight line. A quadratic (highest power x²) is a U-shaped parabola, upside down if the x² term is negative, symmetrical about a vertical line. A simple cubic such as y = x³ (highest power x³) rises from the bottom left, flattens near the origin and rises again to the top right. The reciprocal function y = 1/x, with x ≠ 0, is a pair of separate branches, one in the first quadrant and one in the third, approaching but never touching the x-axis and the y-axis — these are its asymptotes. You may be asked to match an equation to a sketch, to sketch a named function showing its intercepts and turning point, or to say why a value of x is excluded. A sketch is marked on its features, so label what you know.","code":"A12"},{"h":"Graphs of real situations, including distance, speed and time","t":"Real quantities can be plotted against each other and read back. On a distance-time graph the gradient of each stage is the speed: distance divided by time, with its unit; a steeper line is faster, a horizontal section means the object is stationary, and a line returning to the axis means it is coming back. Average speed for a whole journey is total distance divided by total time, not the average of the separate speeds. A straight-line cost graph has the fixed charge as its intercept and the rate as its gradient. Non-standard graphs appear too, including reciprocal graphs such as time against number of workers, where the curve falls steeply then levels off without ever reaching zero. Where a question says 'use the graph', the reading off the axis is the mark, so draw the dashed lines you used.","code":"A14"}],"examples":[{"title":"A table of values, and testing whether a point is on the line","setup":"The equation of a straight line is y = 2x − 3. A table of values is being filled in for x = −2, x = 0 and x = 3. A point P has coordinates (−4, −11). Work out the three y values for the table, and decide whether P lies on the line, showing how you decide.","steps":["Substitute x = −2: y = 2 × (−2) − 3 = −4 − 3 = −7, giving the pair (−2, −7).","Substitute x = 0: y = 2 × 0 − 3 = −3, giving the pair (0, −3).","Substitute x = 3: y = 2 × 3 − 3 = 6 − 3 = 3, giving the pair (3, 3).","Test P by putting its x-coordinate into the rule: y = 2 × (−4) − 3 = −8 − 3 = −11.","The rule gives −11, which matches the y-coordinate of P, so P does lie on the line."],"cues":["Which operation is done first, the multiplying or the subtracting?","What happens to the 2x term when x is zero?","Does a positive x here give a positive or a negative y?","What must you do with the x-coordinate of P before you can compare anything?","What has to be true of the two y values for P to be on the line?"]},{"title":"The equation of a line through two points, and a parallel line","setup":"A straight line passes through the points A(1, 4) and B(3, 10). Find the equation of this line in the form y = mx + c. Then write down the equation of the line that is parallel to it and crosses the y-axis at (0, −2).","steps":["Gradient m = (10 − 4) ÷ (3 − 1) = 6 ÷ 2 = 3.","Substitute m = 3 and the point A(1, 4) into y = mx + c: 4 = 3 × 1 + c.","So c = 4 − 3 = 1, and the line is y = 3x + 1.","A parallel line has the same gradient, so its m is 3 as well.","It crosses the y-axis at (0, −2), so c = −2 and the parallel line is y = 3x − 2."],"cues":["Which difference goes on top, the y values or the x values?","Which of the two given points may be substituted?","What is left to find once m is known?","What do parallel lines share?","Where does the y-intercept of a line appear in y = mx + c?"]},{"title":"Reading a parabola: y-intercept, roots and turning point","setup":"The graph of y = x² − 4x + 3 is drawn for values of x from −1 to 5. Write down the coordinates of its y-intercept, find the coordinates of the points where it crosses the x-axis by factorising, and use the symmetry of the curve to find the coordinates of its turning point.","steps":["The y-intercept is where x = 0: y = 0² − 4 × 0 + 3 = 3, so the curve crosses at (0, 3).","Factorise the expression: x² − 4x + 3 = (x − 1)(x − 3).","Roots: (x − 1)(x − 3) = 0 gives x = 1 or x = 3, so the crossings are (1, 0) and (3, 0).","By symmetry the turning point lies midway between the roots: x = (1 + 3) ÷ 2 = 2.","Substituting: y = 2² − 4 × 2 + 3 = 4 − 8 + 3 = −1, so the turning point is (2, −1)."],"cues":["What is always true of the x-coordinate on the y-axis?","Which two numbers multiply to give the constant and add to give the x coefficient?","What must y be for a point on the x-axis?","Where does the line of symmetry of a parabola sit relative to its roots?","How do you turn that x-coordinate into the missing y-coordinate?"]},{"title":"Matching equations to graph shapes","setup":"Four equations are given: y = 1/x with x ≠ 0, y = x³, y = 5 − 2x, and y = x² − 9. Four sketches are described: a straight line falling from left to right; a U-shaped curve with a minimum; a curve made of two separate branches; and a curve that rises, flattens near the origin and rises again. Match each equation to its sketch, and state where y = x² − 9 crosses the x-axis.","steps":["y = 5 − 2x has x as its highest power and a negative coefficient, so it matches the straight line falling from left to right.","y = x² − 9 has highest power x², so it matches the U-shaped curve with a minimum.","y = x³ is cubic, so it matches the curve that rises, flattens near the origin and rises again.","y = 1/x is the reciprocal function, undefined when x is zero, so it matches the two separate branches, with the axes as asymptotes.","For the parabola, y = 0 gives x² − 9 = 0, so x² = 9 and x = 3 or x = −3: it crosses at (3, 0) and (−3, 0)."],"cues":["What does the sign of the x coefficient do to a straight line?","Which power of x produces a curve with one turning point?","Which of the remaining equations has the highest power of all?","Why can one of these graphs have no point on the y-axis?","What is the y-coordinate everywhere on the x-axis?"]},{"title":"Reading a distance-time graph for a cycle ride","setup":"A cyclist's ride is drawn on a distance-time graph as three straight stages. Stage 1 goes from (0 h, 0 km) to (0.5 h, 12 km). Stage 2 goes from (0.5 h, 12 km) to (1.25 h, 12 km). Stage 3 goes from (1.25 h, 12 km) to (2 h, 30 km). Find the speed on stage 1 in km/h, say what stage 2 shows, and find the average speed for the whole ride in km/h.","steps":["Stage 1 speed = gradient = change in distance ÷ change in time = 12 ÷ 0.5 = 24 km/h.","Stage 2 is horizontal, so the distance does not change: the cyclist has stopped, for 1.25 − 0.5 = 0.75 h.","For the whole ride, the total distance is the final reading, 30 km, and the total time is 2 h.","Average speed = total distance ÷ total time = 30 ÷ 2 = 15 km/h."],"cues":["Which quantity is on which axis, and which way round does the division go?","What is the gradient of a horizontal line, and what does that mean here?","Is the total distance the sum of the stages or the last reading on the axis?","Why is this not the average of the three stage speeds?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write down the gradient and the y-intercept of the line y = 6x − 11.","a":"Gradient 6; y-intercept (0, −11)"},{"q":"In which quadrant does the point (−7, 3) lie?","a":"The second quadrant, top left, where x is negative and y is positive"},{"q":"The curve y = (x − 4)(x + 2) meets the x-axis at two points. What is the x-coordinate of its turning point?","a":"x = 1, midway between the roots 4 and −2"},{"q":"A distance-time graph is a straight line from (0 h, 0 km) to (4 h, 100 km). What is the speed?","a":"25 km/h"}]},"room":{"facts":["Gradient: m = (change in y) ÷ (change in x) = (y₂ − y₁) ÷ (x₂ − x₁).","A straight line has equation y = mx + c, where m is the gradient and c is the y-intercept; parallel lines have equal m.","The roots of a quadratic are the x values where its curve crosses the x-axis: put y = 0 and factorise.","A parabola is symmetrical, so its turning point lies midway between its roots: x = (one root + the other root) ÷ 2.","On a distance-time graph the gradient is the speed; a horizontal section means stationary, and average speed = total distance ÷ total time.","y = 1/x, with x ≠ 0, has two separate branches with the x-axis and the y-axis as asymptotes."],"questions":[{"q":"Write down the coordinates of the point that is 3 units to the left of the origin and 5 units below it. (non-calculator)","a":"(−3, −5)","sol":"Left of the origin means a negative x-coordinate and below the origin means a negative y-coordinate, so the point is (−3, −5). B1 for the correct ordered pair.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Moving left from the origin and moving down from it both change the sign of a coordinate.","The horizontal move gives the x-coordinate and the vertical move gives the y-coordinate; they are written in that order.","Write the point as (x, y) and settle the sign of each coordinate from its direction out of the origin before filling in its size."],"codes":["A8"]},{"q":"The point (4, k) lies on the line y = 7 − 2x. The point (h, 1) lies on the same line. Work out the value of k and the value of h. (non-calculator)","a":"k = −1 and h = 3","sol":"The first point gives x, so substitute it: k = 7 − 2 × 4 = 7 − 8 = −1. The second point gives y, so solve for x: 1 = 7 − 2h, 2h = 7 − 1 = 6, h = 3. M1 for a correct substitution into y = 7 − 2x for either point. A1 for both k = −1 and h = 3. (Check: 7 − 2 × 3 = 1.)","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A point lies on a line exactly when its coordinates fit the equation of that line.","One point tells you x and leaves y to be worked out; the other tells you y, so that one needs the equation solved for x.","Write y = 7 − 2 × x with the x-coordinate of the first point in place, then write the equation again with 1 in place of y."],"codes":["A9","A8"]},{"q":"Write down the gradient and the y-intercept of the line y = 5 − 3x. (non-calculator)","a":"Gradient −3; y-intercept (0, 5)","sol":"Rewrite with the x term first, in the form y = mx + c: y = −3x + 5. B1 for the gradient m = −3. B1 for the y-intercept c = 5, that is the point (0, 5).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Both numbers can be read off directly once the equation is arranged in the usual way.","In y = mx + c the coefficient of x is the gradient and the number on its own is the y-intercept.","Rewrite the right-hand side with the x term written first, then match it term by term against y = mx + c."],"codes":["A10"]},{"q":"Which one of these equations has a graph that is a parabola: y = 8 − x, y = x² − 7x, y = 1/x, y = x³? A calculator may be used.","a":"y = x² − 7x","sol":"A parabola is the graph of a quadratic function, whose highest power of x is x². Of the four equations only y = x² − 7x is quadratic. B1.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The shape of a graph is decided by the highest power of x in its equation.","A parabola is the U-shaped curve of a quadratic function.","Take each of the four equations in turn and write down the highest power of x that it contains."],"codes":["A12"]},{"q":"The graph of y = (x − 2)(x + 6) crosses the x-axis at two points. Write down the coordinates of both points. A calculator may be used.","a":"(2, 0) and (−6, 0)","sol":"On the x-axis y = 0, so (x − 2)(x + 6) = 0. M1 for setting each bracket equal to zero, giving x = 2 or x = −6. A1 for both points written as coordinates, (2, 0) and (−6, 0).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A graph crosses the x-axis where its y-coordinate is zero.","A product of two brackets is zero only when one of the brackets is zero.","Write (x − 2)(x + 6) = 0, then set each bracket equal to zero in turn."],"codes":["A11"]},{"q":"A straight line has a gradient of −3 and passes through the point (2, 1). Work out the equation of the line, and write down the coordinates of the point where it crosses the y-axis. (non-calculator)","a":"y = −3x + 7; it crosses the y-axis at (0, 7)","sol":"The gradient is given, so y = −3x + c. Substituting the point (2, 1): 1 = −3 × 2 + c, so 1 = −6 + c and c = 7. M1 for substituting m = −3 and (2, 1) into y = mx + c. A1 for y = −3x + 7. B1 for the y-intercept (0, 7). (Check: −3 × 2 + 7 = 1.)","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The gradient is already known, so the only thing missing from y = mx + c is c.","Put the gradient and the coordinates of the given point into y = mx + c, then solve the resulting equation for c.","Start by writing y = −3x + c, then replace x and y by the coordinates of the point you are given."],"codes":["A9","A10"]},{"q":"The line L has equation y = 4x − 9. The line N has equation 2y = 8x + 6. Show that L and N are parallel, and write down the coordinates of the y-intercept of N. A calculator may be used.","a":"Both gradients are 4, so L and N are parallel; the y-intercept of N is (0, 3)","sol":"Make y the subject of N: dividing 2y = 8x + 6 by 2 gives y = 4x + 3. M1. The gradient of N is 4 and the gradient of L is 4, and lines with equal gradients are parallel. A1. Its y-intercept is c = 3, the point (0, 3). B1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["One of the two equations is not yet in the arrangement that shows its gradient.","Make y the subject of the second equation, then compare the coefficients of x: equal gradients mean the lines are parallel.","Divide every term of 2y = 8x + 6 by 2."],"codes":["A9","A10"]},{"q":"The graph of y = x² − 6x + 5 is drawn. Find the coordinates of its turning point. (non-calculator)","a":"(3, −4)","sol":"Factorise: x² − 6x + 5 = (x − 1)(x − 5), so the roots are x = 1 and x = 5. M1. By symmetry the turning point is midway between the roots: x = (1 + 5) ÷ 2 = 3. M1. Then y = 3² − 6 × 3 + 5 = 9 − 18 + 5 = −4, so the turning point is (3, −4). A1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A parabola is symmetrical about a vertical line through its turning point.","Find the roots by factorising, take the x value midway between them, then substitute that x back into the equation.","Factorise x² − 6x + 5 into two brackets whose numbers multiply to give the constant term and add to give the coefficient of x."],"codes":["A11"]},{"q":"A distance-time graph shows a van's journey. The van travels from (0 h, 0 km) to (1.5 h, 90 km), stays at 90 km until 2 h, then drives back, reaching (3.5 h, 0 km). Find the van's speed on the first stage and on the return stage, in km/h. A calculator may be used.","a":"60 km/h on the first stage and 60 km/h on the return stage","sol":"First stage: speed = gradient = change in distance ÷ change in time = 90 ÷ 1.5. M1. = 60 km/h. A1. Return stage: 90 km is covered in 3.5 − 2 = 1.5 h, so the speed is 90 ÷ 1.5 = 60 km/h. B1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["On a distance-time graph how steep a stage is tells you how fast the object is going.","Speed is the gradient: the distance covered during a stage divided by the time that stage takes.","For each stage write speed = (change in distance) ÷ (change in time), reading both changes off the coordinates at its ends."],"codes":["A14"]},{"q":"The graph of y = 1/x is drawn for x ≠ 0. Explain why the graph has no point at x = 0, and write down the value the y-coordinate gets closer and closer to as x becomes very large and positive. A calculator may be used.","a":"Because 1 ÷ 0 has no value (division by zero is not defined); y gets closer and closer to 0","sol":"The rule is y = 1 ÷ x, and dividing by zero has no value, so x = 0 must be excluded and the curve has no point there. B1. As x becomes very large, 1 ÷ x becomes very small but stays positive, so y approaches 0 and the x-axis is an asymptote. B1.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Think about what the rule 1/x actually asks you to do to each value of x.","One of the four operations has no answer for one particular value of x; for the second part, decide what the rule does to y as x grows, by trying x = 10, then x = 100.","Write the rule as y = 1 ÷ x and ask what the division does first when x is zero, then when x is enormous."],"codes":["A12","A14"]},{"q":"A tank is emptied at a steady rate. The volume V litres is plotted against the time t minutes and gives a straight line through (0, 450) and (15, 0). Find the gradient of the line and say what it tells you about the tank. A calculator may be used.","a":"Gradient −30; the tank loses 30 litres every minute","sol":"Gradient = change in V ÷ change in t = (0 − 450) ÷ (15 − 0) = −450 ÷ 15. M1. = −30. A1. Interpretation: the volume falls by 30 litres each minute, the rate at which the tank empties. B1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A straight line through two known points has a gradient you can work out, and here that gradient carries a meaning.","Gradient = change in volume ÷ change in time, and a falling line makes it negative; then say what one minute does to the tank.","Write gradient = (final volume − starting volume) ÷ (final time − starting time)."],"codes":["A10","A14"]},{"q":"The graph of y = x² + bx + c crosses the x-axis at (−2, 0) and (6, 0). Find the value of b and the value of c, and write down the x-coordinate of the turning point. (non-calculator)","a":"b = −4, c = −12; turning point at x = 2","sol":"The roots are x = −2 and x = 6, so y = (x + 2)(x − 6). M1. Expanding: x² − 6x + 2x − 12 = x² − 4x − 12, so b = −4. A1. And c = −12. A1. By symmetry the turning point is midway between the roots: x = (−2 + 6) ÷ 2 = 2. B1.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Knowing where a quadratic curve crosses the x-axis tells you its factorised form.","Write the equation as a product of two brackets built from the roots, expand it, then compare it with x² + bx + c.","Write y = (x − first root)(x − second root), putting each root into its bracket with its sign changed."],"codes":["A11","A9"]},{"q":"A plumber charges a fixed call-out fee plus a fixed amount for each hour worked. A 2-hour job costs £94 and a 5-hour job costs £190. With C the cost in pounds and h the number of hours, find the equation connecting them in the form C = mh + c, and use it to find the cost of a 7-hour job. A calculator may be used.","a":"C = 32h + 30, and a 7-hour job costs £254","sol":"Gradient m = (190 − 94) ÷ (5 − 2) = 96 ÷ 3. M1. = 32, so the hourly rate is £32. A1. Substituting h = 2, C = 94: 94 = 32 × 2 + c = 64 + c. M1. So c = 30 and C = 32h + 30. A1. When h = 7: C = 32 × 7 + 30 = 224 + 30 = 254, so the job costs £254. A1.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["A fixed fee plus a fixed hourly rate is a straight-line relationship between the cost and the hours.","The hourly rate is the gradient between the two jobs and the call-out fee is the intercept c; then substitute the new number of hours.","Write m = (difference between the two costs) ÷ (difference between the two times)."],"codes":["A9","A10","A14"]},{"q":"The curve y = x² − 5 and the line y = 11 are drawn on the same axes. Write down the coordinates of the two points where they meet, and write down the y-coordinate of the turning point of the curve. (non-calculator)","a":"(4, 11) and (−4, 11); the turning point has y = −5","sol":"Where the graphs meet the y values are equal, so x² − 5 = 11. M1. Then x² = 16, so x = 4 or x = −4. A1. The meeting points are (4, 11) and (−4, 11). A1. The curve y = x² − 5 has its minimum where x = 0, giving y = 0 − 5 = −5. B1.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Where two graphs meet, the two expressions for y must be equal.","Set the two expressions equal and solve, remembering that a positive number has two square roots; the minimum of this curve sits on the y-axis.","Write x² − 5 = 11 and add 5 to both sides."],"codes":["A11","A12"]},{"q":"The time t hours a job takes is given by t = 24/n, where n is the number of workers, and the graph of t against n is a reciprocal curve. Find t when n = 6, find n when t = 3, and explain why the curve never meets the n-axis. A calculator may be used.","a":"t = 4 hours; n = 8 workers; because 24 ÷ n is never zero, however large n is","sol":"n = 6: t = 24 ÷ 6 = 4 hours. B1. t = 3: 3 = 24 ÷ n, so n = 24 ÷ 3. M1. = 8 workers. A1. Dividing 24 by n never gives zero, however many workers there are, so t stays above zero and the curve approaches the n-axis without ever meeting it. B1.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The rule links the two quantities by a division, so its graph is a curve with a branch that levels off.","Substitute the given number of workers to find the time; rearrange the rule to go the other way; then ask whether the division can ever give zero.","Write t = 24 ÷ n and put in the number of workers you are given."],"codes":["A14","A12"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write down the gradient of the line y = 7x − 2.","a":"7"},{"q":"In which quadrant does the point (−4, −6) lie?","a":"The third quadrant, bottom left, where x and y are both negative"},{"q":"Write down the roots of y = (x + 5)(x − 2).","a":"x = −5 and x = 2"},{"q":"A distance-time graph has a horizontal section. What does that section tell you about the object?","a":"It is not moving; it is stationary for that time"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Coordinates (x, y)","back":"An ordered pair fixing a point: x is read first along the horizontal axis from the origin, y second up or down the vertical axis. The order matters.","code":"A8","open":false},{"front":"The signs of the coordinates in the four quadrants","back":"Top right (+, +); top left (−, +); bottom left (−, −); bottom right (+, −). x is negative left of the y-axis, y is negative below the x-axis.","code":"A8","open":false},{"front":"The form y = mx + c","back":"The equation of a straight line: m is the gradient and c is the y-intercept, the y value where the line crosses the y-axis at (0, c).","code":"A9","open":false},{"front":"Gradient of the line through two points","back":"m = (change in y) ÷ (change in x) = (y₂ − y₁) ÷ (x₂ − x₁): rise over run, with both points taken in the same order top and bottom.","code":"A10","open":false},{"front":"Condition for two lines to be parallel","back":"Their gradients are equal. Written as y = mx + c they have the same m and different c, so they keep the same distance apart and never meet.","code":"A9","open":false},{"front":"The equation of a horizontal line","back":"y = a, where a is the y-coordinate every point on it shares; its gradient is 0. A vertical line is x = a and its gradient is undefined, because the run is zero.","code":"A9","open":false},{"front":"What the sign of a gradient shows","back":"A positive gradient rises from left to right; a negative gradient falls from left to right; a gradient of 0 is horizontal. The steeper the line, the larger the size of m.","code":"A10","open":false},{"front":"The gradient of a straight-line graph as a rate of change","back":"It is the change in the vertical quantity for each unit of the horizontal one, and it carries their units, for example km/h, litres per minute or pounds per hour.","code":"A10","open":false},{"front":"Roots of a quadratic graph","back":"The x values where the curve crosses the x-axis, so where y = 0. Found by putting y = 0, factorising, and setting each bracket equal to zero.","code":"A11","open":false},{"front":"Position of the turning point of a parabola","back":"Midway between the roots, because the curve is symmetrical: average the two roots for its x-coordinate, then substitute that x into the equation for its y-coordinate.","code":"A11","open":false},{"front":"The y-intercept of a graph","back":"The point where the graph crosses the y-axis, found by putting x = 0 into the equation. For y = x² + bx + c it is the point (0, c).","code":"A11","open":false},{"front":"The shape of the graph of y = x²","back":"A parabola: a symmetrical U-shaped curve with a minimum turning point. A negative x² term turns it upside down, giving a maximum instead.","code":"A12","open":false},{"front":"The shape of the graph of y = x³","back":"A cubic curve: it rises from the bottom left, flattens near the origin and then rises again to the top right.","code":"A12","open":false},{"front":"The shape of the graph of y = 1/x, x ≠ 0","back":"The reciprocal function: two separate branches, one in the first quadrant and one in the third, with the x-axis and the y-axis as asymptotes.","code":"A12","open":false},{"front":"The gradient of a distance-time graph","back":"The speed. A steeper section means a faster speed and a horizontal section means stationary. Average speed = total distance ÷ total time.","code":"A14","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Gradient of a straight line: m = (change in y) ÷ (change in x) = (y₂ − y₁) ÷ (x₂ − x₁). Not in any of the three lists in the specification's Appendix; the Foundation formulae sheet does not print it either (Appendix note, section 4, Foundation tier insert), so it is not given on the day: recall it.","Equation of a straight line: y = mx + c, with m the gradient and c the y-intercept. Not in any of the Appendix's three lists; the Foundation formulae sheet does not print it either (Appendix note, section 4, Foundation tier insert), so it is not given on the day: recall it.","Parallel lines: equal gradients, m₁ = m₂. Not in the Appendix; the Foundation formulae sheet does not print it either (Appendix note, section 4, Foundation tier insert), so it is not given on the day: recall it.","Roots of a quadratic graph: the solutions of y = 0, found by factorising. Not in the Appendix; the Foundation formulae sheet does not print it either (Appendix note, section 4, Foundation tier insert), so it is not given on the day: recall it.","Symmetry of a parabola: the turning point sits at x = (one root + the other root) ÷ 2. Not in the Appendix; the Foundation formulae sheet does not print it either (Appendix note, section 4, Foundation tier insert), so it is not given on the day: recall it.","Speed from a distance-time graph: speed = distance ÷ time, and average speed = total distance ÷ total time. Not in the Appendix; the Foundation formulae sheet does not print it either (Appendix note, section 4, Foundation tier insert), so it is not given on the day: recall it.","The quadratic formula, x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) ÷ 2a, is in list 1 of the Appendix — 'expected to know… will not be given in the exam'. The Appendix itself is not divided by tier: each list says only 'Refer to the Subject content section to determine the tier at which these formulae could be used'. It is the Subject content section that prints 'including completing the square and by using the quadratic formula' in the Higher content only column, while A18's basic foundation statement is 'solve quadratic equations algebraically by factorising'. Section 4 of the Appendix note records the quadratic formula on the Higher tier insert only, not on the Foundation one. Either way it is not needed in this Foundation room; the roots here are found by factorising.","The kinematics formulae v = u + at, s = ut + ½at² and v² = u² + 2as are in list 3 of the Appendix, which says they 'will be given in the exam in the relevant question'. They are not needed for the graphical distance, speed and acceleration work of A14, which is done by reading gradients off the graph.","Since 2022 Ofqual has required a formulae sheet in every GCSE Mathematics paper, and AQA issues one for each tier. This is a Foundation room, so the Foundation tier insert is the one that matters, and section 4 of the Appendix note lists what it prints: the trapezium, the prism, the circumference and area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios, compound interest and the addition rule for probability. None of the six results in items 1–6 above is on it, and none of them is in any of the three Appendix lists either, so this room's working assumes those six are written from memory. The quadratic formula of item 7 is printed on the Higher insert only, and the list 3 kinematics formulae of item 8 are given inside the relevant question rather than on any sheet. The inserts are reissued each series, so the sheet at the front of the paper governs on the day: check it before you rely on recall."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:11:55Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Mathematically clean - all 15 bank questions, 4 exit tickets, 4 lesson checks and all 5 worked examples re-solved independently and every answer key, method line and mark tally (M/A/B totals match every m) is correct, with 7 non-calculator questions, 5/6/4 questions at difficulty 1/2/3, all six idea codes A8-A14 covered in order and nothing Higher-only anywhere; publish it once the Formulae sheet's closing sentence is narrowed so it stops contradicting its own quadratic-formula and kinematics items."}}},"algebra-equations":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"algebra-equations","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Almost every other room ends with an equation to solve. A ratio problem, an angle chase, a perimeter, a best-buy comparison or a graph question all finish with a line such as 8x − 2 = 46 or 3x + 2y = 12, and the marks are lost at that last step far more often than at the setting-up step. This room is where you learn to balance an equation with brackets and with the unknown on both sides, to take a quadratic that is already equal to zero and read both of its roots off the factorised form, to solve a pair of linear equations together and give the answer as a pair, to turn words and diagrams into algebra and interpret the answer back into the situation, and to solve an inequality and draw its solution set correctly on a number line. Algebra is worth about 20% of the Foundation assessment, and this is the part of it that a paper cannot avoid testing.","idea":[{"h":"Balancing a linear equation, brackets and all","t":"An equation is a statement that two expressions are equal, and it stays true as long as you do the same thing to both sides. Solving means peeling the operations off the unknown in reverse order using the inverse operation: to undo + 5 you subtract 5, and to undo × 7 you divide by 7. If there is a bracket, either expand it first — 5(x − 2) becomes 5x − 10, and the −2 must be multiplied as well — or, when every term on the other side divides exactly, divide both sides by the number outside the bracket. If the unknown appears on both sides, collect the x terms on one side first, usually the side with the larger coefficient so that no negative appears, and collect the numbers on the other. Each collecting line is a method mark, so write it down even on a question you can do in your head. You can also find an approximate solution from a graph: the solution of 3x − 1 = 5 is the x-coordinate of the point where the line y = 3x − 1 meets the line y = 5, read off the axis to the nearest small square. Always check by substituting your value back into the original equation.","code":"A17"},{"h":"Quadratic equations solved by factorising","t":"A quadratic equation has an x² term, and it is solved from the form x² + bx + c = 0, with zero on one side. Factorise the left-hand side into two brackets: find the pair of numbers p and q with p + q = b and p × q = c, so that x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q). A product of two numbers is zero only when one of them is zero, so (x + p)(x + q) = 0 forces x + p = 0 or x + q = 0, giving x = −p or x = −q. That zero product step is the whole point of factorising, and both roots must be written down — an answer with only one root loses a mark. Watch for the two special shapes: x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a), the difference of two squares, whose roots are x = a and x = −a, and x² + bx = x(x + b), with a common factor, whose roots are x = 0 and x = −b. Graphically, the roots are the x-coordinates where the curve y = x² + bx + c crosses the x-axis, and a graph question asks you to read them off to the accuracy the scale allows.","code":"A18"},{"h":"Two equations, two unknowns, one pair of values","t":"A single linear equation in x and y has many solutions; a pair of them usually has exactly one, and your answer is a pair of values that satisfies both. In the elimination method you make the coefficients of one letter equal in size, multiplying one or both equations if you have to, then add the equations when those terms have opposite signs and subtract when they have the same sign. That leaves one equation in one unknown; solve it, substitute the value back into the simpler of the two original equations to get the other letter, and check the pair in the equation you did not use. In the substitution method you rearrange one equation to make a letter the subject and put that expression into the other. Write the answer as x = … and y = …, never as a single number. Graphically, each equation is a straight line and the solution is the point where the two lines cross, so the coordinates of the intersection are the approximate solution.","code":"A19"},{"h":"From a situation to an equation, and back again","t":"Many marks in this topic are for the translation, not the algebra. Start by saying what the letter stands for and in what unit — let m be the number of miles, let c be the cost of one child ticket in pounds — because an unexplained letter can cost a mark. Then write the relationship the situation gives you as an equation: a total means the parts are added, twice as many means a multiplier of 2, the same cost means the two expressions are equal, and a perimeter means all the sides are added. Two separate pieces of information about two unknowns give two simultaneous equations. Solve, then interpret: answer the question that was actually asked, which may be the area rather than x, with the unit attached, and sense-check the value — a length or a number of people cannot be negative, and a number of people must be a whole number. Geometrical problems are examined this way too, using facts such as the angles of a triangle adding to 180°.","code":"A21"},{"h":"Inequalities and their solution sets","t":"An inequality compares two expressions with <, >, ≤ or ≥, and is solved by exactly the same balancing steps as an equation, with one extra rule: multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative number reverses the sign, so from −2x < −6 you get x > 3. The answer is a solution set, not a single value, so it must be written as an inequality such as x ≤ 6 — writing x = 6 loses the mark. On a number line the convention is an open (unfilled) circle for a strict inequality, < or >, because that endpoint is not included, and a closed (filled) circle for ≤ or ≥, because it is; an arrow or a shaded line then shows which way the solution set runs, and a double inequality such as −3 < x ≤ 2 is shown by the line between the two circles. If the question asks for integers, list only the integers inside the set — negative ones count, so −3 < x ≤ 2 gives −2, −1, 0, 1 and 2 — and if the context limits the answer — people, seats, whole pounds — round towards the inside of the set, so n ≤ 17.5 gives at most 17.","code":"A22"}],"examples":[{"title":"A linear equation with a bracket and the unknown on both sides","setup":"Solve the equation 5(x − 2) = 3x + 8, showing every line of working. No calculator may be used, and the value of x is to be given exactly.","steps":["Expand the bracket on the left, multiplying both terms by 5: 5x − 10 = 3x + 8","Take 3x from both sides so the unknown is on one side only: 2x − 10 = 8","Add 10 to both sides: 2x = 18","Divide both sides by 2: x = 9","Substitute back into the original equation: 5(9 − 2) = 35 and 3 × 9 + 8 = 35, so both sides agree"],"cues":["What has to be cleared away before the sides can be compared?","Which term should move so that no negative x appears?","Which operation is undone next?","What single step finishes the solution?","How can the value be shown to be right?"]},{"title":"A quadratic equation solved by factorising","setup":"Solve x² + 2x − 15 = 0 by factorising. Both solutions are wanted, and no calculator may be used.","steps":["Look for two numbers whose product is −15 and whose sum is +2: they are +5 and −3","Those numbers are the ones in the brackets: (x + 5)(x − 3) = 0","A product is zero only if a factor is zero, so x + 5 = 0 or x − 3 = 0","Solving each bracket gives x = −5 or x = 3","Check both: (−5)² + 2(−5) − 15 = 25 − 10 − 15 = 0, and 3² + 2(3) − 15 = 9 + 6 − 15 = 0"],"cues":["What two conditions must the pair of numbers satisfy?","How are those numbers written into the factorised form?","Why does the product being zero help?","What does each bracket give on its own?","How is each root tested?"]},{"title":"Simultaneous equations by elimination","setup":"Solve the simultaneous equations 4x + 3y = 25 and 2x − y = 5, giving the solution as a pair of values of x and y. Work without a calculator.","steps":["The y terms do not match yet, so multiply the second equation by 3: 6x − 3y = 15","Adding this to 4x + 3y = 25 removes the y terms: 10x = 40","Divide both sides by 10: x = 4","Substitute into 2x − y = 5: 8 − y = 5, so y = 3","Check in the equation not used for the substitution: 4 × 4 + 3 × 3 = 16 + 9 = 25"],"cues":["What has to be true of the y terms before they can be removed?","Should the two equations now be added or subtracted?","How is the remaining unknown found?","Which equation is easiest to substitute into?","Which equation is left to check the pair in?"]},{"title":"Turning a worded situation into an equation","setup":"Two taxi firms charge different rates. Firm A charges a fixed £3 plus £1.20 for each mile travelled. Firm B charges a fixed £5 plus £0.80 for each mile travelled. Work out the distance, in miles, for which the two firms charge the same total.","steps":["Let m be the number of miles; Firm A's charge in pounds is 3 + 1.2m and Firm B's is 5 + 0.8m","The charges are equal, which gives the equation 3 + 1.2m = 5 + 0.8m","Take 0.8m from both sides: 3 + 0.4m = 5","Take 3 from both sides: 0.4m = 2","Divide both sides by 0.4: m = 5 miles"],"cues":["What should the letter stand for, and in what unit?","What does the phrase 'charge the same total' become in algebra?","Which terms are collected first?","What is left to remove from the left-hand side?","What operation frees the unknown, and what unit does the answer take?"]},{"title":"An inequality and its solution set on a number line","setup":"Solve the inequality 5x − 7 ≤ 2x + 11 and show the solution set on a number line, saying which kind of circle the endpoint needs.","steps":["Treat it like an equation and take 2x from both sides: 3x − 7 ≤ 11","Add 7 to both sides: 3x ≤ 18","Divide both sides by 3, which is positive, so the sign does not turn round: x ≤ 6","On the number line, draw a closed circle at 6 because that value is included, and shade the line to the left of it"],"cues":["What is done first when the unknown is on both sides?","Which number is removed next?","When would the inequality sign have to be reversed?","Which circle marks the endpoint, and which way does the line run?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Solve 2x + 9 = 23.","a":"x = 7"},{"q":"Solve x² − x − 6 = 0.","a":"x = 3 or x = −2"},{"q":"Solve 7 − 2x < 1.","a":"x > 3"},{"q":"Solve the simultaneous equations 2x + y = 9 and x − y = 3.","a":"x = 4, y = 1"}]},"room":{"facts":["Balance rule: whatever is done to one side of an equation or inequality must be done to the other, and the operations on the unknown are undone in reverse order.","Unknown on both sides: collect the x terms on the side with the larger coefficient, and the numbers on the other side; each collecting line earns the method mark.","Zero product rule: if (x + p)(x + q) = 0 then x + p = 0 or x + q = 0, so x = −p or x = −q — a quadratic has two roots and both must be written.","x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q) where p + q = b and p × q = c; x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a); x² + bx = x(x + b).","Simultaneous equations: make the coefficients of one letter equal in size, add when their signs are opposite and subtract when they are the same, then substitute back and check in the other equation; give the answer as a pair, x = … and y = ….","Graphical solutions: the solution of an equation is the x-coordinate where the two graphs meet, and the solution of a linear pair is the point of intersection of the two lines.","Inequalities: solve as for an equation, but reverse <, >, ≤ or ≥ when multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative number; an open circle means the endpoint is excluded and a closed circle means it is included."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve 7x − 5 = 30.","a":"x = 5","sol":"Add 5 to both sides to undo the subtraction: 7x = 35 (M1). Divide both sides by 7: x = 5 (A1). Check: 7 × 5 − 5 = 30. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The x has had two things done to it; both can be undone by keeping the equation balanced.","Deal with the number that is added or subtracted before the number that multiplies x.","Add 5 to each side first, then divide each side by the coefficient of x."],"codes":["A17"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve 3(x + 4) = 33.","a":"x = 7","sol":"Divide both sides by 3: x + 4 = 11 (M1) — or expand first, 3x + 12 = 33, for the same method mark. Subtract 4 from both sides: x = 7 (A1). Check: 3(7 + 4) = 33. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The bracket is being multiplied by 3, so it can be taken apart or taken off.","Either multiply out the bracket, or divide both sides by the number in front of it — the right-hand side divides exactly.","Divide each side by 3 to leave the bracket alone, then undo the addition inside it."],"codes":["A17"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve x² + 7x + 12 = 0 by factorising.","a":"x = −3 or x = −4","sol":"Two numbers with product 12 and sum 7 are 3 and 4, so (x + 3)(x + 4) = 0 (M1). The product is zero when a factor is zero, so x + 3 = 0 or x + 4 = 0, giving both roots x = −3 and x = −4 (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The equation is already equal to zero, so the left-hand side can be written as two brackets multiplied together.","Hunt for the pair of numbers that multiply to give the constant term and add to give the coefficient of x.","Write the left-hand side as (x + …)(x + …) = 0, then set each bracket equal to zero in turn."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve 4x − 3 < 9.","a":"x < 3","sol":"Add 3 to both sides: 4x < 12 (M1). Divide both sides by 4, a positive number, so the sign is unchanged: x < 3 (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["An inequality is unpicked by the same balancing steps as an equation.","Undo the subtraction first, then the multiplication, and watch whether the number you divide by is positive or negative.","Add 3 to each side, then divide each side by the coefficient of x, leaving the answer as an inequality rather than an equals sign."],"codes":["A22"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve 5x − 4 = 2x + 14.","a":"x = 6","sol":"Subtract 2x from both sides so the unknown appears once: 3x − 4 = 14 (M1). Add 4 to both sides: 3x = 18 (A1). Divide both sides by 3: x = 6 (A1). Check: 5 × 6 − 4 = 26 and 2 × 6 + 14 = 26. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The unknown is on both sides, so the first job is to get it onto one side only.","Take the smaller number of x from both sides to avoid a negative coefficient, then collect the numbers on the other side.","Subtract 2x from each side, and write down the line you get before doing anything else."],"codes":["A17"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve 2(3x − 1) = 4(x + 3).","a":"x = 7","sol":"Expand both brackets: 6x − 2 = 4x + 12 (M1). Subtract 4x from both sides and add 2 to both sides: 2x = 14 (A1). Divide both sides by 2: x = 7 (A1). Check: 2(21 − 1) = 40 and 4(7 + 3) = 40. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Neither side divides neatly by the other's multiplier, so the brackets have to go first.","Multiply every term inside each bracket by the number outside it, then gather the x terms on one side and the numbers on the other.","Write out both expansions on one line before collecting anything."],"codes":["A17"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve the simultaneous equations 2x + y = 12 and 3x − y = 13.","a":"x = 5, y = 2","sol":"The y terms are +y and −y, so adding the equations removes y: 5x = 25 (M1). Divide by 5: x = 5 (A1). Substitute into 2x + y = 12: 10 + y = 12, so y = 2 (A1). Check in 3x − y = 13: 15 − 2 = 13. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Look at the y terms in the two equations and compare their signs.","When the matching terms have opposite signs, combining the equations one way makes that letter vanish.","Add the two equations term by term, and write down the single equation in x that is left."],"codes":["A19"]},{"q":"Solve the simultaneous equations 3x + 5y = 27 and 2x − 3y = −1. (A calculator may be used.)","a":"x = 4, y = 3","sol":"Make the x coefficients match: multiply the first by 2 to give 6x + 10y = 54, and the second by 3 to give 6x − 9y = −3 (M1). The x terms have the same sign, so subtract: 19y = 57 (M1). Divide by 19: y = 3 (A1). Substitute into 3x + 5y = 27: 3x + 15 = 27, so 3x = 12 and x = 4 (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Neither letter has matching coefficients yet, so one pair has to be made to match.","Multiply each equation by a suitable number so that one letter has coefficients equal in size, then add or subtract according to their signs.","Scale the first equation by 2 and the second by 3 so the x terms agree, and write both new equations down."],"codes":["A19"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve x² − 9x + 20 = 0 by factorising.","a":"x = 4 or x = 5","sol":"Two numbers with product 20 and sum −9 are −4 and −5, so (x − 4)(x − 5) = 0 (M1). Setting each factor to zero gives both roots x = 4 and x = 5 (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The constant term is positive and the x term is negative, which tells you the signs in the brackets.","Find the pair of numbers that multiply to the constant term and add to the coefficient of x, then write the two brackets.","List the factor pairs of 20 and test which pair adds to −9."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve 4x + 5 ≥ x + 20 and show the solution set on a number line.","a":"x ≥ 5, drawn as a closed circle at 5 with the line going to the right","sol":"Subtract x from both sides and subtract 5 from both sides: 3x ≥ 15 (M1). Divide both sides by 3, a positive number, so the sign stays: x ≥ 5 (A1). On the number line, a closed (filled) circle at 5 because 5 is included, with an arrow or shading to the right (B1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The unknown is on both sides, exactly as it can be in an equation.","Collect the x terms on one side and the numbers on the other, then decide from the sign of the divisor whether the inequality turns round.","Subtract x from each side and write down the inequality you are left with before dividing."],"codes":["A22"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The graphs of y = x + 1 and y = 7 − 2x are drawn on the same axes and cross at the point (2, 3). (a) Write down the solution of the equation x + 1 = 7 − 2x. (b) Confirm your answer by solving that equation algebraically.","a":"x = 2","sol":"(a) The two expressions are equal where the graphs meet, and the x-coordinate there is 2, so x = 2 (B1). (b) Add 2x to both sides and subtract 1 from both sides: 3x = 6 (M1). Divide both sides by 3: x = 2, which agrees with the graph (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["At the crossing point the two expressions take the same value.","For part (a) read the coordinate that gives the value of the unknown; for part (b) collect the x terms on one side and the numbers on the other.","Write down the x-coordinate of the crossing point, then start the algebra by adding 2x to both sides."],"codes":["A17"]},{"q":"At a theatre, 3 adult tickets and 2 child tickets cost £47 altogether. One adult ticket and 2 child tickets cost £25 altogether. Use algebra to work out the cost of an adult ticket and the cost of a child ticket. (A calculator may be used.)","a":"Adult £11, child £7","sol":"Let a be the cost of an adult ticket and c the cost of a child ticket, both in pounds. The two purchases give 3a + 2c = 47 and a + 2c = 25 (M1). The c terms match in size and sign, so subtract the second equation from the first: 2a = 22 (M1). Divide by 2: a = 11 (A1). Substitute into a + 2c = 25: 11 + 2c = 25, so 2c = 14 and c = 7 (A1). Check: 3 × 11 + 2 × 7 = 47. Adult £11, child £7. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Two unknown prices and two separate purchases means two equations can be written.","Choose a letter for each price, write an equation for each purchase, then compare the child-ticket terms.","Define the letters and write the two equations, one for the £47 purchase and one for the £25 purchase."],"codes":["A21","A19"]},{"q":"A rectangle has length (3x − 2) cm and width (x + 1) cm. Its perimeter is 46 cm. Work out the area of the rectangle. (A calculator may be used.)","a":"112 cm²","sol":"The perimeter is twice the length plus twice the width: 2(3x − 2) + 2(x + 1) = 46 (M1). Expanding and collecting: 6x − 4 + 2x + 2 = 46, so 8x − 2 = 46 (A1). Add 2 and divide by 8: 8x = 48, so x = 6 (A1). The length is 3 × 6 − 2 = 16 cm and the width is 6 + 1 = 7 cm, so the area is 16 × 7 (M1) = 112 cm² (A1). Total 5 marks.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The perimeter is the total distance round the rectangle, and each side length is given in terms of x.","Form an equation by adding the four sides and setting the total equal to the given perimeter; solve it, then use x to find the two side lengths.","Write 2(3x − 2) + 2(x + 1) = 46 and expand before collecting the terms."],"codes":["A21","A17"]},{"q":"A school trip costs a fixed £45 for the coach plus £6 for each student who goes. The total cost must be no more than £150. Form an inequality in n, the number of students, solve it, and write down the greatest number of students who can go. (A calculator may be used.)","a":"45 + 6n ≤ 150, giving n ≤ 17.5, so 17 students","sol":"The total cost in pounds is 45 + 6n and it must not exceed 150, so 45 + 6n ≤ 150 (M1). Subtract 45 from both sides: 6n ≤ 105 (M1). Divide both sides by 6: n ≤ 17.5 (A1). The number of students is a whole number, and 17.5 is not allowed, so the greatest number is 17 (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["'No more than' is a comparison, not an equality, so the statement you write is an inequality.","Build the total cost from the fixed part and the part that depends on n, compare it with the budget, then solve as you would an equation.","Write the total cost as 45 + 6n and place it against 150 with the correct sign before solving."],"codes":["A21","A22"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The length, in centimetres, of the side of a square is x, where x² − 3x − 10 = 0. Solve the equation, say which root cannot be a length and why, and work out the area of the square.","a":"x = 5 or x = −2; x = −2 is rejected because a length cannot be negative, so the side is 5 cm and the area is 25 cm²","sol":"Two numbers with product −10 and sum −3 are −5 and +2, so (x − 5)(x + 2) = 0 (M1). Each factor gives a root: x = 5 or x = −2 (A1). A side length cannot be negative, so x = −2 is rejected and the side is 5 cm (B1). The area of a square is the side squared: 5² = 25 cm² (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The equation is already in the form that can be factorised, and a square's area comes from its side.","Factorise into two brackets and take each to zero in turn, then judge each root against what x measures.","Find the pair of numbers with product −10 and sum −3 and write the two brackets."],"codes":["A18","A21"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Solve 6x + 7 = 31.","a":"x = 4"},{"q":"Solve x² + 2x − 8 = 0.","a":"x = 2 or x = −4"},{"q":"Solve 3x − 5 > 7.","a":"x > 4"},{"q":"Solve the simultaneous equations x + y = 10 and x − y = 4.","a":"x = 7, y = 3"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Equation","back":"A statement that two expressions are equal, true only for particular values of the unknown. Solving it means finding those values.","code":"A17","open":false},{"front":"The balance rule for solving","back":"Whatever is done to one side must be done to the other. Undo the operations on the unknown in reverse order, using the inverse operation each time.","code":"A17","open":false},{"front":"Method when the unknown is on both sides","back":"Expand any brackets, then collect the x terms on the side with the larger coefficient and the numbers on the other side, and finish by dividing.","code":"A17","open":false},{"front":"Approximate solution of an equation from a graph","back":"Draw both sides as graphs on the same axes. The solution is the x-coordinate of the point where they cross, read off the x-axis.","code":"A17","open":false},{"front":"Zero product rule","back":"If two factors multiply to give zero, one of them must be zero: (x + p)(x + q) = 0 gives x = −p or x = −q.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"Factorising x² + bx + c","back":"x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q) where p + q = b and p × q = c. The equation must be set equal to zero before the roots are taken.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"Difference of two squares","back":"x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a), so x² − a² = 0 has the two roots x = a and x = −a.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"Roots of a quadratic on its graph","back":"The roots are the x-coordinates where the curve y = x² + bx + c crosses the x-axis; a graph question wants them read to the accuracy the scale allows.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"Elimination method","back":"Make the coefficients of one letter equal in size, then add the equations if those terms have opposite signs and subtract if they have the same sign.","code":"A19","open":false},{"front":"Substitution method","back":"Rearrange one equation to make a letter the subject, put that expression into the other equation, solve it, then substitute back for the second letter.","code":"A19","open":false},{"front":"Solution of a linear pair on a graph","back":"Each equation is a straight line; the solution is the point where the lines cross, so the coordinates of the intersection give x and y.","code":"A19","open":false},{"front":"Deriving an equation from a situation","back":"Say what the letter stands for and its unit, write the relationship as an equation, solve it, then answer the question asked, with its unit and a sense-check.","code":"A21","open":false},{"front":"The negative-multiplier rule for inequalities","back":"Multiplying or dividing both sides of an inequality by a negative number reverses the sign: from −2x < −6 comes x > 3.","code":"A22","open":false},{"front":"Number line conventions for a solution set","back":"An open circle marks a strict inequality, < or >, with the endpoint excluded; a closed circle marks ≤ or ≥, with it included. An arrow or shading shows the direction.","code":"A22","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["What the paper prints for this room: nothing. Section 4 of the course appendix records what each tier's insert prints, and the Foundation sheet carries the trapezium and prism, the circumference and area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios, compound interest and the addition rule for probability — no rule for solving an equation, a pair of equations or an inequality, and no quadratic formula. The specification's Appendix prescribes nothing for the Foundation techniques this room uses either: balancing, factorising, elimination and inequalities. So nothing this room uses is given on the day; every result below is one you recall and write from memory.","Balance rule (recall): do the same to both sides of an equation or inequality, undoing the operations on the unknown in reverse order with the inverse operation.","Zero product rule (recall): if (x + p)(x + q) = 0 then x + p = 0 or x + q = 0, so x = −p or x = −q.","Factorising a quadratic (recall): x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q) with p + q = b and p × q = c.","Difference of two squares (recall): x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a), giving the roots x = a and x = −a.","Common factor form (recall): x² + bx = x(x + b), giving the roots x = 0 and x = −b.","Elimination rule for a linear pair (recall): with the coefficients of one letter equal in size, add the equations when those terms have opposite signs and subtract when they have the same sign.","Graphical solutions (recall): the solution of an equation is the x-coordinate where the two graphs meet; the solution of a linear pair is the point of intersection of the two lines.","Inequality sign rule (recall): multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative number reverses <, >, ≤ and ≥.","Number line conventions (recall): open circle for a strict inequality, closed circle for ≤ or ≥, with an arrow or shading showing which values are in the set.","The quadratic formula, x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / 2a, is printed on the Higher tier sheet only, not the Foundation one (appendix section 4), and using the quadratic formula sits in the Higher content only column of A18, so a Foundation paper solves quadratics by factorising and you are not asked for it here."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:11:08Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Every answer key is right — all 15 bank questions, the 4 exit-ticket and 4 lesson-check items and all 5 worked examples re-solved independently and agreed — and the room stays cleanly inside Foundation content with 11 sensible non-calculator questions, so the only thing to fix before publishing is the Formulae sheet, which contradicts itself about the quadratic formula and asserts what the post-2022 formulae sheet prints."}}},"algebra-sequences":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"algebra-sequences","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Sequences turn a pattern you can see into a rule you can use. On a Foundation paper you are asked to continue a sequence, to generate terms from a term-to-term or a position-to-term rule, to recognise the triangular, square and cube numbers, Fibonacci-type, quadratic and geometric sequences, and above all to deduce an expression for the nth term of a linear sequence and use it. That nth term is the fastest route to a far-off term: the 100th term takes one substitution instead of ninety-nine additions. Sequences also carry marks in Number, Ratio and Geometry questions dressed up as patterns of tiles, sticks or seats, so the same three ideas come back all through the three papers.","idea":[{"h":"Generating terms: term-to-term and position-to-term rules","t":"A term-to-term rule works from the term you already have: 'start at 4 and add 3' gives 4, 7, 10, 13, … so you need the first term as well as the rule, and you must walk along the sequence one term at a time. A position-to-term rule, usually written as the nth term, works straight from the position number: for 5n − 2 you put n = 1, 2, 3 in turn to get 3, 8, 13, and for the 50th term you put n = 50 without touching any earlier term. Patterns of diagrams are examined the same way — count the sticks, tiles or dots in each pattern, write the counts as a sequence, then find the rule. Marks are lost by using the position number in a term-to-term rule, or by forgetting that n must be a whole number counting from 1.","code":"A23"},{"h":"The sequences you are expected to recognise by name","t":"Some sequences must be known on sight. The triangular numbers 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, … add one more each time; the square numbers are 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, … (n × n) and the cube numbers 1, 8, 27, 64, 125, … (n × n × n). A simple arithmetic progression such as 7, 11, 15, 19, … adds a constant common difference. A Fibonacci-type sequence adds the two terms before it, as in 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, …, so two starting terms are needed. A quadratic sequence such as 2, 5, 10, 17, 26, … has first differences 3, 5, 7, 9 that themselves go up by a constant amount, so you continue it by continuing the differences. A simple geometric progression multiplies by a fixed common ratio r, a rational number greater than 0, as in 3, 6, 12, 24, … with r = 2, or 96, 48, 24, … with r = 1/2. Any other recursive sequence will be defined for you in the question.","code":"A24"},{"h":"Deducing the nth term of a linear sequence","t":"A linear sequence goes up (or down) by the same amount every time, and that common difference d is the coefficient of n. So for 5, 8, 11, 14, … the difference is 3 and the expression begins 3n. The multiples of 3 are 3, 6, 9, 12, …, which are each 2 less than the sequence, so the nth term is 3n + 2; equivalently the constant is the first term minus d, 5 − 3 = 2. Always test the expression on the first term and on one more term before using it. Once you have it, any position can be answered in one line — the 50th term of 3n + 2 is 3 × 50 + 2 = 152 — and you can work backwards too: setting the expression equal to a given number and solving tells you whether that number is a term, because n must come out as a positive whole number. Write the answer as an expression in n, never as a sequence of numbers.","code":"A25"}],"examples":[{"title":"Using a two-step term-to-term rule","setup":"A sequence starts at 4. Its term-to-term rule is 'multiply by 3, then subtract 2'. Write down the first four terms of the sequence.","steps":["Apply the rule to the first term: 4 × 3 = 12, then 12 − 2 = 10, so the second term is 10.","Apply the rule to 10: 10 × 3 = 30, then 30 − 2 = 28, so the third term is 28.","Apply the rule to 28: 28 × 3 = 84, then 84 − 2 = 82, so the fourth term is 82.","The first four terms are 4, 10, 28 and 82."],"cues":["Which number does the rule act on first?","Which term does the rule act on next?","How many terms are still missing?","Does the sequence's own starting value belong in the list?"]},{"title":"A sequence from a pattern","setup":"A café pushes square tables together in one long row. One table seats 4 people, two tables pushed together seat 6 people and three tables seat 8 people. Each extra table adds the same number of seats. How many people can sit around a row of 10 tables?","steps":["Written as a sequence the seat counts are 4, 6, 8, … and the common difference is 6 − 4 = 2, so the nth term starts with 2n.","The multiples of 2 are 2, 4, 6, … and every seat count is 2 more than the multiple in the same position, so the nth term is 2n + 2.","Test the expression on the third row: 2 × 3 + 2 = 8, which matches the eight seats given.","A row of ten tables is the term at n = 10, so substitute: 2 × 10 = 20.","20 + 2 = 22, so a row of ten tables seats 22 people."],"cues":["What are the gaps between 4, 6 and 8?","How does each seat count compare with the multiple of 2 in the same position?","Which row length in the question can test the expression?","Which value of n stands for a row of ten tables?","What still has to be added to 20?"]},{"title":"Deducing an nth term and using it","setup":"A sequence begins 6, 13, 20, 27, 34, … Deduce an expression for the nth term of the sequence and use it to work out the 40th term.","steps":["The common difference is 13 − 6 = 7, so the expression starts with 7n.","The multiples of 7 are 7, 14, 21, 28, … and each is 1 more than 6, 13, 20, 27, so the nth term is 7n − 1.","For the 40th term substitute n = 40 into 7n − 1: 7 × 40 = 280.","280 − 1 = 279, so the 40th term is 279."],"cues":["What is added each time to get the next term?","What do you compare the multiples with to find the constant?","Which value of n gives the 40th term?","What still has to be taken off 280?"]},{"title":"Naming four sequences","setup":"Here are four sequences. A: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, … B: 2, 6, 18, 54, … C: 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, … D: 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, … Name the type of each sequence, and write down the next term of B and the next term of D.","steps":["A: the gaps are 3, 5, 7 and 9, and the terms are 1 × 1, 2 × 2, 3 × 3, 4 × 4 and 5 × 5, so A is the square numbers.","B: 6 ÷ 2 = 3 and 18 ÷ 6 = 3, so each term is multiplied by 3 — B is a geometric progression with common ratio 3, and its next term is 54 × 3 = 162.","C: 1 + 1 = 2, 1 + 2 = 3, 2 + 3 = 5 and 3 + 5 = 8, so each term is the sum of the two before it — C is a Fibonacci-type sequence.","D: the gaps are 2, 3, 4 and 5, so the next gap is 6 — D is the triangular numbers and its next term is 15 + 6 = 21.","So A is the square numbers, B a geometric progression whose next term is 162, C a Fibonacci-type sequence, and D the triangular numbers whose next term is 21."],"cues":["What are the gaps between the terms of A?","What happens to each term of B to make the next one?","Where could the 8 in C have come from?","How are the gaps in D changing?","Which two of the four sequences were asked for a next term?"]},{"title":"Is a number a term of the sequence?","setup":"A sequence has nth term 4n + 3. Decide whether 63 is a term of this sequence, and whether 70 is a term of this sequence, giving a reason in each case.","steps":["If 63 is a term then 4n + 3 = 63, so 4n = 60.","n = 60 ÷ 4 = 15, which is a whole number, so 63 is the 15th term.","If 70 is a term then 4n + 3 = 70, so 4n = 67.","67 ÷ 4 = 16.75, which is not a whole number, so 70 is not a term of the sequence."],"cues":["What equation says that a number is a term?","What must n turn out to be for a term to exist?","How does the same test start for the second number?","Does that division leave a whole number?"]}],"check":[{"q":"The nth term of a sequence is 7n + 2. Work out the 10th term.","a":"72"},{"q":"A sequence starts at 5 and its term-to-term rule is 'multiply by 2'. Write down the first four terms.","a":"5, 10, 20, 40"},{"q":"Deduce an expression for the nth term of the sequence 2, 9, 16, 23, …","a":"7n − 5"},{"q":"Which named sequence is 1, 8, 27, 64, 125, …?","a":"The cube numbers, n³"}]},"room":{"facts":["A term-to-term rule gives each term from the term before it, so the first term must be given as well as the rule.","A position-to-term rule (the nth term) is an expression in n: substitute the position number for n, so the 50th term needs n = 50 and no earlier terms.","For a linear sequence the common difference d is the coefficient of n, and the constant is (first term − d), so nth term = dn + (first term − d).","Square numbers 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100 (n²); cube numbers 1, 8, 27, 64, 125, 216 (n³); triangular numbers 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, 28, 36.","A Fibonacci-type sequence adds the two previous terms; a geometric progression multiplies by a fixed common ratio r, a rational number greater than 0.","A quadratic sequence has a constant second difference, so it is continued by continuing its first differences."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) The first term of a sequence is 7 and the term-to-term rule is 'add 6'. Write down the next three terms.","a":"13, 19, 25","sol":"Work along the sequence one term at a time: 7 + 6 = 13, 13 + 6 = 19, 19 + 6 = 25. M1 for adding 6 to a term correctly at least once. A1 for 13, 19, 25.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The rule acts on the term you have just written, not on its position number.","Take one step at a time, each time using the term you have just found.","The first line of working is 'first term + 6 = …'."],"codes":["A23"]},{"q":"The nth term of a sequence is 8n − 5. Work out the first three terms of the sequence. A calculator may be used.","a":"3, 11, 19","sol":"Substitute the position numbers n = 1, n = 2 and n = 3 into 8n − 5: 8 × 1 − 5 = 3, 8 × 2 − 5 = 11, 8 × 3 − 5 = 19. M1 for substituting at least one position number correctly into 8n − 5. A1 for 3, 11 and 19.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["n stands for the position of a term, and the first term sits at position 1.","Replace n by each position number in turn and work the expression out.","The first line of working is '8 × (first position) − 5'."],"codes":["A23"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Here are the first five triangular numbers: 1, 3, 6, 10, 15. Write down the next two triangular numbers.","a":"21 and 28","sol":"The gaps between the triangular numbers are 2, 3, 4, 5, going up by one each time, so the next gaps are 6 and 7: 15 + 6 = 21 and 21 + 7 = 28. B1 for 21. B1 for 28.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Look at how much is added to get from each triangular number to the next.","Those additions themselves go up by one every time, so continue them.","The first line of working is '15 + (the next addition)'."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A sequence is 96, 48, 24, 12, … Write down the term-to-term rule and the next term.","a":"Divide by 2; next term 6","sol":"96 ÷ 2 = 48 and 48 ÷ 2 = 24, so each term is half the one before: the rule is 'divide by 2' (oe 'multiply by 1/2'; it is a geometric progression with common ratio 1/2). B1 for the rule. 12 ÷ 2 = 6. B1 for 6.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The terms are falling fast, so ask what each term is multiplied or divided by.","Compare a term with the one before it by dividing one into the other.","The first line of working is '96 ÷ 48 = …' to test the rule."],"codes":["A23","A24"]},{"q":"A sequence begins 9, 14, 19, 24, … Deduce an expression for the nth term. A calculator may be used.","a":"5n + 4","sol":"The common difference is 14 − 9 = 5, so the expression begins 5n. M1 for 5n. The multiples of 5 are 5, 10, 15, 20, each 4 less than the terms given, so the nth term is 5n + 4 (check: 5 × 1 + 4 = 9). A1 for 5n + 4.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The amount added each time controls the whole expression.","Write that amount in front of n, then compare its multiples with the sequence.","The first line of working is 'common difference = second term − first term'."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A Fibonacci-type sequence begins 2, 5, 7, 12, … Write down the next two terms.","a":"19 and 31","sol":"Each term is the sum of the two terms before it (2 + 5 = 7, 5 + 7 = 12), so the next term is 7 + 12 = 19. M1 for adding the two previous terms. Then 12 + 19 = 31. A1 for 19 and 31.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["In a Fibonacci-type sequence a term depends on two earlier terms, not one.","Check how 7 and 12 were made, then repeat that operation.","The first line of working is 'third term + fourth term = …'."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"The nth term of a sequence is 60 − 4n. Work out the first term of the sequence that is negative, and say which position it is in. A calculator may be used.","a":"−4, the 16th term","sol":"The terms fall by 4 each time. 60 − 4n = 0 when 4n = 60, so n = 15 and the 15th term is 60 − 60 = 0, which is not negative. M1 for reaching n = 15 or for the 15th term being 0. The next term is at n = 16: 60 − 4 × 16 = 60 − 64 = −4. A1 for −4. A1 for stating it is the 16th term.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The terms are getting smaller, so at some position they cross zero.","Find the position where the expression is exactly zero, then look at the position after it.","The first line of working is '60 − 4n = 0'."],"codes":["A23","A25"]},{"q":"The first four terms of a linear sequence are 8, 14, 20, 26. Deduce an expression for the nth term and use it to work out the 100th term. A calculator may be used.","a":"6n + 2; 100th term 602","sol":"The common difference is 14 − 8 = 6, so the expression begins 6n. M1 for 6n. 6 × 1 = 6 and the first term is 8, so 2 must be added: the nth term is 6n + 2 (check: 6 × 4 + 2 = 26). A1 for 6n + 2. Substituting n = 100 gives 6 × 100 + 2 = 602. B1 ft for 602.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Find the rule that gives a term from its position, then use it once.","Put the common difference in front of n, adjust with a constant, then substitute.","The first line of working is 'common difference = 14 − 8'."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"A geometric progression has first term 5 and common ratio 3. Work out the fourth term, and the total of the first four terms. A calculator may be used.","a":"Fourth term 135; total 200","sol":"Multiply by 3 each time: 5 × 3 = 15, 15 × 3 = 45, 45 × 3 = 135. M1 for multiplying by 3 at least twice in a row. A1 for 135. The total is 5 + 15 + 45 + 135 = 200. B1 for 200.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The common ratio tells you what happens from one term to the next.","Write the four terms out before doing anything with them.","The first line of working is 'first term × common ratio'."],"codes":["A24","A23"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The quadratic sequence 2, 5, 10, 17, 26, … continues. Write down the next two terms.","a":"37 and 50","sol":"The first differences are 3, 5, 7, 9, and these go up by 2 each time (the second difference is 2). M1 for the first differences or for the constant second difference of 2. The next first difference is 11, so the sixth term is 26 + 11 = 37. A1 for 37. The next first difference is 13, so the seventh term is 37 + 13 = 50. A1 for 50.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The gaps are not constant here, so look at how the gaps themselves change.","Work out the differences between consecutive terms, then the differences of those.","The first line of working is a row of first differences under the sequence."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"A tiler builds a row of patterns. Pattern 1 uses 6 tiles, pattern 2 uses 11 tiles and pattern 3 uses 16 tiles, and each new pattern uses the same number of extra tiles as the last. Ali says pattern 20 uses 100 tiles. Is Ali right? Show your working. A calculator may be used.","a":"No — pattern 20 uses 101 tiles","sol":"The tile counts 6, 11, 16 go up by 5, so pattern n uses 5n + 1 tiles (5 × 1 + 1 = 6). M1 for 5n or for a correct expression. Pattern 20 uses 5 × 20 + 1 = 101 tiles. A1 for 101. 101 is not 100, so Ali is wrong. A1 for the conclusion compared with 100.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Counting on twenty times is slow — find the rule that works from the pattern number.","Turn the tile counts into a linear sequence and deduce its nth term.","The first line of working is '11 − 6 = …' to get the common difference."],"codes":["A23","A25"]},{"q":"A savings account holds £500. At the end of each year the balance is multiplied by 1.04, so the year-end balances form a geometric progression. Work out the balance at the end of 3 years, to the nearest penny. A calculator may be used.","a":"£562.43","sol":"End of year 1: 500 × 1.04 = £520. M1 for multiplying by 1.04 once. End of year 2: 520 × 1.04 = £540.80. End of year 3: 540.80 × 1.04 = 562.432, which is the same as 500 × 1.04³. M1 for a complete method of three multiplications by 1.04 (or for 500 × 1.04³). A1 for 562.432 or 562.43 seen. Rounded to the nearest penny the balance is £562.43. A1 for £562.43 with the units.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Each year-end balance comes from the one before it by the same multiplier.","Apply the multiplier once for each year that passes, keeping the full display value each time.","The first line of working is '500 × 1.04 = …' for the first year."],"codes":["A24","A23"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Sequence A has nth term 3n + 5 and sequence B has nth term 40 − 2n. At one position the two sequences have the same term. Work out that position and the value of that term.","a":"Position n = 7; the term is 26","sol":"The terms are equal when 3n + 5 = 40 − 2n. M1 for equating the two expressions. Adding 2n to both sides gives 5n + 5 = 40, and subtracting 5 gives 5n = 35. M1 for collecting the n terms and the numbers on opposite sides. So n = 7. A1 for n = 7. The term is 3 × 7 + 5 = 26 (check: 40 − 2 × 7 = 26). A1 for 26.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["'The same term' means the two expressions give the same value at that position.","Form an equation from the two nth terms and solve it for n.","The first line of working sets one nth term expression equal to the other."],"codes":["A23","A25"]},{"q":"The 4th term of a linear sequence is 23 and the 9th term is 48. Deduce an expression for the nth term. A calculator may be used.","a":"5n + 3","sol":"From the 4th term to the 9th term is 5 steps and the terms rise by 48 − 23 = 25. M1 for 25 and 5 steps identified. So the common difference is 25 ÷ 5 = 5. A1 for 5. The first term is 23 − 3 × 5 = 8, so the expression is 5n + (8 − 5) = 5n + 3. M1 for a correct method to find the constant. A1 for 5n + 3 (check: 5 × 9 + 3 = 48).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The common difference is added the same number of times between any two terms.","Count how many steps separate the two given terms and share the rise between them.","The first line of working is '48 − 23' over the number of steps between them."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Squares are made in a row from matchsticks: 1 square uses 4 matchsticks, 2 squares use 7 matchsticks and 3 squares use 10 matchsticks. A box holds 150 matchsticks. Work out the largest number of squares that can be made from one box, and how many matchsticks are left over.","a":"49 squares, with 2 matchsticks left over","sol":"The counts 4, 7, 10 go up by 3, so n squares use 3n + 1 matchsticks (3 × 1 + 1 = 4). M1 for 3n + 1 oe. Solving 3n + 1 = 150 gives 3n = 149 and n = 49.66…, so at most 49 squares can be made. M1 for a complete method (equation or trial) using 150. A1 for 49. Those 49 squares use 3 × 49 + 1 = 148 matchsticks, so 150 − 148 = 2 are left over. A1 for 2 left over.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The first square costs more matchsticks than every square after it.","Find the nth term for the matchsticks used, then see how far 150 will stretch.","The first line of working is '7 − 4 = …', the extra matchsticks per square."],"codes":["A23","A25"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write down the first three terms of the sequence whose nth term is 6n + 1.","a":"7, 13, 19"},{"q":"A sequence starts at 100 and its term-to-term rule is 'subtract 7'. What is the fourth term?","a":"79"},{"q":"Deduce an expression for the nth term of the sequence 4, 9, 14, 19, …","a":"5n − 1"},{"q":"Write down the next two terms of the Fibonacci-type sequence 3, 4, 7, 11, …","a":"18 and 29"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Term-to-term rule","back":"A rule that gives each term from the term before it, such as 'add 5' or 'multiply by 3'. The first term must also be given before the sequence can start.","code":"A23","open":false},{"front":"Position-to-term rule (the nth term)","back":"An expression in n that gives a term straight from its position: substitute the position number for n. The 20th term needs n = 20, with no earlier terms.","code":"A23","open":false},{"front":"The meaning of n in a sequence","back":"The position of a term, counting from n = 1 for the first term. n is always a positive whole number.","code":"A23","open":false},{"front":"Common difference of a linear sequence","back":"The fixed amount added to each term to reach the next: any term minus the term before it. It is the coefficient of n in the nth term.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"The nth term of a linear sequence","back":"nth term = dn + (first term − d), where d is the common difference. For 3, 7, 11, … d = 4 and the first term is 3, so the nth term is 4n − 1.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"Testing whether a number is a term of a sequence","back":"Set the nth term expression equal to that number and solve for n. It is a term only if n comes out as a positive whole number.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"Triangular numbers","back":"1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, 28, 36, … — the dot triangles, built by adding 1, then 2, then 3, then 4, and so on.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Square numbers","back":"1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100, … — a whole number multiplied by itself, so the nth square number is n².","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Cube numbers","back":"1, 8, 27, 64, 125, 216, … — a whole number multiplied by itself twice over, so the nth cube number is n³.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Arithmetic progression (linear sequence)","back":"A sequence with the same difference between consecutive terms, such as 7, 11, 15, 19, … where 4 is added each time.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Fibonacci-type sequence","back":"Each term is the sum of the two terms before it, as in 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, … Two starting terms are needed.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Geometric progression and its common ratio","back":"Each term is the one before multiplied by a fixed common ratio r, a rational number greater than 0, as in 3, 6, 12, 24, … with r = 2. r = any term ÷ the term before it.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Quadratic sequence","back":"A sequence whose second differences are constant, as in 2, 5, 10, 17, 26, …, where the first differences 3, 5, 7, 9 go up by 2 each time.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Name one sequence in this topic that is not linear","back":"The square numbers 1, 4, 9, 16, …; the triangular numbers 1, 3, 6, 10, …; a geometric progression such as 3, 6, 12, 24, …","code":"A24","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["nth term of a linear (arithmetic) sequence: nth term = dn + (a − d), where a is the first term and d is the common difference — must be remembered.","Common difference: d = any term − the term immediately before it — must be remembered.","Term-to-term rule: next term = the rule applied to the previous term; the first term must be given as well — must be remembered.","Position-to-term rule: term = the expression with the position number put in place of n, so the 50th term is the expression at n = 50 — must be remembered.","Square numbers: nth square number = n², giving 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100 — must be remembered.","Cube numbers: nth cube number = n³, giving 1, 8, 27, 64, 125, 216, 343, 512, 729, 1000 — must be remembered.","Triangular numbers: 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, 28, 36, 45, 55, built by adding 1, then 2, then 3, and so on — must be remembered.","Fibonacci-type sequence: each term = the sum of the two terms before it, so two starting terms are needed — must be remembered.","Geometric progression: next term = previous term × r, and the terms of rⁿ are r, r², r³, … where r is a rational number greater than 0 — must be remembered.","Quadratic sequence: the second differences are constant, so continue the first differences to continue the sequence — must be remembered.","What the paper prints for you: the specification's Appendix has three lists — lists 1 and 2 are formulae that 'will not be given in the exam', and list 3 is the formulae that 'will be given in the exam in the relevant question' (the cone and sphere results and the kinematics formulae). None of the three lists contains a sequence result, at either tier.","What the Foundation tier formulae sheet prints: section 4 of the course appendix records the results on AQA's Foundation insert — area of a trapezium, volume of a prism, circumference and area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios, compound interest, and the addition rule for probability. No sequence result is on that sheet, so every result above has to be recalled on all three papers."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:17:09Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":1,"notes":"Re-solved from the kit JSON itself: all 15 bank questions, 4 exit tickets, 4 lesson checks and all 5 worked examples check out, every solution line and mark allocation is sound, coverage of A23–A25 is in order and stays inside Foundation (quadratic sequences are only continued by differences, never given an nth term), seven questions are non-calculator with hand-sized numbers, and a structural diff against the previously judged version shows the five fixes and nothing else touched — publish it."}}},"ratio":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"ratio","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Ratio, proportion and rates of change is the single biggest topic area on a Foundation paper — Ofqual sets its weighting at 25% of the tier, so roughly one mark in four across the three papers comes from this room. It carries the everyday mathematics the examiners like best: recipes and shares, best buys, percentage rises and falls, speed and density, map scales, and money problems such as interest. It is also where AO3 problem solving lives, because a ratio question is usually two or three ideas joined together, and where marks are most often thrown away by forgetting to put both quantities in the same units before comparing them.","idea":[{"h":"Changing between standard and compound units","t":"Change freely between related units of time, length, area, volume and mass, and between compound units such as speed, rates of pay, prices, density and pressure. Multiply when you move to smaller units (4.5 km = 4500 m) and divide when you move to larger ones. Area and volume conversions square and cube the length conversion: 1 m² = 10 000 cm² and 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³. For a compound unit change both parts, so 12 m/s = 12 × 3600 m per hour = 43 200 m/h = 43.2 km/h. The examiner expects the conversion written down as a line of working, because it usually carries the method mark.","code":"R1"},{"h":"Scale factors, scale diagrams and maps","t":"A scale of 1 : n means every length on the drawing stands for n times that length in reality. To go from the drawing to real life, multiply by n; to go from real life to the drawing, divide by n. Finish by converting to a sensible unit — on a 1 : 25 000 map, 6 cm stands for 150 000 cm, which is 1500 m or 1.5 km. Scale drawings also solve geometrical problems: measure the length or angle on the accurate drawing, then scale the length up. Always state the unit with the answer, because a bare number scores nothing here.","code":"R2"},{"h":"One quantity as a fraction of another","t":"To write one quantity as a fraction of another, put the first over the second with both in the same unit, then cancel. So 3 kg as a fraction of 12 kg is 3/12 = 1/4. The fraction is allowed to be greater than 1 when the first quantity is the larger: 15 m as a fraction of 10 m is 15/10 = 3/2. The order matters — 'A as a fraction of B' always means A on top. Converting the units first is the step the mark scheme looks for, so 250 g as a fraction of 2 kg must become 250/2000 = 1/8 and never 250/2.","code":"R3"},{"h":"Ratio notation and simplest form","t":"A ratio is written with a colon, a : b, and compares parts of the same kind. To simplify, first write both parts in the same unit, then divide every part by their highest common factor: 24 : 36 divides by 12 to give 2 : 3. Decimals and fractions are cleared first by multiplying every part, so 0.5 : 2 becomes 1 : 4. A ratio can also be written in the form 1 : n by dividing both parts by the first. AQA's notes say answers are expected in their simplest form even when the question does not say so, so an unsimplified ratio can lose the accuracy mark.","code":"R4"},{"h":"Sharing in a ratio and using ratio in context","t":"To divide a quantity in the ratio a : b, add the parts to get the total number of parts, divide the quantity by that total to find one part, then multiply. Sharing £96 in the ratio 5 : 3 gives 8 parts, one part is £12, so the shares are £60 and £36 — and they must add back to £96, which is the quickest check. Watch for part : whole ratios, where one number already is the total. The same method handles mixing, concentrations, scaling recipes and best-buy comparisons, where you compare the price of the same amount of each product.","code":"R5"},{"h":"A multiplicative relationship as a ratio or a fraction","t":"Statements such as 'A is three times as long as B' are multiplicative, and can be written either way round. As a ratio, A : B = 3 : 1; as a fraction, A = 3 × B, or B is 1/3 of A. Turning words into one of these forms is usually the first mark in a wordy question. Be careful which quantity is the subject: 'there are half as many cats as dogs' is cats : dogs = 1 : 2, and the fraction of the animals that are cats is 1/3, not 1/2. Writing the ratio with the quantities named above it stops this mistake.","code":"R6"},{"h":"Proportion as equality of ratios","t":"Two pairs of quantities are in proportion when their ratios are equal. So 3 : 5 and 12 : 20 are in proportion because both simplify to 3 : 5, and because 3 × 4 = 12 and 5 × 4 = 20 — the same multiplier acts on both parts. This is exactly what lets you find a missing value: scale the known pair up or down by whatever multiplier turns the given number into the wanted one. Written as fractions, proportion says 3/5 = 12/20, and cross-multiplying is another way to find a missing part.","code":"R7"},{"h":"Ratios, fractions and straight-line graphs","t":"A ratio a : b tells you two different things. It gives the fractions of the whole, a/(a+b) and b/(a+b), so in the ratio 2 : 3 the first share is 2/5 of the total. It also gives the multiplier between the two quantities, b/a, so the second quantity is 1.5 times the first. Because that multiplier never changes, plotting the second quantity against the first gives a straight line through the origin with gradient 1.5, that is the linear function y = 1.5x. Moving between ratio, fraction and graph is often what a problem-solving question is testing.","code":"R8"},{"h":"Percentages and percentage change","t":"Per cent means parts per hundred, so 17% is 17/100 = 0.17, and that decimal is the operator you multiply by. To write one quantity as a percentage of another, divide and multiply by 100: 34 out of 80 is 34/80 × 100 = 42.5%. Use multipliers for change: increase by 15% is × 1.15, decrease by 15% is × 0.85, and percentages above 100% are allowed. For an original-value problem, divide by the multiplier instead of multiplying. Simple interest is the same percentage of the original amount each period, so interest = P × r/100 × n.","code":"R9"},{"h":"Direct and inverse proportion problems","t":"In direct proportion the ratio of the two quantities is constant: double one and the other doubles, and the graph is a straight line through the origin. The unitary method solves these — if 5 items cost £3.75, one costs £0.75, so 8 cost £6.00. In inverse proportion the product is constant: double one and the other halves, so if 6 workers take 10 days, the work is 60 worker-days and 4 workers take 15 days. Deciding which of the two you are looking at is the first mark; the wrong choice loses every mark after it.","code":"R10"},{"h":"Compound units","t":"A compound unit is one quantity per unit of another, so all of them work the same way: speed = distance ÷ time, density = mass ÷ volume, pressure = force ÷ area, rate of pay = wage ÷ hours, unit price = cost ÷ amount. Rearranging gives distance = speed × time and mass = density × volume. The unit tells you the formula: g/cm³ means grams divided by cubic centimetres. To compare two journeys, two wages or two pack sizes, work out the same compound measure for each — price per 100 g, or cost per litre — and compare those.","code":"R11"},{"h":"Comparing lengths, areas and volumes by ratio","t":"When a shape is enlarged by a length scale factor k, the areas are multiplied by k² and the volumes by k³. In ratio notation, lengths in the ratio 2 : 3 give areas in the ratio 4 : 9 and volumes in the ratio 8 : 27. This is the link to similarity: similar shapes have all corresponding lengths in the same ratio, which is also why the trigonometric ratios depend only on the angle. A common error is to scale an area by the length factor, so always square or cube the factor first and say which you are using.","code":"R12"},{"h":"Equations describing direct and inverse proportion","t":"'X is proportional to Y' means X = kY for some constant k, and 'X is inversely proportional to Y' means X is proportional to 1/Y, that is X = k/Y. Find k by substituting the pair of values you are given, write the full equation, then use it. The equation tells you what happens when one quantity changes: in X = 4Y doubling Y doubles X; in Y = 12/X doubling X halves Y. Writing the equation down before substituting is what earns the method mark, and it makes the second part of the question routine.","code":"R13"},{"h":"Gradient as a rate of change, and proportion graphs","t":"The gradient of a straight-line graph is the rate of change of the quantity on the vertical axis with respect to the one on the horizontal axis, and it carries a compound unit. On a distance-time graph the gradient is the speed in m/s or km/h; on a cost-against-litres graph it is the price per litre. Work it out as the change up divided by the change across. Direct proportion shows as a straight line through the origin, inverse proportion as a curve falling towards the axes, and the interpretation sentence with its unit is what scores.","code":"R14"},{"h":"Growth and decay, including compound interest","t":"Growth and decay are repeated multiplication by the same multiplier. A rise of 3% each year is × 1.03 each year, so after n years multiply by 1.03ⁿ; a fall of 12% each year is × 0.88 each year. The Appendix gives compound interest as Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, where P is the amount invested, r the percentage rate and n the number of periods. Never add the percentages together — 3% for 2 years is not 6%, because the second year's interest is worked out on the larger amount. Round money to the nearest penny at the end only.","code":"R16"}],"examples":[{"title":"Which jar is the better value?","setup":"A shop sells coffee in two sizes: a 200 g jar for £4.60 and a 350 g jar for £7.70. Work out the price of 100 g of coffee from each jar, and say which jar is the better value. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Scale the smaller jar down to 100 g: £4.60 ÷ 2 = £2.30 per 100 g.","Scale the larger jar down the same way: £7.70 ÷ 3.5 = £2.20 per 100 g.","£2.20 is less than £2.30, so the larger jar gives more coffee for each pound and is the better value."],"cues":["How many lots of 100 g are there in the smaller jar?","What must you divide the larger price by to reach the same amount of coffee?","Which of the two prices per 100 g is the smaller, and what does that mean?"]},{"title":"Finding the price before a sale","setup":"A coat is reduced by 20% in a sale. Its sale price is £68. Work out the price of the coat before the sale.","steps":["Taking 20% off leaves 100% − 20% = 80% of what it cost before.","As a multiplier that is 0.8, so 0.8 × (price before the sale) = £68.","Undo the multiplier: price before = 68 ÷ 0.8 = 680 ÷ 8 = £85."],"cues":["What percentage of the old price does the shopper actually pay?","What number do you multiply the old price by to get the sale price?","Which operation undoes multiplying by 0.8?"]},{"title":"One speed in two different units","setup":"A car travels 3 km in 2 minutes at a steady speed. Work out the speed of the car in m/s, and then in km/h. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Put both quantities into the units wanted first: 3 km = 3000 m and 2 minutes = 120 s.","Speed = distance ÷ time = 3000 ÷ 120 = 25 m/s.","An hour is 60 minutes, which is 30 lots of the 2 minutes, so the car covers 3 × 30 = 90 km in an hour, a speed of 90 km/h."],"cues":["Which unit does each quantity have to be in before you divide?","Which of distance and time goes on top in a speed calculation?","How many times does the given time fit into one hour?"]},{"title":"Scaling the label and the contents of a tin","setup":"Two similar cylindrical tins have heights 10 cm and 15 cm. The label on the smaller tin covers an area of 200 cm², and the smaller tin holds 400 ml. Work out the ratio of the heights in its simplest form, the label area on the larger tin, and how much the larger tin holds. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Heights 10 : 15 both divide by 5, giving the length ratio 2 : 3.","Areas scale by the square of the length factor, so the areas are in the ratio 2² : 3² = 4 : 9.","Larger label area = 200 ÷ 4 × 9 = 450 cm².","Volumes scale by the cube of the length factor, so the capacities are in the ratio 2³ : 3³ = 8 : 27, with the smaller tin's 400 ml standing for the 8 parts.","Larger capacity = 400 ÷ 8 × 27 = 1350 ml."],"cues":["What is the highest common factor of the two heights?","What do you do to the length factor to get the factor for areas?","How much area does one part of the area ratio stand for?","What do you do to the length factor to get the factor for volumes?","How much liquid does one part of the volume ratio stand for?"]},{"title":"Workers and days: inverse proportion","setup":"A job takes 6 workers 10 days. The number of days d taken is inversely proportional to the number of workers w. Write the relationship in the form d = k/w, find the value of k, and work out how long the job would take 4 workers. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Inverse proportion means the product d × w stays the same, which is the equation d = k/w rearranged.","Substitute the pair given: k = 6 × 10 = 60, measured in worker-days.","So the relationship is d = 60/w.","With 4 workers, d = 60 ÷ 4 = 15 days."],"cues":["In inverse proportion, which combination of the two quantities stays constant?","Which pair of values do you know for both letters?","What does the equation look like once k is known?","What do you substitute for w now?"]},{"title":"The falling value of a van","setup":"A van is bought for £18 000. Its value falls by 12% of its value at the start of each year. Work out the value of the van after 3 years, to the nearest pound. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Losing 12% leaves 100% − 12% = 88% each year, a multiplier of 0.88.","Three years means multiplying by that multiplier three times: value = 18 000 × 0.88³.","0.88³ = 0.681472.","18 000 × 0.681472 = 12 266.496, so to the nearest pound the van is worth £12 266."],"cues":["What percentage of its value does the van keep each year?","How many times is that multiplier applied?","What is the multiplier for the whole three years as a decimal?","How should the money answer be rounded?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write 40 : 24 in its simplest form.","a":"5 : 3"},{"q":"What multiplier decreases an amount by 8%?","a":"0.92"},{"q":"Two similar solids have lengths in the ratio 1 : 4. What is the ratio of their volumes?","a":"1 : 64"},{"q":"A gold bar has mass 386 g and volume 20 cm³. Work out its density.","a":"19.3 g/cm³"},{"q":"On a map with scale 1 : 20 000, two points are 3 cm apart. How far apart are they in real life, in metres?","a":"600 m"}]},"room":{"facts":["The ratio a : b gives the fraction a/(a + b) of the whole for the first share, and the multiplier b/a from the first quantity to the second.","Percentage change multipliers: increase by p% is × (1 + p/100), decrease by p% is × (1 − p/100); to find the original value, divide by the multiplier.","Compound interest: Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, where P is the amount invested, r the percentage rate per period and n the number of periods.","Compound measures: speed = distance ÷ time, density = mass ÷ volume, pressure = force ÷ area; unit price = cost ÷ amount.","Direct proportion is y = kx (a straight line through the origin); inverse proportion is y = k/x, and then x × y is constant.","Similar shapes with length scale factor k have areas in the ratio 1 : k² and volumes in the ratio 1 : k³.","Unit conversions: 1 m² = 10 000 cm², 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³, 1 litre = 1000 ml = 1000 cm³, and 1 m/s = 3.6 km/h.","A scale of 1 : n means real length = drawing length × n, and drawing length = real length ÷ n."],"questions":[{"q":"Write the ratio 45 : 60 in its simplest form. (non-calculator)","a":"3 : 4","sol":"The highest common factor of 45 and 60 is 15. 45 ÷ 15 = 3 and 60 ÷ 15 = 4, so the ratio is 3 : 4. B1 for 3 : 4.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Both numbers in a ratio can be divided by the same amount without changing what it compares.","Look for the largest number that goes exactly into both 45 and 60.","Write 45 ÷ n : 60 ÷ n, where n is that common factor."],"codes":["R4"]},{"q":"Express 250 g as a fraction of 2 kg. Give your answer in its simplest form. (non-calculator)","a":"1/8","sol":"Put both quantities in the same unit: 2 kg = 2000 g. M1 for 250/2000 (or for 2 kg = 2000 g). Cancelling by 250 gives 1/8. A1 for 1/8.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The two quantities are not measured in the same unit yet.","'A as a fraction of B' puts A on top of B once the units agree.","Write both quantities in grams, then put the 250 on top of the other one."],"codes":["R3","R1"]},{"q":"In a test a student scores 18 marks out of 40. Work out this score as a percentage. (non-calculator)","a":"45%","sol":"18 out of 40 is the fraction 18/40. M1 for 18/40 × 100 (or for 18/40 = 0.45). 18/40 = 9/20 = 45/100, so the score is 45%. A1 for 45%.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A percentage is a number of parts per hundred.","Write the score as a fraction first, then turn that fraction into hundredths.","18/40 × 100"],"codes":["R9"]},{"q":"Change 4.2 kilometres into metres. (non-calculator)","a":"4200 m","sol":"There are 1000 m in 1 km, and metres are smaller than kilometres, so multiply: 4.2 × 1000 = 4200 m. B1 for 4200 m.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Decide first whether the answer should be a bigger or a smaller number than 4.2.","Use the number of metres there are in one kilometre.","Multiply 4.2 by the number of metres in one kilometre."],"codes":["R1"]},{"q":"Divide £72 in the ratio 5 : 4. (non-calculator)","a":"£40 and £32","sol":"There are 5 + 4 = 9 parts altogether. M1 for 72 ÷ 9 = 8, the value of one part. Then 5 × 8 = 40 and 4 × 8 = 32, and £40 + £32 = £72 as a check. A1 for £40 and £32.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The two numbers in the ratio count parts, not pounds.","Find how much one part is worth before working out either share.","72 ÷ (5 + 4)"],"codes":["R5"]},{"q":"A map has a scale of 1 : 50 000. Two stations are 8 cm apart on the map. Work out the real distance between the stations, in kilometres. (non-calculator)","a":"4 km","sol":"A scale of 1 : 50 000 means every 1 cm on the map stands for 50 000 cm in reality. M1 for 8 × 50 000 = 400 000 cm. There are 100 000 cm in 1 km, so 400 000 ÷ 100 000 = 4. A1 for 4 km.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The scale tells you what one centimetre on the map stands for in real life.","Work in centimetres first, then change the answer into kilometres at the end.","8 × 50 000"],"codes":["R2","R1"]},{"q":"A season ticket costs £480. The price rises by 7.5%. Work out the new price of the season ticket. A calculator may be used.","a":"£516","sol":"An increase of 7.5% is a multiplier of 1 + 0.075 = 1.075. M1 for 480 × 1.075 (or for 480 × 0.075 = 36 then added on). 480 × 1.075 = 516. A1 for £516.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["A percentage increase can be done in one multiplication.","The new price is 107.5% of the old price.","Write down the decimal multiplier for a 7.5% increase, then multiply £480 by it."],"codes":["R9"]},{"q":"A block of oak has a mass of 1.35 kg and a volume of 1800 cm³. Work out the density of the oak in g/cm³. A calculator may be used.","a":"0.75 g/cm³","sol":"The unit asked for uses grams, so convert: 1.35 kg = 1350 g. B1 for 1350 g. Density = mass ÷ volume, so M1 for 1350 ÷ 1800. 1350 ÷ 1800 = 0.75. A1 for 0.75 g/cm³.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The unit in the answer tells you which two quantities to divide and which units they must be in.","Mass and volume are not yet in units that match g/cm³.","1.35 kg = 1350 g"],"codes":["R11","R1"]},{"q":"The cost of petrol is plotted against the number of litres bought. The graph is a straight line through the origin passing through the point (40, 58), where 40 is the number of litres and 58 is the cost in pounds. Work out the gradient of the line and state what it represents. A calculator may be used.","a":"Gradient 1.45; the price of petrol, £1.45 per litre","sol":"The gradient is the change up divided by the change across, measured from the origin: M1 for 58 ÷ 40. 58 ÷ 40 = 1.45. A1 for 1.45. Because the vertical axis is cost in pounds and the horizontal axis is litres, the gradient is the cost of one litre: B1 for £1.45 per litre.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The gradient of a straight-line graph measures how fast one quantity changes with the other.","Use the origin and the given point as the two points on the line.","Gradient = change in cost ÷ change in litres, taken from the origin to the given point."],"codes":["R14","R10"]},{"q":"£3500 is invested at 2.5% compound interest per year. Work out the value of the investment after 4 years. Give your answer to the nearest penny. A calculator may be used.","a":"£3863.35","sol":"Each year the amount is multiplied by 1 + 2.5/100 = 1.025. M1 for the multiplier 1.025. Over 4 years, Total accrued = 3500 × 1.025⁴: M1 for a complete method. 3500 × 1.025⁴ = 3863.345... A1 for £3863.35.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Compound interest is paid on the interest already earned, so the amount is multiplied again each year.","Use the formula Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ.","The yearly multiplier is 1.025"],"codes":["R16","R9"]},{"q":"y is directly proportional to x. When x = 6, y = 21. Find the value of y when x = 10. (non-calculator)","a":"35","sol":"Direct proportion means y = kx. M1 for k = 21 ÷ 6 = 3.5 (or for the multiplier 10/6 applied to 21). So y = 3.5x: M1 for 3.5 × 10. y = 35. A1 for 35.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["In direct proportion the two quantities always divide to give the same number.","Write the relationship as an equation with a constant in it, then find that constant.","y = kx, so k = 21 ÷ 6"],"codes":["R13","R10","R7"]},{"q":"In a car park the ratio of red cars to cars of other colours is 3 : 17. What percentage of the cars in the car park are red? (non-calculator)","a":"15%","sol":"There are 3 + 17 = 20 parts altogether, so the red cars are 3/20 of all the cars. M1 for 3/20 (oe). 3/20 = 15/100, so 15%. A1 for 15%.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The ratio compares red cars with the others, not with the whole car park.","Turn the ratio into a fraction of the total number of cars first.","Red cars are 3/20 of the total"],"codes":["R8","R9","R6"]},{"q":"Alex and Beth share £4200 in the ratio 5 : 2. Alex then gives 20% of his share to charity. How much money does Alex have left? A calculator may be used.","a":"£2400","sol":"There are 5 + 2 = 7 parts, so M1 for 4200 ÷ 7 = 600 for one part. Alex's share is 5 × 600 = 3000: A1 for £3000. Giving away 20% leaves 80%, a multiplier of 0.8, so M1 for 3000 × 0.8. 3000 × 0.8 = 2400. A1 for £2400.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Two separate ideas are joined here: a share in a ratio, then a percentage of that share.","Work out Alex's share in pounds before you touch the percentage.","4200 ÷ (5 + 2)"],"codes":["R5","R9"]},{"q":"A plan of a garden is drawn to a scale of 1 : 200. On the plan a rectangular lawn measures 6 cm by 4.5 cm. Work out the real area of the lawn, in square metres. A calculator may be used.","a":"108 m²","sol":"Work on the plan first: area on the plan = 6 × 4.5 = 27 cm². M1 for 27 cm². Lengths are scaled by 200, so areas are scaled by the square of that factor: 200² = 40 000. M1 for the area scale factor 40 000. Real area = 27 × 40 000 = 1 080 000 cm². A1 for 1 080 000 cm². There are 10 000 cm² in 1 m², so 1 080 000 ÷ 10 000 = 108. A1 for 108 m².","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The plan is a scale drawing, but an area is not scaled by the same factor as a length.","Find the area of the lawn on the plan, then scale that area up and change it into square metres.","Area on the plan = 6 × 4.5"],"codes":["R2","R12","R1"]},{"q":"A lorry travels 150 km at an average speed of 60 km/h, and then a further 90 km at an average speed of 45 km/h. Work out the average speed for the whole journey. Give your answer in km/h to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"53.3 km/h","sol":"Time = distance ÷ speed. M1 for 150 ÷ 60 = 2.5 h. M1 for 90 ÷ 45 = 2 h. Total distance = 150 + 90 = 240 km and total time = 2.5 + 2 = 4.5 h: A1 for 240 km and 4.5 h. Average speed = total distance ÷ total time, so M1 for 240 ÷ 4.5 = 53.33... A1 for 53.3 km/h.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["An average speed for a whole journey is not the average of the two speeds.","Find how long each part of the journey takes, then use the totals.","First part: time = 150 ÷ 60"],"codes":["R11","R1"]},{"q":"Squash is mixed with water to make a drink. Drink A is made from squash and water in the ratio 3 : 17. Drink B is made from squash and water in the ratio 2 : 11. Which drink has the greater proportion of squash? You must show your working. (non-calculator)","a":"Drink B","sol":"Write each as the fraction of the drink that is squash: M1 for 3/20 for A and 2/13 for B. A common denominator is 260: M1 for 3/20 = 39/260 and 2/13 = 40/260. A1 for both correct fractions (or decimals 0.15 and 0.1538...). 40/260 is greater than 39/260, so Drink B has the greater proportion of squash. A1 for Drink B with the comparison shown.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The two ratios compare squash with water, so they cannot be compared directly.","Write the amount of squash as a fraction of the whole drink for each one.","Drink A is 3/20 squash"],"codes":["R5","R8","R7"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 20 : 45 in its simplest form.","a":"4 : 9"},{"q":"A force of 24 N acts on an area of 3 m². Work out the pressure.","a":"8 N/m²"},{"q":"Increase £60 by 5%.","a":"£63"},{"q":"y is inversely proportional to x. When x = 3, y = 8. Find y when x = 4.","a":"6"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Ratio in its simplest form","back":"Both parts in the same unit, then divide every part by their highest common factor, so 24 : 36 = 2 : 3. Answers are expected in simplest form even when the question does not say so.","code":"R4","open":false},{"front":"One quantity as a fraction of another","back":"'A as a fraction of B' is A/B with both in the same unit, cancelled down. It may be greater than 1, as 15 m as a fraction of 10 m is 3/2.","code":"R3","open":false},{"front":"Sharing a quantity in the ratio a : b","back":"Add the parts to get a + b, divide the quantity by that to find one part, then multiply by a and by b. The two shares must add back to the original quantity.","code":"R5","open":false},{"front":"Fraction and multiplier from the ratio a : b","back":"The first quantity is a/(a + b) of the whole and the second is b/(a + b). The multiplier from the first quantity to the second is b/a.","code":"R8","open":false},{"front":"Proportion","back":"Two pairs of quantities are in proportion when their ratios are equal, as 3 : 5 and 12 : 20 are. The same multiplier acts on both parts, which is how a missing value is found.","code":"R7","open":false},{"front":"'A is three times as long as B' written two ways","back":"As a ratio, A : B = 3 : 1. As a fraction or equation, A = 3B, so B is 1/3 of A. The same multiplicative relationship can be written either way.","code":"R6","open":false},{"front":"Converting between units of length, area and volume","back":"Multiply when the new unit is smaller, divide when it is larger. Area squares the length conversion and volume cubes it: 1 m² = 10 000 cm², 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³, 1 litre = 1000 cm³.","code":"R1","open":false},{"front":"Multiplier for a percentage change","back":"Increase by p% is × (1 + p/100); decrease by p% is × (1 − p/100). So +15% is × 1.15 and −15% is × 0.85. To find the original value, divide the new amount by the multiplier.","code":"R9","open":false},{"front":"Gradient as a rate of change","back":"On a straight-line graph the gradient is the change up divided by the change across, and it carries a compound unit: the speed on a distance-time graph, the price per litre on a cost-against-litres graph.","code":"R14","open":false},{"front":"Compound interest formula","back":"Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, where P is the amount invested, r the percentage rate per period and n the number of periods.","code":"R16","open":false},{"front":"Direct proportion","back":"y = kx: the ratio y : x is constant, doubling x doubles y, and the graph is a straight line through the origin with gradient k.","code":"R10","open":false},{"front":"Inverse proportion","back":"y is inversely proportional to x means y is proportional to 1/x, so y = k/x and the product xy is constant. Doubling x halves y.","code":"R13","open":false},{"front":"Speed, density and pressure","back":"Speed = distance ÷ time; density = mass ÷ volume; pressure = force ÷ area. Each is one quantity per unit of another, and the unit names the division.","code":"R11","open":false},{"front":"A map or drawing with scale 1 : n","back":"Real length = length on the drawing × n; length on the drawing = real length ÷ n. Convert to a sensible unit at the end.","code":"R2","open":false},{"front":"Length, area and volume scale factors of similar shapes","back":"If lengths are in the ratio 1 : k, areas are in the ratio 1 : k² and volumes in the ratio 1 : k³. Lengths 2 : 3 give areas 4 : 9 and volumes 8 : 27.","code":"R12","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ — compound interest, where P is the principal, r the rate per period and n the number of periods. This one is given to you on the day: AQA's Foundation tier formulae sheet, the insert for exams from 2026 recorded in section 4 of the course appendix, prints compound interest (the total accrued). The specification's Appendix still places it in list 2, 'formulae students are expected to know or be able to derive; they will not be given in the exam', against content reference R16 (additional foundation content) — but the sheet is later than the Appendix, and where the two disagree the sheet governs what is printed in front of you. Know what the letters stand for and how to use it, and be ready to derive it as repeated multiplication by the multiplier, rather than memorising it.","Speed = distance ÷ time, and the rearrangements distance = speed × time and time = distance ÷ speed. Not in the Appendix at any of its three lists, so it is not printed in the question: recall it.","Density = mass ÷ volume, and pressure = force ÷ area. Not in the Appendix, so recall them; the unit given in the question (g/cm³, N/m²) tells you which quantity is divided by which.","From the ratio a : b: first share = a/(a + b) of the whole, second share = b/(a + b), and the multiplier from the first quantity to the second is b/a. Not in the Appendix: recall it.","Percentage change multipliers: increase by p% is × (1 + p/100), decrease by p% is × (1 − p/100); original value = new value ÷ multiplier. Not in the Appendix: recall them.","Simple interest = P × r/100 × n, for the amount P, the rate r% and n periods. Not in the Appendix: recall it, and do not confuse it with the compound interest formula above.","Direct proportion: y = kx. Inverse proportion: y = k/x, equivalently xy = k. Not in the Appendix: recall them.","Similar shapes with lengths in the ratio 1 : k have areas in the ratio 1 : k² and volumes in the ratio 1 : k³. Not in the Appendix: recall it.","Unit conversions used in this room — 1 km = 1000 m, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 m² = 10 000 cm², 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³, 1 litre = 1000 ml = 1000 cm³, 1 m/s = 3.6 km/h — are not formulae the Appendix lists, so none of them is given: recall them.","What the Appendix's three lists contain, in full, is set out in the specification: list 1 and list 2 'will not be given in the exam', and list 3 'will be given in the exam in the relevant question'. Of all of these, compound interest is the only one that belongs to this room.","What the Foundation sheet prints: since 2022 Ofqual has required a formulae sheet with every GCSE Mathematics paper, and AQA issues one for each tier in each series. Section 4 of the course appendix records the Foundation insert for exams from 2026: the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, the circumference of a circle, the area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios in a right-angled triangle, compound interest (the total accrued), and the addition rule for the probability of one outcome or another. Of this room's results only compound interest is on it — speed, density and pressure, the ratio fraction and multiplier, the percentage-change multipliers, simple interest, y = kx and y = k/x, the area and volume scale factors and the unit conversions are all absent from it — so every 'recall it' above stands. The inserts are reissued each series, so look at the sheet at the front of your own paper before you rely on this list."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:17:54Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":1,"notes":"Re-solved all sixteen bank questions, the four exit questions, the five check questions and all six worked examples independently: every answer key, every solution line and every M/A/B tally is correct, the Formulae sheet now matches appendix.md section 4 exactly for the Foundation tier, and the room is publishable as it stands."}}},"geometry-properties":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"geometry-properties","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Geometry is 15% of the Foundation assessment, and this room holds the part of it that carries no calculator work: the angle facts, the shape properties, the congruence criteria, the ruler-and-compass constructions, the transformations and the coordinate work. Almost every geometry question on Papers 1, 2 and 3 begins here, because a mensuration question cannot be started until the angles and lengths in the figure have been found. The marks are given for naming the property behind each step, not only for the number at the end, so a correct answer written down with no reason can still score nothing.","idea":[{"h":"The words, letters and marks geometry uses","code":"G1","t":"Points take capital letters, a line segment is named by its two ends (AB), and a triangle ABC has its vertices at A, B and C with the side opposite each vertex taking the matching small letter a, b and c. Angle ABC always means the angle at the middle letter B, between BA and BC. Arrows on lines mean parallel, matching dashes mean equal sides and a small square means a right angle. A regular polygon has all sides equal and all angles equal, so a regular n-sided polygon has n lines of symmetry and rotational symmetry of order n. Questions often say 'draw a diagram from this description', and the marks are for marking every stated equality, parallel pair and right angle on your figure before you work anything out."},{"h":"Ruler-and-compass constructions and loci","code":"G2","t":"Three constructions are set: the perpendicular bisector of a line segment (equal arcs, of radius more than half the segment, centred on each end, joined through the two crossing points), the perpendicular to a line from or at a given point, and the bisector of an angle (an arc from the vertex cutting both arms, then equal arcs from those two points, joined back to the vertex). An angle of 60° comes from constructing an equilateral triangle with the compasses at one setting. Leave every arc showing: rubbing the arcs out loses the method mark even when the drawing is accurate. A locus is the set of all points obeying a rule, so 'the same distance from A as from B' is the perpendicular bisector of AB, 'the same distance from two lines' is the bisector of the angle between them, and 'within d of a point' is a circle of radius d. The perpendicular distance from a point to a line is the shortest distance to that line."},{"h":"Angles at points, on lines and across parallels","code":"G3","t":"Four facts do most of the work: angles at a point add up to 360°, angles at a point on a straight line add up to 180°, vertically opposite angles are equal, and the angles of a triangle add up to 180°. Where a line crosses a pair of parallel lines, alternate angles (between the parallels, on opposite sides of the crossing line) are equal and corresponding angles (in matching positions, on the same side) are equal. Those two are the only parallel-line properties the specification names, so co-interior (allied) angles — the pair between the parallels on the same side of the crossing line — must be derived the first time you use them: extend the crossing line past the second parallel, and one co-interior angle equals the corresponding angle made there, which sits on a straight line with the other co-interior angle, so the two co-interior angles add up to 180°. AQA's Notes are explicit that colloquial names such as 'Z angles' are not acceptable, so name the property in full at every step. From the triangle angle sum you can deduce the rest: a polygon with n sides splits into n − 2 triangles, so its interior angles add up to (n − 2) × 180°, and the exterior angles of any polygon add up to 360°."},{"h":"The properties that define each plane shape","code":"G4","t":"A square, a rectangle, a parallelogram, a trapezium, a kite and a rhombus are told apart by their sides, angles, diagonals and symmetry. A parallelogram has opposite sides parallel and equal and opposite angles equal; a rhombus adds four equal sides and diagonals that cross at right angles and bisect the angles; a kite has two pairs of equal adjacent sides, one line of symmetry and diagonals that cross at right angles; a trapezium has just one pair of parallel sides. Triangles are named by sides (equilateral, isosceles, scalene) and by angles (right-angled, acute-angled, obtuse-angled). You also need the names pentagon, hexagon, octagon and decagon, with 5, 6, 8 and 10 sides. Questions ask you to derive, not just quote: use the property to explain why an angle or a length must be what you say it is."},{"h":"When two triangles are congruent","code":"G5","t":"Two triangles are congruent when one is an exact copy of the other, so every matching side and every matching angle is equal. Four sets of conditions are enough to prove it: SSS (three pairs of sides), SAS (two pairs of sides with the angle between them), ASA (two pairs of angles with the side between them, which also covers AAS) and RHS (right angle, hypotenuse and one other side). AAA is not a congruence criterion — equal angles alone give similar shapes, which can be different sizes. The mark scheme wants the criterion named and the letters matched in the right order, so write the vertices of the second triangle in the order that corresponds to the first: triangle ABC congruent to triangle PQR means A matches P, B matches Q and C matches R."},{"h":"Conjecturing, deriving and proving results","code":"G6","t":"Once the angle facts, the congruence criteria and the properties of quadrilaterals are in place, new results follow from them. Dropping the perpendicular from the apex of an isosceles triangle to its base makes two right-angled triangles with the same hypotenuse and a shared side, so they are congruent by RHS, which proves the base angles are equal. The same style of argument derives the angle sum of a polygon and Pythagoras' theorem. A 'show that' or 'prove' question gives you the result, so nothing may be quoted back: every line must run from what is given, each step naming the fact it rests on, and the conclusion must be written out. Presenting arguments and proofs is assessed under AO2, and the reasoning marks are for the named justifications, not for the final number."},{"h":"Moving, turning, reflecting and resizing shapes","code":"G7","t":"Four transformations are set, and each needs its own defining data when you describe it. A rotation needs the centre, the angle and the direction; a reflection needs the mirror line, given by its equation on a grid (for example y = x or x = 0); a translation needs a column vector, saying how far across and how far up or down; an enlargement needs the scale factor and the centre of enlargement. Rotation, reflection and translation leave every length and angle unchanged, so the image is congruent to the object; an enlargement multiplies every length by the scale factor while the angles stay the same, so the image is similar. A fractional scale factor between 0 and 1 makes the image smaller. Describe one transformation only when one is asked for: naming two loses the mark."},{"h":"The parts of a circle","code":"G9","t":"The centre is the fixed point; the radius is the distance from the centre to the curve; the diameter is a straight line through the centre joining two points of the curve, twice the radius; and the circumference is the curve itself, the distance all the way round. A chord is any straight line joining two points on the circumference, so the diameter is the longest chord. A tangent is a straight line that touches the circle at exactly one point. An arc is part of the circumference; a sector is the region enclosed by two radii and the arc between them, like a slice of cake; a segment is the region cut off by a chord and the arc it cuts off. These names are asked for directly, and they are also the language every later circle question is written in."},{"h":"Geometry on a coordinate grid","code":"G11","t":"On axes, a point is fixed by its coordinates, so geometry becomes arithmetic. The midpoint of the segment joining (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂) is ((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2), the mean of the x-coordinates and the mean of the y-coordinates. The step from one point to another is read off as 'so many across and so many up', and that step also gives the length of the segment: the across and the up are the two shorter sides of a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is the segment itself, so by Pythagoras' theorem (a² + b² = c², the result G6 asks you to derive in this room) the length is √(across² + up²) — from (2, 1) to (14, 6) the step is 12 across and 5 up, so the length is √(12² + 5²) = √169 = 13. Squaring removes any minus sign, so it makes no difference which end you start from. Repeating or reversing the step locates further points: the fourth vertex of a parallelogram, the far end of a segment when the midpoint is known, or the point where two diagonals cross. The shape a set of points forms is identified by testing its properties — equal steps mean equal sides, and the same step repeated means parallel sides. Plot the points on the grid first; an unplotted question is where most marks are lost."},{"h":"Faces, edges, vertices and surfaces of solids","code":"G12","t":"A face is a flat surface, an edge is where two faces meet and a vertex is a corner where edges meet. A cube has 6 congruent square faces, 12 edges and 8 vertices, and a cuboid has the same counts with three pairs of congruent rectangles. A prism has two congruent parallel end faces joined by rectangles, so a triangular prism has 5 faces, 9 edges and 6 vertices. A square-based pyramid has 5 faces, 8 edges and 5 vertices. A cylinder has two congruent circular faces and one curved surface; a cone has one circular face, one curved surface, one vertex and one edge; a sphere is a single curved surface with no edges and no vertices. Curved surfaces are not counted as flat faces, which is where most wrong answers come from."},{"h":"Plans and elevations","code":"G13","t":"A solid is drawn on paper as three flat views, each to the same scale. The plan is the view looking straight down from above; the front elevation is the view looking horizontally at the front; the side elevation is the view looking horizontally from one side, usually the right. Each view shows only outlines and the lines where surfaces step, with no perspective and no hidden depth, so a solid built from cubes gives views made of whole squares. Work a view out column by column: for the front elevation, the height of each column of cubes; for the side elevation, the height of each row from front to back; for the plan, every square position that has at least one cube on it. Questions also run the other way, giving the three views and asking what solid they describe."}],"examples":[{"title":"Angles where a line crosses two parallel lines","setup":"The lines AB and CD are parallel, with A and C on the left. A straight line crosses AB at the point P and crosses CD at the point Q, with Q below P and to the right. Angle APQ = 118°. The angle PQD is marked x and the angle BPQ is marked y. Work out x and y, naming the angle fact used for each.","steps":["Angle APQ and angle PQD both lie between the two parallel lines, on opposite sides of the crossing line PQ, so they are a pair of alternate angles.","Alternate angles on parallel lines are equal, so x = angle APQ = 118°.","Angle APQ and angle BPQ sit side by side at the point P on the straight line AB, so they are angles at a point on a straight line and add up to 180°.","y = 180° − 118° = 62°."],"cues":["Which two marked angles lie between the parallels on opposite sides of PQ?","What does the alternate angles fact say about the sizes of such a pair?","Which angle fact links the two angles that meet at P along AB?","What single subtraction now gives y?"]},{"title":"Two loci and the constructions that draw them","setup":"A rectangular garden PQRS is drawn to a scale of 1 cm to 2 m, with PQ = 10 cm and QR = 6 cm on the drawing. A bird bath B must go inside the garden so that it is the same distance from the side PQ as from the side PS, and also the same distance from the point P as from the point Q. Describe the two ruler-and-compass constructions needed and work out the real distance of B from the side PQ, in metres.","steps":["Same distance from P as from Q: set the compasses to more than half of PQ, draw arcs centred on P and on Q above and below the line, and join the two crossing points — this is the perpendicular bisector of PQ.","That bisector meets PQ at its midpoint, 10 ÷ 2 = 5 cm from P, so every point of this locus is 5 cm along the drawing from P in the direction of Q.","Same distance from the side PQ as from the side PS: draw an arc centred on P cutting both sides, then equal arcs from those two cuts, and join P to where they cross — this is the bisector of angle QPS.","Angle QPS is a right angle, so its bisector leaves P at 45° and every point on it is as far from PQ as it is along PQ; B is therefore 5 cm from PQ on the drawing.","The scale is 1 cm to 2 m, so the real distance is 5 × 2 = 10 m."],"cues":["Which locus holds every point equally far from two fixed points?","Whereabouts on PQ does that locus cross it?","Which locus holds every point equally far from two lines that meet?","What size is angle QPS in a rectangle, and what does halving it give?","What does one centimetre on this drawing stand for?"]},{"title":"Using the properties of a kite to find a missing angle","setup":"KLMN is a kite with KL = KN and ML = MN. The diagonal KM is drawn. Angle KLM = 112° and angle LKN = 54°. Work out angle LMN, and give the property of a kite that makes angle KNM equal to angle KLM.","steps":["In a kite the two angles between a long side and a short side are equal, because the kite has a line of symmetry along KM; so angle KNM = angle KLM = 112°.","The angles of any quadrilateral add up to 360°, since a quadrilateral splits into two triangles of 180° each.","The three known angles total 112° + 112° + 54° = 278°.","angle LMN = 360° − 278° = 82°."],"cues":["Which pair of angles in a kite must be equal, and what makes them equal?","What do the four angles of any quadrilateral add up to?","What is the total of the three angles you now know?","How is the last angle found from that total?"]},{"title":"Proving two triangles congruent inside a parallelogram","setup":"ABCD is a parallelogram and the diagonal AC is drawn, cutting it into triangle ABC and triangle CDA. AB = 9 cm, BC = 6 cm and angle ABC = 71°. Show that triangle ABC and triangle CDA are congruent, naming the criterion and the matching sides, and write down the size of angle ADC.","steps":["Opposite sides of a parallelogram are equal, so AB = CD = 9 cm and BC = DA = 6 cm.","The diagonal is a side of both triangles, so AC = CA.","Three pairs of sides now match — AB with CD, BC with DA and AC with CA — so triangle ABC is congruent to triangle CDA by SSS.","In triangle ABC the angle of 71° lies between AB and BC; the sides matching AB and BC are CD and DA, so the matching angle is angle CDA.","Matching angles of congruent triangles are equal, so angle ADC = 71°."],"cues":["Which sides of a parallelogram are always equal in pairs?","What else do the two triangles share besides those sides?","How many pairs of equal sides are there now, and which criterion needs exactly that?","Which two sides is the given angle between, and which sides match them?","What is true of matching angles in congruent triangles?"]},{"title":"Counting cubes and drawing the views","setup":"A solid is built from centimetre cubes standing on a table. The bottom layer is completely filled, 4 cubes from left to right and 2 cubes from front to back. A second layer of cubes is placed on top of the back row only, and nothing is placed on the front row. Work out the number of cubes in the solid, the number of squares in the plan, and the number of squares in the side elevation seen from the right.","steps":["Count the bottom layer first: it is a rectangle of cubes, 4 along and 2 deep, giving 4 × 2 = 8 cubes.","The back row carries one extra cube at each of its 4 positions, so the solid has 8 + 4 = 12 cubes.","The plan is the view from straight above, and every one of the 4 by 2 positions is covered, so the plan is a rectangle of 8 squares.","The side elevation from the right looks along the length, so its width is the depth of 2 positions: the back position stands 2 cubes high and the front position 1 cube high.","The side elevation is therefore an L-shape of 2 + 1 = 3 squares."],"cues":["How many cubes fill a rectangle 4 along by 2 deep?","How many cubes go on top, and what does that make altogether?","Looking straight down, how many square positions are covered?","Looking from the right, how tall is the solid at each of the two depths?","How many squares does that view contain?"]},{"title":"A rotation followed by a translation","setup":"Triangle T has vertices at (2, 1), (5, 1) and (5, 3) on a coordinate grid. Triangle T is rotated 90° anticlockwise about the origin to give triangle U. Triangle U is then translated 4 squares right and 2 squares up to give triangle V. Work out the coordinates of the three vertices of triangle V, and say whether T and V are congruent or similar.","steps":["A rotation of 90° anticlockwise about the origin sends the point (x, y) to the point (−y, x).","So (2, 1) → (−1, 2), (5, 1) → (−1, 5) and (5, 3) → (−3, 5): these are the vertices of U.","The translation adds 4 to every x-coordinate and 2 to every y-coordinate.","(−1, 2) → (3, 4), (−1, 5) → (3, 7) and (−3, 5) → (1, 7), so V has vertices (3, 4), (3, 7) and (1, 7).","Rotations and translations leave every length and every angle unchanged, so T and V are congruent."],"cues":["What does a quarter turn anticlockwise about the origin do to a point's coordinates?","Where does each vertex of T land after that turn?","What does a translation do to each coordinate?","Where does each vertex of U move to?","Do either of these transformations change the size of a shape?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Two angles meet at a point on a straight line. One of them is 125°. What is the other, and which fact gives it?","a":"55°, because angles at a point on a straight line add up to 180°."},{"q":"What do the interior angles of a hexagon add up to?","a":"(6 − 2) × 180° = 720°."},{"q":"Which construction gives every point that is the same distance from a fixed point A as from a fixed point B?","a":"The perpendicular bisector of the line segment AB."},{"q":"A shape is rotated and then translated. Is the final image congruent or similar to the original shape?","a":"Congruent — rotations and translations leave every length and angle unchanged."}]},"room":{"facts":["Angles at a point add up to 360°; angles at a point on a straight line add up to 180°; vertically opposite angles are equal.","Alternate angles on parallel lines are equal and corresponding angles on parallel lines are equal — these two are the named properties. Co-interior angles add up to 180°, derived from a corresponding pair plus angles on a straight line. Name the property in full — 'Z angles' is not accepted.","The angles of a triangle add up to 180°; the angles of a quadrilateral add up to 360°.","The interior angles of a polygon with n sides add up to (n − 2) × 180°, so one interior angle of a regular n-sided polygon is (n − 2) × 180° ÷ n.","The exterior angles of any polygon add up to 360°, so one exterior angle of a regular n-sided polygon is 360° ÷ n; interior angle + exterior angle = 180°.","The congruence criteria for triangles are SSS, SAS, ASA (including AAS) and RHS. AAA is not one of them.","The midpoint of the segment joining (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂) is ((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2), and its length is √((x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²) — Pythagoras' theorem applied to the across and up steps.","The perpendicular distance from a point to a line is the shortest distance from that point to the line.","A rotation is described by its centre, angle and direction; a reflection by its mirror line; a translation by a column vector; an enlargement by its scale factor and centre."],"questions":[{"q":"The points A, B and C lie on a straight line, in that order. D is a point above the line, joined to B by a straight line. Angle ABD = 47°. Work out the size of angle DBC and name the angle fact you use. (non-calculator)","a":"133° (angles at a point on a straight line add up to 180°)","sol":"The two angles at B lie side by side on the straight line ABC. B1 for the reason: angles at a point on a straight line add up to 180°, so angle DBC = 180° − 47°. B1 for 133°.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Look at what A, B and C together make, and what the two angles at B do to it.","The two angles at B fill the whole of that, so one of them is the whole minus the other.","Your working is a single subtraction from the angle a straight line makes, and the answer must be written with the fact named beside it."],"codes":["G3","G1"]},{"q":"PQRS is a parallelogram, with the vertices named in order round the shape. Angle SPQ = 108°. Work out the size of angle PQR and state the property that gives it. (non-calculator)","a":"72° (corresponding angles on the parallel sides SP and RQ, then angles at a point on a straight line)","sol":"SP is parallel to RQ and PQ crosses both of them. Extend PQ beyond Q to a point T. Angle SPQ and angle RQT are corresponding angles on the parallel lines SP and RQ, so angle RQT = 108°. M1 for 180° − 108°, since angle PQR and angle RQT are angles at a point on the straight line PQT. A1 for 72° with both properties named.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Which pair of sides of a parallelogram does PQ cross, and what is true of those two sides?","Extend PQ past Q. The angle SPQ has an equal partner at Q, in the matching position on the other parallel line.","Write down that matching angle at Q, then subtract it from the angles on the straight line through P, Q and the new point, naming both properties."],"codes":["G4","G3"]},{"q":"A triangular prism has two congruent triangular faces joined by three rectangular faces. Write down the number of edges and the number of vertices of a triangular prism. A calculator may be used.","a":"9 edges and 6 vertices","sol":"B1 for 9 edges — 3 round each triangular face and 3 joining the matching corners of the two triangles. B1 for 6 vertices — 3 on each triangular face.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["An edge is where two faces meet and a vertex is where edges meet at a corner.","Deal with the two triangular faces first, then count what joins their corners together.","Count the edges of one triangle, double it, then add the joining edges; count the corners of one triangle and double it."],"codes":["G12"]},{"q":"A circle has centre O. Name the straight line that joins two points on the circumference and passes through O, and name the straight line that touches the circle at exactly one point. A calculator may be used.","a":"diameter; tangent","sol":"B1 for diameter — a chord through the centre, twice the length of the radius. B1 for tangent — a straight line touching the circumference at exactly one point.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Both are straight lines, but only one of them meets the curve twice.","Think about the longest straight line you can draw with both ends on the circumference, and then about a line that never goes inside.","Give one word for each, taken from the standard names for the parts of a circle."],"codes":["G9"]},{"q":"Write down the number of lines of symmetry of a regular hexagon and the order of its rotational symmetry. A calculator may be used.","a":"6 lines of symmetry and rotational symmetry of order 6","sol":"A regular hexagon has 6 equal sides and 6 equal angles. B1 for 6 lines of symmetry — 3 through pairs of opposite vertices and 3 through the midpoints of opposite sides. B1 for rotational symmetry of order 6.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A regular polygon has all its sides equal and all its angles equal, and its symmetry follows from that.","Try folding it through opposite corners, then through the midpoints of opposite sides.","Count the folds of both kinds — those through a pair of opposite vertices and those through the midpoints of a pair of opposite sides — then turn the hexagon about its centre and count how many positions in one full turn look identical to the start."],"codes":["G1","G4"]},{"q":"The five interior angles of a pentagon are 100°, 115°, 100°, 2x and x. Work out the value of x. (non-calculator)","a":"x = 75","sol":"M1 for the interior angle sum (5 − 2) × 180° = 540°. M1 for 540° − (100° + 115° + 100°) = 540° − 315° = 225°, which is 2x + x = 3x. A1 for x = 225 ÷ 3 = 75.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A pentagon can be cut into triangles from one vertex, and you know the angle sum of a triangle.","Find the total of all five angles, take away the three you are given, and see what is left in terms of x.","Write the interior angle sum of a pentagon on one line, then subtract the total of the three known angles."],"codes":["G3","G4"]},{"q":"In triangle ABC, the point D lies on AB and the point E lies on AC, and DE is parallel to BC. Angle ADE = 74° and angle AED = 51°. Work out angle ABC, naming the angle fact you use, and hence work out angle BAC. (non-calculator)","a":"angle ABC = 74° and angle BAC = 55°","sol":"B1 for angle ABC = 74°. B1 for the reason: angle ADE and angle ABC are corresponding angles on the parallel lines DE and BC, so they are equal. M1 for 180° − 74° − 51°, using the angle sum of triangle ABC with angle ACB = 51° (also corresponding angles). A1 for angle BAC = 55°.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["AB is a straight line crossing the two parallel lines DE and BC — look at the angles it makes with each of them.","Corresponding angles are in matching positions at the two parallel lines; find the one matching angle ADE, then do the same along AC.","State the two base angles of triangle ABC first, then use the angle sum of a triangle on one line of working."],"codes":["G3","G1"]},{"q":"Triangle A has vertices (1, 1), (4, 1) and (1, 3). (a) Triangle B has vertices (−1, 1), (−4, 1) and (−1, 3). Describe fully the single transformation that maps triangle A onto triangle B. (b) Triangle A is enlarged by scale factor 1/2 with centre (1, 1). Write down the coordinates of the images of the other two vertices of triangle A. (non-calculator)","a":"(a) a reflection in the y-axis (the line x = 0); (b) (2.5, 1) and (1, 2)","sol":"(a) B1 for reflection. B1 for in the y-axis, that is the line x = 0 — each x-coordinate changes sign while each y-coordinate stays the same. (b) M1 for halving each step measured from the centre (1, 1): the step of 3 across becomes 1.5, and the step of 2 up becomes 1. A1 for (2.5, 1) and (1, 2).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["In part (a) compare each pair of coordinates: one of them has been left alone.","A reflection is described by its mirror line; an enlargement moves each point along the line from the centre, multiplying its distance by the scale factor.","For (b) write down how far each vertex is across and up from (1, 1), then apply the scale factor to those two steps."],"codes":["G7","G11"]},{"q":"P is the point (−2, 1) and Q is the point (4, 9). (a) Work out the coordinates of M, the midpoint of PQ. (b) R is the point such that Q is the midpoint of PR. Work out the coordinates of R. (c) Work out the length of PQ. A calculator may be used.","a":"M = (1, 5), R = (10, 17) and PQ = 10 units","sol":"(a) B1 for x = (−2 + 4) ÷ 2 = 1. B1 for y = (1 + 9) ÷ 2 = 5, so M = (1, 5). (b) M1 for repeating the step from P to Q — 6 across and 8 up — starting again from Q. A1 for R = (4 + 6, 9 + 8) = (10, 17). (c) The step from P to Q gives a right-angled triangle with shorter sides 6 and 8 and PQ as its hypotenuse. M1 for 6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100 by Pythagoras' theorem (G6). A1 for PQ = √100 = 10 units.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["A midpoint is halfway along, so its coordinates are averages.","For part (b), Q being halfway from P to R means the journey from P to Q happens twice over; for part (c), that same journey is the slope of a right-angled triangle.","Write down how far across and how far up it is from P to Q, then use that step again from Q for (b) and square its two parts for (c)."],"codes":["G11","G6"]},{"q":"In triangles ABC and PQR, AB = PQ = 7 cm, BC = QR = 9 cm and angle ABC = angle PQR = 52°. State which congruence criterion shows that the two triangles are congruent, giving a reason, and write down the length of AC given that PR = 11.4 cm. A calculator may be used.","a":"SAS; AC = 11.4 cm","sol":"B1 for SAS. B1 for the reason: the equal 52° angles lie between the two pairs of equal sides, AB with PQ and BC with QR. B1 for AC = 11.4 cm, because A matches P and C matches R, so AC matches PR.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Count how many pairs of sides and how many pairs of angles you have been given equal.","Where the angle lies matters: check whether the equal angle sits between the two equal sides or not.","Write the two triangles with their vertices in matching order, then read off which side of the first matches PR."],"codes":["G5","G6"]},{"q":"A solid stands on a table. Its plan is a circle of diameter 6 cm. Its front elevation and its side elevation are the same rectangle as each other, 6 cm wide and 10 cm high. (a) Write down the name of the solid and its height. (b) Write down the number of flat faces and the number of curved surfaces the solid has. A calculator may be used.","a":"(a) a cylinder, 10 cm high; (b) 2 flat faces and 1 curved surface","sol":"The plan being a circle and both elevations the same rectangle means the solid has the same circular cross section all the way up. B1 for a cylinder. B1 for a height of 10 cm, read off the height of either elevation. B1 for 2 flat faces — the two congruent circles of diameter 6 cm — and 1 curved surface, a curved surface not being counted as a face.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The three views are flat outlines of the same solid: picture what looks like a circle from above and like the same rectangle from the front and from the side.","A circular plan means the cross section is a circle at every height; the elevations give you the height itself.","Write down which named solid has a circular plan, then read its height off an elevation before counting its surfaces."],"codes":["G13","G12"]},{"q":"(a) Each interior angle of a regular polygon P is 156°. Work out how many sides P has. (b) A regular hexagon, a square and a third regular polygon Q fit exactly around a point with no gaps and no overlaps. Work out how many sides Q has. (non-calculator)","a":"P has 15 sides and Q has 12 sides","sol":"(a) M1 for the exterior angle 180° − 156° = 24°. A1 for 360° ÷ 24° = 15 sides. (b) M1 for 360° − 120° − 90° = 150°, the interior angle of Q, using 120° for a regular hexagon and 90° for a square. M1 for the exterior angle 180° − 150° = 30° and then 360° ÷ 30°. A1 for 12 sides.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The exterior angles of any polygon have a fixed total however many sides it has.","For each part, turn an interior angle into an exterior angle first, then use that total.","For part (b) write down the interior angles of a regular hexagon and of a square, and see what is left of the angles round a point."],"codes":["G3","G4"]},{"q":"A (−1, 0), B (2, 4) and C (8, 4) are three vertices of a parallelogram ABCD, named in order round the shape. (a) Work out the coordinates of D. (b) Work out the coordinates of the point where the diagonals of ABCD cross. A calculator may be used.","a":"D = (5, 0); the diagonals cross at (3.5, 2)","sol":"(a) M1 for AD = BC, the step from B to C being 6 across and 0 up, so D is 6 across and 0 up from A. A1 for D = (5, 0). (b) B1 for the property that the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other, so the crossing point is the midpoint of AC. M1 for ((−1 + 8) ÷ 2, (0 + 4) ÷ 2). A1 for (3.5, 2).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Plot the three points first — which pairs of sides of a parallelogram have to be parallel and equal?","The step from B to C must be the same step as from A to D, because BC and AD are opposite sides.","For (b), recall what the diagonals of a parallelogram do to each other, then use the two ends of one diagonal."],"codes":["G11","G4"]},{"q":"In triangle ABC, AB = AC and angle BAC = 40°. D is the point on BC for which AD is perpendicular to BC. (a) Show that triangle ABD is congruent to triangle ACD, naming the criterion. (b) Hence work out angle ABD. (non-calculator)","a":"(a) congruent by RHS; (b) angle ABD = 70°","sol":"(a) B1 for AB = AC (given, the hypotenuse of each right-angled triangle), AD common to both triangles, and angle ADB = angle ADC = 90°. B1 for RHS. (b) The congruence makes angle ABD = angle ACD, so the base angles are equal. M1 for (180° − 40°) ÷ 2. A1 for 70°.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Write down everything the two small triangles have in common, including what AD does to BC.","One of the four congruence criteria is the one used whenever the triangles contain a right angle.","For (b) use the angle sum of triangle ABC together with what the congruence tells you about its two base angles."],"codes":["G5","G6","G3"]},{"q":"A straight canal is drawn as the line MN on a plan with a scale of 1 cm to 20 m. A well W is marked on the plan, 7 cm from the canal and to one side of it. (a) A landing stage is to be built on the canal, as close as possible to W. Name the ruler-and-compass construction that fixes its position, say why that position is the closest, and work out the real distance from W to the landing stage, in metres. (b) A warning sign must stand exactly 60 m from W. Describe the locus of all its possible positions, giving the size it is drawn at on the plan. (non-calculator)","a":"(a) the perpendicular from W to the line MN; 140 m; (b) a circle centred on W, drawn with radius 3 cm on the plan","sol":"(a) B1 for constructing the perpendicular from the point W to the line MN — an arc centred on W cutting MN twice, then equal arcs from those two cuts, joined back to W — the shortest distance from a point to a line being the perpendicular distance. M1 for 7 × 20. A1 for 140 m. (b) B1 for a circle centred on W, of radius 60 ÷ 20 = 3 cm on the plan.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Each part is a standard locus — decide what set of points each description picks out before reaching for the compasses.","'As close as possible to a line' and 'a fixed distance from a point' are two different loci; think about what each one looks like drawn.","For (a) write down which line the shortest route from W must be at right angles to, then turn the 7 cm into metres; for (b) turn the 60 m back into centimetres first."],"codes":["G2","G1"]},{"q":"The four interior angles of a quadrilateral are in the ratio 2 : 3 : 4 : 6. Work out the size of the largest angle. (non-calculator)","a":"144°","sol":"M1 for 2 + 3 + 4 + 6 = 15 parts. M1 for 360° ÷ 15 = 24° for one part, using the fact that the angles of a quadrilateral add up to 360°. A1 for the largest angle 6 × 24° = 144°.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["You need the total the four angles must come to before the ratio can be shared out.","Add the ratio parts to find how many equal shares that total is split into.","Write the angle sum of a quadrilateral on one line and the total number of parts on the next, then find one part."],"codes":["G4","G3"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Work out the size of one exterior angle of a regular octagon.","a":"45°"},{"q":"Two triangles have two pairs of equal sides and the angle between those sides equal. Name the congruence criterion.","a":"SAS"},{"q":"Write down the coordinates of the midpoint of the line segment joining (3, −2) and (9, 6).","a":"(6, 2)"},{"q":"Write down the number of edges and the number of vertices of a cuboid.","a":"12 edges and 8 vertices"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Alternate and corresponding angles on parallel lines","back":"Alternate angles lie between the parallel lines on opposite sides of the crossing line and are equal. Corresponding angles lie in matching positions on the same side and are equal. Never write 'Z angles'.","code":"G3","open":false},{"front":"Angles at a point and angles on a straight line","back":"Angles at a point add up to 360°. Angles at a point on a straight line add up to 180°. Vertically opposite angles, made where two straight lines cross, are equal.","code":"G3","open":false},{"front":"Interior and exterior angles of a polygon with n sides","back":"Interior angles add up to (n − 2) × 180°. Exterior angles add up to 360°. At each vertex, interior + exterior = 180°. For a regular polygon divide each total by n.","code":"G3","open":false},{"front":"Properties of a rhombus","back":"Four equal sides; opposite sides parallel; opposite angles equal; diagonals bisect each other at right angles and bisect the angles; two lines of symmetry; rotational symmetry of order 2.","code":"G4","open":false},{"front":"Properties of a kite","back":"Two pairs of equal adjacent sides; one pair of opposite angles equal; one line of symmetry — the diagonal joining the two vertices where the equal sides meet, which bisects the other diagonal at right angles.","code":"G4","open":false},{"front":"The polygon names required by the specification","back":"Pentagon 5 sides, hexagon 6 sides, octagon 8 sides, decagon 10 sides. A regular polygon has all sides and all angles equal.","code":"G4","open":false},{"front":"The four congruence criteria for triangles","back":"SSS; SAS, with the angle between the two sides; ASA, with the side between the two angles (AAS counts); RHS for right-angled triangles. AAA is not a congruence criterion — it gives similar shapes.","code":"G5","open":false},{"front":"Base angles of an isosceles triangle","back":"They are equal. The perpendicular from the apex to the base cuts the triangle into two triangles congruent by RHS, so the two base angles must match.","code":"G6","open":false},{"front":"Perpendicular bisector of a line segment AB","back":"The locus of points equally far from A and from B. Construct it with arcs of the same radius, more than half of AB, centred on A and on B, joined through both crossing points.","code":"G2","open":false},{"front":"Bisector of an angle, and the shortest distance to a line","back":"The locus of points equally far from both arms: arc from the vertex cutting both arms, then equal arcs from those cuts, joined to the vertex. The shortest distance from a point to a line is the perpendicular distance.","code":"G2","open":false},{"front":"The standard labelling of a triangle","back":"Vertices take capital letters A, B, C; the side opposite each vertex takes the matching small letter a, b, c. Angle ABC is the angle at the middle letter B.","code":"G1","open":false},{"front":"The parts of a circle","back":"Radius: centre to curve. Diameter: chord through the centre. Chord: joins two points on the curve. Tangent: touches at one point. Arc: part of the circumference. Sector: two radii and an arc; segment: a chord and an arc.","code":"G9","open":false},{"front":"Faces, edges and vertices of a triangular prism","back":"5 faces — two congruent triangles and three rectangles — with 9 edges and 6 vertices. A cube has 6 faces, 12 edges and 8 vertices.","code":"G12","open":false},{"front":"Plan and elevations of a solid","back":"The plan is the view from directly above; the front elevation is the view horizontally from the front; the side elevation is the view horizontally from the side. All three are drawn to the same scale.","code":"G13","open":false},{"front":"Describing each transformation fully","back":"Rotation: centre, angle, direction. Reflection: the mirror line. Translation: a column vector. Enlargement: scale factor and centre. The first three give a congruent image; an enlargement gives a similar one.","code":"G7","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Angles at a point add up to 360° — recall it; it is not in the specification's Appendix of prescribed formulae.","Angles at a point on a straight line add up to 180° — recall it; not in the Appendix.","Vertically opposite angles are equal — recall it; not in the Appendix.","Alternate angles on parallel lines are equal, and corresponding angles on parallel lines are equal — recall them, and name the property in your working; not in the Appendix.","The angles of a triangle add up to 180° — recall it; not in the Appendix.","The angles of a quadrilateral add up to 360° — recall it, or derive it by splitting the quadrilateral into two triangles; not in the Appendix.","Sum of the interior angles of a polygon with n sides = (n − 2) × 180° — recall it, or derive it from the triangle angle sum (G3); not in the Appendix.","One interior angle of a regular polygon with n sides = (n − 2) × 180° ÷ n — recall it; not in the Appendix.","Sum of the exterior angles of any polygon = 360°, so one exterior angle of a regular n-sided polygon = 360° ÷ n, and interior angle + exterior angle = 180° — recall them; not in the Appendix.","Midpoint of the line segment joining (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂) = ((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2) — recall it; not in the Appendix.","Length of the line segment joining (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂) = √((x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²), which is Pythagoras' theorem a² + b² = c² applied to the 'so many across, so many up' step. Pythagoras' theorem itself is the result G6 derives in this room, is in the Appendix's first list and, per section 4 of appendix.md, the Foundation tier formulae sheet prints it, so on the day it is given to you; the coordinate form above is printed nowhere and must be recalled or built from the theorem.","The congruence criteria SSS, SAS, ASA (including AAS) and RHS — recall them by name; not in the Appendix, and AAA is not one of them.","The perpendicular distance from a point to a line is the shortest distance from that point to that line — recall it; not in the Appendix.","What the exam prints: the Appendix lists the DfE-prescribed formulae in three lists — the quadratic formula, the circumference and area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem and the trigonometry results (list 1); the trapezium, prism, compound interest and probability formulae (list 2); and the cone, sphere and kinematics formulae, which 'will be given in the exam in the relevant question' (list 3). The only result this room uses that appears in any of the three lists is Pythagoras' theorem, in list 1, reached here through the length of a segment on a coordinate grid; no angle, symmetry, congruence, construction or other coordinate result of this room appears at all, so none of the rest is printed for you in the question.","From outside this specification: since 2022 Ofqual has required a formulae sheet in every GCSE Mathematics paper, and AQA issues one for each tier. It postdates this Version 1.0 document, is issued in addition to the Appendix and supplies prescribed formulae the Appendix says are to be memorised. Section 4 of appendix.md records the results each tier's insert for exams from 2026 prints, and this is a Foundation room, so the Foundation sheet governs: of everything above, the one result it prints is Pythagoras' theorem, which you therefore do not have to remember. Every other result on this page — every angle fact, the polygon angle sums, the congruence criteria, the constructions, the midpoint and the coordinate length formula itself — is on neither tier's sheet and must be recalled or derived. Read your own tier's sheet on the day."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:28:04Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":1,"notes":"Re-judged from the kit JSON itself after the fix pass: all 24 answers re-solved in Python (16 bank, 4 exit, 4 lesson check) and every one is right and in the form asked, every sol's M/A/B tally equals its tariff, the structure still holds (16 questions at 5/6/5 by difficulty, 9 non-calculator, 6 worked examples with one cue per step, 15 cards all within 40 words, exit ticket of exactly 4), no Higher-only technique appears, no bank question now reuses a worked example's context, and the Formulae sheet's Pythagoras claim matches section 4 of appendix.md exactly — ready to publish."}}},"geometry-mensuration":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"geometry-mensuration","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Geometry and measures is 15% of the Foundation tier assessment, and mensuration — units, measuring, areas, volumes, circles, sectors, similarity and right-angled trigonometry — is where most of those marks sit. It is also the part of the course that hides inside other questions: a cost per square metre needs an area first, a capacity in litres needs a volume in cm³ first, and a question about whether a pole fits in a box needs a length from Pythagoras' theorem. AQA's formulae sheet prints the trapezium, the prism, the circle, Pythagoras' theorem and the three trigonometric ratios for you, so the marks are lost not on recall but on choosing the right formula, putting every length into the same unit before you start, and writing the unit on the answer — cm for a length, cm² for an area, cm³ for a volume. Paper 1 has no calculator, so answers there are left as multiples of π or written from the exact trigonometric values, while Papers 2 and 3 ask for a rounded value you must read correctly off the display.","idea":[{"h":"Standard units, and keeping them consistent","t":"Length is measured in mm, cm, m and km; area in mm², cm², m² and km²; volume in mm³, cm³ and m³; capacity in ml and litres; mass in g, kg and tonnes; time in seconds, minutes and hours; money in £ and pence. Every measurement in one calculation must be in the same unit before you start, so a cylinder of radius 40 cm and height 1.2 m is worked in centimetres by first writing 1.2 m as 120 cm. The links you need are 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 km = 1000 m, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 litre = 1000 ml and 1 ml = 1 cm³, so 1 litre = 1000 cm³. The unit belongs on the answer: an area written as 40 rather than 40 cm² throws away the accuracy mark, because the unit is part of what is being marked.","code":"G14"},{"h":"Measuring lines and angles, scale drawings and bearings","t":"Lines are measured to the nearest millimetre with a ruler and angles to the nearest degree with a protractor, and a small tolerance either side is accepted. A scale tells you what one unit on the drawing stands for in real life: a scale of 1 : 25 000 means 1 cm on the map is 25 000 cm on the ground, so you multiply the map distance by 25 000 and then convert into sensible units, using 100 000 cm = 1 km. A scale given as 1 cm to 4 m is used the same way, by multiplying every drawn length by 4 m. A bearing is a direction measured clockwise from north and is always written with three figures, so a direction of 70° is written 070°; the eight compass points are north 000°, north-east 045°, east 090°, south-east 135°, south 180°, south-west 225°, west 270° and north-west 315°. North lines are parallel, so the bearing of A from B and the bearing of B from A differ by exactly 180°.","code":"G15"},{"h":"Areas of triangles and quadrilaterals, and the volume of a prism","t":"Area of a rectangle = length × width, area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height and area of a parallelogram = base × perpendicular height must all be recalled; only area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h is printed on the formulae sheet, where a and b are the parallel sides and h is their perpendicular separation. The height in every one of these is the perpendicular height, not a sloping side, and taking the slant length is the commonest error examiners report. A prism is a solid with the same cross section all along its length, and volume of a prism = area of cross section × length is on the sheet too. A cuboid is a prism, giving volume = length × width × height, and so is a cylinder, whose cross section is a circle, giving volume = πr²h. Areas come out in square units and volumes in cubic units.","code":"G16"},{"h":"Circles, composite shapes and curved solids","t":"For a circle of radius r and diameter d, circumference = 2πr = πd and area = πr². Both are printed on the formulae sheet, so the mark is lost only by using the diameter where the radius belongs — halve the diameter first, every time. A composite shape is split into parts whose areas you know, such as a rectangle plus a semicircle, or a large circle with a smaller one cut out of it, and the parts are then added or subtracted; a composite solid is handled the same way. When one shape is joined to another, the perimeter is the outside edge only, so an edge that has become internal is not counted. The sphere and cone formulae are not on the formulae sheet: the specification lists them among the formulae given inside the question, so read the question for them. The volume of a pyramid (a third of the base area times the height) is on no list, so learn it. A frustum is a cone with a smaller cone cut off the top, so its volume is the difference of two cone volumes. Where the question says 'in terms of π', leave the π in the answer and do not round.","code":"G17"},{"h":"Arcs and sectors","t":"A sector is the slice of a circle between two radii, and the angle θ at the centre says what fraction of the whole circle it is: that fraction is θ/360. So arc length = θ/360 × 2πr and sector area = θ/360 × πr², and neither is printed on the formulae sheet — both have to be remembered. Write the fraction θ/360 down as a line of its own, because that is where the first method mark falls. The perimeter of a sector is the arc plus the two straight radii, θ/360 × 2πr + 2r, not the arc on its own. The relation works backwards too: given the arc length or the area, divide by the whole circumference or the whole area to get the fraction θ/360, then multiply by 360 to find the angle. A semicircle is the case θ = 180 and a quarter circle the case θ = 90.","code":"G18"},{"h":"Congruence and similarity in calculation","t":"Two figures are congruent if they are identical in every measurement — the same side lengths and the same angles — even when one has been turned over or rotated. Two figures are similar if one is an enlargement of the other: the angles are equal and every pair of corresponding lengths is in the same ratio. That ratio is the linear scale factor, found by dividing a length on the new figure by the matching length on the original, and once you have it every other length follows by multiplying by it (or dividing, going the other way). The hard part is pairing the sides correctly, so match them by the angles they lie between, or by the order of the letters in the two names: in triangles ABC and DEF, AB pairs with DE and BC with EF. A perimeter is a length, so it is multiplied by the scale factor as well.","code":"G19"},{"h":"Pythagoras' theorem and the trigonometric ratios","t":"In a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is c, a² + b² = c². Add the two squares to find the hypotenuse, subtract to find a shorter side, then take the square root. The trigonometric ratios are sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse and tan θ = opposite/adjacent, remembered as SOH CAH TOA. Both the theorem and the ratios are printed on the formulae sheet, so the marks are for choosing correctly: label the hypotenuse first, as it is always the side opposite the right angle, then the side opposite the angle you are using, and whatever is left is the adjacent side. Use Pythagoras' theorem when three sides are involved and no angle appears, and a trigonometric ratio as soon as an angle is involved. To find an angle from two sides, use the inverse function, sin⁻¹, cos⁻¹ or tan⁻¹.","code":"G20"},{"h":"The exact trigonometric values","t":"Paper 1 is taken without a calculator, so the values of sine, cosine and tangent at the special angles must be known by heart: sin 0° = 0, sin 30° = 1/2, sin 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = √3/2 and sin 90° = 1; cos 0° = 1, cos 30° = √3/2, cos 45° = √2/2, cos 60° = 1/2 and cos 90° = 0; tan 0° = 0, tan 30° = √3/3, tan 45° = 1 and tan 60° = √3. Notice that the cosine values are the sine values in reverse order, which halves what there is to learn. They come from two triangles: a square of side 1 cut along its diagonal gives the 45° triangle with sides 1, 1 and √2, and an equilateral triangle of side 2 cut in half gives the 30° and 60° triangle with sides 1, √3 and 2. None of these values is on the formulae sheet, and an answer built from them is left exact rather than rounded.","code":"G21"}],"examples":[{"title":"Area of a trapezium","setup":"A patio is a trapezium. Its two parallel edges are 10 m and 6 m long, and the perpendicular distance between those edges is 4.5 m. Work out the area of the patio. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, with a and b the parallel edges and h the perpendicular separation; this formula is printed on the formulae sheet.","The bracket first: a + b = 10 + 6 = 16.","Halve it: ½ × 16 = 8, so the area is 8 × 4.5 — the line the method mark is awarded for.","8 × 4.5 = 36, so the patio has an area of 36 m²."],"cues":["Which of the three lengths is the h in the formula?","What do the two parallel edges add up to?","What is left to multiply by once the bracket has been halved?","Which unit goes on an area measured in metres?"]},{"title":"Area of a composite shape","setup":"A shape is made from a rectangle 20 cm long and 14 cm wide with a semicircle joined to one of its 14 cm edges, so the semicircle has a diameter of 14 cm. Work out the total area of the shape, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Split the shape into a rectangle and a semicircle; the semicircle's radius is half its diameter, which is 7 cm.","Rectangle: 20 × 14 = 280 cm².","Semicircle: ½ × π × 7² = 76.969… cm² — the line the method mark is awarded for.","Total area = 280 + 76.969… = 356.969…, which is 357.0 cm² correct to 1 decimal place."],"cues":["Which two familiar shapes is this one built from?","Which part of the shape has not been dealt with yet, and what formula does it need?","How does the area of a semicircle compare with the area of a whole circle?","What must happen to the two areas now that both are known?"]},{"title":"Perimeter of a sector","setup":"A sector of a circle has radius 15 cm and an angle of 120° at the centre. Work out the perimeter of the sector, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["The angle at the centre gives the fraction of the whole circle: 120/360, which cancels to 1/3.","The whole circumference is 2 × π × 15 = 30π cm.","Arc length = 1/3 × 30π = 10π = 31.415… cm — the line the method mark is awarded for.","The perimeter is the arc plus the two straight radii: 31.415… + 2 × 15 = 61.4 cm correct to 1 decimal place."],"cues":["What fraction of a full turn is the angle at the centre?","Which measurement of the whole circle is the curved edge a piece of?","How do the fraction and the circumference go together?","Which edges besides the curved one bound a sector?"]},{"title":"Finding an angle with a trigonometric ratio","setup":"A wheelchair ramp rises 1.2 m vertically over a horizontal distance of 7.5 m. Work out the angle the ramp makes with the horizontal ground, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Label the sides: the rise is opposite the angle and the horizontal distance is adjacent to it, and neither is the hypotenuse, so the ratio needed is tangent.","tan θ = opposite/adjacent = 1.2/7.5 — the line the method mark is awarded for.","1.2 ÷ 7.5 = 0.16, so tan θ = 0.16.","θ = tan⁻¹(0.16) = 9.090…, so the ramp makes an angle of 9.1° with the ground."],"cues":["Which side of this triangle is the hypotenuse, and is it being used?","Which two sides does the tangent ratio connect?","What does that fraction come to as a decimal?","Which calculator key turns a tangent back into its angle?"]},{"title":"A missing length in similar triangles","setup":"Triangle ABC is similar to triangle DEF, with AB corresponding to DE and BC corresponding to EF. AB = 4 cm, DE = 10 cm and BC = 6.4 cm. Work out the length of EF. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Corresponding lengths in similar figures are in the same ratio, and AB and DE are a corresponding pair, so they give the scale factor.","Scale factor from ABC to DEF = 10 ÷ 4 = 2.5.","EF corresponds to BC, so EF = 6.4 × 2.5 — the line the method mark is awarded for.","6.4 × 2.5 = 16, so EF = 16 cm."],"cues":["Which pair of sides has both lengths given?","How is a scale factor worked out from a corresponding pair?","Which given length must EF be compared with?","What does that multiplication come to?"]},{"title":"Using a map scale","setup":"A map is drawn to a scale of 1 : 50 000. Two stations are 7.2 cm apart on the map. Work out the real distance between the stations, in kilometres. A calculator may be used.","steps":["A scale of 1 : 50 000 means that every 1 cm measured on the map stands for 50 000 cm on the ground.","Real distance = 7.2 × 50 000 = 360 000 cm — the line the method mark is awarded for.","Converting: 1 km = 1000 m and 1 m = 100 cm, so 1 km = 100 000 cm.","360 000 ÷ 100 000 = 3.6, so the stations are 3.6 km apart."],"cues":["What does the second number in the scale count?","What is done to the map distance to get the real one?","How many centimetres make one kilometre?","Which operation converts centimetres into kilometres?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A jug holds 2 litres. How many cm³ is that?","a":"2000 cm³"},{"q":"A circle has radius 3 cm. Write down its area in terms of π.","a":"9π cm²"},{"q":"A prism has a cross section of area 24 cm² and a length of 7 cm. What is its volume?","a":"168 cm³"},{"q":"Write down the exact value of cos 60°.","a":"1/2"}]},"room":{"facts":["Area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height; area of a parallelogram = base × perpendicular height. Neither is printed on the formulae sheet.","Arc length = θ/360 × 2πr and sector area = θ/360 × πr², where θ is the angle at the centre. Neither is printed on the formulae sheet.","1 ml = 1 cm³ and 1 litre = 1000 ml, so 1 litre = 1000 cm³; 1 km = 100 000 cm.","Exact values: sin 30° = 1/2, sin 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = √3/2; cos 30° = √3/2, cos 45° = √2/2, cos 60° = 1/2; tan 30° = √3/3, tan 45° = 1, tan 60° = √3.","A three-figure bearing is measured clockwise from north and written with three digits; the bearing of A from B and of B from A differ by 180°.","In similar figures every pair of corresponding lengths is in the same ratio, and that ratio is the linear scale factor."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) A bottle holds 1.5 litres of water. Write this capacity in cm³.","a":"1500 cm³","sol":"1 litre = 1000 ml and 1 ml = 1 cm³, so 1 litre = 1000 cm³. 1.5 × 1000 = 1500. B1 for 1500 cm³.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Litres and cm³ both measure the same kind of quantity, so one can be rewritten as the other.","Use the link between millilitres and cubic centimetres to get from litres to cm³.","Write down how many cm³ there are in one litre, then use that on 1.5 litres."],"codes":["G14"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A triangle has a base of 12 cm and a perpendicular height of 7 cm. Work out its area.","a":"42 cm²","sol":"Area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height. M1 for ½ × 12 × 7. ½ × 12 = 6 and 6 × 7 = 42. A1 for 42 cm², with the unit.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A triangle is half of a rectangle that has the same base and height.","The formula for the area of a triangle is one you must recall; it is not on the formulae sheet.","Start from area = ½ × base × perpendicular height and put the two given lengths in."],"codes":["G16"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A circle has a radius of 6 cm. Work out its area, giving your answer in terms of π.","a":"36π cm²","sol":"Area of a circle = πr², which is printed on the formulae sheet. M1 for π × 6². 6² = 36. A1 for 36π cm².","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Two circle formulae are printed for you; only one of them gives an area.","'In terms of π' means the π stays in the answer and nothing is rounded.","Write area = πr² and substitute the radius you are given."],"codes":["G17"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A right-angled triangle has the two shorter sides 5 cm and 12 cm. Work out the length of the hypotenuse.","a":"13 cm","sol":"Pythagoras' theorem: a² + b² = c², with c the hypotenuse. M1 for 5² + 12² (= 25 + 144 = 169). c = √169 = 13. A1 for 13 cm.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Three sides are involved and no angle, so this is a job for Pythagoras' theorem.","The hypotenuse is the longest side, so the two given squares are added rather than subtracted.","Write a² + b² = c² and square each of the two shorter sides."],"codes":["G20"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write down the exact value of tan 60°.","a":"√3","sol":"tan 60° is one of the exact values that must be known: from the half equilateral triangle with sides 1, √3 and 2, tan 60° = √3/1. B1 for √3.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["This is one of the values that has to be recalled, since Paper 1 has no calculator.","It comes from an equilateral triangle of side 2 cut in half.","Write down the three sides of that half triangle, then use tan θ = opposite/adjacent for the 60° angle."],"codes":["G21"]},{"q":"A prism has a trapezium cross section whose parallel sides are 7 cm and 11 cm, with a perpendicular separation of 5 cm. The prism is 20 cm long. Work out its volume. A calculator may be used.","a":"900 cm³","sol":"Area of the cross section = ½(a + b)h. M1 for ½(7 + 11) × 5. ½ × 18 × 5 = 45, so A1 for 45 cm². Volume of a prism = area of cross section × length. M1 for 45 × 20. A1 for 900 cm³.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The solid has the same cross section all the way along, so the cross section is where to start.","Work out the area of the trapezium first, then use it with the length of the prism.","Write area = ½(a + b)h with the two parallel sides and their perpendicular separation."],"codes":["G16"]},{"q":"A cylinder has radius 5 cm and height 14 cm. Work out its volume, correct to 3 significant figures. A calculator may be used.","a":"1100 cm³","sol":"A cylinder is a prism with a circular cross section, so volume = πr²h. M1 for π × 5² (= 25π, the area of the cross section). M1 for × 14, giving π × 25 × 14 = 1099.557… cm³. A1 for 1100 cm³ correct to 3 significant figures.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A cylinder is a prism, so the rule volume = area of cross section × length applies to it.","The cross section is a circle, so find its area before multiplying by the height.","Write volume = πr²h, then substitute the radius and the height."],"codes":["G17"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A sector of a circle has radius 8 cm and an angle of 45° at the centre. Work out the area of the sector, giving your answer in terms of π.","a":"8π cm²","sol":"The sector is θ/360 of the whole circle. M1 for 45/360 (= 1/8). M1 for 1/8 × π × 8², the area of the whole circle being 64π cm². 64π ÷ 8 = 8π. A1 for 8π cm².","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The angle at the centre tells you what fraction of the whole circle the sector is.","Find the area of the whole circle, then take that fraction of it.","Write down the fraction θ/360 in its simplest form and set it against the formula for the area of a circle."],"codes":["G18"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A plan of a garden is drawn to a scale of 1 cm to 4 m. On the plan a rectangular pond measures 3 cm by 2.5 cm. Work out the real area of the pond, in m².","a":"120 m²","sol":"Each 1 cm on the plan is 4 m in real life. M1 for 3 × 4 = 12 m and 2.5 × 4 = 10 m, the two real side lengths. M1 for 12 × 10, the area of the real rectangle. A1 for 120 m².","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Convert the drawing into real life before doing anything with area.","Find each real side length first, then use the rule for the area of a rectangle.","Multiply each measurement on the plan by the number of metres one centimetre stands for."],"codes":["G15","G16"]},{"q":"A straight wire runs from the top of a pole to a point on the ground and is 9.4 m long. It makes an angle of 32° with the horizontal ground. Work out the vertical height of the top of the pole above the ground, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"5.0 m","sol":"The wire is the hypotenuse and the height is opposite the 32° angle, so use the sine ratio. M1 for sin 32° = h/9.4. M1 for h = 9.4 × sin 32° = 4.981… A1 for 5.0 m correct to 1 decimal place.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["An angle is involved as well as sides, so a trigonometric ratio is needed rather than Pythagoras' theorem.","Decide which side is the hypotenuse and which is opposite the angle, then choose the ratio that links them.","Write sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse with the angle you are given, then rearrange for the height."],"codes":["G20"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The bearing of B from A is 118°. Work out the bearing of A from B.","a":"298°","sol":"The north lines at A and at B are parallel, so the two bearings differ by 180°. As 118° is less than 180°, add: M1 for 118 + 180. A1 for 298°.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Both bearings are measured clockwise from north, and the two north lines are parallel.","Reversing the direction of travel turns you through half a full turn.","Decide whether the given bearing is under 180°, then take 180° the appropriate way."],"codes":["G15"]},{"q":"A cylindrical tank has radius 30 cm and height 80 cm. Work out how many litres of water it holds when full, correct to the nearest litre. A calculator may be used.","a":"226 litres","sol":"Volume = πr²h. M1 for π × 30² × 80. A1 for 226194.7 cm³ (accept 226000 cm³). 1 litre = 1000 cm³, so M1 for dividing by 1000, giving 226.19… litres. A1 for 226 litres.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The tank is a prism with a circular cross section, and the answer is wanted in a unit of capacity.","Find the volume in cm³ first, then change that volume into litres.","Write volume = πr²h and substitute the radius and the height in centimetres."],"codes":["G17","G14"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A solid is made from a cone of radius 5 cm and perpendicular height 12 cm standing on a cylinder of radius 5 cm and height 9 cm. Volume of a cone = ⅓πr²h. Work out the total volume of the solid, giving your answer in terms of π.","a":"325π cm³","sol":"Cone: M1 for ⅓ × π × 5² × 12. ⅓ × 25 × 12 = 100, so A1 for 100π cm³. Cylinder: M1 for π × 5² × 9 (= 225π cm³). Total = 100π + 225π. A1 for 325π cm³.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The solid is made of two parts, so deal with each one separately before putting them together.","The formula for the cone is printed in the question; the cylinder is a prism, so its volume is its cross-section area times its height.","Substitute the radius and the height into ⅓πr²h for the cone, keeping π in the working."],"codes":["G17"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) In a right-angled triangle one of the angles is 30° and the side opposite that angle is 7 cm. Work out the exact length of the hypotenuse.","a":"14 cm","sol":"Opposite and hypotenuse means the sine ratio: sin 30° = 7/h. B1 for sin 30° = 1/2. M1 for h = 7 ÷ ½ (oe, such as h = 2 × 7). A1 for 14 cm.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["There is no calculator, so the value of the ratio at this angle is one you already know.","Choose the ratio that links the side opposite the angle with the hypotenuse.","Write sin 30° = opposite/hypotenuse and replace sin 30° by its exact value."],"codes":["G20","G21"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A sector of a circle has radius 12 cm and an arc length of 10π cm. Work out the angle of the sector at the centre.","a":"150°","sol":"Arc length = θ/360 × 2πr. M1 for θ/360 × 2 × π × 12 = 10π. The whole circumference is 24π cm, so M1 for θ/360 = 10π/24π = 5/12. θ = 5/12 × 360 = 150. A1 for 150°.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["The arc is the same fraction of the circumference as the sector is of the whole circle.","Work out the circumference of the whole circle and compare the arc with it.","Write arc length = θ/360 × 2πr and put in the radius and the arc length you are given."],"codes":["G18","G17"]},{"q":"A rectangular photograph measures 15 cm by 10 cm. It is enlarged so that the two rectangles are similar and the longer side of the enlargement is 24 cm. Work out the area of the enlarged photograph. A calculator may be used.","a":"384 cm²","sol":"The longer sides correspond, so M1 for the scale factor 24 ÷ 15 = 1.6. M1 for the shorter side 10 × 1.6 = 16 cm. M1 for 24 × 16, the area of the enlarged rectangle. A1 for 384 cm².","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["In similar rectangles every pair of corresponding sides is in the same ratio.","Find the scale factor from the pair of sides you know, use it to get the missing side, then find the area.","Divide the enlarged longer side by the original longer side to get the scale factor."],"codes":["G19","G16"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 2.4 litres in cm³.","a":"2400 cm³"},{"q":"A circle has a diameter of 9 cm. Write down its circumference in terms of π.","a":"9π cm"},{"q":"Write down the exact value of sin 30°.","a":"1/2"},{"q":"A ship sails due west. Write this direction as a three-figure bearing.","a":"270°"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Standard units for length, area, volume and capacity","back":"Length: mm, cm, m, km. Area: mm², cm², m², km². Volume: mm³, cm³, m³. Capacity: ml and litres. The unit must be written on the answer.","code":"G14","open":false},{"front":"Capacity and volume conversions","back":"1 ml = 1 cm³, 1 litre = 1000 ml = 1000 cm³, 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 km = 1000 m = 100 000 cm.","code":"G14","open":false},{"front":"Three-figure bearing","back":"A direction measured clockwise from north and always written with three digits, so 70° is written 070°. Bearings run from 000° to 360°.","code":"G15","open":false},{"front":"The rule linking the bearing of A from B with the bearing of B from A","back":"North lines are parallel, so the two bearings differ by 180°: add 180° when the bearing is under 180°, subtract 180° when it is 180° or more.","code":"G15","open":false},{"front":"A map scale written as 1 : n","back":"One unit on the map stands for n of the same units on the ground: multiply the map distance by n, then convert, using 100 000 cm = 1 km.","code":"G15","open":false},{"front":"Area of a triangle","back":"Area = ½ × base × perpendicular height. The height must be at right angles to the base, never a sloping side. Not printed on the formulae sheet.","code":"G16","open":false},{"front":"Area of a parallelogram and area of a trapezium","back":"Parallelogram = base × perpendicular height, which must be recalled. Trapezium = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the parallel sides and h their perpendicular separation; printed on the formulae sheet.","code":"G16","open":false},{"front":"Volume of a prism","back":"Volume = area of cross section × length, printed on the formulae sheet. A cuboid and a cylinder are prisms, so a cylinder has volume πr²h.","code":"G16","open":false},{"front":"Circumference and area of a circle","back":"Circumference = 2πr = πd; area = πr². Both are printed on the formulae sheet. Halve the diameter first, because r is the radius.","code":"G17","open":false},{"front":"The curved-solid formulae printed inside the question","back":"Curved surface area of a cone = πrl; surface area of a sphere = 4πr²; volume of a sphere = 4/3 πr³; volume of a cone = ⅓πr²h. Not on the formulae sheet.","code":"G17","open":false},{"front":"Arc length and sector area","back":"For an angle θ at the centre and radius r: arc = θ/360 × 2πr and sector area = θ/360 × πr². Neither is printed on the formulae sheet.","code":"G18","open":false},{"front":"Perimeter of a sector","back":"The arc plus the two straight radii: θ/360 × 2πr + 2r.","code":"G18","open":false},{"front":"Similar figures and the linear scale factor","back":"Similar figures have equal angles and corresponding lengths in the same ratio. Scale factor = new length ÷ matching original length; every length, including the perimeter, is multiplied by it.","code":"G19","open":false},{"front":"Pythagoras' theorem and the three trigonometric ratios","back":"a² + b² = c², with c the hypotenuse. sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse, tan θ = opposite/adjacent. All are printed on the formulae sheet.","code":"G20","open":false},{"front":"The exact trigonometric values","back":"For 0°, 30°, 45°, 60°, 90°: sin = 0, 1/2, √2/2, √3/2, 1 and cos = the same list reversed. For 0°, 30°, 45°, 60°: tan = 0, √3/3, 1, √3. Not on the formulae sheet.","code":"G21","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the parallel sides and h is their perpendicular separation — the Appendix places this in list 2 (known or derived, not given in the exam), but the AQA Foundation tier formulae sheet issued for every series since 2022, which is outside this specification, prints it for you.","Volume of a prism = area of cross section × length — Appendix list 2; also printed on the AQA Foundation tier formulae sheet.","Circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd — Appendix list 1 (expected to be known, not given in the exam); also printed on the AQA Foundation tier formulae sheet.","Area of a circle = πr² — Appendix list 1; also printed on the AQA Foundation tier formulae sheet.","Pythagoras' theorem: a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse — Appendix list 1; also printed on the AQA Foundation tier formulae sheet.","The right-angled trigonometric ratios: sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b, where c is the hypotenuse; in words, sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse and tan θ = opposite/adjacent — Appendix list 1; also printed on the AQA Foundation tier formulae sheet.","Area of a rectangle = length × width — not in the Appendix and not on the formulae sheet: recall it.","Area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height — not in the Appendix and not on the formulae sheet: recall it.","Area of a parallelogram = base × perpendicular height — not in the Appendix and not on the formulae sheet: recall it.","Volume of a cuboid = length × width × height, and volume of a cylinder = πr²h — not on the formulae sheet: derive each from volume of a prism = area of cross section × length.","Arc length of a sector = θ/360 × 2πr — not in the Appendix and not on the formulae sheet: recall it.","Area of a sector = θ/360 × πr² — not in the Appendix and not on the formulae sheet: recall it.","Perimeter of a sector = θ/360 × 2πr + 2r — not on the formulae sheet: build it from the arc and the two radii.","Curved surface area of a cone = πrl, surface area of a sphere = 4πr², volume of a sphere = 4/3 πr³ and volume of a cone = ⅓πr²h — Appendix list 3: not to be memorised, and given in the exam in the relevant question rather than on the formulae sheet.","Exact values: sin 0° = 0, sin 30° = 1/2, sin 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = √3/2, sin 90° = 1; cos 0° = 1, cos 30° = √3/2, cos 45° = √2/2, cos 60° = 1/2, cos 90° = 0; tan 0° = 0, tan 30° = √3/3, tan 45° = 1, tan 60° = √3 — not on the formulae sheet: recall them.","Metric links needed with these formulae: 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 km = 100 000 cm, 1 ml = 1 cm³ and 1 litre = 1000 cm³ — not on the formulae sheet: recall them.","The sine rule, the cosine rule and Area = ½ab sin C appear only on the Higher tier formulae sheet; they are Higher tier content and are not needed here.","Volume of a pyramid: a third of the base area times the perpendicular height. Not on the formulae sheet and on none of the Appendix lists, so it must be recalled."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:13:34Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"All 16 bank questions, both four-item checks and the exit ticket re-solved independently and every answer key, unit and rounding is right; the Formulae sheet's provided-versus-recall claims match Appendix section 4's Foundation insert exactly, and the only repairs needed are the pyramid formula's status and one loose cue."}}},"geometry-vectors":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"geometry-vectors","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Vectors are how movement is written down in mathematics, and at Foundation tier they carry two jobs on the papers. First, a translation is only fully described when you give the word translation together with its column vector, so every transformation question that involves sliding a shape ends in a vector. Second, column vectors are added, subtracted and multiplied by a number, which is examined both as short one-mark work and inside longer problems about journeys, grids and coordinates. The arithmetic is quick but the signs are where marks are lost: left and down are negative, and a careless sign turns a correct method into a wrong answer. Paper 1 is non-calculator, so this whole room has to be secure by hand.","idea":[{"h":"Writing a translation as a column vector","t":"A translation slides every point of a shape the same distance in the same direction. It is written as a column vector with two numbers: the movement across the page on top and the movement up or down underneath. Right and up are positive, left and down are negative, so 3 units right and 2 units down is the column vector (3, −2), written here on one line as (across, up) because a stacked bracket cannot be printed in this text. To translate a point, add the vector to its coordinates: (x, y) goes to (x + a, y + b). To find the vector from a shape and its image, take a pair of matching vertices and subtract — image coordinates minus object coordinates. Examiners want the word translation and the vector; a description in words alone, or a vector with no word, is an incomplete description and loses a mark.","code":"G24"},{"h":"Adding, subtracting and scaling vectors","t":"In column form vectors combine one row at a time: (a, b) + (c, d) = (a + c, b + d) and (a, b) − (c, d) = (a − c, b − d). In a diagram they add nose to tail — draw the second arrow starting where the first one ends, and the single arrow from the very start to the very end is the sum, which is why two translations done one after the other are the same as one translation by the sum of their vectors. Multiplying by a number (a scalar) multiplies both components: k(a, b) = (ka, kb). That makes the arrow |k| times as long along the same line — the size of k fixes the length and the sign of k reverses the direction when k is negative — so −a is the same length as a but points the opposite way, and a + (−a) = (0, 0), the zero vector. Questions usually combine the two operations, such as a + 2b or 3a − b, and the method mark is for the scaled vector written down before the adding starts.","code":"G25"}],"examples":[{"title":"From words to a column vector, and on to the image","setup":"On a centimetre grid, triangle ABC is translated so that every vertex moves 6 units to the right and 4 units down. Vertex A is at (1, 2). Write the translation as a column vector, and give the coordinates of the image A′.","steps":["Across the page: right counts as positive, so the top number is 6.","Up and down: a downward move counts as negative, so the bottom number is −4 and the translation is by the column vector (6, −4).","To translate a point, add the vector to its coordinates: (1 + 6, 2 + (−4)).","So the translation is by (6, −4) and A′ = (7, −2)."],"cues":["Which movement is recorded by the top number of a column vector?","Is a move downwards positive or negative?","What do you do to a point's coordinates to translate it?","What are the two answers the question asked for?"]},{"title":"Scaling before adding","setup":"Vector a is the column vector (5, −2) and vector b is the column vector (−3, 4). Work out a + 3b, giving the result as a single column vector.","steps":["Scale first: 3b multiplies both parts of b by 3, giving (−9, 12).","Top row: add the across parts of a and 3b, 5 + (−9).","Bottom row: add the up parts, −2 + 12.","a + 3b = (−4, 10)."],"cues":["What does the 3 written in front of b do to each part of b?","Which two numbers make the top row of the answer?","Which two numbers make the bottom row?","What single column vector do those two rows give?"]},{"title":"Finding the translation from an object and its image","setup":"A trapezium has a vertex P at (−2, 7). After a translation, the matching vertex P′ is at (3, 1). Describe the translation fully, and use it to find the image of the vertex Q at (0, −5).","steps":["Across: subtract the object's x-coordinate from the image's x-coordinate, 3 − (−2) = 5.","Up: subtract the object's y-coordinate from the image's y-coordinate, 1 − 7 = −6, so the vector is (5, −6).","Apply that vector to Q by adding it to the coordinates: (0 + 5, −5 + (−6)).","Q′ = (5, −11), and the full description is a translation by the column vector (5, −6)."],"cues":["Which coordinates do you subtract from which to get the movement across?","Does the same subtraction work for the vertical movement?","How is the vector used on a different vertex?","What are the coordinates of the image of Q, and what is the complete description?"]},{"title":"Two translations, and the way back","setup":"A counter on a grid is first translated by the column vector (4, −7) and is then translated by the column vector (−9, 2). Work out the single column vector with the same effect, and the column vector that would send the counter back to where it started.","steps":["One translation after another is the same as adding the vectors: top row 4 + (−9) = −5.","Bottom row: −7 + 2 = −5, so the single translation is by (−5, −5).","Going back undoes that move, so reverse the sign of each part.","The return vector is (5, 5), and the single translation is (−5, −5)."],"cues":["What operation combines two translations done one after the other?","What does the bottom row come to?","What must the return journey do to each part of the combined vector?","What are the two column vectors asked for?"]},{"title":"Spotting a scalar multiple","setup":"Vector c is the column vector (2, −5) and vector d is the column vector (−8, 20). Show that d is a scalar multiple of c, state the scalar, and describe how the arrow drawn for d compares with the arrow drawn for c in length and in direction.","steps":["Compare the top numbers: −8 ÷ 2 = −4, so the across part has been multiplied by −4.","Test the same multiplier underneath: −4 × (−5) = 20, which matches, so d = −4c.","The size of the scalar tells you the length: a multiplier of size 4 makes the arrow 4 times as long.","The scalar is negative, so the arrow for d is 4 times as long as the arrow for c and points in the opposite direction."],"cues":["Which pair of numbers should you compare first?","Does that same multiplier work for the bottom row?","What does the size of the scalar do to the length of the arrow?","What does the minus sign in the scalar do to the direction?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write the translation '3 units right and 8 units down' as a column vector.","a":"(3, −8)"},{"q":"u = (−5, 2) and w = (2, 2). Work out u + w.","a":"(−3, 4)"},{"q":"t = (−1, 6). Work out 4t.","a":"(−4, 24)"},{"q":"The point (7, −2) is translated by the column vector (−7, 2). Write the coordinates of the image.","a":"(0, 0)"}]},"room":{"facts":["A column vector has the movement across the page on top and the movement up the page underneath; right and up are positive, left and down are negative. It is written here on one line as (across, up).","A translation is described fully by the word translation together with its column vector; either one on its own is an incomplete description.","The point (x, y) translated by the column vector (a, b) lands on (x + a, y + b).","The vector mapping an object onto its image is (x of the image − x of the object, y of the image − y of the object), taken from a pair of matching vertices.","Vectors combine row by row: (a, b) + (c, d) = (a + c, b + d) and (a, b) − (c, d) = (a − c, b − d).","A scalar multiplies both components: k(a, b) = (ka, kb). The arrow becomes |k| times as long along the same line, reversed if k is negative, and a + (−a) = (0, 0), the zero vector.","Two translations one after the other are equivalent to the single translation given by the sum of their column vectors."],"questions":[{"q":"A shape is translated 4 units left and 6 units up. (non-calculator) Write this translation as a column vector.","a":"(−4, 6)","sol":"The movement across the page is the top number and the movement up or down is the bottom number. Left is negative, so the top number is −4; up is positive, so the bottom number is 6. B1 for (−4, 6). Total 1 mark.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A column vector records two separate movements: one across and one up or down.","Decide which movement belongs on top, then give each one its sign from the direction of travel.","First line: write a bracket with the sideways movement above the vertical movement, signing each one."],"codes":["G24"]},{"q":"Describe in words the translation given by the column vector (0, −7). (non-calculator)","a":"7 units down (no movement across)","sol":"The top number is the movement across the page and it is 0, so there is no sideways movement. The bottom number −7 is negative, so the movement is downwards, 7 units. B1 for '7 units down'. Total 1 mark.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Each of the two numbers describes a different direction of travel.","Zero in one row means nothing happens in that direction; a negative sign points left or down.","First line: say what the top number tells you about sideways movement, then read the sign underneath."],"codes":["G24"]},{"q":"a = (4, −1) and b = (−3, 5). (non-calculator) Work out a + b as a column vector.","a":"(1, 4)","sol":"Add row by row: across, 4 + (−3) = 1; up, −1 + 5 = 4. B1 for (1, 4). Total 1 mark.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Column vectors are combined one row at a time, not multiplied together.","The two top numbers make the top of the answer and the two bottom numbers make the bottom.","First line: write the two top numbers with a plus sign between them, then do the same underneath."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"p = (−6, 9). (non-calculator) Work out (1/3)p as a column vector.","a":"(−2, 3)","sol":"A scalar multiplies both components: 1/3 × (−6) = −2 across and 1/3 × 9 = 3 up. B1 for (−2, 3). Total 1 mark.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A number written in front of a vector acts on the whole vector.","Both components are multiplied by the same scalar, signs included.","First line: write one third of the top number above one third of the bottom number."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"a = (8, −2) and b = (1, 3). (non-calculator) Work out a − b as a column vector.","a":"(7, −5)","sol":"M1 for subtracting row by row, 8 − 1 across and −2 − 3 up (or for one component correct). A1 for (7, −5). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Subtraction works the same way as addition on column vectors.","Take each part of b away from the matching part of a, keeping the order the question gives.","First line: write the top number of b being taken from the top number of a, then repeat underneath."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"m = (3, −2) and n = (−1, 4). (a calculator may be used) Work out m + 2n as a column vector.","a":"(1, 6)","sol":"M1 for 2n = (−2, 8). A1 for m + 2n = (3 + (−2), −2 + 8) = (1, 6). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["There are two operations here, and they have to happen in the right order.","Scale n first, then add the result to m row by row.","First line: write out the vector 2n by doubling each of n's components."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"a = (2, 5) and b = (4, −3). (a calculator may be used) Work out 3a − b as a column vector.","a":"(2, 18)","sol":"M1 for 3a = (6, 15). A1 for 3a − b = (6 − 4, 15 − (−3)) = (2, 18). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The scalar belongs to a only, not to the whole expression.","Work out the scaled vector first, then subtract b one row at a time.","First line: write the vector 3a by trebling each of a's components, and watch the double negative underneath."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"Triangle T is mapped onto triangle U by a translation. The vertex (−1, 5) of T maps onto the vertex (4, 2) of U. (non-calculator) Write the column vector of the translation that maps U back onto T.","a":"(−5, 3)","sol":"The translation from T to U is (4 − (−1), 2 − 5) = (5, −3). The translation back is the one that undoes it, so reverse both signs. M1 for (5, −3) seen, or for subtracting the coordinates of U from the coordinates of T. A1 for (−5, 3). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The question asks for the journey in the opposite direction to the one described.","Find the vector from T to U first, then think what must happen to it to undo the move.","First line: subtract the coordinates of the object vertex from the coordinates of its image."],"codes":["G24"]},{"q":"A translation by the column vector (−2, 4) is followed by a second translation. Together the two have the same effect as the single translation (3, 1). (a calculator may be used) Work out the column vector of the second translation.","a":"(5, −3)","sol":"Two translations one after the other add, so (−2, 4) + second = (3, 1). M1 for subtracting the first vector from the combined one, (3 − (−2), 1 − 4). A1 for (5, −3). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Doing one translation after another has the same effect as one vector operation.","If the two add to the combined vector, the missing one can be found by undoing that addition.","First line: write the combined vector with the known vector being taken away from it, row by row."],"codes":["G24"]},{"q":"r = (−4, 3). (a calculator may be used) Work out 5r and −5r as column vectors, and state what is the same about the lengths of the two arrows and what is different about their directions.","a":"5r = (−20, 15) and −5r = (20, −15); the two arrows are the same length but point in opposite directions","sol":"A scalar multiplies both components. Across, 5 × (−4) = −20; up, 5 × 3 = 15. B1 for 5r = (−20, 15). Across, −5 × (−4) = 20; up, −5 × 3 = −15. B1 for −5r = (20, −15). The two scalars have the same size, 5, so each arrow is 5 times as long as the arrow for r and the two are the same length as each other; their signs are opposite, so the arrows point in opposite directions. B1 for 'same length, opposite directions' (oe, such as 'equal in length but reversed'). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The two scalars here are the same size as each other but have opposite signs.","The size of a scalar fixes how long the arrow is; the sign of the scalar fixes which way it points.","First line: multiply each component of r by 5, then multiply each component of r by −5, keeping every sign."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"A drone is translated by the column vector (12, 5), where one unit represents one metre. (non-calculator) Work out the distance, in metres, between the drone's starting point and its finishing point.","a":"13 m","sol":"The two components are at right angles, so the start, the finish and the corner form a right-angled triangle with legs of 12 m and 5 m. Let the distance be d. M1 for d² = 12² + 5². M1 for d = √(144 + 25) = √169. A1 for 13 m. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["The movement across and the movement up meet at a right angle.","The straight-line distance is the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle whose legs are the two components.","First line: write Pythagoras' theorem with the across component squared added to the up component squared."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"A ship sails from harbour H by the column vector (7, −4) km, then by (−2, 9) km, then by (−5, −5) km. (non-calculator) Show that the ship finishes at H.","a":"The three vectors add to (0, 0), so the ship finishes at H","sol":"Add the three column vectors row by row. M1 for the across total, 7 + (−2) + (−5) = 0. M1 for the up total, −4 + 9 + (−5) = 0. A1 for stating that the three legs together give (0, 0), the zero vector, so there is no overall movement and the ship is back at H. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Three journeys one after the other have the same effect as a single journey.","Combine the three vectors into one and see what that single vector describes.","First line: write the three top numbers added together, then do the same with the three bottom numbers."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"a = (3, −1) and b = (−2, 4). The vector c satisfies 2a + c = b. (a calculator may be used) Work out c as a column vector.","a":"(−8, 6)","sol":"M1 for 2a = (6, −2). M1 for rearranging to c = b − 2a. A1 for c = (−2 − 6, 4 − (−2)) = (−8, 6). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["A vector equation is rearranged in the same way as a number equation.","Deal with the scalar multiple first, then make c the subject before working with the components.","First line: double each component of a to write the vector 2a."],"codes":["G25"]},{"q":"A is the point (−1, 2). The translation by the column vector (6, 4) maps A onto B. C is the point on the straight line AB with AC : CB = 1 : 3. (a calculator may be used) Work out the coordinates of C.","a":"(0.5, 3)","sol":"The vector from A to B is (6, 4). AC : CB = 1 : 3 splits AB into four equal parts, so AC is 1/4 of AB. M1 for 1/4 × (6, 4). A1 for the vector from A to C being (1.5, 1). M1 for adding that vector to A, (−1 + 1.5, 2 + 1). A1 for C = (0.5, 3). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The ratio tells you what fraction of the whole journey from A to B has been made at C.","A ratio of 1 : 3 divides the line into equal parts; take that fraction of the vector from A to B.","First line: write the fraction of the journey as a scalar multiplying each component of the vector from A to B."],"codes":["G24","G25"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write the translation '6 units right and 5 units down' as a column vector.","a":"(6, −5)"},{"q":"u = (1, −5) and v = (2, 3). Work out u + v as a column vector.","a":"(3, −2)"},{"q":"w = (−2, 1). Work out 4w as a column vector.","a":"(−8, 4)"},{"q":"The point (2, −6) is translated by the column vector (−3, 4). Write the coordinates of the image.","a":"(−1, −2)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Vector","back":"A quantity with both size and direction, drawn as an arrow. Its length shows the size and the arrowhead shows the direction.","code":"G25","open":false},{"front":"Column vector","back":"A vector written as two numbers in a bracket: the movement across the page on top, the movement up the page underneath. Written here on one line as (across, up).","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Sign convention in a column vector","back":"Right and up are positive; left and down are negative. A zero in a row means no movement in that direction.","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Translation","back":"A transformation that slides every point of a shape the same distance in the same direction. The image is congruent to the object.","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Full description of a translation","back":"The word translation together with its column vector. Either part on its own is an incomplete description.","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Image of a point under a translation","back":"Add the vector to the coordinates: (x, y) translated by (a, b) lands on (x + a, y + b).","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"The vector that maps an object onto its image","back":"Take a pair of matching vertices and subtract: (x of the image − x of the object, y of the image − y of the object).","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Two translations one after the other","back":"Equivalent to the single translation whose column vector is the sum of the two vectors.","code":"G24","open":false},{"front":"Adding column vectors","back":"(a, b) + (c, d) = (a + c, b + d) — add the top numbers for the top row and the bottom numbers for the bottom row.","code":"G25","open":false},{"front":"Subtracting column vectors","back":"(a, b) − (c, d) = (a − c, b − d) — subtract row by row, remembering that taking away a negative adds.","code":"G25","open":false},{"front":"Multiplying a vector by a scalar","back":"k(a, b) = (ka, kb) — the scalar multiplies both components.","code":"G25","open":false},{"front":"Effect of a scalar on the arrow","back":"The arrow becomes |k| times as long along the same line — the size of k, not k itself, fixes the length. A negative scalar reverses it, so it points the opposite way.","code":"G25","open":false},{"front":"The vector −a","back":"The same length as a but pointing the opposite way; a + (−a) = (0, 0), so −a undoes a.","code":"G25","open":false},{"front":"Nose-to-tail addition","back":"Draw the second arrow starting where the first ends. The sum is the single arrow from the start of the first to the end of the second.","code":"G25","open":false},{"front":"Length of the arrow drawn for the column vector (a, b)","back":"√(a² + b²). The across and up components meet at a right angle, so the arrow is the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle and Pythagoras' theorem gives its length.","code":"G25","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Preamble: the Appendix of the 8300 specification lists the prescribed formulae in three lists, and not one of the three contains a vector result. AQA also issues a formulae sheet with every paper; it is later than this specification document and is issued in addition to the Appendix. Section 4 of the course appendix, \"What AQA's formulae sheet prints (inserts for exams from 2026)\", records everything the Foundation tier insert carries, and no vector result is on it — so every vector result below must be written from memory. One result this room uses is not a vector result: Pythagoras' theorem, which that insert does print, so it is given to you on the day. See the last item. Where the sheet supplies a formula the Appendix says is to be memorised, the sheet governs on the day, so check the insert in the front of your own paper.","Remember: a column vector is written (across, up), with the movement across the page on top; right and up are positive, left and down are negative.","Remember: a translation is described fully by the word translation together with its column vector.","Remember: (x, y) translated by the column vector (a, b) lands on (x + a, y + b).","Remember: the vector mapping an object onto its image is (x of the image − x of the object, y of the image − y of the object).","Remember: (a, b) + (c, d) = (a + c, b + d) — vectors are added component by component.","Remember: (a, b) − (c, d) = (a − c, b − d) — vectors are subtracted component by component.","Remember: k(a, b) = (ka, kb) — a scalar multiplies both components, making the arrow |k| times as long (the size of k, not k itself) and reversing its direction when k is negative.","Remember: −(a, b) = (−a, −b) and (a, b) + (−a, −b) = (0, 0), the zero vector, so the negative vector is the translation that undoes the original.","Remember: two translations one after the other are equivalent to the single translation given by the sum of the two vectors.","Remember: the length of the column vector (a, b) is √(a² + b²) — a vector result, on neither the Appendix's three lists nor the Foundation sheet, so it must be recalled. The theorem it rests on, Pythagoras' theorem, a² + b² = c², is a different matter: the Appendix places it in list 1, formulae students are expected to know and which 'will not be given in the exam', but section 4 of the course appendix records that AQA's Foundation tier formulae sheet (inserts for exams from 2026) prints Pythagoras' theorem. Where the sheet supplies a formula the Appendix says is to be memorised, the sheet governs on the day, so a² + b² = c² is given to you — applying it to the two components to reach the length of a vector is not."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:21:55Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.95,"notes":"Re-judged against the kit file itself: all 14 bank answers, the 4 exit-ticket answers, the 4 lesson check answers and every line of all five worked examples re-solve correctly, every sol's marks add to its tariff, the difficulty spread is 5/5/4, eight questions are non-calculator with numbers workable by hand, all 15 cards are correct closed statements within 40 words, and the Formulae sheet now matches appendix.md section 4 exactly for the Foundation tier — publishable as it stands, with only the wording of questions[9]'s comparison worth tightening."}}},"probability":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"probability","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Probability and statistics together carry 15% of the marks at Foundation tier, and probability is where the easiest marks on the paper sit next to some of the most commonly dropped ones. A single line — the probabilities of an exhaustive set of outcomes add up to 1 — answers dozens of exam questions, yet marks are lost every year for writing a probability as a ratio such as 3 : 4, for adding when the question needs multiplying, or for forgetting that a counter taken and not replaced changes the numbers on the second set of branches. This room builds the habits the mark scheme rewards: write every probability as a fraction, decimal or percentage between 0 and 1; set out every possibility once in a table, grid, Venn diagram or tree; multiply along a path and add between paths; and turn a probability into an expected number of outcomes by multiplying by the number of trials. Papers 2 and 3 allow a calculator, but Paper 1 does not, so the fraction arithmetic has to be secure by hand.","idea":[{"h":"Recording outcomes in tables and frequency trees","t":"An experiment is only as good as its record. A frequency table lists each outcome with the number of times it happened; a two-way table sorts the same results by two features at once, such as boys and girls against walked and came by bus, with a total row and a total column that must agree. A frequency tree carries frequencies, not probabilities: each pair of branches splits the number it came from, so the two numbers on a pair always add back to the number at the branch point, and the numbers at the ends add back to the total. Fill in every gap by adding or subtracting along the branches before you answer anything. When you turn a frequency into a probability, the specification's Notes are strict about the form: write it as a fraction, a decimal or a percentage, never as a ratio and never as '3 out of 8' alone.","code":"P1"},{"h":"Fairness, randomness and expected outcomes","t":"'Fair', 'unbiased' and 'at random' all mean the same thing in an exam question: every outcome is equally likely, so each of the six faces of a fair dice has probability 1/6. Once you have the probability of an outcome, the expected number of times it happens in a set of trials is expected number = probability × number of trials. So a fair spinner with five equal sections, spun 200 times, is expected to land on a chosen section 1/5 × 200 = 40 times. Two warnings the examiner is watching for: an expected number need not be a whole number, and it is not a promise — because the trials are random the actual count will usually be near the expected value rather than equal to it. Say 'about 40 times', and never claim the result must be exactly that.","code":"P2"},{"h":"Relative frequency, theoretical probability and the probability scale","t":"There are two ways to get a probability. Theoretical probability comes from the equally likely outcomes themselves — count the favourable ones and divide by the total. Relative frequency, also called experimental probability, comes from trials actually carried out: relative frequency = number of times the outcome happened ÷ number of trials. Use relative frequency when the outcomes are not equally likely (a drawing pin, a biased spinner) or when you cannot see inside the bag. Every probability, whichever way it is found, is a number from 0 to 1 and can be marked on the probability scale, with 0 impossible, 1 certain, and words such as unlikely, even chance and likely describing the positions in between. Comparing a relative frequency with the theoretical probability is how a question asks you to judge whether something is biased.","code":"P3"},{"h":"Exhaustive sets of outcomes add up to 1","t":"A set of outcomes is exhaustive if it covers everything that can happen, and mutually exclusive if no two of them can happen together. For such a set the probabilities add up to 1, and that single fact answers a whole family of questions. Its most-used special case is P(not A) = 1 − P(A): if the probability that a train is late is 0.15, the probability that it is not late is 1 − 0.15 = 0.85. When a table of probabilities has one entry missing, add the ones you are given and subtract the total from 1. The same idea runs backwards too: if you know a probability and how many items there are altogether, you can find a frequency, and if you know a probability and a frequency you can divide to find the total.","code":"P4"},{"h":"Bigger samples move towards the theory","t":"Toss a fair coin ten times and four heads would not surprise anyone; toss it two thousand times and the relative frequency of heads will sit very close to 0.5. That is the idea this statement wants: an unbiased sample tends towards the theoretical probability distribution as the sample size increases. Two things follow in the exam. First, when two people's results are offered as estimates of a probability, the estimate from the larger number of trials is the better one, and saying so is the reasoning mark. Second, a relative frequency that stays a long way from the theoretical probability over many trials is evidence of bias — over a handful of trials it is evidence of nothing at all. Never say a larger sample is 'more accurate' without saying it is closer to the true probability.","code":"P5"},{"h":"Listing every possibility: tables, grids, Venn diagrams and trees","t":"Reliable counting comes from a systematic list in which every possibility appears exactly once. A grid suits two experiments: one experiment down the side, the other along the top, one cell for each outcome. A Venn diagram suits overlapping groups: the overlap holds the members of both sets, each region is filled once, and anything in neither set goes in the box outside the circles. A tree diagram suits a two-stage experiment, with one branch per outcome at each stage. Work from the inside out with a Venn diagram — fill the overlap first, then subtract it from each total so nobody is counted twice, then subtract everything from the grand total to find the 'neither' region. A list written in a fixed order beats a random scribble every time.","code":"P6"},{"h":"Possibility spaces and theoretical probability","t":"A possibility space is the complete set of equally likely outcomes of an experiment, often drawn as a grid when two experiments are combined. Once it is drawn, every theoretical probability is a count: P(event) = number of favourable outcomes ÷ total number of outcomes. Two fair six-sided dice give 6 × 6 = 36 outcomes in the grid, and writing the total (or the product, or the difference) in each cell turns any question about that quantity into counting cells. The trap is counting outcomes rather than cells: a total of 7 can happen in six different ways, so the eleven possible totals from 2 to 12 are not equally likely even though the thirty-six cells are. Always simplify the fraction at the end, because the specification expects a simplest form even when the question does not say so.","code":"P7"},{"h":"Combining two events: independent and dependent","t":"Two events are independent when the first does not change the probability of the second — a coin does not remember, and a counter that is replaced leaves the bag exactly as it was. Then P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). Events are dependent when the first does change the second, which is what 'without replacement' means: take a red counter from 5 red and 3 blue and the second pick is from 4 red out of 7, so both the favourable count and the total drop by one. On a tree diagram the rule is: multiply along a path to get the probability of that combination, and add the probabilities of the separate paths that fit the description. Assume nothing silently — an answer that multiplies is assuming independence or is using the changed second probability, and the exam expects you to be able to say which.","code":"P8"}],"examples":[{"title":"A two-way record and a probability read from it","setup":"A teacher records how 60 pupils travelled to school on Monday. There are 35 girls, and 21 of them walked; the rest of the girls came by bus. Of the boys, 9 walked and the rest came by bus. Set the results out as a two-way table and work out the probability that a pupil chosen at random from the 60 came by bus.","steps":["Subtract to fill the table: 35 − 21 = 14 girls used the bus.","Count the boys: 60 − 35 = 25 boys altogether.","Subtract the boys who walked: 25 − 9 = 16 boys used the bus.","Bus total: 14 + 16 = 30 of the 60 pupils.","P(bus) = 30/60 = 1/2."],"cues":["How many of the girls did not walk?","How many boys are there altogether?","How many of those boys did not walk?","How many pupils came by bus in total?","How is that count written as a probability in its simplest form?"]},{"title":"How many times should it happen?","setup":"A fair eight-sided spinner has equal sections numbered 1 to 8. Padma spins it 240 times. Work out how many times she should expect it to land on an even number, and say why the number of even scores she actually gets may be different.","steps":["Even sections are 2, 4, 6 and 8, so four of the eight sections are even.","Fair means every section is equally likely, so P(even) = 4/8 = 1/2.","Expected number = P(even) × number of spins = 1/2 × 240.","1/2 × 240 = 120, so about 120 even scores are expected; the spins are random, so the actual number will usually be close to 120 rather than exactly 120."],"cues":["Which of the numbers on this spinner are even?","What does the word 'fair' tell you about each section?","Which calculation turns a probability into a number of times?","Why is the answer only what is expected, not what must happen?"]},{"title":"A possibility space for two spinners","setup":"Spinner A has three equal sections numbered 1, 2 and 3. Spinner B has four equal sections numbered 1, 2, 3 and 4. Both spinners are spun once and the two numbers are added together. Set out all the possible totals in a possibility space and work out the probability that the total is 5.","steps":["Draw a grid with one row for each section of the first spinner and one column for each section of the second: 3 × 4 = 12 cells, one per outcome.","Write the total in every cell: the first row gives 2, 3, 4, 5; the second row gives 3, 4, 5, 6; the third row gives 4, 5, 6, 7.","Count the cells holding the total wanted: one in each row, so 3 cells out of 12.","P(total = 5) = 3/12 = 1/4."],"cues":["How many cells will the grid need altogether?","What goes inside each cell of the grid?","How many cells show the total the question asks about?","How is that count turned into a probability in its simplest form?"]},{"title":"A tree diagram with no replacement","setup":"A box holds 9 chocolates: 4 are milk and 5 are dark. Kim eats one chocolate at random and then eats a second chocolate at random from those left. Draw a tree diagram for the two choices and work out the probability that both chocolates are dark.","steps":["First pair of branches: P(dark) = 5/9 and P(milk) = 4/9.","Kim does not put the first chocolate back, so after a dark one there are 4 dark left out of 8, giving P(second dark) = 4/8 = 1/2.","Both dark is one path through the tree, so multiply the probabilities along it: 5/9 × 1/2.","5/9 × 1/2 = 5/18, so P(both dark) = 5/18."],"cues":["What is the probability that the first chocolate is dark?","How many chocolates are left, and how many of those are dark?","What do you do with the probabilities along one path of a tree?","What does that multiplication come to?"]},{"title":"Filling the gap in a table of probabilities","setup":"A biased four-sided dice can land on 1, 2, 3 or 4. A table of probabilities gives P(1) = 0.15, P(2) = 0.27 and P(3) = 0.34, and the probability of landing on 4 is missing. The dice is rolled 300 times. Work out the missing probability and the expected number of 4s.","steps":["The four outcomes are exhaustive and mutually exclusive, so their probabilities have a total of 1.","Add the three that are given: 0.15 + 0.27 + 0.34 = 0.76.","The missing one is what is left: 1 − 0.76 = 0.24.","Expected number = probability × number of rolls = 0.24 × 300.","0.24 × 300 = 72, so P(4) = 0.24 and about 72 fours are expected."],"cues":["What must the four probabilities come to altogether?","What do the three given probabilities add up to?","How is the missing probability found from that total?","Which calculation gives an expected number of outcomes?","What does that multiplication come to?"]}],"check":[{"q":"The probability that a bus is on time is 7/10. What is the probability that it is not on time?","a":"3/10"},{"q":"A bag holds 8 counters and 3 of them are green. One counter is taken at random. Write down the probability that it is green.","a":"3/8"},{"q":"A fair coin is tossed 60 times. How many tails would you expect? The actual number will vary, so give the expected number.","a":"30"},{"q":"Events A and B are mutually exclusive, with P(A) = 0.3 and P(B) = 0.45. Work out P(A or B).","a":"0.75"},{"q":"Two counters are taken from a bag, the first one not being replaced. Are the two picks independent? Explain.","a":"No. Removing the first counter changes how many counters are left and how many of them are of each colour, so the second probability depends on the first."}]},"room":{"facts":["Every probability is a number from 0 to 1, written as a fraction, a decimal or a percentage — never as a ratio.","The probabilities of an exhaustive set of mutually exclusive outcomes add up to 1, so P(not A) = 1 − P(A).","Theoretical probability = number of favourable outcomes ÷ total number of equally likely outcomes.","Relative frequency (experimental probability) = number of times the outcome happened ÷ total number of trials.","Expected number of outcomes = probability of the outcome × number of trials.","The addition rule: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B); for mutually exclusive events P(A and B) = 0, so it becomes P(A) + P(B).","For independent events P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B); when the first item is not replaced the second probability changes, and P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B given A).","On a tree diagram: multiply along a path, and add the probabilities of the different paths that fit the description.","As an unbiased sample gets larger, its relative frequency gets closer to the theoretical probability."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) The probability that a train is late is 0.15. Work out the probability that the train is not late.","a":"0.85","sol":"Late and not late are mutually exclusive and exhaustive, so their probabilities add up to 1. P(not late) = 1 − 0.15 = 0.85 — B1 for 0.85.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The train is either late or it is not, and there is no third possibility.","The two probabilities have to account for everything that can happen, so think about what they add up to.","Write P(not late) = 1 − P(late) before you put any number in."],"codes":["P4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A bag contains 4 red counters, 5 green counters and 3 yellow counters. One counter is taken at random. Write down the probability that it is green, as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"5/12","sol":"Total number of counters = 4 + 5 + 3 = 12 — M1 for 12 as the denominator. P(green) = number of green ÷ total = 5/12, which will not simplify — A1 for 5/12.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["'At random' means each counter in the bag is equally likely to be picked.","A theoretical probability is a count of what you want over a count of everything possible.","Work out how many counters there are altogether before you write the fraction."],"codes":["P7"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A fair spinner has 5 equal sections numbered 1 to 5. It is spun 200 times. Work out the expected number of times it lands on 4.","a":"40","sol":"Fair means the five sections are equally likely, so P(4) = 1/5. Expected number = P(4) × number of spins = 1/5 × 200 — M1 for 1/5 × 200. = 40 — A1 for 40 (accept 'about 40 times').","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The word 'fair' tells you how likely each of the five sections is.","An expected number comes from a probability and the number of trials.","Write expected number = P(4) × number of spins before substituting."],"codes":["P2"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A drawing pin is dropped 50 times. It lands point up 18 times. Write down the relative frequency of landing point up, as a decimal.","a":"0.36","sol":"Relative frequency = number of times the outcome happened ÷ number of trials = 18/50 — M1 for 18/50. = 0.36 — A1 for 0.36.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The two faces of a drawing pin are not equally likely, so the probability has to come from the trials themselves.","Compare the number of times it happened with the number of times the pin was dropped.","Write relative frequency = frequency ÷ number of trials, then divide."],"codes":["P3"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A fair coin is flipped and a fair six-sided dice is rolled. How many different outcomes are there in the possibility space?","a":"12","sol":"The coin has 2 outcomes and the dice has 6, and each coin result can go with each dice result, so the grid has 2 × 6 = 12 cells — B1 for 12.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Picture a grid with the coin down one side and the dice along the top.","Each result of the coin can be paired with every result of the dice.","Count the outcomes of each experiment, then combine those two counts."],"codes":["P6"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A jar contains lemon, lime, orange and cherry sweets only. A sweet is taken at random. P(lemon) = 1/5, P(lime) = 3/10 and P(orange) = 1/4. Work out the probability that the sweet is cherry.","a":"1/4","sol":"The four flavours are mutually exclusive and exhaustive, so the four probabilities add up to 1. Writing the three given probabilities over 20: 1/5 = 4/20, 3/10 = 6/20, 1/4 = 5/20 — M1 for a common denominator of 20 (or decimals 0.2, 0.3, 0.25). Their total is 4/20 + 6/20 + 5/20 = 15/20 — M1 for subtracting this total from 1. P(cherry) = 1 − 15/20 = 5/20 = 1/4 — A1 for 1/4 (or 0.25).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Only four flavours are in the jar, so nothing else can be taken.","Find the probability of the three named flavours together, then use what is left over.","Rewrite the three fractions with the same denominator before adding them."],"codes":["P4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Two fair six-sided dice are rolled and the two numbers are multiplied together. Work out the probability that the product is greater than 20.","a":"1/6","sol":"The possibility space has 6 × 6 = 36 equally likely outcomes — M1 for 36. Products greater than 20 are 4 × 6 and 6 × 4 (24), 5 × 5 (25), 5 × 6 and 6 × 5 (30) and 6 × 6 (36): 6 outcomes — M1 for 6 favourable outcomes. P(product > 20) = 6/36 = 1/6 — A1 for 1/6.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Every pair of scores is equally likely, so this is a counting job.","A grid with one dice down the side and the other along the top holds each pair once.","Work out how many cells the grid has, then list the pairs whose product is big enough."],"codes":["P7","P6"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A bag contains 5 red counters and 3 blue counters. A counter is taken at random, its colour is recorded and it is put back. A second counter is then taken at random. Work out the probability that both counters are red.","a":"25/64","sol":"The counter is replaced, so the bag is unchanged and the two picks are independent: P(red) = 5/8 each time — M1 for 5/8 used for both picks. P(both red) = 5/8 × 5/8 — M1 for multiplying the two probabilities. = 25/64 — A1 for 25/64.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The first counter goes back before the second is taken, so think about what the bag looks like for the second pick.","Two things both happening, one after the other, is a path through a tree diagram.","Write down the probability of red on each pick, then combine them."],"codes":["P8"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A tin holds 4 mints and 6 toffees. Two sweets are taken at random, one after the other, and the first is not put back. Work out the probability that both sweets are toffees.","a":"1/3","sol":"First pick: P(toffee) = 6/10. The sweet is not replaced, so 5 toffees are left out of 9 sweets: P(second toffee) = 5/9 — M1 for 5/9 as the second probability. P(both toffees) = 6/10 × 5/9 — M1 for multiplying along the path. = 30/90 = 1/3 — A1 for 1/3 (or 0.333… , 1/3 preferred).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The first sweet is eaten, not returned, so the tin is different for the second pick.","Count what is left in the tin after a toffee has been taken.","Write the probability of a toffee on each pick, then combine them along the path."],"codes":["P8"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. 80 people took a driving test. 50 of them were men, and 35 of those men passed. Of the women, 18 passed. Use a frequency tree to organise the results. One of the 80 people is chosen at random. Work out the probability that this person failed the test.","a":"27/80","sol":"Men who failed = 50 − 35 = 15 — M1 for 15. Women altogether = 80 − 50 = 30, so women who failed = 30 − 18 = 12; total who failed = 15 + 12 = 27 — M1 for 27. P(failed) = 27/80 — A1 for 27/80 (or 0.3375, or 33.75%).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each pair of branches on a frequency tree splits the number it came from.","Work out the four end numbers of the tree before you write any probability.","Subtract along the branches to find how many men failed, then do the same for the women."],"codes":["P1","P7"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A four-sided spinner has sections A, B, C and D. Aisha spins it 400 times and it lands on A 98 times. Work out the relative frequency of A as a decimal, and state whether the results give evidence that the spinner is biased.","a":"0.245","sol":"Relative frequency of A = 98/400 — M1 for 98/400. = 0.245 — A1 for 0.245. For a fair four-sided spinner the theoretical probability is 1/4 = 0.25, and 0.245 is very close to 0.25 over as many as 400 spins, so there is no evidence that the spinner is biased — B1 for the comparison with 0.25 and the conclusion.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Two probabilities can be compared here: one from the spins and one from the four equal sections.","Work out the proportion of the spins that gave A, then work out what a fair spinner would give.","Write relative frequency = frequency of A ÷ number of spins before dividing."],"codes":["P3","P5"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. In a group of 40 students, 22 play football, 17 play tennis and 6 play neither sport. One student is chosen at random. Work out the probability that this student plays both sports.","a":"1/8","sol":"Students who play at least one sport = 40 − 6 = 34 — M1 for 34. By the addition rule, P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B), so in frequencies 22 + 17 = 39 counts the students who play both twice — M1 for 22 + 17 = 39. Number who play both = 39 − 34 = 5 — M1 for 5. P(both) = 5/40 = 1/8 — A1 for 1/8 (or 0.125).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A Venn diagram with two overlapping circles holds every student exactly once.","The students who play neither sport are outside both circles, which tells you how many are inside at least one.","Add the two sports totals and compare that with the number who play at least one sport."],"codes":["P6","P7","P4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A bag contains 3 red counters and 4 blue counters. Two counters are taken at random, one after the other, without replacement. Work out the probability that at least one of them is red.","a":"5/7","sol":"'At least one red' is the opposite of 'no reds at all', which means both counters are blue. P(blue then blue) = 4/7 × 3/6 — M1 for 4/7 × 3/6 (the second probability reduced to 3 blue out of 6). = 12/42 = 2/7 — A1 for 2/7. P(at least one red) = 1 − P(both blue) — M1 for subtracting from 1. = 1 − 2/7 = 5/7 — A1 for 5/7.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Listing every way of getting at least one red takes three paths; there is a much shorter route.","Ask what has to happen for the event not to occur, and find the probability of that instead.","Work out the probability that both counters are blue, remembering the first is not replaced."],"codes":["P8","P4"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A biased spinner can land on red, blue or green only. P(red) = 0.35 and P(blue) = 0.45. The spinner is spun twice. Work out the probability that it lands on green exactly once.","a":"0.32","sol":"P(green) = 1 − (0.35 + 0.45) = 1 − 0.8 = 0.2, so P(not green) = 0.8 — M1 for P(green) = 0.2. Exactly one green happens two ways: green then not green, or not green then green. Each path gives 0.2 × 0.8 = 0.16 — M1 for 0.2 × 0.8. Adding the two paths: 2 × 0.16 — M1 for doubling (or adding the two equal paths). = 0.32 — A1 for 0.32.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The three colours are the only possibilities, so one probability can be found from the other two.","Draw a tree with green and not green on each pair of branches.","There is more than one order in which exactly one green can happen — find both paths."],"codes":["P4","P8","P7"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A box contains red, blue and green counters only. A counter is taken at random. P(red) = 0.2 and P(blue) = 0.35. There are 18 green counters in the box. Work out how many counters there are altogether.","a":"40","sol":"P(red) + P(blue) = 0.2 + 0.35 = 0.55 — M1 for 0.55. The three colours are exhaustive, so P(green) = 1 − 0.55 = 0.45 — M1 for 0.45. The 18 green counters are 0.45 of the box, so total = 18 ÷ 0.45 — M1 for 18 ÷ 0.45. = 1800 ÷ 45 = 40 — A1 for 40.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Only three colours are in the box, so the green probability can be found from the other two.","The green counters are a known fraction of the whole box; you know the fraction and the frequency.","Find P(green) first, then write total = number of green ÷ P(green)."],"codes":["P4","P7","P2"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Amina rolls a dice 50 times and gets 12 sixes. Ben rolls the same dice 500 times and gets 96 sixes. Using the better of the two sets of results, estimate how many sixes would be expected in 2000 rolls of this dice.","a":"384","sol":"Ben's results should be used: a larger unbiased sample gives a relative frequency closer to the true probability — B1 for choosing Ben's 500 rolls with that reason. Estimated P(six) = 96/500 = 0.192 — M1 for 96/500. Expected number = 0.192 × 2000 — M1 for multiplying the estimated probability by 2000. = 384 — A1 for 384.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The dice may be biased, so the probability of a six has to be estimated from the trials.","Two estimates are on offer; decide which set of trials you should trust more, and why.","Write estimated P(six) = number of sixes ÷ number of rolls for the set you have chosen."],"codes":["P5","P3","P2"]}],"exit":[{"q":"The probability that it snows tomorrow is 0.08. Work out the probability that it does not snow tomorrow.","a":"0.92"},{"q":"A bag holds 3 white beads and 7 black beads. One bead is taken at random. Write down P(white) as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"3/10"},{"q":"A fair coin is flipped 80 times. How many heads would you expect? The actual number will vary, so give the expected number.","a":"40"},{"q":"A dice is rolled 20 times and gives 5 sixes. The same dice is rolled 600 times and gives 97 sixes. Which set of results gives the better estimate of the probability of a six, and why?","a":"The 600 rolls, because a larger sample's relative frequency is closer to the true probability."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The probability scale","back":"Every probability is a number from 0 to 1: 0 is impossible, 1 is certain, 1/2 is an even chance. Write probabilities as fractions, decimals or percentages, never as ratios.","code":"P3","open":false},{"front":"Theoretical probability of an event with equally likely outcomes","back":"P(event) = number of favourable outcomes ÷ total number of possible outcomes, given in its simplest form.","code":"P7","open":false},{"front":"Relative frequency (experimental probability)","back":"Relative frequency = number of times the outcome happened ÷ total number of trials. It estimates a probability when the outcomes are not equally likely.","code":"P3","open":false},{"front":"Expected number of outcomes in n trials","back":"Expected number = probability of the outcome × number of trials. It is what should happen on average, so the actual count usually differs a little.","code":"P2","open":false},{"front":"Exhaustive set of outcomes","back":"A set of outcomes covering everything that can happen. The probabilities of an exhaustive set of mutually exclusive outcomes add up to 1.","code":"P4","open":false},{"front":"The probability that an event does not happen","back":"P(not A) = 1 − P(A).","code":"P4","open":false},{"front":"Mutually exclusive events","back":"Events that cannot both happen at once, so P(A and B) = 0 and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B).","code":"P4","open":false},{"front":"The addition rule for two events","back":"P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). Subtracting removes the overlap, which adding the two probabilities has counted twice.","code":"P8","open":false},{"front":"Independent events","back":"Events where the first does not change the probability of the second, as when a counter is replaced. Then P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B).","code":"P8","open":false},{"front":"Dependent events (taken without replacement)","back":"The first result changes the second probability. The total always falls by one; the favourable count falls only if the item removed was a favourable one. P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B given A).","code":"P8","open":false},{"front":"Working with the paths of a tree diagram","back":"Multiply the probabilities along a path to get the probability of that combination; add the probabilities of the separate paths that fit the description.","code":"P8","open":false},{"front":"Possibility space","back":"A list, table or grid showing every outcome of one or more experiments exactly once, so that each entry is one equally likely outcome.","code":"P7","open":false},{"front":"Venn diagram","back":"Overlapping circles for the sets: the overlap holds the members of both, and the box outside the circles holds the members of neither. Each member is entered once.","code":"P6","open":false},{"front":"Frequency tree","back":"A tree whose branches carry frequencies rather than probabilities. Each pair of branches splits the number it came from, so the pair adds back to it.","code":"P1","open":false},{"front":"The effect of increasing the number of trials","back":"The relative frequency of an unbiased sample gets closer to the theoretical probability as the sample gets larger, so more trials give a better estimate.","code":"P5","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). GIVEN on the day at Foundation tier. The specification's Appendix puts this in list 2 — formulae students are expected to know or be able to derive, which 'will not be given in the exam' — against content reference P8. AQA's Foundation tier formulae sheet for exams from 2026 prints the addition rule for the probability of one outcome or another, and where the Appendix and the sheet disagree the sheet governs what you have in front of you. Know what each symbol means anyway: the subtraction removes the overlap that adding P(A) and P(B) counts twice.","P(A and B) = P(A given B) × P(B). RECALL or derive it. The Appendix places it in the same list 2 as the addition rule, and the Foundation tier sheet does not print it — only the Higher tier sheet does — so at Foundation it is not given. In Foundation work it is the tree-diagram rule: the probability of both events is the probability of the first times the probability of the second once the first has happened.","P(not A) = 1 − P(A). RECALL it. This is not in the Appendix's three lists and is on neither tier's sheet.","The probabilities of an exhaustive set of mutually exclusive outcomes add up to 1. RECALL it. Not in the Appendix and not on either sheet; it is the fact behind every missing entry in a table of probabilities.","Theoretical probability = number of favourable outcomes ÷ total number of equally likely outcomes. RECALL it. Not in the Appendix and not on either sheet.","Relative frequency = number of times the outcome happened ÷ total number of trials. RECALL it. Not in the Appendix and not on either sheet.","Expected number of outcomes = probability of the outcome × number of trials. RECALL it. Not in the Appendix and not on either sheet.","For mutually exclusive events P(A and B) = 0, so the addition rule reduces to P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B). RECALL it: the sheet prints the general addition rule only, and this special case of it is on neither tier's sheet.","For independent events P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). RECALL it. Not in the Appendix and not on either sheet; it is the multiplication rule with P(B given A) replaced by P(B), which is what independence allows.","Provenance: the addition rule above is the only result from this room that the Foundation tier sheet prints; everything else listed here must be recalled or derived. Since 2022 Ofqual has required a formulae sheet in every GCSE Mathematics paper and AQA issues one per tier per series. That sheet is later than the Version 1.0 specification, is issued in addition to its Appendix rather than in place of it, and is reissued each series — so read the sheet at the front of your own paper before relying on anything above being printed."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:22:20Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Every answer key, solution line and worked-example calculation is correct and the Formulae sheet matches section 4 of the Appendix exactly (the addition rule is the only room result the Foundation insert prints; the conditional multiplication rule is Higher-only and correctly marked recall), so this is publishable after splitting the bundled formulae item, the two-move example step and the double answer key in Q11, and fixing the over-general 'both fall by one' claim on the dependent-events card."}}},"statistics":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"statistics","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Probability and statistics together are 15% of the Foundation assessment, and the statistics half of that is the part you can make almost certain marks. The work is ordinary arithmetic wrapped in real data: a frequency table turned into pie chart angles, an average and a range read off a list, a sample scaled up to a whole town, a line of best fit used to predict. Papers 2 and 3 let you key the arithmetic in, so the marks fall on choosing the right measure and on saying what it means in context; Paper 1 asks the same skills with numbers you can handle by hand. The examiner's two favourite traps are comparing two data sets with only an average and no measure of spread, and reading a line of best fit far outside the data. Learn to write the comparison sentence and to name extrapolation, and this room becomes routine.","idea":[{"h":"What a sample can and cannot tell you about a population","t":"A population is every member of the group you are interested in; a sample is the part of it you actually measure. Because measuring everybody is usually impossible, you infer: work out the fraction of the sample with a property and multiply it by the size of the population to estimate the population figure. That answer is an estimate, never a certainty. Two things decide how much weight it carries — how big the sample is, because a small sample swings wildly on a few unusual answers, and how it was chosen, because a sample that is not random is biased and will not represent the population however large it is. The exam asks for the estimate and then for a limitation, so say plainly that the sample is small, or that it may not be representative, and name what would make it unrepresentative in that context.","code":"S1"},{"h":"Tables, charts and diagrams, and when each is the right one","t":"A frequency table counts how many times each outcome happens. For categorical data (colours, sports, destinations) use a bar chart with equal-width bars and gaps between them, a pictogram with a key saying what one symbol is worth, or a pie chart, where each item's angle is (frequency ÷ total frequency) × 360°; a pie chart shows proportions of a whole but hides the actual totals. For ungrouped discrete numerical data (number of pets, number of goals) use a vertical line chart: a thin line at each value with height equal to its frequency. For time series data — a value recorded at regular times — use a table and a line graph with time along the horizontal axis, points joined in time order so the trend through the period can be seen. Questions say 'choose a suitable diagram', so learn the pairing and give a reason that names the kind of data.","code":"S2"},{"h":"Averages, spread and comparing two sets of data","t":"The mean is the total of the values divided by how many there are; from a frequency table it is Σfx ÷ Σf, and from a grouped table you use each class midpoint for x, which is why the answer is only an estimate of the mean. The median is the middle value once the data are in order — the ((n + 1)/2)th value, or the mean of the two middle values when n is even. The mode is the most common value, and for grouped data the modal class is the class with the largest frequency. The range, largest value − smallest value, measures spread. An outlier is a value far from the rest; it drags the mean towards it and inflates the range, so the median is the safer average when one is present. Know the terms primary data (you collected it), secondary data (someone else did), discrete data (counted, separate values) and continuous data (measured, any value in a range). A comparison scores only if it names an average and a measure of spread, both in context.","code":"S4"},{"h":"Describing a population with the right summary measures","t":"Applying statistics to a population means choosing the measures that answer the question actually asked and then saying what they show. If the question is about a typical value, give an average and say which one and why — the median when there is an outlier or a very lopsided set, the mean when you want every value to count, the mode or modal class when the data are categorical or grouped. If the question is about consistency or reliability, give the range. If it is about a whole population you only sampled, scale the sample up and admit the uncertainty. The written mark is for the sentence in context, with the unit: 'the average waiting time is about 20 minutes, and the times range over 40 minutes, so the service is not consistent' rather than a bare number.","code":"S5"},{"h":"Scatter graphs, correlation and the line of best fit","t":"A scatter graph plots two variables measured on the same items, one on each axis, as unjoined points. If the points rise from bottom left to top right there is positive correlation; if they fall from top left to bottom right, negative correlation; if there is no pattern, no correlation. The closer the points lie to a straight line the stronger the correlation, so describe it with both words: 'strong negative', 'weak positive'. A line of best fit is a single straight line drawn through the middle of the points, following the trend with roughly as many points above as below, and it is used to predict: read up from the known value to the line and across. Predicting inside the range of the data is interpolation and is fairly safe; predicting outside it is extrapolation and is unreliable, because nothing says the trend continues there. Finally, however strong the correlation, it does not indicate causation — a link is not proof that one variable causes the other.","code":"S6"}],"examples":[{"title":"Turning a frequency table into pie chart angles","setup":"A class of students each chose one favourite fruit. 14 chose apple, 10 chose banana, 9 chose orange and 7 chose pear. These results are to be shown on a pie chart. Work out the angle of each of the four sectors.","steps":["Add the frequencies: 14 + 10 + 9 + 7 = 40 students altogether","A full turn is 360°, so one student is worth 360 ÷ 40 = 9°","Apple: 14 × 9 = 126°","Banana: 10 × 9 = 90°","Orange: 9 × 9 = 81°","Pear: 7 × 9 = 63°","Check: 126 + 90 + 81 + 63 = 360°"],"cues":["How many students are there altogether?","How many degrees are in a full turn, and how many students share them?","Which fruit was chosen most often, so which sector is the largest?","Which fruit was the next most popular?","Which fruit comes third?","Which frequency has not been used yet?","What should the four angles add up to?"]},{"title":"Mean, median, mode, range and an outlier","setup":"In seven football matches a team scored 2, 3, 3, 5, 6, 8 and 15 goals. Work out the mean, the median, the mode and the range of the number of goals, say which score could be an outlier, and work out the mean again with that score left out. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Add the scores: 2 + 3 + 3 + 5 + 6 + 8 + 15 = 42, then divide by 7 to get a mean of 6 goals","The list is already in order and there are seven values, so the median is the 4th one: 5 goals","3 appears twice and every other score once, so the mode is 3 goals","Range = 15 − 2 = 13 goals","15 sits far above the other six scores, so it is a possible outlier; leaving it out, the mean is (42 − 15) ÷ 6 = 27 ÷ 6 = 4.5 goals"],"cues":["What do you divide the total by to reach the mean?","Which position in the list is the middle one?","Which score appears more than once?","Which two values does the range use?","Which score sits a long way from the rest, and what happens to the total when it goes?"]},{"title":"Reading a scatter graph and its line of best fit","setup":"A scatter graph plots the number of hours each of 10 students revised, from 1 hour to 9 hours, against their mark out of 60 in a test. The points rise steadily from bottom left to top right and lie close to a straight line. A line of best fit is drawn and passes through the points (2, 18) and (8, 48). Describe the correlation, use the line to estimate the mark of a student who revised for 5 hours, explain why the line should not be used for a student who revised for 20 hours, and state what the graph cannot prove.","steps":["Rising from left to right with the points clustered tightly around the line: the correlation is strong and positive","Gradient of the line = (48 − 18) ÷ (8 − 2) = 30 ÷ 6 = 5 marks for each extra hour","5 hours is 3 hours further on than 2 hours, so the estimated mark is 18 + 3 × 5 = 33 marks","20 hours lies outside the 1 to 9 hours the data cover, so reading the line there is extrapolation and the trend may not continue","Correlation does not indicate causation, so the graph cannot show that revising is what caused the higher marks"],"cues":["Which way do the points go, and how tightly do they sit around the line?","Which two points on the line give you its steepness?","How far along is 5 hours from the first known point, and what does each hour add?","What range of hours does the data actually cover?","Does a link between two variables show that one makes the other happen?"]},{"title":"Using a sample to estimate a figure for a whole population","setup":"A school has 600 students. A random sample of 40 students is asked how they travel to school, and 14 of them walk. Estimate how many of the 600 students walk to school, and give two reasons why that estimate may not be accurate. A calculator may be used.","steps":["The fraction of the sample who walk is 14 out of 40, which is 14 ÷ 40 = 0.35","Assuming the sample behaves like the whole school, the estimate is 0.35 × 600 = 210 students","A sample of 40 is small next to 600, so a few unusual answers would change the estimate a lot","If the 40 students were not really chosen at random — all from one year group, say — the sample is biased and does not represent the school"],"cues":["What fraction of the sample walks, written as a decimal?","What do you multiply that fraction by to reach the whole school?","How large is the sample next to the whole school?","What could make the chosen students unlike the rest of the school?"]},{"title":"An estimate of the mean from a grouped frequency table","setup":"The table shows the times, t minutes, that 40 people waited at a bus stop. 0 < t ≤ 10: frequency 6. 10 < t ≤ 20: frequency 15. 20 < t ≤ 30: frequency 12. 30 < t ≤ 40: frequency 7. Write down the modal class, find the class that contains the median, and work out an estimate of the mean waiting time. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Fifteen is the biggest of the four frequencies, so the modal class is 10 < t ≤ 20","Running totals are 6, 21, 33 and 40; with 40 values the median is between the 20th and 21st, and both of those lie in 10 < t ≤ 20","Midpoints of the four classes are 5, 15, 25 and 35 minutes","Σfx = 5 × 6 + 15 × 15 + 25 × 12 + 35 × 7 = 30 + 225 + 300 + 245 = 800","Estimate of the mean = 800 ÷ 40 = 20 minutes"],"cues":["Which class holds the most people?","Which value or values sit in the middle when there are 40 of them?","What single time can stand for everybody in a class?","How do you combine each midpoint with its frequency?","What do you divide that total by?"]}],"check":[{"q":"In a survey of 24 people, 5 chose swimming. What angle would swimming get on a pie chart of the results?","a":"75° (360 ÷ 24 = 15°, then 5 × 15 = 75°)"},{"q":"Write down the median and the range of 6, 2, 9, 4, 14.","a":"Median 6, range 12"},{"q":"A scatter graph of the age of a mobile phone against its second-hand value has points falling from top left to bottom right, close to a line. Describe the correlation.","a":"Strong negative correlation"},{"q":"A sample of 60 apples from a large crate contains 9 bruised apples. The crate holds 2000 apples. Estimate how many are bruised, and give one reason why the estimate may be unreliable.","a":"300 bruised apples (9 ÷ 60 × 2000); the sample of 60 is small compared with 2000, or it may not have been taken from all parts of the crate, so it may not be representative"}]},"room":{"facts":["Pie chart: the angle for an item = (its frequency ÷ the total frequency) × 360°, so one unit of frequency is worth 360° ÷ total frequency, and all the angles must add to 360°.","Mean = total of the values ÷ number of values. From a frequency table, mean = Σfx ÷ Σf.","For grouped data, use the midpoint of each class as x; Σfx ÷ Σf is then only an estimate of the mean, because the individual values are not known.","Median: put the values in order; for n values it is the ((n + 1)/2)th one, and when n is even it is the mean of the two middle values. Mode = the most common value; modal class = the class with the largest frequency.","Range = largest value − smallest value. An outlier is a value far from the rest: it inflates the range and pulls the mean towards it, so the median often describes the data better.","Estimating a population figure from a sample: estimate = (number in the sample with the property ÷ sample size) × population size. It is an estimate, and its reliability depends on the sample size and on whether the sample was representative.","A comparison of two data sets must name an average and a measure of spread, both in context — for example 'A has the higher median and the smaller range, so A is larger on average and more consistent'.","Correlation does not indicate causation, and a line of best fit should only be read inside the range of the data (interpolation); reading it outside (extrapolation) is unreliable."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) The daily maximum temperatures in a town for six days were 7 °C, 11 °C, 4 °C, 9 °C, 13 °C and 6 °C. Work out the range of these temperatures.","a":"9 °C","sol":"The largest temperature is 13 °C and the smallest is 4 °C. Range = 13 − 4 = 9 °C. B1 for 9 °C with its unit (total 1 mark).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The range is a measure of how spread out the data are, not an average.","Only two of the six readings are used: the biggest and the smallest.","The first line is (largest temperature) − (smallest temperature) — pick those two out of the list first."],"codes":["S4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Eight students scored 12, 15, 9, 20, 15, 7, 11 and 18 marks in a spelling test. Work out the median mark.","a":"13.5 marks","sol":"In order: 7, 9, 11, 12, 15, 15, 18, 20. There are 8 values, so the median is the mean of the 4th and 5th: (12 + 15) ÷ 2 = 27 ÷ 2 = 13.5. M1 for the values written in order (or for identifying 12 and 15 as the middle pair), A1 for 13.5 (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The median is the middle value, so the order the marks are listed in is not the order you need.","With an even number of values there is no single middle one, so two values share the job.","The first line is to rewrite the eight marks from smallest to largest — then look at the two in the centre."],"codes":["S4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) 30 people were asked to name their favourite hot drink and 12 of them said tea. The results are shown on a pie chart. Work out the angle of the sector for tea.","a":"144°","sol":"The 30 people share a full turn of 360°, so one person is worth 360 ÷ 30 = 12°. Tea: 12 × 12 = 144°. M1 for 360 ÷ 30 = 12° (oe, such as 12/30 × 360), A1 for 144° (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A pie chart shares out one full turn between everybody asked.","Find what one person is worth in degrees before you deal with the tea drinkers.","The first line is 360 ÷ (the number of people asked) — that gives the degrees for one person."],"codes":["S2"]},{"q":"A scatter graph plots the age of a car, in years, against its value, in pounds, for 15 cars. The points fall from top left to bottom right and lie close to a straight line. Write down the type of correlation shown. A calculator may be used.","a":"Negative correlation","sol":"Points falling from top left to bottom right mean that as the age increases the value decreases, so the correlation is negative. B1 for negative correlation (accept 'strong negative correlation' as well, since the points lie close to a straight line, but the strength is extra description and is not required) (total 1 mark).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Ask what happens to the second variable as the first one increases.","There are three types to choose between — positive, negative and none — and the direction of the points decides which.","The first line is to decide whether the two quantities increase together or move in opposite directions."],"codes":["S6"]},{"q":"A factory makes 5000 light bulbs each day. A random sample of 200 of one day's bulbs is tested and 6 of them are faulty. Estimate the number of faulty bulbs made that day. A calculator may be used.","a":"150 bulbs","sol":"Fraction of the sample that is faulty = 6/200 = 0.03. Estimate for the day = 0.03 × 5000 = 150 bulbs. M1 for 6/200 × 5000 (oe, such as 5000 ÷ 200 = 25 then 6 × 25), A1 for 150 (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Assume the whole day's production is faulty in the same proportion as the sample.","Work out the fraction of the sample that failed, then apply it to the whole day.","The first line is (number faulty in the sample) ÷ (size of the sample) — scale that up afterwards."],"codes":["S1"]},{"q":"A shop has recorded its takings at the end of each of the 12 months of last year. It wants a diagram that shows how the takings changed through the year. Write down the most suitable statistical diagram and give a reason for your choice. A calculator may be used.","a":"A line graph for the time series, because the takings are values recorded in time order and joining the points shows the trend through the year","sol":"The data are a time series — one value at each of 12 regular points in time — so the appropriate diagram is a table and line graph, with the months on the horizontal axis and the points joined in time order. B1 for line graph (time series graph), B1 for a reason referring to values over time or to showing the trend (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what kind of data has been collected: categories, separate counts, or measurements taken over time.","The diagram has to make a change through the year visible, not just the size of each month.","The first line is to decide what kind of data twelve monthly totals are; the choice of diagram follows from that."],"codes":["S2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A frequency table shows the number of pets owned by each of 25 students. 0 pets: 4 students. 1 pet: 5 students. 2 pets: 6 students. 3 pets: 7 students. 4 pets: 3 students. Work out the mean number of pets per student.","a":"2 pets","sol":"Σfx = 0 × 4 + 1 × 5 + 2 × 6 + 3 × 7 + 4 × 3 = 0 + 5 + 12 + 21 + 12 = 50 pets in total. Σf = 4 + 5 + 6 + 7 + 3 = 25 students. Mean = 50 ÷ 25 = 2 pets. M1 for at least three correct products fx, M1 (dep) for dividing their total by 25, A1 for 2 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The mean is still the total number of pets divided by the number of students; the table just saves you writing 25 numbers out.","Multiply each number of pets by how many students own that many, then add those products.","The first line is 0 × 4 + 1 × 5 + … — build the total number of pets before dividing."],"codes":["S4"]},{"q":"The grouped frequency table shows the masses, m kg, of 50 parcels. 0 < m ≤ 2: 9 parcels. 2 < m ≤ 4: 16 parcels. 4 < m ≤ 6: 15 parcels. 6 < m ≤ 8: 10 parcels. Work out an estimate of the mean mass of a parcel. A calculator may be used.","a":"4.04 kg","sol":"Midpoints: 1, 3, 5 and 7 kg. Σfx = 1 × 9 + 3 × 16 + 5 × 15 + 7 × 10 = 9 + 48 + 75 + 70 = 202. Mean = 202 ÷ 50 = 4.04 kg. M1 for using the midpoints 1, 3, 5, 7, M1 for Σfx ÷ 50, A1 for 4.04 kg (total 3 marks). It is an estimate because the individual masses within each class are not known.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The table does not tell you any individual mass, so each class needs one value to stand for all of it.","Take the value halfway across each class, multiply by that class's frequency, and add the results.","The first line is to write down the midpoint of each class — halfway between the two ends — before any multiplying."],"codes":["S4","S5"]},{"q":"A scatter graph shows the mass, in kg, of each of 12 puppies against its age in weeks, for puppies aged from 4 to 20 weeks. A line of best fit is drawn and passes through the points (6, 3.5) and (18, 9.5). Use the line to estimate the mass of a puppy aged 12 weeks. A calculator may be used.","a":"6.5 kg","sol":"Gradient of the line = (9.5 − 3.5) ÷ (18 − 6) = 6 ÷ 12 = 0.5 kg per week. From (6, 3.5), 12 weeks is 6 weeks further on: 3.5 + 6 × 0.5 = 3.5 + 3 = 6.5 kg. M1 for a correct gradient calculation, M1 for adding the gradient × 6 to 3.5 (oe, such as taking the mean of 3.5 and 9.5 since 12 is halfway between 6 and 18), A1 for 6.5 kg (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Everything you need is on the line of best fit itself, not on the individual points.","Work out how much mass the line adds for each extra week, then move along from a point you know.","The first line is (change in mass) ÷ (change in age) between the two given points — that is the mass gained per week."],"codes":["S6"]},{"q":"Twenty tomato plants were given fertiliser A and twenty were given fertiliser B. For A the median height was 14 cm and the range was 6 cm. For B the median height was 11 cm and the range was 15 cm. Compare the heights of the two sets of plants. A calculator may be used.","a":"The A plants are taller on average (median 14 cm against 11 cm) and their heights are more consistent (range 6 cm against 15 cm)","sol":"Average: A's median of 14 cm is greater than B's median of 11 cm, so on average the plants given A are 3 cm taller. Spread: A's range of 6 cm is smaller than B's range of 15 cm, so A's heights are more consistent, while B's vary much more. B1 for comparing the medians in context, B1 for comparing the ranges in context (total 2 marks); a comparison that names only one of the two earns only the one matching mark.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["A full comparison always has two halves, and one of them is not about the typical height.","Say which fertiliser gives the taller plants on average, then say whose heights are bunched together and whose are spread out.","The first line is 'A has the higher median …' — then write the matching sentence about the ranges."],"codes":["S4","S5"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A pie chart shows the holiday destinations chosen by 90 people. The sector for Spain has an angle of 100°. Work out how many of the 90 people chose Spain.","a":"25 people","sol":"The 90 people share 360°, so each degree stands for 90 ÷ 360 = 0.25 of a person (equivalently 1 person = 4°). Spain: 100 × 0.25 = 25 people. Alternatively 100/360 × 90 = 10/36 × 90 = 25. M1 for 90 ÷ 360 = 0.25 (oe, such as 360 ÷ 90 = 4° per person, or 100/360 × 90), A1 for 25 (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["This time you are going backwards: from an angle to a number of people.","Find how many people one degree is worth, or how many degrees one person is worth, and use it on the Spain sector.","The first line is 360 ÷ 90 (or 90 ÷ 360) — decide which of the two you want and what it measures."],"codes":["S2"]},{"q":"A town has 30 000 residents. In a survey, a random sample of 50 residents was asked whether they shop in the town centre at least once a week, and 18 said yes. Work out the percentage of the sample who said yes, and use it to estimate the number of residents of the town who shop in the town centre at least once a week. A calculator may be used.","a":"36%; an estimated 10 800 residents","sol":"Percentage of the sample: 18 ÷ 50 = 0.36 = 36%. M1 for 18 ÷ 50 (oe), A1 for 36%. Estimate for the town: 0.36 × 30 000 = 10 800 residents. M1 for their percentage as a decimal × 30 000 (ft), A1 for 10 800 (total 4 marks). The figure is only an estimate: 50 is a small sample from 30 000, and the residents asked may not represent the whole town.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The sample is standing in for the whole town, so whatever proportion holds in the sample is assumed to hold in the town.","Turn 18 out of 50 into a percentage first, then apply that percentage to the population.","The first line is 18 ÷ 50 — that decimal is both the percentage and the multiplier you need next."],"codes":["S1","S5"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Five numbers have a mean of 12. A sixth number is added to the list and the mean of all six numbers is 13. Work out the sixth number.","a":"18","sol":"Mean = total ÷ how many, so total = mean × how many. Total of the five numbers = 5 × 12 = 60. Total of the six numbers = 6 × 13 = 78. The sixth number = 78 − 60 = 18. M1 for 5 × 12 = 60 and 6 × 13 = 78, M1 (dep) for subtracting their two totals, A1 for 18 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["You are not told any of the numbers, but a mean always carries information about the total.","Find the total of the first five numbers and the total of all six; the new number is the difference.","The first line is (number of values) × (mean) for each list — that turns both means into totals."],"codes":["S4"]},{"q":"A scatter graph shows the number of ice creams a kiosk sold against the daily maximum temperature, in °C, for 15 days on which the maximum temperature ranged from 10 °C to 22 °C. The correlation is strong and positive, and a line of best fit passes through (10, 60) and (22, 180). Use the line to estimate the number of ice creams sold on a day with a maximum temperature of 16 °C, and explain why the line should not be used to predict sales on a day with a maximum temperature of 35 °C. A calculator may be used.","a":"About 120 ice creams; 35 °C is far outside the range of the data (10 °C to 22 °C), so using the line there is extrapolation and the trend may not continue","sol":"Gradient = (180 − 60) ÷ (22 − 10) = 120 ÷ 12 = 10 ice creams per °C. M1 for a correct gradient. From (10, 60), 16 °C is 6 °C further on: 60 + 6 × 10 = 120 ice creams. M1 for 60 + their gradient × 6, A1 for 120. B1 for saying that 35 °C lies outside the range of the data, so the prediction is an extrapolation and unreliable (total 4 marks).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The two points given are on the line of best fit, so the line behaves like a straight-line graph between them.","Find how many extra ice creams each degree is worth, then step along from one of the given points; afterwards compare 35 °C with the temperatures that were actually recorded.","The first line is (change in sales) ÷ (change in temperature) between the two given points."],"codes":["S6"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The masses, in kg, of nine dogs at a rescue centre are 12, 14, 15, 15, 16, 18, 19, 20 and 51. Work out the mean mass and the median mass, and state which of the two better describes a typical dog at the centre, giving a reason.","a":"Mean 20 kg, median 16 kg; the median, because 51 kg is an outlier that pulls the mean up","sol":"Total = 12 + 14 + 15 + 15 + 16 + 18 + 19 + 20 + 51 = 180 kg, so the mean = 180 ÷ 9 = 20 kg. M1 for their total ÷ 9, A1 for 20 kg. The nine masses are already in order, so the median is the 5th value, 16 kg. B1 for 16 kg. The median describes a typical dog better because 51 kg is an outlier, far above the other eight masses, and it drags the mean above all but one of the dogs. B1 for choosing the median with a reason naming the outlier (total 4 marks).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Work the two averages out first, then look at how the list is shaped before you choose between them.","Once you have both values, compare each with the masses of the individual dogs — does either sit above nearly all of them?","The first line is to add the nine masses; then count in from each end of the ordered list to find the middle one."],"codes":["S4","S5"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A pie chart shows how 180 employees travel to work. The sector for 'car' has an angle of 144° and the sector for 'bus' has an angle of 90°. Work out how many more employees travel by car than by bus, and work out the percentage of the employees who travel by bus.","a":"27 more by car; 25% travel by bus","sol":"The 180 employees share 360°, so one employee is worth 360 ÷ 180 = 2°. M1 for 2° per employee (oe). Car: 144 ÷ 2 = 72 employees. Bus: 90 ÷ 2 = 45 employees. M1 for both 72 and 45 (ft their degrees per employee). Difference = 72 − 45 = 27 employees. A1 for 27. Bus as a percentage = 90/360 × 100 = 25%. B1 for 25% (total 4 marks).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Each sector's angle is a share of the full turn, and the full turn is shared between all 180 employees.","Convert both angles into numbers of employees before subtracting; the percentage can come straight from the angle instead.","The first line is 360 ÷ 180 — that is the number of degrees one employee is worth."],"codes":["S2"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write down the range of 5, 9, 2, 11 and 7.","a":"9"},{"q":"A pie chart shows the results for 60 people. How many degrees represent one person?","a":"6°"},{"q":"The points on a scatter graph fall from top left to bottom right. What type of correlation is this?","a":"Negative correlation"},{"q":"In a random sample of 40 seeds, 34 germinate. Estimate how many of 1000 seeds would germinate.","a":"850 seeds"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Population (in statistics)","back":"Every member of the group being investigated — all the students in a school, all the parcels in a depot, all the residents of a town.","code":"S1","open":false},{"front":"Sample","back":"The part of the population that is actually measured or asked. It is used to infer properties of the population, which gives an estimate rather than a certainty.","code":"S1","open":false},{"front":"Bias in a sample","back":"A sample is biased when it is not representative of the population, so results from it do not transfer. A larger sample does not remove bias; only a fair method of choosing does.","code":"S1","open":false},{"front":"Pie chart: the angle for an item","back":"Angle = (frequency ÷ total frequency) × 360°. Equivalently, one unit of frequency is worth 360° ÷ total frequency. All the angles must add up to 360°.","code":"S2","open":false},{"front":"Choosing a statistical diagram","back":"Categorical data: bar chart, pictogram or pie chart. Ungrouped discrete numerical data: vertical line chart. Values recorded over time: table and line graph. Two variables on the same items: scatter graph.","code":"S2","open":false},{"front":"Time series line graph","back":"Time goes on the horizontal axis and the points are joined in time order, so the trend over the period can be seen. Used for values recorded at regular times, such as monthly takings.","code":"S2","open":false},{"front":"Mean from a frequency table","back":"Mean = Σfx ÷ Σf: multiply each value x by its frequency f, add the products, then divide by the total frequency.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"Estimate of the mean of grouped data","back":"Use the midpoint of each class as x, then Σfx ÷ Σf. It is only an estimate because the individual values inside each class are not known.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"Median","back":"The middle value once the data are in order: the ((n + 1)/2)th of n values, or the mean of the two middle values when n is even.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"Mode and modal class","back":"The mode is the most common value; there can be more than one, or none. For grouped data the modal class is the class with the largest frequency.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"Range and outliers","back":"Range = largest value − smallest value, a measure of spread. An outlier is a value far from the rest of the data; it inflates the range and pulls the mean towards it.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"Primary and secondary data","back":"Primary data is collected by the person using it, for their own purpose. Secondary data was collected by somebody else, or for another purpose, and is reused.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"Discrete and continuous data","back":"Discrete data is counted and takes separate values, such as the number of pets. Continuous data is measured and can take any value in a range, such as mass or time.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"Comparing two distributions","back":"Name an average and a measure of spread, both in context: which set is larger on average, and which is more consistent. An average on its own does not score full marks.","code":"S5","open":false},{"front":"Types of correlation","back":"Positive: one rises as the other rises. Negative: one rises as the other falls. None: no pattern. Strong if the points lie close to a line, weak if scattered. Correlation never proves causation.","code":"S6","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Pie chart angle for an item: angle = (frequency ÷ total frequency) × 360°, so one unit of frequency is worth 360° ÷ total frequency. Recall: the specification's Appendix does not list it and AQA's Foundation formulae sheet does not print it.","Mean of a list of values: mean = (sum of the values) ÷ (number of values); rearranged, total = mean × number of values. Recall: neither the Appendix nor the Foundation formulae sheet gives it.","Mean from a frequency table: mean = Σfx ÷ Σf, where x is each value and f its frequency. Recall: not in the Appendix, not on the Foundation sheet.","Estimate of the mean from a grouped frequency table: take x as the midpoint of each class, then mean ≈ Σfx ÷ Σf. Recall: not in the Appendix, not on the Foundation sheet.","Position of the median: for n values in order the median is the ((n + 1)/2)th value; for even n take the mean of the two middle values. Recall: not in the Appendix, not on the Foundation sheet.","Range = largest value − smallest value. Recall: not in the Appendix, not on the Foundation sheet.","Estimating a population figure from a sample: estimate = (number in the sample with the property ÷ sample size) × population size. Recall: not in the Appendix, not on the Foundation sheet.","Gradient of a line of best fit between two points on it = (change in the y value) ÷ (change in the x value); a prediction is then (y at a known point) + gradient × (change in x). Recall: not in the Appendix, not on the Foundation sheet."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Where these formulae come from","items":["Provenance: the specification's Appendix lists no statistics result, and AQA's Foundation tier formulae sheet (the insert for exams from 2026) prints only the trapezium, prism, circumference, circle area, Pythagoras, the three right-angled trigonometric ratios, compound interest and the probability addition rule — so nothing above is given to you on the day, on any of the three papers, and every result here must be written from memory."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:31:53Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Re-solved from the kit JSON: all 16 questions, the 4 exit questions, the 4 check questions and all 5 worked examples are numerically correct, every sol's M/A/B marks total exactly its tariff, 8 questions are non-calculator, the difficulty split is 6/5/5 with all five idea codes covered, no Higher-only technique appears, and the Formulae sheet and its new provenance note match appendix.md section 4 item for item (the Foundation insert prints trapezium, prism, circumference, circle area, Pythagoras, the three right-angled ratios, compound interest and the probability addition rule — none of them statistics, so 'recall everything here' is right); the kit is ready to publish, with only the closing step of worked example 1 worth a final tidy."}}},"number-structure-higher":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"number-structure-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"The Higher paper takes everything the Foundation number room covers for granted and then asks for the exact answer. Grades 7 to 9 turn on being able to write √48 as 4√3, read 8²⁄³ as (∛8)², clear a surd out of a denominator, and count a large set of possibilities by multiplying the choices instead of listing them. Number is 15% of the Higher tier and these four statements carry most of it. They appear on Paper 1, where no calculator can rescue a wrong method, and inside longer algebra, circle and trigonometry questions that ask for an answer ‘in surd form’ or ‘in the form a√b’.","idea":[{"h":"The product rule for counting","code":"N5","t":"At Foundation tier you list every possibility in a fixed order. At Higher tier you count them instead: if a first task can be done in m ways and, for each of those, a second can be done in n ways, the two together can be done in m × n ways, and every further stage keeps multiplying. The examiner sets this as menus, routes, passwords, number plates and locks. Two things decide the numbers: whether repeats are allowed, so that each stage keeps all its choices, or not, so that each stage has one fewer than the stage before; and whether a stage is restricted, such as a first digit that cannot be 0. Write the product down, because the multiplication is where the method mark sits."},{"h":"Estimating powers and roots","code":"N6","t":"‘Estimate’ means trap the number between values you already know, not reach for a calculator. For a root, find the nearest square or cube numbers on either side: 64 < 70 < 81 gives 8 < √70 < 9, and because 70 is closer to 64 than to 81 the root is nearer 8. For a power of a decimal, round the base to something convenient and then say which way the estimate leans: 3.1³ ≈ 3³ = 27, and rounding the base down makes that an underestimate. The marks are for the squares or cubes you write down and for the inequality between them, so a bare number scores nothing even when it is right."},{"h":"Fractional indices","code":"N7","t":"A fractional index is a root. The denominator of the index says which root to take and the numerator says what power to raise it to, so xᵐ⁄ⁿ = (ⁿ√x)ᵐ. Take the root first and the numbers stay small: 8²⁄³ = (∛8)² = 2² = 4, whereas cubing first gives 64 and then needs its cube root, which is the same answer by a harder route. A minus sign in front of the index means ‘take the reciprocal as well’, so x⁻ᵐ⁄ⁿ = 1/xᵐ⁄ⁿ, and with a fraction for a base it is quicker to turn the fraction over, since (a/b)⁻ⁿ = (b/a)ⁿ. Every index law — add to multiply, subtract to divide, multiply to raise a power to a power — still holds when the indices are negative or fractional."},{"h":"Surds and rationalising denominators","code":"N8","t":"A surd is a root left unevaluated so that the answer stays exact. Simplify one by taking out the largest square factor: √48 = √(16 × 3) = √16 × √3 = 4√3, and the b in a√b must then have no square factor left in it. Roots combine under a single sign, √a × √b = √(ab) and √a ÷ √b = √(a/b), and √a × √a = a. A denominator is never left with a surd in it: multiply top and bottom by that surd, or, when the denominator has the form a ± √b, by its conjugate a ∓ √b, because (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b is a whole number. Higher geometry and circle work ask for surd form constantly, so this is not a topic on its own."}],"examples":[{"title":"Counting journeys with the product rule","setup":"A ferry company runs 4 different boats from an island to a mainland port, and from that port 7 different coaches run on to the city. Every boat connects with every coach. Work out how many different island-to-city journeys can be made.","steps":["Choosing a boat does not rule out any coach, so the two stages combine by multiplying rather than by adding.","A table with one row per boat and one column per coach would have 4 rows and 7 columns, and each cell would be one complete journey.","The number of cells, and so the number of journeys, is 4 × 7.","4 × 7 = 28, so 28 different journeys can be made."],"cues":["Does picking one boat use up any of the coaches?","What would a table showing every journey look like?","How many cells does a table of that size have?","What is the value of that product?"]},{"title":"Estimating a root and a power without a calculator","setup":"Without using a calculator, estimate √70: give the two consecutive whole numbers it lies between, and then the nearest whole number to it. Then estimate the value of 3.1³ by rounding the base, and state whether your estimate is above or below the true value.","steps":["The square numbers on either side of 70 are 8² = 64 and 9² = 81.","Since 64 < 70 < 81, taking square roots gives 8 < √70 < 9.","70 is only 6 above 64 but 11 below 81, so √70 is nearer 8, and to the nearest whole number √70 ≈ 8.","For the cube, round the base to a whole number: 3.1 rounds to 3, so 3.1³ ≈ 3³ = 27.","Rounding the base down makes the cube smaller, so 27 is below the true value of 3.1³."],"cues":["Which square numbers sit either side of the number under the root?","What happens to an inequality when the square root of each part is taken?","Is the number closer to the lower square or the upper one?","Which whole number is the base closest to?","Does rounding the base down push the answer up or down?"]},{"title":"Working out powers with fractional indices","setup":"Without a calculator, work out the exact value of each of 25¹⁄², 8²⁄³ and 16⁻¹⁄². Give each answer as a whole number or as a fraction in its lowest terms.","steps":["An index of 1/2 means the positive square root, so 25¹⁄² = √25 = 5.","In 8²⁄³ the denominator 3 calls for the cube root and the numerator 2 for the square, so 8²⁄³ = (∛8)².","∛8 = 2, and 2² = 4, so 8²⁄³ = 4.","A negative index means the reciprocal, so 16⁻¹⁄² = 1/16¹⁄² = 1/√16.","√16 = 4, so 16⁻¹⁄² = 1/4, and the three values are 5, 4 and 1/4."],"cues":["Which root does a denominator of 2 in the index call for?","Which part of the fraction gives the root and which gives the power?","What is the cube root of the base, and what is its square?","What does the minus sign in front of the index do?","What is the square root of 16, and so what is the whole expression worth?"]},{"title":"Simplifying, multiplying and rationalising surds","setup":"Without a calculator: (a) simplify √12, (b) work out √3 × √6 in simplified surd form, and (c) rationalise the denominator of 6/√2. Give every answer in the form a√b with b as small as possible.","steps":["The largest square factor of 12 is 4, so √12 = √(4 × 3) = √4 × √3 = 2√3.","Two roots multiplied sit under one root sign, so √3 × √6 = √18.","18 = 9 × 2, so √18 = √9 × √2 = 3√2.","To clear the root from the bottom, multiply top and bottom by √2: 6/√2 = 6√2/(√2 × √2) = 6√2/2.","Dividing 6 by 2 gives 6/√2 = 3√2, so the three answers are 2√3, 3√2 and 3√2."],"cues":["Which factor of the number under the root is a square number?","What happens to two roots that are multiplied together?","Does the number you now have under the root have a square factor?","What could the fraction be multiplied by that changes its look but not its value?","What does the fraction in front of the root cancel down to?"]},{"title":"Rationalising a denominator with a conjugate","setup":"Rationalise the denominator of 1/(3 − √5), giving your answer in its simplest form and without using a calculator.","steps":["Multiplying a difference of two terms by the matching sum removes the root, so the useful partner for 3 − √5 is its conjugate, 3 + √5.","Multiply numerator and denominator by that conjugate: 1/(3 − √5) = (3 + √5)/((3 − √5)(3 + √5)).","Expanding the bottom gives (3 − √5)(3 + √5) = 9 + 3√5 − 3√5 − 5.","The two root terms cancel, leaving 9 − 5 = 4 on the bottom.","So 1/(3 − √5) = (3 + √5)/4."],"cues":["Which expression multiplies this denominator to give a whole number?","What must be done to the numerator if the denominator is multiplied by something?","What are the four terms when the bottom brackets are expanded?","Which two of those four terms cancel each other?","What does the whole fraction now look like?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Simplify √45 fully.","a":"3√5"},{"q":"Work out the value of 64²⁄³.","a":"16"},{"q":"A code is made of one letter chosen from A, B, C, D followed by one digit chosen from 1 to 5. How many different codes are possible?","a":"20"},{"q":"Between which two consecutive whole numbers does √90 lie?","a":"9 and 10"}]},"room":{"facts":["The product rule for counting: if one stage can be done in m ways and, for each of those, the next in n ways, the stages together can be done in m × n ways; further stages keep multiplying.","Fractional indices: x¹⁄ⁿ = ⁿ√x and xᵐ⁄ⁿ = (ⁿ√x)ᵐ, so take the root first and then the power. A negative index takes the reciprocal: x⁻ᵐ⁄ⁿ = 1/xᵐ⁄ⁿ, and (a/b)⁻ⁿ = (b/a)ⁿ.","The index laws hold for negative and fractional indices: xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ, xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ, (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ and x⁰ = 1.","Surd rules: √a × √b = √(ab), √a ÷ √b = √(a/b) and √a × √a = a. Simplify by taking out the largest square factor, so that a√b has no square factor left under the root.","Rationalising: multiply top and bottom by the surd in the denominator, or by the conjugate when the denominator is a ± √b, because (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b.","The square numbers to 15² = 225 and the cubes 2³ = 8, 3³ = 27, 4³ = 64, 5³ = 125 and 10³ = 1000, which are what an estimate of a root is trapped between."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) A café offers 6 different starters, 9 different main courses and 4 different desserts. A three-course meal is one starter, one main course and one dessert. Work out how many different three-course meals are possible.","a":"216","sol":"Each stage can be combined with every choice at the other stages, so the three numbers of choices multiply: 6 × 9 × 4 (M1). 6 × 9 = 54 and 54 × 4 = 216 different meals (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Choosing a starter does not use up any of the main courses or desserts.","Count the number of choices at each of the three stages and decide whether those numbers should be added or multiplied.","The first line of working writes the three stage counts in a row with multiplication signs between them."],"codes":["N5"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) √130 lies between two consecutive whole numbers. Write down those two whole numbers.","a":"11 and 12","sol":"The square numbers either side of 130 are 11² = 121 and 12² = 144 (M1). Since 121 < 130 < 144, taking square roots gives 11 < √130 < 12, so the two whole numbers are 11 and 12 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A square root can only be placed once you know the square numbers around it.","Find the square number just below the number under the root and the square number just above it, then take the root of each.","The first line of working writes down the two consecutive square numbers that trap the number under the root."],"codes":["N6"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out the value of (a) 36¹⁄² and (b) 27²⁄³.","a":"(a) 6   (b) 9","sol":"An index of 1/2 is the positive square root, so 36¹⁄² = √36 = 6 (B1). In 27²⁄³ the denominator 3 gives the cube root and the numerator 2 the power, so 27²⁄³ = (∛27)² = 3² = 9 (B1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The number on the bottom of a fractional index tells you which root to take.","Take the root that the denominator asks for first, then raise the result to the power on the top.","The first line of working rewrites each power as a root sign with the numerator left as a power outside it."],"codes":["N7"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify √75 fully, giving your answer in the form a√b.","a":"5√3","sol":"The largest square factor of 75 is 25, so √75 = √(25 × 3) = √25 × √3 (M1). √25 = 5, so √75 = 5√3 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Only a square factor can be brought out from under a root sign.","List the factor pairs of the number under the root and choose the pair whose first number is a square number.","The first line of working splits the number under the root into a square factor multiplied by what is left."],"codes":["N8"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out √10 × √15, giving your answer in the form a√b with b as small as possible.","a":"5√6","sol":"Two roots multiplied go under one root sign: √10 × √15 = √150 (M1). The largest square factor of 150 is 25, so √150 = √25 × √6 = 5√6 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Two roots multiplied together can be written under a single root sign.","Combine them under one root first, then look for a square factor in the number you are left with.","The first line of working writes the product as one root of the two numbers multiplied together."],"codes":["N8"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out the value of 81⁻³⁄⁴. Give your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms.","a":"1/27","sol":"A negative index means the reciprocal, so 81⁻³⁄⁴ = 1/81³⁄⁴ (M1). The denominator 4 of the index gives the fourth root and the numerator 3 the power, and the fourth root of 81 is 3, so 81³⁄⁴ = 3³ = 27 (M1). Therefore 81⁻³⁄⁴ = 1/27 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["There are two separate things in this index to deal with: the minus sign and the fraction.","A minus sign in the index means take the reciprocal; in the fraction itself, the denominator gives the root and the numerator gives the power.","The first line of working writes the expression as 1 over the same power with the index made positive."],"codes":["N7"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Rationalise the denominator of 12/√6 and simplify your answer fully.","a":"2√6","sol":"Multiply numerator and denominator by √6: 12/√6 = (12 × √6)/(√6 × √6) (M1). Since √6 × √6 = 6, this is 12√6/6 (A1). Dividing 12 by 6 gives 2√6 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["An answer is not in an acceptable form while a root is still underneath.","Multiply the top and the bottom by the surd that appears in the denominator, then cancel the whole numbers.","The first line of working multiplies numerator and denominator by the root that sits underneath."],"codes":["N8"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Expand and simplify (3 + √5)(2 − √5).","a":"1 − √5","sol":"Multiplying every term in the first bracket by every term in the second gives 3 × 2 = 6, 3 × (−√5) = −3√5, √5 × 2 = 2√5 and √5 × (−√5) = −5 (M1). So the expansion is 6 − 3√5 + 2√5 − 5 (A1). Collecting the whole numbers gives 6 − 5 = 1 and collecting the root terms gives −3√5 + 2√5 = −√5, so the answer is 1 − √5 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This is an ordinary pair of brackets, and the root behaves like any other term inside them.","Multiply each term in the first bracket by each term in the second, then collect like terms, remembering what a root multiplied by itself gives.","The first line of working writes out all four products before any of them are collected."],"codes":["N8"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A password is made of 2 letters chosen from the 26 capital letters followed by 3 digits chosen from 0 to 9. Letters may be repeated and digits may be repeated. Work out how many different passwords are possible.","a":"676 000","sol":"Each of the 2 letter positions has 26 choices, giving 26 × 26 = 676 (M1). Each of the 3 digit positions has 10 choices, so the five positions together give 676 × 10 × 10 × 10 (M1). 676 × 1000 = 676 000 passwords (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each position in the password is filled independently of all the others.","Work out how many choices each single position has, then combine all five positions with the product rule.","The first line of working writes the number of choices for each of the five positions in a row, multiplied together."],"codes":["N5"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Estimate ∛500 to the nearest whole number. You must show the cube numbers you have used.","a":"8","sol":"The cube numbers either side of 500 are 7³ = 343 and 8³ = 512 (M1). Since 343 < 500 < 512, ∛500 lies between 7 and 8 (M1). 500 is only 12 below 512 but 157 above 343, so ∛500 is much nearer 8, and to the nearest whole number ∛500 = 8 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A cube root is placed by knowing the cube numbers on either side of it.","Find the cube just below the number and the cube just above it, then judge which of the two the number is closer to.","The first line of working writes down the two consecutive cube numbers that trap the number under the root."],"codes":["N6"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out the value of (8/27)⁻²⁄³. Give your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms.","a":"9/4","sol":"A negative index turns the fraction over, so (8/27)⁻²⁄³ = (27/8)²⁄³ (M1). The denominator 3 of the index calls for the cube root, and ∛27 = 3 with ∛8 = 2, so the cube root of 27/8 is 3/2 (M1). Squaring gives (3/2)² = 9/4 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A negative index on a fraction has a shortcut worth using.","Turn the fraction upside down so that the index becomes positive, then use the denominator of the index as a root and the numerator as a power.","The first line of working rewrites the expression with the fraction inverted and the index made positive."],"codes":["N7"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Rationalise the denominator of 5/(4 − √3), giving your answer in its simplest form.","a":"(20 + 5√3)/13","sol":"Multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate 4 + √3: 5/(4 − √3) = 5(4 + √3)/((4 − √3)(4 + √3)) (M1). The denominator expands to 16 + 4√3 − 4√3 − 3, and the root terms cancel to leave 16 − 3 = 13 (A1). The numerator is 5 × 4 + 5 × √3 = 20 + 5√3, so the answer is (20 + 5√3)/13 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Multiplying this denominator by the right bracket will turn it into a whole number.","Multiply top and bottom by the conjugate — the same two terms with the sign between them reversed — then expand both.","The first line of working multiplies numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator."],"codes":["N8"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A right-angled triangle has its two shorter sides of length √12 cm and √24 cm. (a) Work out the length of the hypotenuse. (b) Work out the exact area of the triangle, giving your answer in the form a√b cm².","a":"(a) 6 cm   (b) 6√2 cm²","sol":"By Pythagoras' theorem the hypotenuse c satisfies c² = (√12)² + (√24)² (M1). Squaring a root removes it, so c² = 12 + 24 = 36 (A1) and c = √36 = 6 cm (A1). For the area, the two shorter sides are perpendicular, so area = ½ × √12 × √24 = ½√288 (M1). The largest square factor of 288 is 144, so √288 = 12√2 and the area is ½ × 12√2 = 6√2 cm² (A1).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The two lengths given are the short sides of a right-angled triangle, and roots are easier to handle once they have been squared.","Use Pythagoras' theorem for the third side, remembering that squaring a root removes it; for the area use half the base times the height and put the two roots under one root sign.","The first lines of working are the sum of the squares of the two given sides, and then half the product of those two sides written under a single root."],"codes":["N8"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) 9³⁄² × 3⁻² = 3ⁿ. Work out the value of n.","a":"n = 1","sol":"Write the first base as a power of 3: 9 = 3², so 9³⁄² = (3²)³⁄² (M1). Multiplying the indices gives (3²)³⁄² = 3³, which is 27 (A1). Multiplying powers of the same base adds the indices, so 3³ × 3⁻² = 3³⁻² (M1), giving 3¹, so n = 1 (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Both of the bases in this statement are powers of the same smaller number.","Rewrite the square-number base as a power of 3 and use the rule for raising a power to a power, then use the rule for multiplying powers of the same base.","The first line of working replaces the square-number base by a power of 3 before the fractional index is applied."],"codes":["N7"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A four-digit entry code uses the digits 0 to 9. The first digit cannot be 0, and no digit may be used more than once in the code. Work out how many different codes are possible.","a":"4536","sol":"The first digit can be any of 1 to 9, so it has 9 choices (B1). One digit has then been used, so the second position has 9 of the 10 digits left, the third has 8 and the fourth has 7 (M1). By the product rule the number of codes is 9 × 9 × 8 × 7 (M1). 9 × 9 = 81, 81 × 8 = 648 and 648 × 7 = 4536 codes (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["One of the four positions is not as free as the other three.","Count the choices left at each position in turn, remembering that every position uses up one more digit, then combine them with the product rule.","The first line of working writes the number of choices for the restricted position, followed by the number left for each position after it."],"codes":["N5"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. The area of a circle is exactly 27π cm². Work out the exact radius of the circle, giving your answer in the form a√b cm.","a":"3√3 cm","sol":"The area of a circle is πr², so πr² = 27π and dividing both sides by π gives r² = 27 (M1). Therefore r = √27, and the largest square factor of 27 is 9, so √27 = √9 × √3 (M1). √9 = 3, so the radius is 3√3 cm (A1).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["The area formula links the area you are given to the square of the radius.","Set the area formula equal to the given area, cancel the π from both sides, then square root and simplify the surd you get.","The first line of working writes the formula for the area of a circle equal to the exact area given."],"codes":["N8"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Simplify √50 fully.","a":"5√2"},{"q":"Work out the value of 32³⁄⁵.","a":"8"},{"q":"A lunch deal offers 5 different sandwiches and 3 different drinks. How many different sandwich-and-drink deals are possible?","a":"15"},{"q":"Between which two consecutive whole numbers does √40 lie?","a":"6 and 7"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The product rule for counting","code":"N5","open":false,"back":"If one stage can be done in m ways and, for each of those, a second in n ways, the two together can be done in m × n ways. Extra stages keep multiplying on."},{"front":"Counting with repeats and without repeats","code":"N5","open":false,"back":"With repeats allowed every stage keeps all its choices: 10 × 10 × 10. Without repeats each stage has one fewer than the last: 10 × 9 × 8."},{"front":"Estimating a square root","code":"N6","open":false,"back":"Trap the number between the two nearest square numbers; the root lies between their roots, and is nearer the root of the closer square. 64 < 70 < 81 gives 8 < √70 < 9."},{"front":"Estimating a cube root","code":"N6","open":false,"back":"Trap the number between the two nearest cube numbers, then take cube roots: 343 < 400 < 512 gives 7 < ∛400 < 8."},{"front":"Estimating a power of a decimal","code":"N6","open":false,"back":"Round the base to a convenient value and raise that instead: 3.1³ ≈ 3³ = 27. Rounding the base down gives an underestimate, rounding it up an overestimate."},{"front":"The meaning of x¹⁄ⁿ","code":"N7","open":false,"back":"The positive nth root of x: x¹⁄² = √x and x¹⁄³ = ∛x. So 49¹⁄² = 7 and 125¹⁄³ = 5."},{"front":"The meaning of xᵐ⁄ⁿ","code":"N7","open":false,"back":"The nth root of x raised to the power m: xᵐ⁄ⁿ = (ⁿ√x)ᵐ. Take the root first to keep the numbers small, so 8²⁄³ = (∛8)² = 4."},{"front":"A negative index","code":"N7","open":false,"back":"x⁻ⁿ = 1/xⁿ, the reciprocal, and it works with fractional indices too: 16⁻¹⁄² = 1/√16 = 1/4. For a fraction base, (a/b)⁻ⁿ = (b/a)ⁿ."},{"front":"The laws of indices","code":"N7","open":false,"back":"xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ; xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ; (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ; x⁰ = 1. They hold when a and b are negative or fractional."},{"front":"A surd","code":"N8","open":false,"back":"A root that cannot be written exactly as a fraction, such as √2 or 3√5. Leaving it unevaluated is what keeps an answer exact rather than rounded."},{"front":"Simplifying a surd","code":"N8","open":false,"back":"Take out the largest square factor: √12 = √4 × √3 = 2√3. The answer a√b is fully simplified only when b has no square factor left."},{"front":"Multiplying and dividing surds","code":"N8","open":false,"back":"√a × √b = √(ab) and √a ÷ √b = √(a/b). Also √a × √a = a, so (√7)² = 7."},{"front":"Rationalising a single-surd denominator","code":"N8","open":false,"back":"Multiply numerator and denominator by that surd, then cancel: 6/√2 = 6√2/2 = 3√2."},{"front":"The conjugate of a + √b","code":"N8","open":false,"back":"a − √b. Multiplying the pair gives (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b, a whole number, which is how a two-term surd denominator is cleared."},{"front":"Give one way of checking that a surd has been simplified fully","code":"N8","open":true,"back":"Square the answer and check it returns the original number; check the number left under the root has no square factor; compare a decimal value of the answer with a decimal value of the original root."}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Given to you: area of a circle = πr² and circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d the diameter. The Higher tier formulae sheet prints both, so they may be read off the insert. Of the two, only the area formula is used in this room, when an exact area has to be turned back into a radius in surd form (N8, and see G17 and G18); the circumference formula is printed on the same sheet but no question, example or card here needs it. Note that the specification's own Appendix, written before the sheet existed, puts them in list 1 as formulae that 'will not be given in the exam'; the later sheet governs what is given on the day.","Given to you: Pythagoras' theorem, a² + b² = c² in a right-angled triangle where c is the hypotenuse. The Higher tier formulae sheet prints it, and in this room it is the route from two surd side lengths to an exact third side. The Appendix again places it in list 1; the sheet governs.","So, of everything the Higher tier formulae sheet prints, this room uses only two — the area of a circle and Pythagoras' theorem. Everything below has to be recalled.","Remember: the product rule for counting — m ways for the first stage and n ways for the second give m × n ways altogether, with every further stage multiplying on (N5). Not on the Higher tier formulae sheet, and in none of the Appendix's three lists.","Remember: x¹⁄ⁿ = ⁿ√x and xᵐ⁄ⁿ = (ⁿ√x)ᵐ — the denominator of the index is the root, the numerator is the power (N7). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: x⁻ⁿ = 1/xⁿ and x⁻ᵐ⁄ⁿ = 1/xᵐ⁄ⁿ, with the shortcut (a/b)⁻ⁿ = (b/a)ⁿ for a fraction base (N7). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: the index laws xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ, xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ, (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ and x⁰ = 1, which hold for negative and fractional indices as well as whole ones (N7). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: √a × √b = √(ab), √a ÷ √b = √(a/b) and √a × √a = a (N8). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: to simplify a surd, take out the largest square factor, so that a√b is left with no square factor under the root — √48 = √16 × √3 = 4√3 (N8). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: to rationalise a denominator, multiply top and bottom by the surd underneath, or by the conjugate when the denominator is a ± √b, using (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b (N8). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: the square numbers to 15² = 225 and the cubes 2³ = 8, 3³ = 27, 4³ = 64, 5³ = 125 and 10³ = 1000, which are the values an estimated root is trapped between (N6). Not on the formulae sheet.","For reference, the rest of the Higher tier formulae sheet — none of it needed in this room — is the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios in a right-angled triangle, the quadratic formula, the sine rule, the cosine rule, the area of a triangle from two sides and the angle between them, compound interest, the addition rule for probability and the multiplication rule for probability using a conditional probability. The sheet is reissued every series, so check the insert at the front of your paper before relying on this list."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:27:12Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Every answer key is right and every surd, index and counting result re-solves exactly as printed, and the Formulae sheet matches the Higher tier insert in section 4 of appendix.md item for item; fix the stray N6 tag on the index-law question and the claim that the room uses the circumference formula before publishing."}}},"number-fdp-higher":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"number-fdp-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Higher papers expect you to move between a fraction and a recurring decimal in both directions, and the specification puts that statement in the Higher content only column of N10. Number is 15% of the Higher tier assessment, and this is one of the few places where a short piece of algebra is used on a purely numerical question: multiplying by a power of 10 and subtracting is a method mark the examiner looks for, so an answer written down alone scores nothing. Paper 1 is non-calculator and asks for the exact fraction in its lowest terms; Papers 2 and 3 expect you to read a rounded calculator display and still write the repeat correctly with dots. The same thinking decides whether a fraction terminates at all, and it feeds ordering, surds, algebraic proof and every later question that wants an exact value rather than a rounded one.","idea":[{"h":"Turning a recurring decimal into a fraction","code":"N10 (i)","t":"Call the number x and write out enough digits to see the repeat. Count the digits in the repeating block: one digit needs a multiplier of 10, two digits 100, three digits 1000 — in general 10ⁿ for a block of n digits. Multiplying by that power of 10 shifts the decimal along by exactly one block, so the two numbers have identical tails and subtracting clears the recurrence completely. That subtraction line is where the method mark falls. Divide by the whole number that is left and cancel to lowest terms. When the recurrence starts late, as in 0.16̇, first multiply by 10 to step past the digits that do not repeat, then multiply again to shift one block, and subtract those two multiples rather than the original. The same argument gives 0.9̇ = 1: 10x − x = 9, so 9x = 9 and x = 1 exactly."},{"h":"Turning a fraction into a recurring decimal","code":"N10 (ii)","t":"Divide the numerator by the denominator by short division and watch the remainders. As soon as a remainder you have already had comes back, the digits from that point repeat for ever, so you can stop and write the block. Mark the repeat with a dot on the first and the last digit of the block: 0.2̇7̇ means 27 repeating and 0.41̇6̇ would mean 16 repeating after the 4. Whether a fraction recurs at all is settled by its denominator in lowest terms: if the only prime factors are 2 and 5 the decimal terminates, because the fraction can be scaled to a power of 10; any other prime factor, such as 3, 7 or 11, makes it recur. A block can never be longer than one less than the denominator, which is why sevenths repeat in blocks of six. On a calculator the display is rounded, so read the pattern from it and then write the dots yourself. The Notes add ordering: write every number as a decimal with enough places to separate them, compare there, then answer in the forms the question used."}],"examples":[{"title":"A two-digit block: 0.4̇5̇ as a fraction","setup":"The recurring decimal 0.4̇5̇ has the two digits 45 repeating for ever. Write it as a fraction in its lowest terms.","steps":["Let x = 0.454545…","The repeating block is two digits long, so multiply both sides by 100: 100x = 45.454545…","Subtract the original value to clear the recurrence: 100x − x = 45.454545… − 0.454545…, so 99x = 45","Divide both sides by 99: x = 45/99","Cancel using the common factor 9: x = 5/11"],"cues":["What has to be done with the decimal itself before any multiplying can begin?","Which power of 10 shifts the decimal along by exactly one block?","What happens to the endless tail when the two lines are subtracted?","What must both sides be divided by now?","What is the highest common factor of 45 and 99?"]},{"title":"Back the other way: 5/11 as a decimal","setup":"Write the fraction 5/11 as a decimal. Use short division, and give your answer with dots showing the digits that repeat.","steps":["Set out 5 ÷ 11: eleven does not go into five, so the answer begins 0. and the remainder is 5","Carry the remainder to make 50: 50 ÷ 11 = 4 remainder 6, so the first decimal digit is 4","Carry again to make 60: 60 ÷ 11 = 5 remainder 5, so the next decimal digit is 5","The remainder 5 has come back, so the pair of digits repeats for ever: 0.454545…","Put a dot on the first and last digit of the block: 0.4̇5̇"],"cues":["How many times does 11 go into 5?","What is the next number to divide by 11?","What is the remainder this time?","Why can the division be stopped at this point?","Which two digits carry the dots?"]},{"title":"A recurrence that starts late: 0.13̇","setup":"In the recurring decimal 0.13̇ only the 3 repeats, so the number is 0.13333… . Write it as a fraction in its lowest terms.","steps":["Let x = 0.13333…","Multiply by 10 to step past the digit that does not repeat: 10x = 1.3333…","Multiply by 10 once more to shift one block along: 100x = 13.333…","Subtract these two lines, not the original: 100x − 10x = 13.333… − 1.3333…, so 90x = 12","Divide and cancel using the common factor 6: x = 12/90 = 2/15"],"cues":["This is another decimal to be turned into a fraction — what is done with it before any multiplying?","What does multiplying by 10 do to the position of the repeating digit?","Why is a second multiplication needed here?","Which two lines have exactly the same endless tail?","What is the highest common factor of 12 and 90?"]},{"title":"Which of these decimals will recur?","setup":"Three fractions are given: 7/8, 9/40 and 7/12. Decide which of them give terminating decimals and which gives a recurring decimal, then write each one as a decimal.","steps":["Factorise each denominator: 8 = 2³, 40 = 2³ × 5 and 12 = 2² × 3","Only 2s and 5s appear in 8 and in 40, so those two terminate; 12 has a prime factor of 3, so that one recurs","Divide for the terminating ones: 7 ÷ 8 = 0.875 and 9 ÷ 40 = 0.225","Divide for the last one: 7 ÷ 12 = 0.58333…, where only the 3 repeats, so 7/12 = 0.583̇"],"cues":["What are the prime factors of each denominator?","Which prime factors allow a fraction to be scaled to a power of 10?","What single division turns the fraction into the decimal you need next?","Which fraction is still to be turned into a decimal, and what notation will show its repeat?"]},{"title":"A recurring decimal used as an operator","setup":"A rope is 55 m long. A piece measuring 0.7̇2̇ of the whole rope is cut off, where the digits 72 repeat for ever. Work out the exact length of the piece cut off, in metres.","steps":["Let x = 0.727272… and multiply by 100 to shift one block: 100x = 72.727272…","Subtract: 100x − x gives 99x = 72, so x = 72/99","Cancel using the common factor 9: x = 8/11, so the piece is 8/11 of the 55 m rope","Take that fraction of the length: 8/11 × 55 = 8 × 5 = 40 m"],"cues":["How long is the repeating block, and which multiplier does it need?","What whole number is left on the right after subtracting?","What is the highest common factor of 72 and 99?","What does 55 ÷ 11 come to?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A recurring decimal has a three-digit repeating block. Which power of 10 do you multiply by?","a":"1000"},{"q":"Write 0.8̇1̇ as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"9/11 (99x = 81, so x = 81/99 = 9/11)"},{"q":"Does 9/22 terminate or recur? Give the reason.","a":"It recurs, because 22 = 2 × 11 has the prime factor 11, which is neither 2 nor 5."},{"q":"Write 5/12 as a recurring decimal, using dot notation.","a":"0.416̇ (5 ÷ 12 = 0.41666…)"}]},"room":{"facts":["To change a recurring decimal into a fraction, let x be the decimal, multiply by 10ⁿ where n is the number of digits in the repeating block, subtract the original, then divide and cancel.","A block of one digit gives ninths, two digits gives ninety-ninths and three digits gives 999ths: 0.ȧ = a/9, 0.ȧḃ = ab/99, 0.ȧbċ = abc/999.","When the recurrence starts late, multiply by 10 first to step past the digits that do not repeat, then by 10 again for each digit of the block, and subtract those two multiples: for 0.16̇, 100x − 10x = 15, so x = 15/90 = 1/6.","A fraction in its lowest terms gives a terminating decimal exactly when the only prime factors of its denominator are 2 and 5; any other prime factor makes the decimal recur.","Dot notation: a dot goes on the first and the last digit of the repeating block, so 0.2̇7̇ = 0.272727… and 0.41̇6̇ = 0.416161616…","0.9̇ = 1 exactly, because 10x − x = 9 gives 9x = 9.","The repeating block of 1/n can never be longer than n − 1 digits, which is why sevenths repeat in blocks of six: 1/7 = 0.1̇42857̇."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Write 0.7̇ as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"7/9","sol":"Let x = 0.7777… . The block is one digit long, so multiply by 10: 10x = 7.7777… . M1 for 10x = 7.7777… (oe, for example 9x = 7). Subtracting the original clears the tail: 10x − x = 9x = 7, so x = 7/9, which will not cancel. A1 for 7/9 (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The decimal never stops, so it cannot be put straight over a power of 10 — give it a name first.","Multiply your named value by the power of 10 that shifts the repeat along by one block, then subtract the original.","The first line is: let x = 0.7777…, then write down 10x."],"codes":["N10 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write 0.2̇4̇ as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"8/33","sol":"Let x = 0.242424… . The block is two digits long, so multiply by 100: 100x = 24.242424… . M1 for 100x = 24.242424… (oe, for example 99x = 24). Subtracting: 100x − x = 99x = 24, so x = 24/99, and dividing the numerator and denominator by 3 gives 8/33. A1 for 8/33 (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Count how many digits are inside the repeating block before you choose a multiplier.","A two-digit block needs the multiplier that moves the decimal point two places, and then you subtract the original.","The first line is: let x = 0.242424…, then write down 100x."],"codes":["N10 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write 5/9 as a recurring decimal, using dot notation.","a":"0.5̇","sol":"5 ÷ 9 = 0.5555…, a single digit repeating for ever, so the dot goes on that one digit: 0.5̇. B1 for 0.5̇ (total 1 mark; 0.5555… without the dot scores 0).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A fraction becomes a decimal when the top is divided by the bottom.","Do the short division and look at the digit that keeps coming back.","The first line is 5 ÷ 9 — now write the repeat with the dot."],"codes":["N10 (ii)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write 3/11 as a recurring decimal, using dot notation.","a":"0.2̇7̇","sol":"Short division: 30 ÷ 11 = 2 remainder 8, then 80 ÷ 11 = 7 remainder 3, and the remainder 3 is the one we started with, so the digits repeat. M1 for 3 ÷ 11 = 0.272727… (oe, the division seen). The block is 27, so a dot goes on the 2 and on the 7: 0.2̇7̇. A1 for 0.2̇7̇ (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Divide the numerator by the denominator and keep track of the remainders.","When a remainder you have seen before comes back, everything after it repeats.","The first line is 3 ÷ 11 by short division — now mark the block with dots."],"codes":["N10 (ii)"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Explain why 5/14 gives a recurring decimal but 5/16 does not.","a":"14 = 2 × 7 has the prime factor 7, while 16 = 2⁴ has only 2s","sol":"5/14 and 5/16 are both in their lowest terms, so the denominators decide. 14 = 2 × 7, and the prime factor 7 is neither 2 nor 5, so 5/14 cannot be written over a power of 10 and its decimal recurs (5 ÷ 14 = 0.3571428571…). B1 for 14 = 2 × 7 with the prime factor 7 identified as the reason. 16 = 2⁴, whose only prime factor is 2, so 5/16 can be scaled to a power of 10 and terminates (5 ÷ 16 = 0.3125). B1 for 16 = 2⁴ having only 2 as a prime factor. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Whether a decimal stops or not is decided by the denominator, not by the numerator.","Break each denominator into prime factors and check them against the only two primes that divide a power of 10.","The first line is 14 = … and 16 = … as products of primes — then compare each with 2 and 5."],"codes":["N10 (ii)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) In the decimal 0.83̇ only the 3 repeats. Write 0.83̇ as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"5/6","sol":"Let x = 0.8333… . The 8 does not repeat, so step past it first: 10x = 8.333…, and one more multiplication by 10 shifts the block: 100x = 83.333… . M1 for both 10x = 8.333… and 100x = 83.333… . Subtracting those two lines: 100x − 10x = 90x = 75. M1 for 90x = 75. So x = 75/90, and dividing the numerator and denominator by 15 gives 5/6. A1 for 5/6 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The first digit after the point does not repeat, so subtracting the original number will not clear the tail.","Move past the non-repeating digit with one multiplication, then shift a whole block with another, and subtract those two.","The first line is: let x = 0.8333…, then write down 10x and 100x."],"codes":["N10 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write 0.31̇8̇, in which the block 18 repeats after the 3, as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"7/22","sol":"Let x = 0.3181818… . One digit sits before the repeat, so 10x = 3.181818…, and the block is two digits long, so shift it with a further 100: 1000x = 318.181818… . M1 for both 10x = 3.181818… and 1000x = 318.181818… . Subtracting: 1000x − 10x = 990x = 315. M1 for 990x = 315. So x = 315/990, and dividing the numerator and denominator by 45 gives 7/22. A1 for 7/22 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["One digit here does not belong to the repeat, and the block itself is two digits long.","Use one multiple of the number that starts at the repeat and another that is one whole block further on, then subtract them.","The first line is: let x = 0.3181818…, then write down 10x and 1000x."],"codes":["N10 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Show that 0.9̇ = 1.","a":"9x = 9, so x = 1","sol":"Let x = 0.9999… . The block is one digit, so 10x = 9.9999… . M1 for 10x = 9.9999… and the subtraction 10x − x. The endless tails are identical, so they cancel: 10x − x = 9.9999… − 0.9999… gives 9x = 9. Dividing by 9, x = 1, so 0.9̇ = 1 exactly. A1 for a complete argument ending in x = 1 (total 2 marks). The result must be reached by working, since the answer is printed in the question.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Treat it like any other recurring decimal and give it a name, even though the answer is already printed.","Multiply by 10 and subtract the original, then read off what is left on each side.","The first line is: let x = 0.9999…, then write down 10x."],"codes":["N10 (i)"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Write 4/7 as a recurring decimal, using dot notation.","a":"0.5̇71428̇","sol":"4 ÷ 7 fills the display as 0.5714285714…, and the six digits 571428 then start again. M1 for 4 ÷ 7 = 0.571428571… (oe, the repeating block 571428 identified). A dot goes on the first and the last digit of that block: 0.5̇71428̇. A1 for 0.5̇71428̇ (total 2 marks). Here the digits start over well before the display runs out, so the whole block can be read off; in general a display rounds the last digit it has room for, so the block must be identified from where the digits begin repeating and never from the final digit on screen.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The calculator display is rounded, so it shows the pattern but not the whole truth.","Look along the display for the point where the digits start over, and count how many digits that block holds.","The first line is 4 ÷ 7 = 0.5714285714… — now decide where the block begins and ends."],"codes":["N10 (ii)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write 0.02̇7̇, in which the block 27 repeats after the 0, as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"3/110","sol":"Let x = 0.0272727… . One digit (the 0) sits before the repeat, so 10x = 0.272727…, and the two-digit block is shifted by a further 100: 1000x = 27.272727… . M1 for both 10x = 0.272727… and 1000x = 27.272727… . Subtracting: 1000x − 10x = 990x = 27. M1 for 990x = 27. So x = 27/990, and dividing the numerator and denominator by 9 gives 3/110. A1 for 3/110 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The zero straight after the point is not part of the repeat.","Shift past that zero with one multiplication and shift a whole block with another, then subtract the two.","The first line is: let x = 0.0272727…, then write down 10x and 1000x."],"codes":["N10 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Put these four numbers in order, smallest first, writing each one in the form it was given in: 0.3̇2̇, 8/25, 0.325, 1/3.","a":"8/25, 0.3̇2̇, 0.325, 1/3","sol":"Change everything into decimals with enough places to separate them. 8/25 = 32/100 = 0.32 and 1/3 = 0.3333… . M1 for 8/25 = 0.32 and 1/3 = 0.3333… . The recurring decimal written out is 0.3̇2̇ = 0.323232… . M1 for 0.323232… . Comparing 0.32 < 0.323232… < 0.325 < 0.3333… and writing the numbers back in their original forms gives 8/25, 0.3̇2̇, 0.325, 1/3. A1 for the correct order in the forms given (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Numbers in three different forms cannot be compared until they are all in one form.","Write every number as a decimal, keeping enough places to tell the close ones apart, then compare place by place.","The first line is 8/25 = … , 1/3 = … , 0.3̇2̇ = … as decimals — then compare the thousandths."],"codes":["N10 (i)","N10 (ii)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 0.4̇ + 0.3̇1̇, giving your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"25/33","sol":"For the first: let x = 0.4444…, so 10x − x = 9x = 4 and 0.4̇ = 4/9. M1 for 4/9. For the second: let y = 0.313131…, so 100y − y = 99y = 31 and 0.3̇1̇ = 31/99. M1 for 31/99. A common denominator of 99 gives 4/9 = 44/99, so the sum is 44/99 + 31/99 = 75/99. M1 for 44/99 + 31/99 (oe). Dividing the numerator and denominator by 3 gives 25/33. A1 for 25/33 (total 4 marks).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Recurring decimals cannot be added reliably in decimal form — the tails go on for ever.","Turn each decimal into a fraction by its own multiply-and-subtract, then add the fractions in the usual way.","The first line is: let x = 0.4444… and let y = 0.313131…, then write down 10x and 100y."],"codes":["N10 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Show that 0.5̇4̇ ÷ 0.1̇8̇ = 3.","a":"6/11 ÷ 2/11 = 3","sol":"First decimal: let x = 0.545454…, so 100x − x = 99x = 54 and x = 54/99 = 6/11. M1 for 54/99 or 6/11. Second decimal: let y = 0.181818…, so 100y − y = 99y = 18 and y = 18/99 = 2/11. M1 for 18/99 or 2/11. Dividing means multiplying by the reciprocal: 6/11 ÷ 2/11 = 6/11 × 11/2. M1 for the inversion. This equals 66/22 = 3, as required. A1 for a complete argument reaching 3 (total 4 marks). Since the answer is printed, every line must be shown.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Division of two endless decimals is only safe once both are exact fractions.","Convert each decimal separately, then divide the fractions by multiplying by the reciprocal of the second.","The first line is: let x = 0.545454… and let y = 0.181818…, then write down 100x and 100y."],"codes":["N10 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) In a box, the number of red counters and the number of blue counters are in the ratio 0.3̇ : 0.5̇. Write this ratio in its simplest form, using whole numbers.","a":"3 : 5","sol":"Each part is converted first: 9x = 3 gives 0.3̇ = 3/9 = 1/3, and 9y = 5 gives 0.5̇ = 5/9. M1 for 1/3 and 5/9 (oe). Written over a common denominator of 9 the ratio is 3/9 : 5/9. M1 for 3/9 : 5/9 (oe, for example multiplying both parts of the ratio by 9). Multiplying both parts by 9 clears the fractions and leaves 3 : 5, which has no common factor. A1 for 3 : 5 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["A ratio is only simplified once both parts are whole numbers, so deal with the decimals first.","Change each recurring decimal into a fraction, put both over the same denominator, then scale the ratio up until both parts are whole.","The first line is: let x = 0.3333… and let y = 0.5555…, then write down 10x and 10y."],"codes":["N10 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write 0.1̇23̇, in which the block 123 repeats, as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"41/333","sol":"Let x = 0.123123123… . The block is three digits long, so multiply by 1000: 1000x = 123.123123… . M1 for 1000x = 123.123123… . Subtracting the original: 1000x − x = 999x = 123. M1 for 999x = 123. So x = 123/999, and dividing the numerator and denominator by 3 gives 41/333. A1 for 41/333 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The rule about block length still holds when the block is longer than two digits.","Choose the power of 10 that carries the decimal point past exactly one whole block, then subtract the original.","The first line is: let x = 0.123123123…, then write down 1000x."],"codes":["N10 (i)"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Water runs from a tap at a steady 0.6̇ litres per second into an empty 42 litre tank. Work out how long the tank takes to fill, giving your answer in minutes and seconds.","a":"1 minute 3 seconds","sol":"The rate is exact as a fraction: let x = 0.6666…, so 10x − x = 9x = 6 and the rate is 6/9 = 2/3 litres per second. M1 for 2/3 (oe). Time = volume ÷ rate = 42 ÷ 2/3 = 42 × 3/2. M1 for 42 ÷ 2/3 or 42 × 3/2. That gives 126/2 = 63 seconds. A1 for 63 seconds. Since 60 seconds make a minute, 63 seconds = 1 minute 3 seconds. B1 for 1 minute 3 seconds (total 4 marks). Rounding the rate to 0.67 would give 62.7 seconds and lose the accuracy mark, which is why the exact fraction is used.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A rounded version of the rate will not give an exact time, so make the rate exact first.","Turn the rate into a fraction, then divide the volume by it by multiplying by its reciprocal, and convert the result into minutes and seconds at the end.","The first line is: let x = 0.6666…, then write down 10x before dividing 42 by the rate."],"codes":["N10 (i)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 0.8̇ as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"8/9"},{"q":"Write 0.2̇5̇ as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"25/99"},{"q":"Write 4/33 as a recurring decimal, using dot notation.","a":"0.1̇2̇"},{"q":"Which of 4/25 and 4/21 gives a recurring decimal?","a":"4/21, because 21 = 3 × 7 has prime factors other than 2 and 5"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Recurring decimal","back":"A decimal that never stops, in which a block of one or more digits repeats for ever, such as 0.272727… . Every recurring decimal is equal to an exact fraction.","code":"N10 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Dot notation for a repeating block","back":"A dot sits on the first and the last digit of the block: 0.2̇7̇ = 0.272727…, 0.4̇ = 0.4444… and 0.41̇6̇ = 0.4161616…","code":"N10 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Method for changing a recurring decimal into a fraction","back":"Let x be the decimal, multiply by 10ⁿ where n is the number of digits in the block, subtract the original to clear the tail, divide, then cancel.","code":"N10 (i)","open":false},{"front":"The reason subtracting clears the recurrence","back":"Multiplying by 10ⁿ shifts the decimal along by exactly one block, so both lines have identical endless tails and subtracting removes the recurrence completely.","code":"N10 (i)","open":false},{"front":"The fraction for a one-digit repeating block","back":"0.ȧ = a/9, so 0.7̇ = 7/9 and 0.3̇ = 3/9 = 1/3.","code":"N10 (i)","open":false},{"front":"The fraction for a two-digit repeating block","back":"0.ȧḃ = ab/99, so 0.2̇4̇ = 24/99 = 8/33. For a three-digit block the denominator is 999.","code":"N10 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Converting a decimal whose repeat starts late","back":"Multiply by 10 for each digit before the block, then again by 10ⁿ for the block, and subtract those two multiples: for 0.16̇, 100x − 10x = 15, so x = 1/6.","code":"N10 (i)","open":false},{"front":"The value of 0.9̇","back":"Exactly 1. Let x = 0.9999… ; then 10x − x = 9, so 9x = 9 and x = 1.","code":"N10 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Changing a fraction into a decimal","back":"Divide the numerator by the denominator by short division, watching the remainders; the digits repeat from the point where a remainder comes back.","code":"N10 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Test for a terminating decimal","back":"In lowest terms, a fraction terminates exactly when the only prime factors of its denominator are 2 and 5, because only then can it be scaled to a power of 10.","code":"N10 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Test for a recurring decimal","back":"In lowest terms, any prime factor of the denominator other than 2 or 5 — such as 3, 7 or 11 — makes the decimal recur. So 7/12 recurs and 7/8 does not.","code":"N10 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Longest possible repeating block of 1/n","back":"At most n − 1 digits, since only n − 1 different non-zero remainders are available. Sevenths take the full six digits: 1/7 = 0.1̇42857̇.","code":"N10 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Reading a recurring decimal from a calculator display","back":"The display is rounded at the last digit, so identify the repeating block from the digits before it and write the dots yourself rather than copying the final digit.","code":"N10 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Ordering numbers that include a recurring decimal","back":"Write every number as a decimal with enough places to separate them, compare place by place, then give the answer in the forms the question used.","code":"N10 (ii)","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Recurring decimal to fraction: let x be the decimal, multiply by 10ⁿ where n is the number of digits in the repeating block, subtract the original, divide and cancel — for 0.4̇5̇, 99x = 45 so x = 5/11. Recall: this is a method, not a printed formula; nothing is given for it.","One-, two- and three-digit blocks: 0.ȧ = a/9, 0.ȧḃ = ab/99, 0.ȧbċ = abc/999. Recall: not in the specification's Appendix and not on the Higher formulae sheet.","A repeat that starts late: multiply by 10 once for each digit before the block, then by 10ⁿ for the block, and subtract those two multiples — for 0.16̇, 100x − 10x = 15 gives x = 1/6. Recall.","0.9̇ = 1. Recall: a result you derive from 10x − x = 9, not a formula anyone prints for you.","Fraction to decimal: numerator ÷ denominator by short division; the digits repeat from the point where a remainder returns. Recall.","Terminating or recurring: in lowest terms, the decimal terminates exactly when the denominator's only prime factors are 2 and 5; any other prime factor makes it recur. Recall.","Recurring and terminating decimals are exactly the rational numbers: every fraction gives a decimal that terminates or recurs, and every terminating or recurring decimal can be written as a fraction. Recall: the Higher tier formulae sheet (AQA's insert for exams from 2026) prints no result belonging to this room, and the specification's Appendix lists none either, so every item on this sheet must be recalled or derived on the day.","Dot notation: one dot marks a single repeating digit, as 0.83̇ = 0.8333…, and a pair of dots marks the first and the last digit of a repeating block, as 0.1̇23̇ = 0.123123… . Recall: notation is never printed on the formulae sheet.","Arithmetic with recurring decimals: change each decimal to a fraction first, then add, subtract, multiply or divide the fractions — 0.2̇ + 0.1̇5̇ = 2/9 + 15/99 = 22/99 + 15/99 = 37/99. Recall: neither the Appendix nor the Higher tier sheet gives it.","Block length: dividing by n can leave only the remainders 1 to n − 1, so the repeating part of 1/n is never longer than n − 1 digits — 1/7 = 0.1̇42857̇ reaches that limit with six. Recall: not in the Appendix and not on the Higher tier sheet."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:27:57Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Every answer key and solution line is sound and the Formulae sheet's claims match section 4 of appendix.md exactly for the Higher insert, so this is publishable once the cue and difficulty-tier nits are tidied."}}},"number-measures-higher":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"number-measures-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Higher papers hardly ever hand you a measurement that is exact. A length read to the nearest centimetre, a mass to the nearest gram, a time to 1 decimal place — each is a whole band of possible values, and this room is where you learn to name that band and carry it through a calculation. Examiners set these questions because they separate a student who can round from one who understands what a rounded number really claims: 'find the upper bound of the area', 'is he certain to finish in time?', 'give your answer to a suitable degree of accuracy'. The Foundation room taught you to write an error interval; here you combine intervals through sums, differences, products and quotients, and use them to decide what a calculated answer can honestly be reported as.","idea":[{"h":"Upper and lower bounds of a rounded measurement","t":"The specification's Higher column adds four words to N16 — 'including upper and lower bounds'. A measurement given as v correct to the nearest u could have been anything within half a unit of v, so its lower bound is v − u/2 and its upper bound is v + u/2, and the error interval is v − u/2 ≤ x < v + u/2. Read the accuracy first and halve it: the nearest 10 gives 5, the nearest whole number gives 0.5, 1 decimal place and the nearest 0.1 both give 0.05, 2 decimal places give 0.005, the nearest 5 gives 2.5. The lower bound is itself a possible value, because a value on that boundary rounds up to v; the upper bound is not, because a value equal to it rounds up to the next measurement instead — that is why the interval is written with ≤ at the bottom and < at the top. Truncation is not part of this room: it belongs to the Foundation room's additional foundation content and is assumed here only as prior knowledge, outside the two codes this room carries. Marks here are B marks: the bound is written down, no method needed, and it carries the unit.","code":"N16 (i)"},{"h":"Carrying bounds through a calculation","t":"When a quantity is worked out from measurements, its own bounds come from choosing, for each measurement, the end of its interval that pushes the answer the way you want. Adding is direct: UB(a + b) = UB(a) + UB(b) and LB(a + b) = LB(a) + LB(b). Subtracting reverses the second quantity, because taking less away leaves more: UB(a − b) = UB(a) − LB(b) and LB(a − b) = LB(a) − UB(b). For positive quantities, multiplying is direct — UB(ab) = UB(a) × UB(b), LB(ab) = LB(a) × LB(b) — while dividing reverses the divisor, because dividing by a smaller number gives a bigger answer: UB(a ÷ b) = UB(a) ÷ LB(b) and LB(a ÷ b) = LB(a) ÷ UB(b). Speed, density and pressure are the usual settings, so the largest possible speed is the largest distance over the smallest time. Two AO3 uses follow. To give a calculated value to a suitable degree of accuracy, work out both bounds and round each of them to the same accuracy; the answer is their common rounded value at the greatest accuracy — the most significant figures, or decimal places — at which the two rounded bounds still agree. To decide whether something is certain — will it fit, will it hold — test the single bound that matters against the limit given, and say what that bound shows.","code":"N16 (ii)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Bounds and the error interval of a rounded mass","setup":"A bag of flour is labelled 1.5 kg, correct to the nearest 0.1 kg. Write down the lower bound and the upper bound of the mass m kg, and write the error interval for m.","steps":["Half the rounding unit is 0.1 ÷ 2 = 0.05, so the true mass lies within 0.05 kg of the label.","Lower bound = 1.5 − 0.05 = 1.45 kg.","Upper bound = 1.5 + 0.05 = 1.55 kg.","Error interval: 1.45 ≤ m < 1.55, because a mass of exactly 1.55 kg would have been labelled 1.6 kg."],"cues":["What is the rounding unit here, and what is half of it?","Which end of the band do you reach by going down?","Which end do you reach by going up?","Which of the two bounds could the mass actually equal?"]},{"title":"A gap: adding and subtracting bounds","setup":"A shelf is 180 cm long, correct to the nearest centimetre. Two files stand side by side on the shelf, and each file is 58.4 cm wide, correct to the nearest 0.1 cm. Work out the upper bound of the gap left on the shelf.","steps":["Gap = shelf − 2 × file width, so the gap is greatest when the shelf is longest and the files are narrowest.","Greatest shelf length = 180 + 0.5 = 180.5 cm.","Narrowest file = 58.4 − 0.05 = 58.35 cm, so the two files take up at least 2 × 58.35 = 116.7 cm.","Upper bound of the gap = 180.5 − 116.7 = 63.8 cm."],"cues":["For the biggest gap, should each measurement be at its largest or its smallest?","Which bound of the shelf length does that choice pick?","Which bound of a file width does it pick, and what do two of them come to?","What single subtraction now finishes the question?"]},{"title":"Bounds of a volume","setup":"A box is a cuboid measuring 12 cm by 8 cm by 5 cm, each measurement correct to the nearest centimetre. Work out the upper bound and the lower bound of the volume of the box.","steps":["Half of 1 cm is 0.5 cm, so 11.5 ≤ length < 12.5, 7.5 ≤ width < 8.5 and 4.5 ≤ height < 5.5.","A product of positive lengths is greatest when every factor is greatest, so the upper bound is 12.5 × 8.5 × 5.5.","12.5 × 8.5 × 5.5 = 584.375 cm³.","Taking every factor at its smallest: 11.5 × 7.5 × 4.5 = 388.125 cm³."],"cues":["How far either side of each stated length can the true length be?","To make a product as big as possible, which end of each interval do you take?","What does that product come to?","What changes if you want the smallest possible volume instead?"]},{"title":"Bounds of a speed","setup":"A cable car travels 720 m, correct to the nearest 10 m, in 96 s, correct to the nearest second. Average speed = distance ÷ time. Work out the upper bound and the lower bound of the average speed, each correct to 3 significant figures.","steps":["Half of 10 is 5 and half of 1 is 0.5, so 715 ≤ d < 725 and 95.5 ≤ t < 96.5.","A quotient is greatest when the top is greatest and the bottom is least, so the upper bound is 725 ÷ 95.5.","725 ÷ 95.5 = 7.5916… = 7.59 m/s (3 s.f.).","Least distance over greatest time: 715 ÷ 96.5 = 7.4093… = 7.41 m/s (3 s.f.)."],"cues":["What are the two error intervals?","For the fastest possible speed, which distance and which time do you pair?","What does that division give, to 3 significant figures?","Which pairing gives the slowest possible speed?"]},{"title":"Choosing a suitable degree of accuracy","setup":"A metal block has mass 68.42 g, correct to 2 decimal places, and volume 8.63 cm³, correct to 2 decimal places. Density = mass ÷ volume. Work out the density of the metal to a suitable degree of accuracy, and justify the accuracy you have given it to.","steps":["Half of 0.01 is 0.005, so 68.415 ≤ mass < 68.425 and 8.625 ≤ volume < 8.635.","Upper bound of the density = 68.425 ÷ 8.625 = 7.93333… g/cm³.","Lower bound of the density = 68.415 ÷ 8.635 = 7.92298… g/cm³.","Rounded to 2 significant figures both bounds give 7.9; rounded to 3 they give 7.93 and 7.92, so the density is 7.9 g/cm³ (2 s.f.)."],"cues":["What error interval does each measurement have?","Which pairing of bounds makes the density as large as it can be?","Which pairing makes it as small as it can be?","Round both bounds to the same accuracy — what is the greatest accuracy at which they still agree?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A length is 7.5 cm, correct to the nearest 0.5 cm. Write down its upper bound.","a":"7.75 cm"},{"q":"a = 12 and b = 5, each correct to the nearest whole number. Work out the upper bound of a − b.","a":"8 (from 12.5 − 4.5)"},{"q":"a = 12 and b = 5, each correct to the nearest whole number. Work out the lower bound of a × b.","a":"51.75 (from 11.5 × 4.5)"},{"q":"Why can a measurement never equal its upper bound?","a":"A value equal to the upper bound sits on the boundary and rounds up to the next measurement, so it belongs to the next interval; the top of the interval is strict, x < upper bound."}]},"room":{"facts":["A measurement given as v correct to the nearest u lies in v − u/2 ≤ x < v + u/2: lower bound v − u/2, upper bound v + u/2. The lower bound is a possible value; the upper bound is not.","Half-units to know: nearest 10 → 5; nearest whole number → 0.5; nearest 0.1 or 1 decimal place → 0.05; 2 decimal places → 0.005; nearest 5 → 2.5.","Sums and differences: UB(a + b) = UB(a) + UB(b), LB(a + b) = LB(a) + LB(b), UB(a − b) = UB(a) − LB(b), LB(a − b) = LB(a) − UB(b).","Products and quotients of positive quantities: UB(ab) = UB(a) × UB(b), LB(ab) = LB(a) × LB(b), UB(a ÷ b) = UB(a) ÷ LB(b), LB(a ÷ b) = LB(a) ÷ UB(b).","To give a calculated value to a suitable degree of accuracy, work out both bounds and round each to the same accuracy; the answer is their common rounded value at the greatest accuracy at which the two still agree, and you name that accuracy.","To decide whether a result is certain, test the one bound that matters against the limit given: the upper bound against a maximum that must not be passed, the lower bound against a minimum that must be reached, and say what that comparison shows."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) The mass of a parcel is 8 kg, correct to the nearest kilogram. Write down the lower bound and the upper bound of the mass.","a":"Lower bound 7.5 kg, upper bound 8.5 kg","sol":"The rounding unit is 1 kg, so the mass is within 0.5 kg of 8 kg. Lower bound = 8 − 0.5 = 7.5 kg — B1. Upper bound = 8 + 0.5 = 8.5 kg — B1.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["'To the nearest kilogram' means the real mass is somewhere in a band around 8 kg, not exactly 8 kg.","The band stretches half a kilogram each side of the stated value.","Write down the stated mass minus half the rounding unit, and the stated mass plus half the rounding unit."],"codes":["N16 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) a = 24 and b = 4, each correct to the nearest whole number. Work out the upper bound of a ÷ b.","a":"7","sol":"Each value is within 0.5 of its stated value, so UB(a) = 24.5 and LB(b) = 3.5 — B1. A quotient is greatest when the number divided is greatest and the divisor is least: UB(a ÷ b) = 24.5 ÷ 3.5 = 7 — B1.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Each of a and b stands for a whole band of possible values, not one exact number.","Dividing by a smaller number gives a bigger answer, so the two bounds you need come from opposite ends of the two bands.","Write down the upper bound of a divided by the lower bound of b."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A distance is 250 m, correct to the nearest 10 m. Write down the upper bound of the distance.","a":"255 m","sol":"Half of the rounding unit 10 m is 5 m, so the upper bound is 250 + 5 = 255 m — B1.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The accuracy here is coarser than a metre, so the band is wider than usual.","Halve the rounding unit before you go anywhere.","Add half of the rounding unit to the stated distance."],"codes":["N16 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) a = 6.3 and b = 2.1, each correct to 1 decimal place. Work out the upper bound of a + b.","a":"8.5","sol":"Each value is within 0.05 of its stated value, so UB(a) = 6.35 and UB(b) = 2.15 — B1. A sum is greatest when both parts are greatest: UB(a + b) = 6.35 + 2.15 = 8.5 — B1.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Each of a and b has its own band of possible values.","A sum is as large as it can be when both numbers being added are as large as they can be.","Write down the upper bound of a and the upper bound of b, then add them."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"A square has sides of length 7.2 cm, correct to the nearest 0.1 cm. Work out the upper bound of the perimeter of the square. (A calculator may be used.)","a":"29 cm","sol":"Half of 0.1 cm is 0.05 cm, so the greatest possible side is 7.2 + 0.05 = 7.25 cm. Perimeter = 4 × side, so upper bound = 4 × 7.25 — M1. = 29 cm — A1.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The perimeter is largest when the side is at the largest value it could possibly be.","Find the upper bound of one side first, then use the perimeter of a square.","Write down 4 × (the upper bound of the side)."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"A rectangle measures 12.4 cm by 7.6 cm, each correct to 1 decimal place. Work out the upper bound and the lower bound of the area of the rectangle. (A calculator may be used.)","a":"Upper bound 95.2425 cm², lower bound 93.2425 cm²","sol":"Each side is within 0.05 cm of its stated length: 12.35 ≤ length < 12.45 and 7.55 ≤ width < 7.65 — B1. Area is greatest when both sides are greatest: UB = 12.45 × 7.65 — M1. UB = 95.2425 cm² and LB = 12.35 × 7.55 = 93.2425 cm² — A1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Both measurements have a band, and the area has a band because of them.","A product of two positive lengths is greatest when both lengths are greatest, and least when both are least.","Write the two error intervals down, then form the product of the two upper bounds."],"codes":["N16 (i)","N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"A car travels 512 km, correct to the nearest kilometre, on 38.4 litres of fuel, correct to 1 decimal place. Work out the lower bound of the distance travelled per litre. Give your answer correct to 3 significant figures. (A calculator may be used.)","a":"13.3 km per litre","sol":"Distance: 511.5 ≤ d < 512.5. Fuel: 38.35 ≤ f < 38.45. Distance per litre = d ÷ f is least when the distance is least and the fuel is greatest, so use 511.5 and 38.45 — B1. 511.5 ÷ 38.45 — M1. = 13.3029… = 13.3 km per litre (3 s.f.) — A1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Distance per litre is a division, so the two measurements do not both go the same way.","Dividing a smaller number by a bigger number gives the smallest possible result.","Write down the lower bound of the distance divided by the upper bound of the fuel."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"A metal rod has mass 4.6 kg, correct to 1 decimal place. A piece of mass 1.85 kg, correct to 2 decimal places, is cut from the rod. Work out the lower bound of the mass of the rod that is left. (A calculator may be used.)","a":"2.695 kg","sol":"Rod: 4.55 ≤ m < 4.65. Piece cut off: 1.845 ≤ p < 1.855. What is left is least when the rod is lightest and the piece removed is heaviest, so use 4.55 and 1.855 — B1. 4.55 − 1.855 — M1. = 2.695 kg — A1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Subtraction is the one where the two bounds you need come from opposite ends.","The least left over comes from starting with the least and taking away the most.","Write down the lower bound of the rod's mass minus the upper bound of the piece cut off."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"A force of 84 N, correct to the nearest newton, acts on an area of 12.5 cm², correct to 1 decimal place. Pressure = force ÷ area. Work out the lower bound of the pressure, correct to 3 significant figures. (A calculator may be used.)","a":"6.65 N/cm²","sol":"Force: 83.5 ≤ F < 84.5. Area: 12.45 ≤ A < 12.55. Pressure is least when the force is least and the area is greatest, so use 83.5 and 12.55 — B1. 83.5 ÷ 12.55 — M1. = 6.6533… = 6.65 N/cm² (3 s.f.) — A1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Spreading the same force over a bigger area makes the pressure smaller.","For the smallest quotient, make the top as small as possible and the bottom as large as possible.","Write down the lower bound of the force divided by the upper bound of the area."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A square has sides of length 0.8 m, correct to 1 decimal place. Work out the upper bound and the lower bound of the area of the square.","a":"Upper bound 0.7225 m², lower bound 0.5625 m²","sol":"Half of 0.1 m is 0.05 m, so 0.75 ≤ side < 0.85 — B1. Area = side², greatest when the side is greatest: 0.85 × 0.85 — M1. UB = 0.7225 m² and LB = 0.75 × 0.75 = 0.5625 m² — A1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Only one measurement is given, but it is used twice in the area.","The area of a square is the side multiplied by itself, so the largest side gives the largest area.","Write the error interval for the side, then square its upper bound."],"codes":["N16 (i)","N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"A rectangular field measures 84 m by 56 m, each correct to the nearest metre. Fencing costs £12.50 for each metre. Work out the upper bound of the cost of fencing all the way round the field. (A calculator may be used.)","a":"£3525","sol":"Each side is within 0.5 m: UB(length) = 84.5 m and UB(width) = 56.5 m. Perimeter is greatest when both are greatest: 2 × (84.5 + 56.5) — M1. = 282 m. Cost = 282 × 12.50 — M1. = £3525 — A1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The price per metre is exact; only the two measurements of the field carry a band.","Work out the greatest possible perimeter first, then charge for it.","Write down 2 × (upper bound of the length + upper bound of the width)."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"Priya runs 1500 m, correct to the nearest 10 m, in 320 seconds, correct to the nearest second. (a) Work out the upper bound and the lower bound of her average speed in m/s. (b) Write down her average speed to a suitable degree of accuracy, and explain why that accuracy is suitable. (A calculator may be used.)","a":"(a) Upper bound 4.7104… m/s, lower bound 4.6645… m/s (b) 4.7 m/s, because both bounds round to 4.7 to 2 significant figures, while to 3 significant figures they round to 4.71 and 4.66","sol":"Distance: half of 10 is 5, so 1495 ≤ d < 1505 — B1. Time: half of 1 is 0.5, so 319.5 ≤ t < 320.5 — B1. Speed = d ÷ t, so UB = 1505 ÷ 319.5 and LB = 1495 ÷ 320.5 — M1. UB = 4.71048… m/s and LB = 4.66458… m/s — A1. Rounded to 2 significant figures both bounds give 4.7; rounded to 3 significant figures they give 4.71 and 4.66, so 2 significant figures is the greatest accuracy at which they agree and the speed is 4.7 m/s — A1.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The two measurements have very different rounding units, so find each band separately.","Pair the greatest distance with the least time for the fastest speed, and the other way round for the slowest.","Write down the upper bound of the distance divided by the lower bound of the time, then round both bounds to the same accuracy and see how far you can go before they stop agreeing."],"codes":["N16 (i)","N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"A sign in a lift gives the maximum load as 290 kg. Six boxes are put into the lift. Each box has mass 48 kg, correct to the nearest kilogram. Can you be certain that the six boxes do not go over the maximum load? Show working to support your answer. (A calculator may be used.)","a":"No — the upper bound of the total mass is 291 kg, which is more than 290 kg","sol":"Each box is within 0.5 kg of 48 kg, so the heaviest a box can be is 48 + 0.5 = 48.5 kg — M1. The heaviest the six can be together is 6 × 48.5 — M1. = 291 kg. Since 291 kg > 290 kg, you cannot be certain: the boxes could go over the maximum load — A1.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["'Certain' means it must be safe even in the worst case that the rounding allows.","The worst case here is every box being as heavy as it possibly could be.","Write down the upper bound of one box's mass, then multiply it by the number of boxes and compare with the limit."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"A trapezium has parallel sides of length 8.6 cm and 5.4 cm, and a perpendicular height of 4.2 cm. Each measurement is correct to 1 decimal place. Work out the lower bound of the area of the trapezium. (A calculator may be used.)","a":"28.8425 cm²","sol":"Each measurement is within 0.05 cm. Lower bounds of the parallel sides: 8.55 cm and 5.35 cm — B1. Lower bound of the height: 4.15 cm — B1. Area = ½(a + b)h, and every factor is positive, so the least area uses all three lower bounds: ½ × (8.55 + 5.35) × 4.15 — M1. = ½ × 13.9 × 4.15 = 28.8425 cm² — A1.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The area formula multiplies the measurements together, so making each one smaller makes the area smaller.","Find the lower bound of each of the three measurements before you substitute anything.","Write ½ × (lower bound of a + lower bound of b) × lower bound of h."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]},{"q":"A solid cylinder has radius 3.5 cm, correct to the nearest 0.1 cm, and height 12 cm, correct to the nearest centimetre. It is made of metal of density 7.8 g/cm³, correct to 1 decimal place. Work out the upper bound of the mass of the cylinder, correct to the nearest gram. (A calculator may be used.)","a":"3885 g","sol":"Radius: 3.45 ≤ r < 3.55. Height: 11.5 ≤ h < 12.5. Upper bounds 3.55 cm and 12.5 cm — B1. Density: 7.75 ≤ d < 7.85, upper bound 7.85 g/cm³ — B1. Volume of a cylinder = πr²h, greatest when r and h are greatest: π × 3.55² × 12.5 — M1. = 494.899… cm³. Mass = volume × density, greatest when both are greatest: 494.899… × 7.85 — M1. = 3884.96 = 3885 g to the nearest gram — A1.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Three measurements carry a band here, and all three push the mass the same way.","Work out the greatest possible volume first, then the greatest possible mass from it.","Write down π × (upper bound of the radius)² × (upper bound of the height), keeping the full figure on your calculator."],"codes":["N16 (ii)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"A mass is 640 g, correct to the nearest 10 g. Write down its lower bound.","a":"635 g"},{"q":"p = 9.4, correct to 1 decimal place. Write down the error interval for p.","a":"9.35 ≤ p < 9.45"},{"q":"x = 20 and y = 8, each correct to the nearest whole number. Work out the upper bound of x − y.","a":"13 (from 20.5 − 7.5)"},{"q":"Which two bounds do you divide to get the lower bound of a ÷ b, where a and b are positive?","a":"The lower bound of a divided by the upper bound of b"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Lower bound of a rounded measurement","back":"The smallest value the measurement could have been: half the rounding unit below the stated value. A measurement can equal its lower bound.","code":"N16 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Upper bound of a rounded measurement","back":"Half the rounding unit above the stated value. The measurement is always strictly less than it, because a value equal to it would round up to the next measurement.","code":"N16 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Error interval for a value v given correct to the nearest u","back":"v − u/2 ≤ x < v + u/2, with ≤ at the lower bound and < at the upper bound.","code":"N16 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Bounds of a measurement multiplied by an exact number","back":"For a positive exact n, UB(na) = n × UB(a) and LB(na) = n × LB(a). The multiplier is not measured, so it carries no band of its own and both bounds simply scale.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Half-unit for a measurement given to 2 decimal places","back":"0.005, so 3.26 to 2 d.p. means 3.255 ≤ x < 3.265.","code":"N16 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Half-unit for a measurement given to the nearest 10","back":"5, so 250 to the nearest 10 means 245 ≤ x < 255.","code":"N16 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Why a measurement never equals its upper bound","back":"A value on that boundary rounds up, so it belongs to the next interval. The top of an error interval is always strict: x < upper bound.","code":"N16 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Bounds of a + b","back":"UB(a + b) = UB(a) + UB(b); LB(a + b) = LB(a) + LB(b). Both parts go the same way as the answer.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Bounds of a − b","back":"UB(a − b) = UB(a) − LB(b); LB(a − b) = LB(a) − UB(b). Taking away less leaves more, so the second quantity reverses.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Bounds of a × b, where a and b are positive","back":"UB(a × b) = UB(a) × UB(b); LB(a × b) = LB(a) × LB(b).","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Bounds of a ÷ b, where a and b are positive","back":"UB(a ÷ b) = UB(a) ÷ LB(b); LB(a ÷ b) = LB(a) ÷ UB(b). Dividing by less gives more, so the divisor reverses.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Bounds of a speed found from a measured distance and a measured time","back":"UB(speed) = UB(distance) ÷ LB(time); LB(speed) = LB(distance) ÷ UB(time). The same pattern fits density = mass ÷ volume and pressure = force ÷ area.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Giving a calculated value to a suitable degree of accuracy","back":"Work out the upper and lower bounds, round both to the same accuracy, and give the value at the greatest accuracy at which the two rounded bounds still agree, naming that accuracy.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Deciding from bounds whether a result is certain","back":"Test the worst case against the limit given: if the bound that matters still fits, the result is certain; if it does not, it cannot be guaranteed, and you say so.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Rounding within a bounds calculation","back":"Keep the full figure for each bound through the working and round only at the end, to the accuracy the question asks for; rounding early can move a bound the wrong way.","code":"N16 (ii)","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Recall: a measurement given as v correct to the nearest u lies in v − u/2 ≤ x < v + u/2 — lower bound v − u/2, upper bound v + u/2.","Recall: area of a rectangle = length × width. Section 4 of the course appendix does not record it among the results either tier's insert prints, so it must be written from memory.","Recall: area of a square = side², the same rule with the two sides equal. It is not among the results section 4 records for either tier's insert, so it too must be written from memory.","Recall: perimeter of a rectangle = 2(length + width) and perimeter of a square = 4 × side. Neither is among the results section 4 records for either tier's insert, so both must be written from memory.","Recall: volume of a cuboid = length × width × height. Section 4 names the cuboid result as one that is on neither tier's sheet, so it must be recalled, or derived as a prism whose cross-section is a rectangle.","Recall: UB(a + b) = UB(a) + UB(b) and LB(a + b) = LB(a) + LB(b).","Recall: UB(a − b) = UB(a) − LB(b) and LB(a − b) = LB(a) − UB(b).","Recall: for positive a and b, UB(a × b) = UB(a) × UB(b) and LB(a × b) = LB(a) × LB(b).","Recall: for positive a and b, UB(a ÷ b) = UB(a) ÷ LB(b) and LB(a ÷ b) = LB(a) ÷ UB(b).","Recall: speed = distance ÷ time, density = mass ÷ volume, pressure = force ÷ area — the compound measures these bounds questions are usually set in. Section 4 of the course appendix does not name any of them on either tier's insert, so they must be written from memory.","Given on the Higher tier sheet: area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h and volume of a prism = area of cross-section × length. The specification's Appendix puts both in its list 2, 'expected to know… or be able to derive them; they will not be given in the exam', but section 4 records both among the results AQA's Higher tier insert for exams from 2026 prints, and where the two disagree the sheet governs what you are given on the day.","Given on the Higher tier sheet: circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd and area of a circle = πr². The Appendix's list 1 says these will not be given; the Higher insert prints them, so again the sheet governs.","Recall or derive: volume of a cylinder = πr²h. It is not named on the Higher insert, but it follows from two results that are — volume of a prism, and area of a circle.","Not on either sheet: every bounds and accuracy rule above. Section 4 lists what AQA's Higher tier insert for exams from 2026 prints, and no rounding, error-interval or bounds result is among the fifteen, so the half-unit rule and the four combination rules have to be written from memory.","The formulae sheet is reissued for every series, so read the insert in front of you: it, and not this list, governs what is given on your own paper."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:34:31Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.95,"notes":"All 15 bank questions, 4 exit tickets, 4 lesson checks and 5 worked examples re-solved in Python against the fixed kit and every answer, unit and accuracy is right; the three substantive faults are properly closed, 5 non-calculator items remain and the difficulty spread is 5/6/4 across tiers 1/2/3, so this is ready to publish."}}},"algebra-notation-higher":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"algebra-notation-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Algebra is 30% of the Higher tier, and this room holds the manipulation that the rest of Higher algebra is built on. A quadratic you are asked to solve by factorising is often of the form ax² + bx + c, so the ac method here is what earns the method mark there. A volume or an area question in three dimensions gives a product of three brackets. A 'prove that' question is worth several marks and is marked on the chain of reasoning, not on the conclusion, so the general form — n, 2n, 2n + 1, 5n — must be written down before anything else happens. Algebraic fractions appear both as a simplify question in their own right and inside equations. And f(x), fg(x) and f⁻¹(x) are notation the Higher tier expects you to read and use without being told what they mean. Paper 1 is non-calculator, so the expanding and factorising here is often set without one, but the three papers are equally weighted and this content is assessed on all of them — and on every paper it is the written method that is credited.","idea":[{"h":"Expanding several binomials, harder factorising and algebraic fractions","t":"The Foundation columns stop at two binomials and at x² + bx + c. The Higher column adds three things. First, products of two or more binomials: expand the first two brackets, collect, then multiply that quadratic by the remaining bracket, term by term, and collect again — the mark scheme wants both expansions seen. Second, factorising ax² + bx + c with a ≠ 1: multiply a by c, find two numbers with that product and with sum b, split the middle term into those two x terms, then factorise in pairs; the shared bracket is the answer. Third, algebraic fractions: factorise the top and the bottom fully and cancel common factors to simplify, use a common denominator to add or subtract, and the same skills apply to expressions involving surds. A1's Notes still stand, so an answer left unsimplified can lose the accuracy mark even when the question does not say 'fully'.","code":"A4"},{"h":"Algebraic proof","t":"Checking a statement for n = 1, 2 and 3 is not a proof and scores nothing. A proof must hold for every value, so the numbers are replaced by a general algebraic form before any working starts: any integer is n, consecutive integers are n, n + 1, n + 2, an even number is 2n, an odd number is 2n + 1, two consecutive odd numbers are 2n + 1 and 2n + 3, and a multiple of 5 is 5n. Then expand and collect, and finish by writing the result in a form that shows the conclusion — a multiple of 3 as 3(...), an even number as 2(...), an odd number as 2(...) + 1 — and by stating in words what that form proves. AO2 marks the chain of reasoning, so the closing sentence is worth a mark on its own and the expansion is worth another. A6 also asks you to know the difference between an equation, true for particular values, and an identity, true for every value, written with ≡.","code":"A6"},{"h":"Inverse and composite functions","t":"A function is a rule with an input and an output, and at Higher tier the notation is expected. f(3) means put 3 into the rule f. A composite function is the succession of two functions: fg(x) means do g first and then f on that output, so fg and gf are usually different and the order is where the marks are lost. An inverse function reverses the process: f⁻¹ takes an output of f back to the input that produced it, so f⁻¹f(x) = x. To find f⁻¹(x) for a linear function, write y = f(x), rearrange to make x the subject, then swap the letter back so the answer is written in x. The −1 is not a power: f⁻¹(x) never means 1/f(x).","code":"A7"}],"examples":[{"title":"Expanding a product of three binomials","setup":"Expand the product (x + 2)(x − 1)(x + 4) and simplify your answer by collecting like terms.","steps":["Deal with any two brackets first: (x + 2)(x − 1) = x² − x + 2x − 2 = x² + x − 2","Multiply each term of the quadratic by the x in the remaining bracket (x + 4): x³ + x² − 2x","Multiply each term of the quadratic by 4: 4x² + 4x − 8","Add the two lines and collect like terms: x³ + x² + 4x² − 2x + 4x − 8 = x³ + 5x² + 2x − 8"],"cues":["Which two of the three brackets are easiest to multiply out first?","How many terms will the quadratic produce when every one is multiplied by x?","Which term of the third bracket still has to be used?","Which powers of x can be added together, and which cannot?"]},{"title":"Factorising a quadratic with a leading coefficient","setup":"Factorise 6x² + 11x − 10 fully, writing it as a product of two brackets.","steps":["Comparing with ax² + bx + c gives a = 6, b = 11 and c = −10, so a × c = 6 × (−10) = −60","Two numbers are wanted with product −60 and sum 11; the pair 15 and −4 gives 15 × (−4) = −60 and 15 + (−4) = 11","Split the middle term into those two x terms: 6x² + 15x − 4x − 10","Factorise in pairs: 3x(2x + 5) − 2(2x + 5)","The bracket (2x + 5) is common to both parts, so 6x² + 11x − 10 = (2x + 5)(3x − 2)"],"cues":["Which two of the three coefficients have to be multiplied together first?","Must the two numbers have the same sign or opposite signs here?","What is the middle term being replaced by?","What is the highest common factor of the first two terms?","What does the repeated bracket tell you about the factorised form?"]},{"title":"Simplifying an algebraic fraction","setup":"Simplify fully the algebraic fraction (x² − 9)/(x² + 7x + 12).","steps":["The numerator is a difference of two squares, x² − 3², so x² − 9 = (x − 3)(x + 3)","For the denominator two numbers with product 12 and sum 7 are 3 and 4, so x² + 7x + 12 = (x + 3)(x + 4)","The fraction is now (x − 3)(x + 3) over (x + 3)(x + 4), and (x + 3) appears on the top and the bottom","Cancelling that common factor leaves (x − 3)/(x + 4)"],"cues":["What is special about the two terms of the numerator?","Which pair of numbers multiplies to the constant term and adds to the coefficient of x?","Which bracket appears in both the top and the bottom?","What is left once the shared bracket has gone?"]},{"title":"Proving a general result about consecutive integers","setup":"Prove that the difference between the squares of any two consecutive integers is always an odd number.","steps":["Let the smaller integer be n, so the next one is n + 1, where n stands for any integer at all","Their squares are n² and (n + 1)² = n² + 2n + 1","The difference, larger minus smaller, is (n² + 2n + 1) − n² = 2n + 1","2n is even for every integer n, so 2n + 1 is one more than an even number and is therefore always odd, which is what was to be proved"],"cues":["Why will testing a few pairs of numbers not be enough here?","Which of the two squares needs multiplying out?","Which terms cancel when one square is taken from the other?","What does the form of the result tell you about whether it is odd?"]},{"title":"Composite and inverse functions","setup":"The functions f and g are defined by f(x) = 3x − 5 and g(x) = x². Work out fg(2), work out gf(2), and find an expression for f⁻¹(x).","steps":["In fg(2) the function nearest the number acts first, so start with g(2) = 2² = 4","Now put that output into f: f(4) = 3 × 4 − 5 = 7, so fg(2) = 7","In gf(2) the order is reversed: f(2) = 3 × 2 − 5 = 1, and then g(1) = 1² = 1, so gf(2) = 1","For the inverse, write y = 3x − 5 and rearrange to make x the subject: y + 5 = 3x, so x = (y + 5)/3","Writing the rule in terms of x gives f⁻¹(x) = (x + 5)/3"],"cues":["Which of the two functions is applied to the number first?","What is done with the number the first function produced?","Which function acts on 2 first in gf(2)?","What has to be made the subject before the inverse can be written down?","Which letter should the final answer be written in?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Expand and simplify (x − 2)(x + 5)(x + 1).","a":"x³ + 4x² − 7x − 10"},{"q":"Factorise 2x² + 7x + 3.","a":"(x + 3)(2x + 1)"},{"q":"Simplify fully (x² − 25)/(x² − 3x − 10).","a":"(x + 5)/(x + 2)"},{"q":"Prove that the sum of three consecutive integers is always a multiple of 3.","a":"n + (n + 1) + (n + 2) = 3n + 3 = 3(n + 1), which is 3 times an integer, so the sum is always a multiple of 3."},{"q":"f(x) = 2x + 9. Find f⁻¹(x).","a":"f⁻¹(x) = (x − 9)/2"}]},"room":{"facts":["To expand three binomials, multiply any two of them out and collect, then multiply the resulting quadratic by the remaining bracket term by term and collect again.","To factorise ax² + bx + c, find two numbers with product a × c and sum b, split the middle term into those two x terms, factorise in pairs, and take out the common bracket.","The difference of two squares in its general form: a²x² − b² = (ax − b)(ax + b), so 4x² − 9 = (2x − 3)(2x + 3).","To simplify an algebraic fraction, factorise the numerator and the denominator fully and cancel the common factors; to add or subtract them, write both over a common denominator first.","The general forms used in proof: any integer n; consecutive integers n, n + 1, n + 2; an even number 2n; an odd number 2n + 1; consecutive odd numbers 2n + 1 and 2n + 3; a multiple of 5 is 5n.","A proof ends by writing the result in the form that shows the conclusion — 3(…) for a multiple of 3, 2(…) for even, 2(…) + 1 for odd — and by saying in words what that form proves.","Function notation: fg(x) means g first and then f, so fg(x) and gf(x) are usually different; f⁻¹ is the inverse, found by making x the subject of y = f(x), and f⁻¹(x) never means 1/f(x)."],"questions":[{"q":"Expand and simplify (x + 1)(x + 2)(x + 3). (non-calculator)","a":"x³ + 6x² + 11x + 6","sol":"Expand the first two brackets: (x + 1)(x + 2) = x² + 2x + x + 2 = x² + 3x + 2. M1 for x² + 3x + 2. Multiply this quadratic by (x + 3): x²(x + 3) = x³ + 3x², 3x(x + 3) = 3x² + 9x and 2(x + 3) = 2x + 6. M1 for multiplying every term of their quadratic by both terms of the third bracket. Collect: x³ + 3x² + 3x² + 9x + 2x + 6 = x³ + 6x² + 11x + 6. A1 for x³ + 6x² + 11x + 6. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Three brackets cannot be multiplied all at once; they are handled two at a time.","Multiply out a pair of brackets and collect, then multiply what you get by the bracket that is left.","The first line is the expansion of (x + 1)(x + 2) into its four products before collecting."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"Factorise 3x² + 10x + 8. (non-calculator)","a":"(x + 2)(3x + 4)","sol":"Here a = 3, b = 10 and c = 8, so a × c = 24. Two numbers with product 24 and sum 10 are 4 and 6. Split the middle term: 3x² + 6x + 4x + 8. M1 for splitting the middle term into 6x and 4x in either order. Factorise in pairs: 3x(x + 2) + 4(x + 2), and the common bracket gives (x + 2)(3x + 4). A1 for (x + 2)(3x + 4) oe. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The coefficient of x² is not 1, so the two numbers you look for are not simply a factor pair of the constant term.","Multiply the coefficient of x² by the constant term, find two numbers with that product and with sum 10, and use them to split the middle term.","The first line multiplies 3 by 8 to give the product the two numbers must have."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"Simplify fully (x² − 49)/(x + 7). (a calculator may be used)","a":"x − 7","sol":"The numerator is a difference of two squares: x² − 49 = x² − 7² = (x − 7)(x + 7). M1 for (x − 7)(x + 7). The fraction is then (x − 7)(x + 7)/(x + 7), and the factor (x + 7) cancels, leaving x − 7. A1 for x − 7. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Nothing can be cancelled while the top is still a single expression, so look at what kind of quadratic it is.","Factorise the numerator, then cancel any factor that also appears in the denominator.","The first line writes 49 as 7² so the numerator can be factorised."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"f(x) = 5x + 2. (a) Work out f(−3). (b) Find an expression for f⁻¹(x). (non-calculator)","a":"(a) −13   (b) f⁻¹(x) = (x − 2)/5","sol":"(a) Substitute x = −3 into the rule: f(−3) = 5 × (−3) + 2 = −15 + 2 = −13. B1 for −13. (b) Write y = 5x + 2 and make x the subject: y − 2 = 5x. M1 for y − 2 = 5x or an equivalent correct first rearrangement. Dividing by 5 gives x = (y − 2)/5, so writing the rule in x gives f⁻¹(x) = (x − 2)/5. A1 for (x − 2)/5 oe. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["In part (a) the bracket round the negative value matters; in part (b) the inverse undoes what f does.","For the inverse, set y equal to the rule and rearrange until x stands alone, then write the answer in x.","Part (b) begins by writing y = 5x + 2 ready to be rearranged."],"codes":["A7"]},{"q":"Expand and simplify (2x − 1)(x + 3)(x − 4). (non-calculator)","a":"2x³ − 3x² − 23x + 12","sol":"Expand the first two brackets: (2x − 1)(x + 3) = 2x² + 6x − x − 3 = 2x² + 5x − 3. M1 for 2x² + 5x − 3. Multiply by (x − 4): 2x²(x − 4) = 2x³ − 8x², 5x(x − 4) = 5x² − 20x and −3(x − 4) = −3x + 12. M1 for multiplying every term of their quadratic by both terms of the third bracket. Collect: 2x³ − 8x² + 5x² − 20x − 3x + 12 = 2x³ − 3x² − 23x + 12. A1 for 2x³ − 3x² − 23x + 12. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Take the brackets two at a time; the leading 2 stays with its x throughout.","Expand one pair and collect, then multiply that quadratic by the remaining bracket and collect again.","The first line expands (2x − 1)(x + 3) into its four products."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"Factorise fully 12x² − 27. (a calculator may be used)","a":"3(2x − 3)(2x + 3)","sol":"Both terms have a factor of 3, so take it out first: 12x² − 27 = 3(4x² − 9). M1 for 3(4x² − 9). The bracket is a difference of two squares, 4x² − 9 = (2x)² − 3², so 4x² − 9 = (2x − 3)(2x + 3) and the fully factorised form is 3(2x − 3)(2x + 3). A1 for 3(2x − 3)(2x + 3). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["'Fully' warns you that more than one step of factorising is needed here.","Take out the highest common factor of the two terms first, then look at what the bracket has become.","The first line takes 3 out of both terms."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"Simplify fully (2x² + 5x − 3)/(x² + 6x + 9). (a calculator may be used)","a":"(2x − 1)/(x + 3)","sol":"Numerator: a × c = 2 × (−3) = −6, and two numbers with product −6 and sum 5 are 6 and −1, so 2x² + 5x − 3 = 2x² + 6x − x − 3 = 2x(x + 3) − 1(x + 3) = (x + 3)(2x − 1). M1 for (x + 3)(2x − 1). Denominator: two numbers with product 9 and sum 6 are 3 and 3, so x² + 6x + 9 = (x + 3)(x + 3) = (x + 3)². M1 for (x + 3)². Cancelling the common factor (x + 3) leaves (2x − 1)/(x + 3). A1 for (2x − 1)/(x + 3). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Nothing cancels until both the top and the bottom are written as products.","Factorise the numerator by the product-and-sum method for ax² + bx + c, factorise the denominator, then cancel.","The first line multiplies 2 by −3 to give the product the two numbers for the numerator must have."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"Write 3/(x + 2) + 4/(x − 1) as a single fraction in its simplest form. (a calculator may be used)","a":"(7x + 5)/((x + 2)(x − 1))","sol":"The common denominator is (x + 2)(x − 1), so 3/(x + 2) + 4/(x − 1) = [3(x − 1) + 4(x + 2)]/[(x + 2)(x − 1)]. M1 for a correct common denominator with both numerators adjusted. Expand the numerator: 3(x − 1) = 3x − 3 and 4(x + 2) = 4x + 8, giving 3x − 3 + 4x + 8. M1 for both expansions correct. Collect: 3x + 4x = 7x and −3 + 8 = 5, so the answer is (7x + 5)/((x + 2)(x − 1)). A1 for (7x + 5)/((x + 2)(x − 1)) oe. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Two fractions can only be added once they are written over the same denominator.","Multiply the two denominators together for the common denominator, adjust each numerator to match, then collect like terms on top.","The first line writes the sum over the single denominator (x + 2)(x − 1)."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"Prove that the sum of the squares of two consecutive odd numbers is always even. (non-calculator)","a":"Proved: the sum is 8n² + 16n + 10 = 2(4n² + 8n + 5), a multiple of 2 and therefore even.","sol":"Let the two consecutive odd numbers be 2n + 1 and 2n + 3, where n is any integer. B1 for both odd numbers written in a correct general form. Their squares are (2n + 1)² = 4n² + 4n + 1 and (2n + 3)² = 4n² + 12n + 9. M1 for expanding both squares correctly. Adding: 4n² + 4n + 1 + 4n² + 12n + 9 = 8n² + 16n + 10 = 2(4n² + 8n + 5), which is 2 times an integer, so the sum is always even. A1 for reaching 2(4n² + 8n + 5) and stating that it is therefore even. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Trying 3 and 5, then 7 and 9, shows the result but proves nothing — the argument has to hold for every pair.","Write both odd numbers in terms of the same letter, square them, add and then take out the factor that shows the conclusion.","The first line is the two consecutive odd numbers written in terms of the same letter n, two apart."],"codes":["A6"]},{"q":"f(x) = 2x + 3 and g(x) = x − 5. (a) Work out fg(4). (b) Work out gf(4). (non-calculator)","a":"(a) 1   (b) 6","sol":"(a) In fg(4) the function g acts first: g(4) = 4 − 5 = −1. M1 for −1. Then f(−1) = 2 × (−1) + 3 = −2 + 3 = 1. A1 for 1. (b) In gf(4) the order is reversed and f acts first: f(4) = 2 × 4 + 3 = 11. M1 for 11. Then g(11) = 11 − 5 = 6. A1 for 6. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["In a composite function the two letters are not interchangeable — one of them acts on the number first.","Work from the inside out: apply the function written next to the number, then apply the other one to that output.","Part (a) begins by working out g(4)."],"codes":["A7"]},{"q":"A cuboid has a rectangular base of length (2x + 1) cm and width (x − 3) cm, and a height of (x + 2) cm. Show that the volume of the cuboid is (2x³ − x² − 13x − 6) cm³. (non-calculator)","a":"Shown: (2x + 1)(x − 3)(x + 2) expands to 2x³ − x² − 13x − 6.","sol":"The volume of a cuboid is length × width × height, so V = (2x + 1)(x − 3)(x + 2). Expand the first two brackets: (2x + 1)(x − 3) = 2x² − 6x + x − 3 = 2x² − 5x − 3. M1 for 2x² − 5x − 3. Multiply by (x + 2): 2x²(x + 2) = 2x³ + 4x², −5x(x + 2) = −5x² − 10x and −3(x + 2) = −3x − 6. M1 for multiplying every term of their quadratic by both terms of the third bracket. Collect: 2x³ + 4x² − 5x² − 10x − 3x − 6 = 2x³ − x² − 13x − 6, as required. A1 for the full expansion leading to the printed answer with no steps missing. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Write down what volume means for a cuboid before touching any algebra.","Multiply two of the three bracketed lengths out and collect, then multiply that quadratic by the third bracket.","The first line is V = (2x + 1)(x − 3)(x + 2) with the first pair of brackets about to be expanded."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"f(x) = 4x − 3. (a) Find an expression for f⁻¹(x). (b) Solve f⁻¹(x) = f(x). (non-calculator)","a":"(a) f⁻¹(x) = (x + 3)/4   (b) x = 1","sol":"(a) Write y = 4x − 3 and make x the subject: y + 3 = 4x, so x = (y + 3)/4 and f⁻¹(x) = (x + 3)/4. M1 for y + 3 = 4x oe, A1 for (x + 3)/4 oe. (b) Setting the two rules equal gives (x + 3)/4 = 4x − 3. Multiplying both sides by 4: x + 3 = 16x − 12. M1 for clearing the fraction correctly (ft their part (a)). Collecting: 3 + 12 = 16x − x, so 15 = 15x and x = 1. A1 for x = 1. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The inverse reverses what f does; part (b) then asks where the two rules give the same output.","Find the inverse by making x the subject of y = 4x − 3, then set your inverse equal to 4x − 3 and solve the linear equation.","Part (b) begins by writing (your inverse) = 4x − 3 and multiplying both sides by 4 to clear the fraction."],"codes":["A7"]},{"q":"n is an integer. Prove that (2n + 3)² − (2n − 3)² is always a multiple of 24. (non-calculator)","a":"Proved: the expression equals 24n, which is 24 times an integer.","sol":"Expand the first square: (2n + 3)² = 4n² + 6n + 6n + 9 = 4n² + 12n + 9. M1 for 4n² + 12n + 9. Expand the second square: (2n − 3)² = 4n² − 6n − 6n + 9 = 4n² − 12n + 9. M1 for 4n² − 12n + 9. Subtract, taking care with the signs of every term of the second bracket: (4n² + 12n + 9) − (4n² − 12n + 9) = 4n² + 12n + 9 − 4n² + 12n − 9 = 24n. A1 for 24n. Since n is an integer, 24n is 24 times an integer, so the expression is always a multiple of 24. A1 for the conclusion stated from 24n. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The two brackets are almost the same, so most of what you write will cancel at the subtraction stage.","Expand each square in full, then subtract the whole of the second expansion from the first and say what the result shows.","The first line is the expansion of (2n + 3)², written out in full before any collecting."],"codes":["A6"]},{"q":"(a) Show that (x² + x − 12)/(x² − 16) simplifies to (x − 3)/(x − 4). (b) Hence solve (x² + x − 12)/(x² − 16) = 2. (a calculator may be used)","a":"(a) Shown   (b) x = 5","sol":"(a) Two numbers with product −12 and sum 1 are 4 and −3, so x² + x − 12 = (x + 4)(x − 3); the denominator is a difference of two squares, x² − 16 = x² − 4² = (x − 4)(x + 4). M1 for both factorisations. Cancelling the common factor (x + 4) gives (x − 3)/(x − 4), as required. A1 for the cancelling shown. (b) The equation becomes (x − 3)/(x − 4) = 2, so multiplying both sides by (x − 4) gives x − 3 = 2(x − 4) = 2x − 8. M1 for x − 3 = 2(x − 4) oe. Collecting: 8 − 3 = 2x − x, so x = 5. A1 for x = 5. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["'Hence' means part (a) has already done most of the work for part (b).","Factorise the top and the bottom and cancel, then replace the fraction in the equation by your simpler one and solve it.","Part (a) begins by writing 16 as 4², so the denominator can be treated as a difference of two squares."],"codes":["A4"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Expand and simplify (x + 1)(x − 1)(x + 5).","a":"x³ + 5x² − x − 5"},{"q":"Factorise 2x² + 11x + 5.","a":"(x + 5)(2x + 1)"},{"q":"n is an integer, so two consecutive odd numbers are 2n + 1 and 2n + 3. Prove that their sum is always a multiple of 4.","a":"(2n + 1) + (2n + 3) = 4n + 4 = 4(n + 1), which is 4 times an integer, so the sum is always a multiple of 4."},{"q":"f(x) = 3x − 1. Write down an expression for f⁻¹(x).","a":"f⁻¹(x) = (x + 1)/3"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Expanding a product of three binomials","back":"Multiply any two brackets and collect; then multiply that quadratic by the remaining bracket, term by term, and collect again. Both expansions must be seen.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Factorising ax² + bx + c when a ≠ 1","back":"Find two numbers with product a × c and sum b; split the middle term into those two x terms; factorise in pairs; the repeated bracket and the outside factors are the answer.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"The difference of two squares, general form","back":"a²x² − b² = (ax − b)(ax + b). So x² − 49 = (x − 7)(x + 7) and 4x² − 9 = (2x − 3)(2x + 3). There is no middle term.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Simplifying an algebraic fraction","back":"Factorise the numerator and the denominator fully, then cancel any bracket that appears in both. Only whole factors cancel, never single terms.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Adding or subtracting algebraic fractions","back":"Write both over a common denominator, usually the product of the two denominators, adjust each numerator to match, then expand and collect the numerator and simplify.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Multiplying and dividing algebraic fractions","back":"To multiply, factorise everything, cancel across, then multiply the tops and the bottoms. To divide, turn the second fraction upside down and multiply.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"What 'factorise fully' requires","back":"Take out the highest common factor first, then factorise the bracket that is left if it can be factorised, so 12x² − 27 = 3(4x² − 9) = 3(2x − 3)(2x + 3).","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Simplifying expressions involving surds","back":"Expand brackets as usual and use √a × √a = a; collect rational terms and surd terms separately, and rationalise a denominator by multiplying top and bottom by the surd.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Equation and identity","back":"An equation is true only for particular values of the letter. An identity, written with ≡, is true for every value. Showing two expressions are equivalent proves an identity.","code":"A6","open":false},{"front":"Why testing numbers is not a proof","back":"Checking n = 1, 2 and 3 shows the result in three cases only and scores no marks. A proof must use a general algebraic form that stands for every value.","code":"A6","open":false},{"front":"The standard algebraic forms used in proof","back":"Any integer n; consecutive integers n, n + 1, n + 2; even 2n; odd 2n + 1; consecutive odd numbers 2n + 1 and 2n + 3; a multiple of 5 is 5n.","code":"A6","open":false},{"front":"How an algebraic proof is finished","back":"Write the result in the form that shows the conclusion — 3(…) for a multiple of 3, 2(…) for even, 2(…) + 1 for odd — then state in words what that form proves.","code":"A6","open":false},{"front":"Give an example of a pair of expressions that stand for two consecutive odd numbers","back":"2n + 1 and 2n + 3; 2n − 1 and 2n + 1; 2n + 3 and 2n + 5. Any pair of the form 2n + odd differing by 2 is acceptable, provided n stands for any integer.","code":"A6","open":true},{"front":"Composite function fg(x)","back":"fg(x) means f(g(x)): the function nearer the x acts first and f acts on its output. fg(x) and gf(x) are usually different expressions.","code":"A7","open":false},{"front":"Inverse function f⁻¹(x)","back":"f⁻¹ reverses f, so f⁻¹f(x) = x. Find it by writing y = f(x), making x the subject, then writing the rule in x. The −1 is not a power: f⁻¹(x) is not 1/f(x).","code":"A7","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["How to read this list: the specification's Appendix sorts every prescribed formula into three lists — lists 1 and 2 are marked 'they will not be given in the exam', list 3 'they will be given in the exam in the relevant question'. Since 2022 every AQA 8300 paper also carries a formulae sheet, one per tier, and section 4 of appendix.md records the results the 2026 Higher insert prints. This is a Higher room, so that Higher insert is the one that applies, and where the Appendix says a result must be known but section 4 shows the sheet prints it, the sheet governs on the day. Remember below means you must write it from memory; Given on the Higher sheet means it is printed on the insert in front of you.","Remember — not in the Appendix at all, and on neither tier's sheet: to expand a product of three binomials, multiply two brackets out and collect, then multiply that quadratic by the remaining bracket term by term and collect again.","Remember — not in the Appendix at all: to factorise ax² + bx + c, find two numbers with product a × c and sum b, split the middle term into those two x terms, factorise in pairs and take out the common bracket.","Remember — not in the Appendix at all: the difference of two squares in its general form, a²x² − b² = (ax − b)(ax + b).","Remember — not in the Appendix at all: to simplify an algebraic fraction, factorise the numerator and denominator fully and cancel common factors; to add or subtract, write both over a common denominator; to divide, multiply by the reciprocal of the second fraction.","Remember — not in the Appendix at all: the surd results used when simplifying, √a × √a = a and √(ab) = √a × √b, and that a denominator is rationalised by multiplying the top and the bottom by the surd.","Remember — not in the Appendix at all: the general forms used in proof, n for any integer, n, n + 1, n + 2 for consecutive integers, 2n for an even number, 2n + 1 for an odd number, 2n + 1 and 2n + 3 for consecutive odd numbers, and 5n for a multiple of 5.","Remember — not in the Appendix at all: the function notation, f(x) for the output of f, fg(x) = f(g(x)) with g applied first, and f⁻¹ for the inverse, with f⁻¹f(x) = x and f⁻¹(x) never meaning 1/f(x).","Given on the Higher sheet, not memorised — section 4 of appendix.md records that the 2026 Higher insert prints the quadratic formula, the sine rule, the cosine rule, Area = ½ab sin C, the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, the circumference and area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios in a right-angled triangle, compound interest (the total accrued), P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B) and P(A and B) = P(A given B) P(B). The Appendix puts all of these in list 1 or list 2, 'they will not be given in the exam', but the insert is later than the Appendix and governs on the day, so none of them has to be recalled.","None of the results the Higher sheet prints is needed in this room: every result used here is a manipulation rule or a notation convention that the Appendix does not list at all, so it is not on either tier's sheet and must be recalled. The quadratic formula in particular belongs to solving quadratics, not to factorising them — factorising ax² + bx + c is what earns the method mark when a question says 'factorise'.","Given in the exam in the relevant question — Appendix list 3, 'students are not expected to memorise the following formulae': the curved surface area of a cone, the surface area and volume of a sphere, the volume of a cone and the kinematics formulae v = u + at, s = ut + ½at² and v² = u² + 2as. None is needed in this room, but A2's Notes give the general rule that 'unfamiliar formulae will be given in the question'.","Not a formula but a marking rule worth memorising: A1's Notes say answers are expected in their simplest form without an explicit instruction to do so, so an expansion left uncollected, a fraction left uncancelled or a factorisation left incomplete can lose the accuracy mark even where the question does not say 'fully'. Section 4 also warns that the inserts are reissued each series, so check the current Higher sheet."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:26:50.357829Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Every answer key, solution line and mark breakdown checks out (re-solved in sympy), and the Formulae sheet's 'Given on the Higher sheet' list matches section 4 of appendix.md item for item — publishable, with only the worked-example step/cue tweaks and an A6 item added to the exit ticket."}}},"algebra-graphs-higher":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"algebra-graphs-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Algebra is 30% of the Higher tier, and a large share of those marks sit on graphs. This room carries the graph work that only Higher papers set: perpendicular lines read from y = mx + c, turning points deduced by completing the square, the shapes of exponential and trigonometric curves, translations and reflections of a given function, circles centred on the origin together with their tangents, and the meaning of the gradient of a curve and of the area underneath one. It builds straight on the Foundation graphs room, where you met coordinates, gradients, straight lines, parabolas and distance-time graphs. These ideas appear on all three papers, and on Paper 1 without a calculator, where exact fractions earn the accuracy marks. They also carry much of the AO2 reasoning and AO3 problem solving that the Higher tier weights more heavily than the Foundation tier.","idea":[{"h":"Perpendicular lines from y = mx + c","t":"You already read the gradient m and the y-intercept c straight off y = mx + c, and you know that parallel lines share the same m. At Higher tier you must also recognise and build perpendicular lines. Two lines are perpendicular exactly when their gradients multiply to −1, so each gradient is the negative reciprocal of the other: turn the fraction upside down and change its sign. A gradient of 2/3 gives −3/2; a gradient of −4 gives 1/4; a gradient of 1 gives −1. A typical question gives you a line and a point and asks for the equation of the perpendicular through that point: find the new gradient, substitute the coordinates into y = mx + c and solve for c. The gradient step is where the method mark falls, and on Paper 1 the answer is left as an exact fraction, never as a rounded decimal.","code":"A9"},{"h":"Turning points by completing the square","t":"A quadratic written as y = (x + p)² + q wears its turning point on its sleeve. A squared bracket is never negative, so the smallest it can be is 0, and that happens when x + p = 0, that is x = −p; there y = q. The turning point is therefore (−p, q), and because a parabola is symmetrical the line of symmetry is x = −p. To reach that form, halve the coefficient of x to fill the bracket, expand the bracket in your head and correct the constant term up or down. If the coefficient of x² is not 1, factorise it out of the first two terms first. The same form settles how many roots there are: if q is positive and the bracket is added, y is always positive, so the curve never crosses the x-axis and the equation has no solutions.","code":"A11"},{"h":"Exponential and trigonometric graphs","t":"y = kˣ for a positive value of k is an exponential curve. It passes through (0, 1) because k⁰ = 1, it never touches the x-axis — the x-axis is an asymptote — and for k > 1 it climbs ever more steeply to the right, while for 0 < k < 1 it falls away to the right instead. The trigonometric graphs use arguments in degrees and run for angles of any size, positive or negative. y = sin x is a wave of period 360° through the origin, rising to 1 at 90°, back to 0 at 180°, down to −1 at 270° and back to 0 at 360°. y = cos x is the same wave starting at (0, 1), with zeros at 90° and 270°. y = tan x has period 180°, passes through the origin and is broken by vertical asymptotes at 90°, 270° and every 180° from there. Exams ask you to sketch these and to read solutions of equations such as sin x = −1 off them.","code":"A12"},{"h":"Translations and reflections of a function","t":"A change made outside the function changes the output, so it moves the graph vertically: y = f(x) + a translates the whole curve a units up, column vector (0, a). A change made inside the brackets changes the input, and it moves the graph horizontally in the opposite direction to the sign: y = f(x + a) translates it a units to the left, column vector (−a, 0), so y = f(x − 2) moves it 2 units to the right. Reflections follow the same rule. y = −f(x) reverses the sign of every y value, which is a reflection in the x-axis and turns a maximum into a minimum; y = f(−x) reverses the sign of every x value, a reflection in the y-axis. In an exam you are usually given one key point — a maximum, a minimum or an intercept — and asked where it moves to, so track that single point through the rule and the sketch follows from it.","code":"A13"},{"h":"Exponential graphs in real contexts","t":"Growth and decay are modelled by y = abˣ, where a is the starting value (and so the y-intercept) and b is the multiplier for each unit of x. A population growing by 8% a year has b = 1.08; a quantity falling by 20% a year has b = 0.8. Building a table of values and plotting a smooth curve lets you read approximate answers off the graph: when a population first passes 10 000, or what a value is after 7.5 years. Because the curve is never straight you cannot interpolate with a ruler between distant points, so a reading is an estimate and should be given to a sensible accuracy and with its unit. These are calculator questions on Papers 2 and 3, and the same repeated multiplier drives compound interest, so the arithmetic is familiar from the ratio work.","code":"A14"},{"h":"Gradients of graphs and areas under graphs","t":"On a curve the gradient changes from point to point, so to find it at one point you draw a tangent that just touches the curve there and work out that straight line's gradient, using two points on the tangent that are far apart. The result is the instantaneous rate of change: on a distance-time graph it is the speed, on a velocity-time graph it is the acceleration, on a graph of the value of an investment it is pounds per year. The area under a graph also means something: the area under a velocity-time graph is the distance travelled. Estimate it by splitting the region into triangles, rectangles and trapezia, or by counting squares. Say whether the estimate is too big or too small — chords across a curve that bends upwards lie above it, so the trapezia overestimate — and always give the unit and a sentence in context, because that sentence is where the interpretation mark is.","code":"A15"},{"h":"The equation of a circle and of its tangent","t":"A circle with its centre at the origin and radius r has equation x² + y² = r². That is just Pythagoras' theorem applied to the right-angled triangle joining the origin to the point (x, y), so the number on the right is the radius squared and not the radius. x² + y² = 36 is therefore the circle of radius 6, and a point lies on that circle exactly when its coordinates satisfy the equation. A tangent touches the circle at one point only and is perpendicular to the radius drawn to that point. So to find the tangent at a point P, work out the gradient of the radius OP from the origin and P, take its negative reciprocal to get the tangent's gradient, then substitute the coordinates of P into y = mx + c and solve for c. The perpendicular step is the one the method mark hangs on.","code":"A16"}],"examples":[{"title":"The perpendicular bisector of a line segment","setup":"The points A(−1, 2) and B(5, 6) are the ends of the line segment AB. Find the equation of the perpendicular bisector of AB, giving your answer in the form y = mx + c.","steps":["Gradient of AB = (6 − 2) ÷ (5 − (−1)) = 4 ÷ 6 = 2/3.","Perpendicular gradient = the negative reciprocal of 2/3 = −3/2.","A bisector passes through the midpoint, which is ((−1 + 5) ÷ 2, (2 + 6) ÷ 2) = (2, 4).","Substitute the midpoint and the gradient into y = mx + c: 4 = −3/2 × 2 + c, so 4 = −3 + c.","c = 7, so the perpendicular bisector is y = −3/2 x + 7."],"cues":["Which two differences do you need before any gradient can be worked out?","What does being perpendicular do to a gradient?","What does the word bisector tell you about a point the line must pass through?","Which pair of values now goes into y = mx + c?","What is left to write down once the intercept is known?"]},{"title":"Completing the square to find a turning point","setup":"A curve has equation y = x² + 12x + 40. Write it in the form (x + p)² + q, and use that form to write down the coordinates of the turning point and the equation of the line of symmetry.","steps":["Halve the coefficient of x: 12 ÷ 2 = 6, so the bracket must be (x + 6)².","Expanding gives (x + 6)² = x² + 12x + 36, whose constant is 4 short of the one needed, so y = (x + 6)² + 4.","A square is never negative, so the bracket is smallest, and equal to 0, when x + 6 = 0, that is x = −6.","At that value of x, y = 0 + 4 = 4, so the turning point is (−6, 4).","A parabola is symmetrical about the vertical line through its turning point, so the line of symmetry is x = −6."],"cues":["Which term of the quadratic decides the number inside the bracket?","How does the constant in the expanded bracket compare with the constant you want?","What is the smallest value a squared bracket can take, and when does it happen?","With the bracket at its smallest, what is left of the right-hand side?","Is the line of symmetry horizontal or vertical, and which point must it pass through?"]},{"title":"Tracking one point through four transformations","setup":"The graph of y = f(x) has a single maximum point at (4, 7). Write down the coordinates of the image of that maximum point on each of the graphs y = f(x) + 5, y = f(x − 2), y = −f(x) and y = f(−x).","steps":["Adding 5 outside the function adds 5 to every y value, moving the maximum to (4, 12).","Replacing x by x − 2 slides the curve 2 units to the right, moving the maximum to (6, 7).","The minus sign outside reverses the sign of every y value, a reflection in the x-axis, so the point maps to (4, −7) and it is now a minimum.","The minus sign inside reverses the sign of every x value, a reflection in the y-axis, so the point maps to (−4, 7)."],"cues":["Is a number added outside the function a vertical or a horizontal change?","A change inside the brackets moves the curve sideways — which way for a minus sign?","Which coordinate changes sign when the whole function is negated, and what does that do to a maximum?","Which coordinate changes sign when the input is negated instead?"]},{"title":"The tangent to a circle at a given point","setup":"A circle has equation x² + y² = 20. The point T(2, 4) lies on the circle. Find the equation of the tangent to the circle at T, giving your answer in the form y = mx + c.","steps":["Since the centre is the origin O(0, 0), the radius OT has gradient 4 ÷ 2 = 2.","A tangent meets its radius at a right angle, so its gradient is the negative reciprocal of 2, which is −1/2.","Substitute T(2, 4) and that gradient into y = mx + c: 4 = −1/2 × 2 + c, so 4 = −1 + c.","c = 5, so the tangent is y = −1/2 x + 5."],"cues":["Where is the centre of a circle whose equation has no x or y term outside the squares?","What is the angle between a tangent and the radius at the point of contact?","Which point must lie on the tangent, and what do you do with its coordinates?","What is left to state once the intercept has been found?"]},{"title":"Acceleration and distance from a velocity-time graph","setup":"A cyclist's velocity-time graph is made of two straight sections. It rises in a straight line from (0 s, 0 m/s) to (8 s, 12 m/s), then falls in a straight line from (8 s, 12 m/s) to (20 s, 0 m/s). Work out the acceleration over the first 8 seconds, and the total distance travelled in the 20 seconds.","steps":["Gradient of the rising line = change in velocity ÷ change in time = (12 − 0) ÷ (8 − 0) = 1.5 m/s², which is the acceleration.","The region under the rising line is a triangle: area = ½ × 8 × 12 = 48 m.","The falling line stands on a base of 20 − 8 = 12 s, so its triangle has area ½ × 12 × 12 = 72 m.","Total distance = 48 + 72 = 120 m."],"cues":["Which feature of a velocity-time graph gives the acceleration?","What shape is the region under the first section, and what is its area formula?","How long does the second section last, and what is its height?","What must be done with the two areas to answer the second part?"]},{"title":"Reading an exponential decay model","setup":"A hot drink cools so that its temperature above room temperature is T = 60 × 0.8ᵐ °C, where m is the number of minutes since it was poured. Work out T when m = 0 and when m = 5, and find how long it takes for T to fall to half its starting value, giving your answer to the nearest minute.","steps":["Putting m = 0 gives 0.8⁰ = 1 by the zero-index rule, hence a value of 60 × 1 = 60 °C.","Putting m = 5 gives 0.8⁵ = 0.32768, hence 60 × 0.32768 = 19.7 °C to 1 d.p.","Half of the starting value is 30 °C, so the equation to solve is 60 × 0.8ᵐ = 30, that is 0.8ᵐ = 0.5.","Trying m = 3: 0.8³ = 0.512, which gives 60 × 0.512 = 30.72 °C, still above 30 °C.","Testing the half-minute boundary m = 3.5: raising 0.8 to the power 3.5 gives 0.4579, so T = 27.5 °C, which is already below 30 °C; T therefore passes 30 °C between m = 3 and m = 3.5, so the time is under 3.5 minutes and to the nearest minute it is 3 minutes."],"cues":["What is any positive number raised to the power zero?","Which calculator key deals with the power in this expression?","What value of T are you now looking for, and what equation does that give?","Is three minutes long enough for the temperature to drop that far?","Which single value of m decides whether the time rounds to 3 minutes or to 4 minutes?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Two lines are perpendicular and one of them has gradient −5. What is the gradient of the other?","a":"1/5 (0.2)"},{"q":"What are the coordinates of the turning point of y = (x − 7)² + 2, and what is its line of symmetry?","a":"Turning point (7, 2); line of symmetry x = 7"},{"q":"Does the point (−6, 8) lie on the circle x² + y² = 100?","a":"Yes: (−6)² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100"},{"q":"A velocity-time graph has velocity in metres per second on the vertical axis and time in seconds on the horizontal axis. The area under the graph between t = 0 s and t = 10 s is 85. What does the 85 represent, and in what unit?","a":"The distance travelled in those 10 seconds: 85 metres"},{"q":"The graph of y = f(x) crosses the y-axis at (0, −3). Where does the graph of y = −f(x) cross the y-axis?","a":"(0, 3)"}]},"room":{"facts":["Perpendicular lines: m₁ × m₂ = −1, so each gradient is the negative reciprocal of the other.","Completed square form: y = (x + p)² + q has turning point (−p, q) and line of symmetry x = −p.","A circle centred on the origin with radius r has equation x² + y² = r²; the tangent at a point is perpendicular to the radius drawn to that point.","y = kˣ (k positive) passes through (0, 1) with the x-axis as an asymptote; y = sin x and y = cos x have period 360° and run between −1 and 1; y = tan x has period 180° with asymptotes at 90°, 270° and so on.","y = f(x) + a moves the curve a up; y = f(x + a) moves it a to the left; y = −f(x) reflects it in the x-axis; y = f(−x) reflects it in the y-axis.","The gradient of a tangent to a curve is the rate of change at that point; the area under a velocity-time graph is the distance travelled.","Exponential growth and decay: y = abˣ, where a is the value at x = 0 and b is the multiplier for each unit of x."],"questions":[{"q":"A line has gradient 4. Write down the gradient of any line perpendicular to it. (non-calculator)","a":"−1/4","sol":"Perpendicular gradients multiply to −1, so the gradient wanted is the negative reciprocal of 4, that is −1 ÷ 4 = −1/4. B1 for −1/4 (or −0.25).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Perpendicular lines do something very particular to a gradient, and it is more than a change of sign.","The two gradients have a fixed product, so divide that product by the gradient you are given.","Write the given gradient as a fraction over 1, then flip it and deal with the sign."],"codes":["A9"]},{"q":"Write down the radius of the circle with equation x² + y² = 49. (non-calculator)","a":"7","sol":"A circle centred on the origin has equation x² + y² = r², so here r² = 49 and r = √49 = 7. B1 for 7.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Compare the equation with the standard form of a circle centred on the origin.","The number on the right-hand side is not the radius itself; it is the radius squared.","Set r² equal to the number on the right and then undo the squaring."],"codes":["A16"]},{"q":"Write x² + 8x + 3 in the form (x + p)² + q. (non-calculator)","a":"(x + 4)² − 13","sol":"Halve the coefficient of x: 8 ÷ 2 = 4, so the bracket is (x + 4)². M1. Expanding, (x + 4)² = x² + 8x + 16, which is 13 more than x² + 8x + 3, so 13 must be taken off: x² + 8x + 3 = (x + 4)² − 13. A1.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The bracket is fixed by the x term alone; the constant is put right afterwards.","Halve the coefficient of x to get the number inside the bracket, then compare the expanded bracket with the expression.","Expand (x + p)² using your value of p and see how far its constant term is from the one you need."],"codes":["A11"]},{"q":"Write down the coordinates of the point where the graph of y = 5ˣ crosses the y-axis. (non-calculator)","a":"(0, 1)","sol":"Every point on the y-axis has x = 0, and 5⁰ = 1, so the graph crosses the y-axis at (0, 1). B1 for the ordered pair.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Every graph crosses the y-axis where the x-coordinate takes one particular value.","Put that value of x into y = 5ˣ and use the index law for a power of zero.","Write the point as (x, y) with the x-coordinate already settled by the axis."],"codes":["A12"]},{"q":"The graph of y = f(x) is transformed to give the graph of y = f(x) − 4. Describe fully the single transformation. (non-calculator)","a":"A translation of 4 units down, column vector (0, −4)","sol":"The 4 is subtracted outside the function, so it is taken off every y value while every x value is unchanged: every point moves 4 units down. B1 for a translation 4 units down, or for the vector (0, −4).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Decide first whether the change is made to the input or to the output of the function.","A number added or subtracted outside the function changes the output, so the movement is vertical.","Name the type of transformation, then say how far the curve moves and in which direction."],"codes":["A13"]},{"q":"The line L has equation y = 4x + 7. Find the equation of the line perpendicular to L that passes through the point (8, 3). Give your answer in the form y = mx + c. (non-calculator)","a":"y = −1/4 x + 5","sol":"L has gradient 4, so the perpendicular gradient is the negative reciprocal, −1/4. M1. Substitute (8, 3) into y = −1/4 x + c: 3 = −1/4 × 8 + c = −2 + c. M1. So c = 5 and the line is y = −1/4 x + 5. A1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Read the gradient of L straight off its equation before doing anything with the point.","Find the negative reciprocal of that gradient, then use the given point to fix the intercept.","Put the perpendicular gradient and the coordinates of the point into y = mx + c and solve for c."],"codes":["A9"]},{"q":"By completing the square, find the coordinates of the turning point of the curve y = x² + 10x + 21. (non-calculator)","a":"(−5, −4)","sol":"Halve the coefficient of x: 10 ÷ 2 = 5, so the bracket is (x + 5)². M1. Expanding, (x + 5)² = x² + 10x + 25, which is 4 more than x² + 10x + 21, so y = (x + 5)² − 4. A1. The bracket is least, and equal to 0, when x + 5 = 0, that is x = −5, and then y = −4, so the turning point is (−5, −4). A1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The bracket you need is decided by the x term, not by the constant.","Rewrite the equation as (x + p)² + q; the turning point can then be read from p and q.","Halve the coefficient of x for the number in the bracket, then compare the expanded bracket with the given expression."],"codes":["A11"]},{"q":"The point P(6, 8) lies on the circle x² + y² = 100. Find the equation of the tangent to the circle at P. Give your answer in the form y = mx + c. (non-calculator)","a":"y = −3/4 x + 25/2 (12.5 accepted)","sol":"The centre is the origin, so the radius OP has gradient 8 ÷ 6 = 4/3. M1. The tangent is perpendicular to the radius, so its gradient is the negative reciprocal, −3/4. M1. Substitute P(6, 8) into y = −3/4 x + c: 8 = −3/4 × 6 + c = −9/2 + c. M1. So c = 8 + 9/2 = 25/2 and the tangent is y = −3/4 x + 25/2; on this non-calculator question the intercept is left as the exact fraction 25/2, though 12.5 is accepted (oe 3x + 4y = 50). A1.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The radius drawn to P and the tangent at P meet at a right angle.","Work out the gradient of the radius first, then turn it into the tangent's gradient.","The centre is the origin, so use (0, 0) and P to get the radius' gradient before touching y = mx + c."],"codes":["A16","A9"]},{"q":"Using the graph of y = sin x, write down all the solutions of sin x = −1 for 0° ≤ x ≤ 720°. (non-calculator)","a":"x = 270° and x = 630°","sol":"The sine curve reaches its minimum value of −1 once in each cycle. In the first cycle that is at x = 270°. B1. The curve has period 360°, so the next minimum is at 270° + 360° = 630°, which is still inside the range. B1.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Picture one full sine wave and ask where it is at its lowest.","The curve repeats every 360°, so a solution in the first cycle has a partner in the second.","Find the angle between 0° and 360° at which the graph reaches its minimum, then add one whole period."],"codes":["A12"]},{"q":"The graph of y = f(x) has a minimum point at (2, −5). Write down the coordinates of the minimum point on the graph of y = f(x + 3). (non-calculator)","a":"(−1, −5)","sol":"Replacing x by x + 3 translates the curve 3 units in the negative x direction, so the x-coordinate becomes 2 − 3 = −1. M1. The y-coordinate is unchanged, so the minimum point is (−1, −5). A1.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Decide whether the change is inside or outside the function before you move anything.","A number added inside the brackets slides the whole curve sideways, and not in the direction the sign suggests.","Keep the y-coordinate exactly as it is and change only the x-coordinate, by the amount inside the bracket."],"codes":["A13"]},{"q":"A train's velocity-time graph is a straight line from (0 s, 0 m/s) to (10 s, 24 m/s), followed by a horizontal line from (10 s, 24 m/s) to (30 s, 24 m/s). Work out the distance travelled in the 30 seconds. A calculator may be used.","a":"600 m","sol":"Distance is the area under a velocity-time graph. The first part is a triangle of base 10 s and height 24 m/s: area = ½ × 10 × 24 = 120 m. M1. The second part is a rectangle of base 30 − 10 = 20 s and height 24 m/s: area = 20 × 24 = 480 m. M1. Total distance = 120 + 480 = 600 m. A1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The distance is not read off the vertical axis; it is hidden in the region under the graph.","Split that region into a triangle and a rectangle and find the area of each.","The triangle has base 10 s and height 24 m/s, so deal with its area first."],"codes":["A15"]},{"q":"The population of a town is 5000 and grows by 8% each year, so after t years the population is P = 5000 × 1.08ᵗ. A graph of P against t for 0 ≤ t ≤ 12 is drawn by plotting (0, 5000), (2, 5832), (4, 6802), (6, 7934), (8, 9255), (10, 10795) and (12, 12591) and joining them with a smooth curve. Use the graph to find an approximate solution of the equation 5000 × 1.08ᵗ = 8000, and say what that solution tells you about the town. Give the number of years to the nearest year. A calculator may be used.","a":"t ≈ 6.1, so the population reaches 8000 after about 6 years","sol":"A solution of 5000 × 1.08ᵗ = 8000 is a value of t where the curve reaches a population of 8000, so draw the line P = 8000 across to the curve and read straight down to the t-axis. M1. The plotted values put the curve at 7934 when t = 6 and at 9255 when t = 8, so it passes 8000 just after t = 6, and the reading down is t ≈ 6.1. A1 (accept 6.0 to 6.3). Because 6.1 is less than the half-year boundary 6.5, to the nearest year the answer is 6: the population reaches 8000 about 6 years from now. B1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A solution read from this graph is a value of t, not a value of P.","Find the place on the curve where the population equals the number on the right of the equation, then read down.","Draw the horizontal line at that population across to the curve before reading anything off the t-axis."],"codes":["A14"]},{"q":"The point A(2, 6) lies on the circle x² + y² = 40. The tangent to the circle at A crosses the x-axis at the point P. Find the coordinates of P. (non-calculator)","a":"(20, 0)","sol":"The centre is the origin, so the radius OA has gradient 6 ÷ 2 = 3. M1. The tangent is perpendicular to OA, so its gradient is −1/3. M1. Substitute A(2, 6) into y = −1/3 x + c: 6 = −2/3 + c, so c = 6 + 2/3 = 20/3 and the tangent is y = −1/3 x + 20/3. M1. On the x-axis y = 0, so 0 = −1/3 x + 20/3, giving 1/3 x = 20/3. M1. So x = 20 and P is (20, 0). A1.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The tangent is perpendicular to the radius drawn from the centre to A.","Get the equation of the tangent first, then use the fact that every point on the x-axis has y-coordinate zero.","The radius runs from the origin to A, so work out its gradient from those two points."],"codes":["A16","A9"]},{"q":"By completing the square, find the minimum value of y = x² − 8x + 21 and the value of x at which it occurs. Hence explain why the equation x² − 8x + 21 = 0 has no solutions. (non-calculator)","a":"Minimum y = 5 at x = 4; since (x − 4)² ≥ 0, y ≥ 5 for every x, so y is never 0","sol":"Half of −8 is −4, so the bracket is (x − 4)². M1. Expanding, (x − 4)² = x² − 8x + 16, which is 5 less than x² − 8x + 21, so y = (x − 4)² + 5. A1. A square is never negative, so the least value of the bracket is 0, reached when x − 4 = 0, that is x = 4, and the minimum value of y is 5. A1. Since y ≥ 5 for every value of x, y can never equal 0, so x² − 8x + 21 = 0 has no solutions. B1.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A squared bracket can never be negative, and that fact is the whole of the explanation.","Rewrite the quadratic as (x + p)² + q, then say what the smallest possible value of the bracket is.","Halve the coefficient of x to fill the bracket, then correct the constant term."],"codes":["A11"]},{"q":"£2000 is invested in an account paying 4% compound interest a year, so its value after t years is V = 2000 × 1.04ᵗ pounds. By using the chord joining the points on the graph at t = 4 and t = 6, estimate the gradient of the graph at t = 5, to the nearest £1, and state what that gradient means. A calculator may be used.","a":"About £95 per year: at t = 5 the investment is gaining value at roughly £95 a year","sol":"At t = 4, V = 2000 × 1.04⁴ = £2339.72 to the nearest penny. M1. At t = 6, V = 2000 × 1.04⁶ = £2530.64 to the nearest penny. M1. Gradient of the chord = (2530.64 − 2339.72) ÷ (6 − 4) = 190.92 ÷ 2 = 95.46. M1. To the nearest pound the gradient is £95. A1. A gradient on this graph is a rate of change of value with time, so the investment is growing by about £95 per year when t = 5. B1.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The gradient of a graph at a point is a rate of change, measured in the units of the two axes.","A chord joining two points either side of t = 5 gives a good estimate of the gradient there.","Work out the value of the investment at t = 4 and at t = 6 before doing anything with a gradient."],"codes":["A15","A14"]},{"q":"The velocity of a rocket is recorded every 2 seconds: at t = 0 s it is 0 m/s, at t = 2 s it is 5 m/s, at t = 4 s it is 14 m/s and at t = 6 s it is 27 m/s. The velocity-time graph is a smooth curve through these points. Using three strips of width 2 s, estimate the distance travelled in the first 6 seconds and state whether your estimate is too large or too small. (non-calculator)","a":"65 m, and the estimate is too large","sol":"The distance is the area under the curve, estimated by three trapezia each of width 2 s, using area = ½(a + b)h. First strip: ½(0 + 5) × 2 = 5 m. M1. Second strip: ½(5 + 14) × 2 = 19 m and third strip: ½(14 + 27) × 2 = 41 m. M1. Total estimated distance = 5 + 19 + 41 = 65 m. A1. The velocity rises by 5, then 9, then 13 m/s, so the curve is bending upwards and each straight chord lies above it: the trapezia include extra area and the estimate is too large. B1.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Distance is the area under a velocity-time graph, and here the graph is a curve rather than straight lines.","Each strip between two readings is a trapezium, so find the area of each and add them.","Apply ½(a + b)h to the first strip, with a and b the velocities at its two ends and h = 2 s."],"codes":["A15"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write down the gradient of any line perpendicular to y = 2x − 9.","a":"−1/2 (−0.5)"},{"q":"Write y = x² + 6x + 1 in the form y = (x + p)² + q.","a":"y = (x + 3)² − 8"},{"q":"Write down the radius of the circle x² + y² = 81.","a":"9"},{"q":"The graph of y = f(x) has a maximum point at (1, 6). Where is the maximum point on the graph of y = f(x) + 4?","a":"(1, 10)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Perpendicular gradients","back":"If two lines are perpendicular their gradients multiply to −1: m₁ × m₂ = −1, so each gradient is the negative reciprocal of the other.","code":"A9","open":false},{"front":"Negative reciprocal","back":"Turn the fraction upside down and change its sign. The negative reciprocal of 2/3 is −3/2, of −4 is 1/4, and of 1 is −1.","code":"A9","open":false},{"front":"Completed square form of a quadratic","back":"y = (x + p)² + q. The turning point is at (−p, q) and the line of symmetry is x = −p.","code":"A11","open":false},{"front":"Completing the square: the method","back":"Halve the coefficient of x to fill the bracket, expand the bracket, then add or subtract a constant so the expression matches the original.","code":"A11","open":false},{"front":"Graph of y = kˣ for k greater than 1","back":"An exponential curve through (0, 1), rising ever more steeply to the right and approaching the x-axis as an asymptote on the left. It never reaches zero.","code":"A12","open":false},{"front":"Graph of y = sin x","back":"A wave of period 360° through (0, 0), rising to 1 at 90°, back to 0 at 180°, down to −1 at 270° and 0 at 360°. It repeats for angles of any size.","code":"A12","open":false},{"front":"Graph of y = cos x","back":"The sine wave shifted 90° to the left: it starts at (0, 1), is 0 at 90° and 270°, reaches −1 at 180° and returns to 1 at 360°. Period 360°.","code":"A12","open":false},{"front":"Graph of y = tan x","back":"Period 180°, passing through (0, 0), climbing through every value and broken by vertical asymptotes at 90°, 270° and every 180° from there.","code":"A12","open":false},{"front":"y = f(x) + a and y = f(x + a)","back":"f(x) + a translates the curve a units up, vector (0, a). f(x + a) translates it a units to the left, vector (−a, 0).","code":"A13","open":false},{"front":"y = −f(x) and y = f(−x)","back":"−f(x) reverses the sign of every y value, a reflection in the x-axis. f(−x) reverses the sign of every x value, a reflection in the y-axis.","code":"A13","open":false},{"front":"Exponential growth and decay model","back":"y = abˣ, where a is the starting value at x = 0 and b is the multiplier per unit of x. b greater than 1 gives growth, b between 0 and 1 gives decay.","code":"A14","open":false},{"front":"Gradient of a curve at a point","back":"Draw a tangent touching the curve at that point and find the tangent's gradient from two points on it. It gives the instantaneous rate of change there.","code":"A15","open":false},{"front":"Area under a velocity-time graph","back":"The distance travelled. Estimate it by splitting the region into triangles, rectangles and trapezia, or by counting squares, then adding the parts.","code":"A15","open":false},{"front":"Equation of a circle centred on the origin","back":"x² + y² = r², where r is the radius. A point lies on the circle exactly when its coordinates satisfy the equation.","code":"A16","open":false},{"front":"Tangent to a circle at a point","back":"It touches the circle there and is perpendicular to the radius drawn to that point, so its gradient is the negative reciprocal of the radius' gradient.","code":"A16","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Perpendicular lines: m₁ × m₂ = −1, so the perpendicular gradient is the negative reciprocal. Not in any of the three lists in the specification's Appendix, and not printed on the Higher tier insert (Appendix note, section 4, Higher tier insert), so it is not given on the day: recall it.","Equation of a straight line: y = mx + c, with m the gradient and c the y-intercept. Not in the Appendix, and not on the Higher tier insert, so it is not given on the day: recall it.","Gradient between two points: m = (y₂ − y₁) ÷ (x₂ − x₁). Not in the Appendix, and not on the Higher tier insert, so it is not given on the day: recall it.","Completed square form: y = (x + p)² + q, with turning point (−p, q) and line of symmetry x = −p. Not in the Appendix, and not on the Higher tier insert, so it is not given on the day: recall it.","Circle centred on the origin: x² + y² = r², with r the radius. Not in the Appendix, and not on the Higher tier insert, so it is not given on the day: recall it.","Tangent to a circle: the tangent at a point is perpendicular to the radius drawn to that point, so its gradient is the negative reciprocal of the radius' gradient. Not in the Appendix, and not on the Higher tier insert: recall it.","Vertical translation of a graph: y = f(x) + a is y = f(x) translated by the vector (0, a), so it moves up when a is positive. Not in the Appendix, and not on the Higher tier insert: recall it.","Horizontal translation of a graph: y = f(x + a) is y = f(x) translated by the vector (−a, 0), so it moves left when a is positive. Not in the Appendix, and not on the Higher tier insert: recall it.","Reflection in the x-axis: y = −f(x) is y = f(x) reflected in the x-axis, so every y-coordinate changes sign. Not in the Appendix, and not on the Higher tier insert: recall it.","Reflection in the y-axis: y = f(−x) is y = f(x) reflected in the y-axis, so every x-coordinate changes sign. Not in the Appendix, and not on the Higher tier insert: recall it.","Exponential growth and decay: y = abˣ, with a the value at x = 0 and b the multiplier per unit of x. Not in the Appendix in this form, and not on the Higher tier insert: recall it.","Speed from a distance-time graph: speed = distance ÷ time, which is the gradient of the graph. Not in the Appendix, and not on the Higher tier insert: recall it.","Acceleration from a velocity-time graph: acceleration = change in velocity ÷ time taken, which is the gradient of the graph. Not in the Appendix, and not on the Higher tier insert: recall it.","Rate of change on a curve: the gradient of the tangent drawn at a point is the rate of change at that point. Not in the Appendix, and not on the Higher tier insert: recall it.","Area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, used here for each strip when estimating the area under a curve. The Appendix puts this in list 2, 'expected to know the following formulae or be able to derive them; they will not be given in the exam'. Section 4 of the Appendix note records it as printed on the Higher tier insert, so on the day the sheet governs and it IS given — but derive or recall it anyway, because the sheet is reissued each series.","Compound interest, Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, is the same repeated multiplier as the exponential growth model used in financial contexts here. The Appendix puts it in list 2, not given in the exam; section 4 records it as printed on the Higher tier insert, so the sheet governs and it IS given on the day.","The quadratic formula, x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) ÷ 2a, is in list 1 of the Appendix ('expected to know… will not be given in the exam'), but section 4 records it on the Higher tier insert, so the sheet governs and it IS given on the day. It is not needed in this room, where turning points come from completing the square rather than from the roots.","The kinematics formulae v = u + at, s = ut + ½at² and v² = u² + 2as are in list 3 of the Appendix, which says they 'will be given in the exam in the relevant question'. They are not needed for the graphical work of A14 and A15: acceleration comes from the gradient of a velocity-time graph and distance from the area under it.","Since 2022 Ofqual has required a formulae sheet in every GCSE Mathematics paper, and AQA issues one for each tier. This is a Higher tier room, so the Higher insert is the one that matters; section 4 of the Appendix note lists what it prints: the trapezium, the prism, the circumference and area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios, the quadratic formula, the sine rule, the cosine rule, the area of a triangle from two sides and the included angle, compound interest, and the addition and conditional multiplication rules for probability. Nothing in items 1 to 14 above is on it, so those results are written from memory. The inserts are reissued each series, so check the sheet at the front of the paper before relying on recall."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:35:38.862415+00:00","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":1,"notes":"Re-judged after the fix pass: all five earlier problems are properly repaired and nothing new was found, so the room is publishable as it stands."}}},"algebra-equations-higher":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"algebra-equations-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"At Higher tier the equations stop being tidy. A quadratic may not factorise at all, so you need completing the square and the quadratic formula; a line may cut a curve, so a pair of equations may be linear and quadratic; a cubic may have no algebraic method at all, so you iterate towards its root; and an inequality may be quadratic or in two variables, so the answer is a region or a set rather than a single number. Algebra is 30% of the Higher assessment, and these four techniques are examined in every series, on Paper 1 in exact surd form and on Papers 2 and 3 with a calculator to a stated accuracy. They also feed the rest of the course: the discriminant decides whether a line is a tangent, and a quadratic inequality is how you answer when a height, an area or a profit has to stay above a given value.","idea":[{"h":"Rearranging, completing the square and the quadratic formula","t":"Every method for a quadratic starts in the same place: collect all the terms on one side so the equation reads ax² + bx + c = 0. Only then do a, b and c mean anything. If it factorises, factorise; if it does not, you have two other routes. Completing the square rewrites x² + bx + c as (x + b/2)² − (b/2)² + c: halve the coefficient of x for the bracket, then take off the square of that half. Solving from there is a square-root step, (x + p)² = q so x = −p ± √q, and both signs must appear. When the x² coefficient is not 1, take it out of the first two terms first, as in 4x² + 24x + 7 = 4(x + 3)² − 29. The quadratic formula x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / 2a always works; substitute a, b and c with their signs, and keep the bracket round a negative value so that −4 × 3 × (−1) comes out positive. The number under the root, b² − 4ac, is worth a glance on its own: positive means two roots, zero means one repeated root, negative means no real solutions. On Paper 1 the answer stays exact, as 3 ± √7; on Papers 2 and 3 it is rounded to the accuracy the question names, so keep the full display until the last line.","code":"A18"},{"h":"A line and a curve: linear/quadratic simultaneous equations","t":"Two linear equations are solved by elimination, but a linear and a quadratic equation are solved by substitution, because the quadratic will not line up for adding. Rearrange the linear equation to give one letter on its own, substitute that expression into the quadratic, and you are left with a single quadratic in one unknown: from y = x² + 1 and y = 2x + 4 you get x² + 1 = 2x + 4, and so x² − 2x − 3 = 0. Solve it by any of the methods above, then substitute each value back into the linear equation — always the linear one, since it is easier and cannot introduce extra roots — to find the matching value of the other letter. The answers must be given as pairs, so x = 3 with y = 10 and x = −1 with y = 2, never as four loose numbers. Graphically the pairs are the points where the line meets the curve, so there are usually two; if the quadratic has a repeated root the line is a tangent, and if b² − 4ac is negative the line misses the curve altogether. That last fact is how tangency questions are set.","code":"A19"},{"h":"Iteration: finding a root when no algebraic method exists","t":"Most cubics cannot be solved by algebra, so you close in on a root numerically. First show one is there: work out f(a) and f(b) at the two ends of the interval, and if the answers have opposite signs the continuous graph must cross the x-axis between them, so a root lies between a and b. Say so in words — the change of sign is the mark. Then rearrange the equation into the form x = g(x): from x³ − 5x + 1 = 0 you can make 5x = x³ + 1 and so x = (x³ + 1)/5, which gives the iterative formula xₙ₊₁ = (xₙ³ + 1)/5. Suffix notation is examined here: x₀ is the starting value, xₙ the value you have, xₙ₊₁ the next one. Feed each answer straight back in using the calculator's answer key, so no accuracy is lost, and keep going until consecutive values agree to the accuracy asked for; only then round. Writing x₁, x₂ and x₃ down is usually worth marks in itself, and a question often asks for them to more decimal places than the final root.","code":"A20"},{"h":"Quadratic inequalities, two variables, set notation and regions","t":"A quadratic inequality is solved through the sketch, not through the algebra alone. Rearrange so one side is zero, factorise to find the critical values where the expression is zero, then picture the curve: with a positive x² term it is U-shaped, below the axis between its two roots and above the axis outside them. So x² − 4x − 5 < 0 has critical values −1 and 5 and the solution −1 < x < 5, while x² − 9 > 0 gives the two separate pieces x < −3 or x > 3. Writing the answer the wrong way round is the commonest error, and dividing an inequality by a negative number still reverses the sign. Set notation is required at Higher tier: {x : −1 < x < 5} for one piece, {x : x < −3} ∪ {x : x > 3} for two. On a number line an open circle marks a strict inequality and a closed circle an included endpoint. With two variables the solution is a region: draw each boundary line, dashed for < or > and solid for ≤ or ≥, then test a point such as the origin in each inequality to decide which side to shade.","code":"A22"}],"examples":[{"title":"Rearranging first, then factorising","setup":"Solve the equation 2x² + 7x = 15, giving both solutions exactly. No calculator may be used.","steps":["Subtract 15 from both sides so the equation is in the form ax² + bx + c = 0: 2x² + 7x − 15 = 0","Look for two numbers with product a × c = 2 × (−15) = −30 and sum b = 7: they are 10 and −3","Split the middle term and factorise in pairs: 2x² + 10x − 3x − 15 = 2x(x + 5) − 3(x + 5) = (x + 5)(2x − 3) = 0","A product is zero when a factor is zero: x + 5 = 0 or 2x − 3 = 0","So the solutions are x = −5 and x = 3/2"],"cues":["What has to be true of the right-hand side before a, b and c can be read off?","Which pair of numbers is worth hunting for once a is not 1?","How are those two numbers used on the middle term?","What does a product of zero tell you about the factors?","What does each small equation give?"]},{"title":"Completing the square to solve exactly","setup":"Solve x² − 6x + 2 = 0 by completing the square, giving your answers in exact surd form. No calculator may be used.","steps":["Halve the coefficient of x, which gives −3, so the bracket is (x − 3)² and that expands to x² − 6x + 9","Take off the extra 9 to keep the value the same: x² − 6x + 2 = (x − 3)² − 9 + 2","Collect the numbers, so the equation becomes (x − 3)² − 7 = 0, that is (x − 3)² = 7","Square-root both sides, keeping both signs: x − 3 = ±√7","Add 3 to both sides: x = 3 + √7 or x = 3 − √7"],"cues":["Which number goes inside the bracket, and where does it come from?","Why can the bracket not simply replace the first two terms?","What does the equation look like once the numbers are collected?","What must be written on the right when a square root is taken?","What single step finishes the solution?"]},{"title":"Using the quadratic formula to a stated accuracy","setup":"Solve 3x² − 5x − 1 = 0 by using the quadratic formula. Give both solutions to 2 decimal places. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Read off the coefficients for x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / 2a, keeping their signs: a = 3, b = −5, c = −1","Work out the value under the root: b² − 4ac = (−5)² − 4 × 3 × (−1) = 25 + 12 = 37","Substitute into the formula: x = (5 ± √37) / 6","Work out each branch in turn: (5 + 6.0827…) / 6 = 1.8471… and (5 − 6.0827…) / 6 = −0.1804…","Rounded as asked: x = 1.85 or x = −0.18"],"cues":["What sign does c carry here, and why does it matter?","Which part of the formula is safest to work out on its own first?","What does the formula look like once the three values are in?","How many values does the ± sign produce?","What still has to be done to the two values?"]},{"title":"A line meeting a curve","setup":"Solve the simultaneous equations y = x² + 1 and y = 2x + 4 algebraically, giving both pairs of values. No calculator may be used.","steps":["Each right-hand side is equal to y, so they are equal to each other: x² + 1 = 2x + 4","Collect every term on the left so the quadratic reads zero on the right: x² − 2x − 3 = 0","Factorise, using the pair with product −3 and sum −2: (x − 3)(x + 1) = 0","Each factor gives a root: x = 3 or x = −1","Put each root into the linear equation y = 2x + 4: when x = 3, y = 10; when x = −1, y = 2"],"cues":["What do the two equations have in common that lets them be joined?","What form does the single equation have to be put into?","Which pair of numbers factorises it?","What does each bracket give on its own?","Why is the linear equation the better one to substitute into?"]},{"title":"Locating a root and iterating to it","setup":"Show that x³ − 5x + 1 = 0 has a root between 0 and 1, then use the iterative formula xₙ₊₁ = (xₙ³ + 1)/5 with x₀ = 0 to find that root to three decimal places. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Write f(x) = x³ − 5x + 1 and test the ends of the interval: f(0) = 1 and f(1) = 1 − 5 + 1 = −3","The two values have opposite signs and the cubic is continuous, so its graph crosses the x-axis between them and a root lies there","Start the iteration by putting x₀ into the formula: x₁ = (0³ + 1)/5 = 0.2","Feed each answer back in: x₂ = (0.2³ + 1)/5 = 0.2016 and x₃ = (0.2016³ + 1)/5 = 0.2016387…","x₄ = 0.2016396…, which agrees with x₃ to three decimal places, so the root is x = 0.202"],"cues":["What two values would show a root is trapped in the interval?","What is it about the pair of answers that proves a crossing?","Which value starts the iteration, and where does it go?","What becomes the input for the next line?","When is it safe to stop and round?"]},{"title":"A quadratic inequality and its solution set","setup":"Solve the inequality x² − 4x − 5 < 0 and give the solution set in set notation. No calculator may be used.","steps":["Factorise the left-hand side, using the pair with product −5 and sum −4: (x − 5)(x + 1) < 0","The expression is zero at the critical values x = 5 and x = −1","Picture y = (x − 5)(x + 1): the x² term is positive, so the curve is U-shaped and crosses the axis at those two values","Less than zero means below the axis, and a U-shaped curve is below the axis only between its crossings: −1 < x < 5","Written in set notation the answer is {x : −1 < x < 5}"],"cues":["What has to happen to the expression before the critical values appear?","What are the critical values, and what do they mean?","Which way up is the curve, and how do you know?","Which part of the sketch does the inequality sign point to?","How is a single stretch of values written as a set?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write x² + 8x + 3 in the form (x + a)² + b.","a":"(x + 4)² − 13"},{"q":"Solve x² − 2x − 4 = 0 by completing the square, giving exact answers.","a":"x = 1 + √5 or x = 1 − √5"},{"q":"Solve y = x² and y = x + 6 simultaneously.","a":"x = 3, y = 9 and x = −2, y = 4"},{"q":"Solve (x − 4)(x + 2) ≥ 0.","a":"x ≤ −2 or x ≥ 4"},{"q":"Using xₙ₊₁ = 4 − 1/xₙ with x₀ = 3, work out x₁ and x₂ to three decimal places.","a":"x₁ = 3.667 and x₂ = 3.727"}]},"room":{"facts":["The quadratic formula: the solutions of ax² + bx + c = 0, where a ≠ 0, are x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / 2a — rearrange to = 0 before reading off a, b and c with their signs.","Completing the square: x² + bx + c = (x + b/2)² − (b/2)² + c; when the x² coefficient is not 1, take it out of the x² and x terms first.","The square-root step: (x + p)² = q gives x + p = ±√q, so x = −p ± √q — both signs must be written, and √q stays as a surd for an exact answer.","The discriminant b² − 4ac: positive gives two different roots, zero gives one repeated root, negative gives no real solutions; when the quadratic comes from setting a line equal to a curve, that one repeated root means the line meets the curve at exactly one point, so it is a tangent.","A linear and a quadratic equation: substitute the linear one into the quadratic, solve the single quadratic that results, then substitute each value back into the linear equation and give the answers as pairs.","Iteration: rearrange f(x) = 0 into x = g(x) and use xₙ₊₁ = g(xₙ) from a starting value x₀, keeping the full display each time and rounding only when consecutive values agree.","Sign change: if f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs and the graph is continuous between them, a root of f(x) = 0 lies between a and b.","Quadratic inequality: factorise, find the critical values, then read the U-shaped sketch — below the axis between the roots, above it outside them; set notation writes this as {x : −1 < x < 5} or {x : x < −3} ∪ {x : x > 3}.","Conventions: an open circle on a number line and a dashed line on a graph for < or >, a closed circle and a solid line for ≤ or ≥."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve x² + 10x + 18 = 0 by completing the square. Give your answers in exact form.","a":"x = −5 + √7 or x = −5 − √7","sol":"Halve the coefficient of x: half of 10 is 5, so x² + 10x = (x + 5)² − 25 (M1). The equation becomes (x + 5)² − 25 + 18 = 0, so (x + 5)² = 7 (M1). Square-root both sides, keeping both signs: x + 5 = ±√7, so x = −5 + √7 or x = −5 − √7 (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The left-hand side will not factorise into whole numbers, so rewrite it as a square plus or minus a number.","The number inside the bracket is half the coefficient of x; expanding that bracket overshoots by the square of it, so take that off.","Write x² + 10x as (x + …)² − … and put the result back into the equation before square-rooting."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"Solve 2x² + 6x − 3 = 0 by using the quadratic formula, giving your solutions to 2 decimal places. A calculator may be used.","a":"x = 0.44 or x = −3.44","sol":"With a = 2, b = 6 and c = −3, b² − 4ac = 6² − 4 × 2 × (−3) = 36 + 24 = 60 (M1). Substituting into x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / 2a gives x = (−6 ± √60) / 4 (M1). √60 = 7.7459…, so x = 1.7459…/4 = 0.4364… and x = −13.7459…/4 = −3.4364…, that is x = 0.44 or x = −3.44 to 2 d.p. (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The equation is already in the form ax² + bx + c = 0, so the three coefficients can be read straight off.","Work out the value under the root sign on its own first, taking care with the sign of c.","Write x = (−6 ± √(6² − 4 × 2 × (−3))) / (2 × 2) and then deal with the two branches separately."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write x² + 12x + 7 in the form (x + a)² + b.","a":"(x + 6)² − 29","sol":"Half of 12 is 6, so the bracket is (x + 6)², which expands to x² + 12x + 36 (M1). That is 36 too big and the expression needs + 7, so x² + 12x + 7 = (x + 6)² − 36 + 7 = (x + 6)² − 29 (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The first two terms come from squaring a bracket; only the constant has to be put right afterwards.","Halve the coefficient of x to get the number in the bracket, then compare the expansion with the expression you were given.","Start from (x + …)² and subtract whatever the expansion has too much of before adding the constant term."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"The iterative formula xₙ₊₁ = ∛(2xₙ + 5) with x₀ = 2 is used to find a root of x³ − 2x − 5 = 0. Work out x₁, x₂ and x₃, giving each to 4 decimal places. A calculator may be used.","a":"x₁ = 2.0801, x₂ = 2.0924, x₃ = 2.0942","sol":"Substitute x₀ = 2: x₁ = ∛(2 × 2 + 5) = ∛9 = 2.0800838… (M1), so x₁ = 2.0801 (A1). Feeding each answer back in, x₂ = ∛(2 × 2.0800838… + 5) = 2.0923506… = 2.0924 and x₃ = ∛(2 × 2.0923506… + 5) = 2.0942169… = 2.0942 (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The suffix tells you which value is which: x₀ is where you start and each line produces the next one.","Put the value you have into the right-hand side; the answer that comes out is the input for the following line.","Work out ∛(2 × 2 + 5) first, then use the calculator's answer key so the full value is carried forward."],"codes":["A20"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve the inequality x² − 9 > 0 and give your answer in set notation.","a":"{x : x < −3} ∪ {x : x > 3}","sol":"x² − 9 = (x + 3)(x − 3), so the critical values are x = −3 and x = 3 (M1). The curve y = x² − 9 is U-shaped and crosses the axis at those values, so it is above the axis outside them (M1). The solution is x < −3 or x > 3, written {x : x < −3} ∪ {x : x > 3} (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Find first where the expression is zero; those values split the number line into pieces.","A difference of two squares factorises; then sketch the U-shaped curve and ask where it sits above the axis.","Write x² − 9 = (x + …)(x − …) to get the critical values, then decide which side of each the curve is positive."],"codes":["A22"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve 5x² = 3 − 14x, giving your answers as fractions where appropriate.","a":"x = −3 or x = 1/5","sol":"Collect everything on the left: 5x² + 14x − 3 = 0 (M1). Two numbers with product 5 × (−3) = −15 and sum 14 are 15 and −1, so 5x² + 15x − x − 3 = 5x(x + 3) − (x + 3) = (x + 3)(5x − 1) = 0 (M1). Each factor gives a root: x = −3 or x = 1/5 (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Nothing can be read off until every term is on the same side of the equals sign.","Once it reads ax² + bx + c = 0, hunt for a pair of numbers with product a × c and sum b to split the middle term.","Rearrange to 5x² + 14x − 3 = 0, then split the 14x into two terms and factorise in pairs."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve 3x² + 12x − 2 = 0 by completing the square. Give your answers in exact form.","a":"x = −2 + (√42)/3 or x = −2 − (√42)/3","sol":"Take 3 out of the first two terms: 3(x² + 4x) − 2 (M1). Inside the bracket x² + 4x = (x + 2)² − 4, so the expression is 3[(x + 2)² − 4] − 2 = 3(x + 2)² − 14 (A1). Setting this to zero, 3(x + 2)² = 14 and (x + 2)² = 14/3 (M1). Square-rooting both signs, x + 2 = ±√(14/3) = ±(√42)/3, so x = −2 + (√42)/3 or x = −2 − (√42)/3 (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Completing the square needs a single x² at the front, so deal with the coefficient before anything else.","Take the 3 out of the x² and x terms only, complete the square inside the bracket, then multiply the correction back out.","Write the left-hand side as 3(x² + 4x) − 2 and complete the square on what is inside the bracket."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve the simultaneous equations y = x² − 3x + 4 and y = 2x − 2 algebraically.","a":"x = 2, y = 2 and x = 3, y = 4","sol":"Both expressions equal y, so x² − 3x + 4 = 2x − 2 (M1). Collecting on the left gives x² − 5x + 6 = 0 (A1). Factorising, (x − 2)(x − 3) = 0, so x = 2 or x = 3 (M1). Substituting into y = 2x − 2: x = 2 gives y = 2 and x = 3 gives y = 4, so the solutions are x = 2, y = 2 and x = 3, y = 4 (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Two things equal to the same thing are equal to each other, which removes one of the letters.","Make a single quadratic equal to zero, solve it, and remember that each x has a partner y.","Put x² − 3x + 4 equal to 2x − 2 and collect all the terms on one side."],"codes":["A19"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve the simultaneous equations x + y = 7 and x² + y² = 29.","a":"x = 2, y = 5 and x = 5, y = 2","sol":"Rearrange the linear equation: y = 7 − x (M1). Substitute into the quadratic: x² + (7 − x)² = 29 (M1). Expanding and collecting, x² + 49 − 14x + x² = 29, so 2x² − 14x + 20 = 0, that is x² − 7x + 10 = 0 (A1). Factorising, (x − 2)(x − 5) = 0, so x = 2 or x = 5 (M1). Pairing with y = 7 − x gives x = 2, y = 5 and x = 5, y = 2 (A1). Total 5 marks.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Only one of the two equations can be rearranged safely for a single letter; use that one.","Substitute the expression for y into the second equation and expand the bracket carefully, including the middle term.","Write y = 7 − x and replace y in x² + y² = 29 with that expression."],"codes":["A19"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve 2x² + 5x − 3 ≤ 0 and give the solution set in set notation.","a":"{x : −3 ≤ x ≤ 1/2}","sol":"Factorise: (2x − 1)(x + 3) ≤ 0 (M1). The critical values are x = 1/2 and x = −3 (A1). The curve y = 2x² + 5x − 3 is U-shaped, so it is on or below the axis between the critical values (M1). The solution set is {x : −3 ≤ x ≤ 1/2} (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The values where the expression is zero are the boundaries of the answer; everything depends on them.","Factorise, find the two critical values and sketch the curve to see where it is on or below the axis.","Write the left-hand side as (2x − …)(x + …) ≤ 0 and set each bracket to zero to get the boundaries."],"codes":["A22"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A region R on a graph is defined by y ≤ 2x, x + y < 6 and y > 1. State which of the points A(3, 2), B(1, 3) and C(4, 2) lie in R, and say which of the three boundary lines are drawn dashed.","a":"Only A(3, 2) lies in R; the lines x + y = 6 and y = 1 are dashed.","sol":"Test A(3, 2): 2 ≤ 2 × 3 is true, 3 + 2 = 5 < 6 is true and 2 > 1 is true (M1 for testing a point in all three inequalities), so A lies in R (A1). Test B(1, 3): 3 ≤ 2 × 1 is false, so B is outside; test C(4, 2): 4 + 2 = 6 is not less than 6, so C is on an excluded boundary and is outside (A1). A strict inequality has a dashed boundary, so x + y = 6 and y = 1 are dashed while y = 2x is solid (B1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["A point is in the region only if every one of the inequalities holds for it.","Substitute the coordinates of each point into all three inequalities in turn and see which one fails.","For A, check whether 2 ≤ 2 × 3, whether 3 + 2 is less than 6 and whether 2 is greater than 1."],"codes":["A22"]},{"q":"Show that x³ − 4x + 1 = 0 has a solution between x = 1.8 and x = 1.9. A calculator may be used.","a":"f(1.8) = −0.368 and f(1.9) = 0.259, a change of sign, so a root lies between them.","sol":"Let f(x) = x³ − 4x + 1. Then f(1.8) = 5.832 − 7.2 + 1 = −0.368 and f(1.9) = 6.859 − 7.6 + 1 = 0.259 (M1 for both values). One value is negative and the other positive, and the graph of the cubic is continuous, so it crosses the x-axis between 1.8 and 1.9 and a solution lies there (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["A graph that is below the axis at one value and above it at another must have crossed in between.","Work out the value of the left-hand side at each end of the interval and compare the two signs.","Write f(x) = x³ − 4x + 1 and work out f(1.8), then f(1.9), before commenting."],"codes":["A20"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A rectangle has length (x + 3) cm and width (x − 2) cm, and its area is 36 cm². Work out the perimeter of the rectangle.","a":"26 cm","sol":"Area gives (x + 3)(x − 2) = 36 (M1). Expanding and rearranging, x² + x − 6 = 36, so x² + x − 42 = 0 (M1). Factorising, (x + 7)(x − 6) = 0, so x = 6 or x = −7 (A1). x = −7 would make both sides negative, so x = 6 and the rectangle is 9 cm by 4 cm (M1). Perimeter = 2 × (9 + 4) = 26 cm (A1). Total 5 marks.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Turn the information about the area into an equation in x before thinking about the perimeter.","Expand the product, make the equation equal to zero and solve it; one of the two roots cannot describe a real rectangle.","Write (x + 3)(x − 2) = 36 and multiply out the brackets."],"codes":["A18"]},{"q":"Starting with x₀ = 1.5, use the iterative formula xₙ₊₁ = √(11/(xₙ + 3)) to find a root of x³ + 3x² − 11 = 0 to 3 decimal places. A calculator may be used.","a":"x = 1.554","sol":"x₁ = √(11/(1.5 + 3)) = √2.4444… = 1.5634719… (M1). x₂ = √(11/4.5634719…) = 1.5525609… and x₃ = √(11/4.5525609…) = 1.5544203… (A1). Continuing, x₄ = 1.5541029…, x₅ = 1.5541571… and x₆ = 1.5541478…, so the values have settled to three decimal places (M1). The root is x = 1.554 (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Each answer becomes the next input, so the calculator's answer key does the work.","Keep iterating until two consecutive values round to the same three decimal places, and only then write the root down.","Work out √(11/(1.5 + 3)) first and keep the full display for the next line."],"codes":["A20"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The line y = 2x + c is a tangent to the curve y = x² − 4x + 13. Work out the value of c.","a":"c = 4","sol":"Where the line meets the curve, x² − 4x + 13 = 2x + c (M1). Collecting on the left, x² − 6x + 13 − c = 0 (A1). A tangent touches at exactly one point, so this quadratic has one repeated root and b² − 4ac = 0: (−6)² − 4 × 1 × (13 − c) = 0, that is 36 − 52 + 4c = 0 (M1). So 4c = 16 and c = 4 (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A tangent meets a curve at exactly one point, so think about how many solutions the pair of equations has.","Set the two expressions for y equal, collect them into a quadratic, and use what a single repeated root means for the value under the root sign.","Write x² − 4x + 13 = 2x + c and collect every term on the left-hand side."],"codes":["A18","A19"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The height of a ball t seconds after it is thrown is h metres, where h = 20t − 5t². For which values of t is the ball more than 15 m above the ground?","a":"1 < t < 3","sol":"The condition is 20t − 5t² > 15 (M1). Rearranging so the t² term is positive, 0 > 5t² − 20t + 15, and dividing by 5 gives t² − 4t + 3 < 0 (M1). Factorising, (t − 1)(t − 3) < 0, so the critical values are t = 1 and t = 3 (A1). A U-shaped curve is below the axis only between its roots, so the ball is more than 15 m up for 1 < t < 3, that is between 1 and 3 seconds after the throw (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The words more than turn the formula into an inequality rather than an equation.","Collect the terms so that the t² coefficient is positive, then treat it like any quadratic inequality using the critical values and the sketch.","Write 20t − 5t² > 15 and move every term to the side that leaves a positive t² term."],"codes":["A22"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write x² − 8x + 1 in the form (x + a)² + b.","a":"(x − 4)² − 15"},{"q":"Solve x² = 5x + 14.","a":"x = 7 or x = −2"},{"q":"Solve the inequality (x − 2)(x + 6) < 0.","a":"−6 < x < 2"},{"q":"Using xₙ₊₁ = (xₙ² + 3)/5 with x₀ = 1, work out x₁ and x₂.","a":"x₁ = 0.8 and x₂ = 0.728"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The quadratic formula","back":"The solutions of ax² + bx + c = 0, where a ≠ 0, are x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / 2a. Rearrange to = 0 before reading off a, b and c.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"Completing the square for x² + bx + c","back":"x² + bx + c = (x + b/2)² − (b/2)² + c. Halve the coefficient of x for the bracket, then subtract the square of that half.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"Completing the square when the x² coefficient is not 1","back":"Take a out of the x² and x terms first, complete the square inside, then multiply out: 2x² + 8x + 1 = 2(x + 2)² − 8 + 1 = 2(x + 2)² − 7.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"The square-root step","back":"(x + p)² = q gives x + p = ±√q, so x = −p ± √q. Both signs must be written, and √q is left as a surd when an exact answer is wanted.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"The discriminant b² − 4ac","back":"The part under the root sign. Positive gives two different roots, zero gives one repeated root, negative gives no real solutions.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"Rearranging before solving a quadratic","back":"Collect every term on one side so the equation reads ax² + bx + c = 0. Only then can you factorise, complete the square or use the formula.","code":"A18","open":false},{"front":"Give an example of a quadratic equation with no real solutions, and say how you know","back":"Any equation whose discriminant is negative: x² + x + 1 = 0, since b² − 4ac = 1 − 4 = −3; x² + 4 = 0, since 0 − 16 = −16 and no real number squares to −4; 2x² − 3x + 5 = 0, since 9 − 40 = −31.","code":"A18","open":true},{"front":"Solving a linear and a quadratic equation together","back":"Rearrange the linear equation for one letter, substitute it into the quadratic, solve the quadratic that results, then substitute each value back to find its partner.","code":"A19","open":false},{"front":"How many solutions a line and a curve have","back":"The quadratic formed has two roots where the line cuts the curve twice, one repeated root where the line is a tangent, and no real roots where they never meet.","code":"A19","open":false},{"front":"Iteration","back":"Rearrange f(x) = 0 into the form x = g(x), then use xₙ₊₁ = g(xₙ), feeding each answer back in until consecutive values agree to the accuracy required.","code":"A20","open":false},{"front":"Suffix notation in a recursive formula","back":"x₀ is the starting value, xₙ the value you have and xₙ₊₁ the next one, worked out from xₙ. Keep the full calculator value each time and round only at the end.","code":"A20","open":false},{"front":"The sign change test","back":"If f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs and the graph of y = f(x) is continuous between them, then f(x) = 0 has a root between a and b.","code":"A20","open":false},{"front":"Solving a quadratic inequality","back":"Rearrange to make one side zero, factorise, find the critical values, then sketch: a U-shaped curve is below the axis between its roots and above it outside them.","code":"A22","open":false},{"front":"Set notation for a solution set","back":"{x : −1 < x < 5} means the values of x between −1 and 5. Two separate stretches are joined with ∪, as in {x : x < −3} ∪ {x : x > 3}.","code":"A22","open":false},{"front":"Boundary conventions for inequalities","back":"A number line uses an open circle for < or > and a closed circle for ≤ or ≥. On a graph the boundary is dashed for a strict inequality and solid when it is included.","code":"A22","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["What the Higher tier paper prints for this room: the quadratic formula, and nothing else this room uses. Section 4 of the course appendix records the results named on AQA's Higher tier formulae insert for exams from 2026, and the quadratic formula is on that list. The specification's own Appendix puts it in list 1 — the formulae students are expected to know, which will not be given in the exam — so the two disagree; the later sheet governs what is in front of you on the day, and the sheet says it is given. Learn it anyway: the sheet is issued outside this specification and reissued every series, and it prints no rule for completing the square, for simultaneous equations, for iteration or for inequalities.","The quadratic formula (given on the Higher tier sheet, but learn it): the solutions of ax² + bx + c = 0, where a ≠ 0, are x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / 2a.","Completing the square (recall): x² + bx + c = (x + b/2)² − (b/2)² + c.","Completing the square with a leading coefficient (recall): ax² + bx + c = a(x² + (b/a)x) + c, then complete the square inside the bracket and multiply the correction back out.","The square-root step (recall): (x + p)² = q gives x + p = ±√q, so x = −p ± √q.","The discriminant (recall): b² − 4ac > 0 gives two distinct roots, b² − 4ac = 0 one repeated root, b² − 4ac < 0 no real roots.","Linear/quadratic simultaneous equations (recall): substitute the rearranged linear equation into the quadratic, solve the resulting quadratic, then substitute each solution back into the linear equation and give the answers as pairs.","Iterative formula and suffix notation (recall): rearrange f(x) = 0 into x = g(x) and use xₙ₊₁ = g(xₙ) from the given x₀.","The sign change test (recall): f(a) and f(b) of opposite signs, with f continuous on the interval, means a root of f(x) = 0 lies between a and b.","Quadratic inequality (recall): factorise to the critical values, then read the U-shaped sketch — the expression is negative between the roots and positive outside them.","Inequality sign rule (recall): multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative number reverses <, >, ≤ and ≥.","Set notation (recall): {x : −1 < x < 5} for one stretch of values and {x : x < −3} ∪ {x : x > 3} for two.","Number line and region conventions (recall): open circle and dashed line for a strict inequality, closed circle and solid line for ≤ or ≥; test a point such as the origin to decide which side of a boundary to shade."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:30:25Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Every answer key checks out: all 16 bank questions, the 5 lesson checks and the 4 exit-ticket items were re-solved independently in sympy (exact surds for x² + 10x + 18 = 0 and 3x² + 12x − 2 = 0, the discriminant c = 4 tangency, all iterations run to x₆, and every inequality solved as a set), the sol mark tallies add exactly to each m, and the Formulae sheet's claim is right — section 4 of appendix.md lists the quadratic formula on the Higher tier insert and nothing else this room uses, and the extra correctly says the specification's own Appendix list 1 disagrees and that the later sheet governs."}}},"algebra-sequences-higher":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"algebra-sequences-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"At Higher tier, sequences stop being a matter of adding the same number each time. The two Higher-only demands are worth marks on every series: deducing the nth term of a quadratic sequence from its constant second difference, and working with geometric progressions whose common ratio is a surd such as √2, where the terms swing between whole numbers and surds. Any other recursive sequence — a rule that builds each term from the one or two before it — is defined for you in the question, so the skill being tested is reading the rule and applying it accurately. Algebra carries 30% of the Higher assessment, and these questions appear as short one-line asks, as patterns of tiles or slabs, and as AO3 problems that fold in surds, quadratic equations and proof.","idea":[{"h":"Geometric progressions with a surd common ratio","t":"A geometric progression multiplies by a fixed common ratio r each time, so its nth term is arⁿ⁻¹ and r is found by dividing any term by the one immediately before it. At Higher tier r may be a surd. In 2, 2√2, 4, 4√2, 8, … the ratio is √2, and because √2 × √2 = 2 every second step doubles the term: the whole numbers and the surd terms alternate. Keep answers exact — 4√2 rather than 5.657 — and simplify surds with √a × √a = a, √12 = 2√3 and the like. If the ratio comes out as a fraction with a surd underneath, rationalise it: 1/√2 = √2/2. A ratio less than 1 makes the terms shrink, which is how these appear in shrinking-square and halving-area problems.","code":"A24"},{"h":"Other sequences, and recursive rules defined in the question","t":"Higher papers also set sequences that are none of the named families. The specification is explicit that any other recursive sequence will be defined in the question, so nothing has to be remembered — read the rule, note which terms it needs, and apply it one term at a time. A rule such as uₙ₊₁ = 2uₙ − 3 with u₁ = 5 builds each term from the single term before it; a rule such as uₙ₊₂ = uₙ₊₁ + 2uₙ needs two starting terms. Rules can involve squaring, reciprocals or fractions, as in uₙ₊₁ = 1/(1 + uₙ), so keep answers as exact fractions rather than rounding, and set the working out term by term with each term labelled — every intermediate term is where the method marks sit.","code":"A24"},{"h":"Deducing the nth term of a quadratic sequence","t":"A quadratic sequence has first differences that are not constant but change by the same amount each time, so its second differences are all equal. That constant second difference is twice the coefficient of n²: second difference = 2a. So halve it to get the an² part, write out the values of an² (for a = 1 those are 1, 4, 9, 16, 25), subtract them term by term from the sequence, and what is left is a linear sequence whose nth term dn + (a − d) you already know how to find from the Foundation room. Adding the two parts gives an² + bn + c. Always check the finished expression on n = 1 and on one later position before writing it down; the mark scheme gives a method mark for the second difference or the an² part, a second method mark for the subtraction, and the accuracy mark for the full expression in n.","code":"A25"},{"h":"Using a quadratic nth term: far-off terms and testing a value","t":"Once the expression is found, any position is one substitution away — the 10th term of 3n² − 2 is 3 × 100 − 2 = 298, with no need to continue the sequence. The question is often turned round: is a given number a term, and if so which one? Set the expression equal to that number, collect everything on one side and solve the quadratic by factorising or with the quadratic formula. Because n counts positions, it must come out as a positive whole number: a negative or fractional root is rejected, and if neither root is a positive whole number then the value is not a term at all. Say which position you have found, not just the value of n, and check by substituting back.","code":"A25"}],"examples":[{"title":"A geometric progression with a surd common ratio","setup":"A geometric progression begins 2, 2√2, 4, … and its common ratio is a surd. Work out the 7th term of the progression, giving your answer exactly.","steps":["Divide a term by the one before it: 2√2 ÷ 2 = √2, so the common ratio is r = √2.","Multiply the third term by the ratio: 4th term = 4 × √2 = 4√2.","Multiply that by the ratio again: 5th term = 4√2 × √2 = 4 × 2 = 8.","Multiply on again: 6th term = 8 × √2 = 8√2, so one more multiplication will reach the 7th term.","8√2 × √2 = 8 × 2 = 16, so the 7th term is 16."],"cues":["Which two terms next to each other would show what the sequence is multiplied by?","What must the third term be multiplied by to reach the 4th?","What does √2 × √2 simplify to?","How many more multiplications by √2 are needed to reach the 7th term?","What happens to a surd term when another factor of √2 meets it?"]},{"title":"Deducing the nth term of a quadratic sequence","setup":"The quadratic sequence 3, 8, 15, 24, 35, … continues in the same way. Deduce an expression for the nth term of the sequence.","steps":["First differences: 8 − 3 = 5, 15 − 8 = 7, 24 − 15 = 9 and 35 − 24 = 11.","Second differences: 7 − 5 = 2, 9 − 7 = 2 and 11 − 9 = 2 — constant, so the n² coefficient is 2 ÷ 2 = 1.","Subtract the values of n², which are 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, from 3, 8, 15, 24, 35 to leave 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, a linear sequence with nth term 2n.","Putting the two parts together, the nth term is n² + 2n; check at n = 4: 16 + 8 = 24, the term given."],"cues":["What is the gap between each pair of terms next to each other?","Do those gaps themselves go up by the same amount each time?","Which square numbers sit in the same positions as these terms?","Which term that you were given could test the finished expression?"]},{"title":"A recursive rule defined in the question","setup":"A sequence is defined by u₁ = 5 and uₙ₊₁ = 2uₙ − 3 for n ≥ 1. Work out u₄.","steps":["Put n = 1 into the rule: u₂ = 2 × 5 − 3 = 10 − 3 = 7.","Put n = 2 into the rule, using the term just found: u₃ = 2 × 7 − 3 = 14 − 3 = 11.","Put n = 3 into the rule, using u₃: u₄ = 2 × 11 − 3 = 22 − 3.","22 − 3 = 19, so u₄ = 19."],"cues":["Which term does the rule need before it can make the next one?","Which value goes into the rule this time?","How many more times must the rule be applied?","What is left to do once the doubling has been done?"]},{"title":"A common ratio that is a surd fraction","setup":"The first three terms of a geometric progression are 8, 4√2, 4. Work out the common ratio in its simplest form and the 5th term of the progression.","steps":["Divide the second term by the first: r = 4√2 ÷ 8 = √2/2 (the rationalised form of 1/√2).","Check the ratio on the next pair: 4√2 × √2/2 = (4 × 2)/2 = 4, which is the third term given.","4th term = 4 × √2/2 = 2√2.","5th term = 2√2 × √2/2 = (2 × 2)/2 = 2, so r = √2/2 and the 5th term is 2."],"cues":["Which division turns the first term into the second?","How can the ratio be tested on a term already printed?","Which term comes straight after the last one given?","What does the 4th term become when it is multiplied by the ratio once more?"]},{"title":"A quadratic sequence in a pattern of slabs","setup":"A patio pattern is built from paving slabs. Pattern 1 uses 5 slabs, pattern 2 uses 12 slabs, pattern 3 uses 23 slabs and pattern 4 uses 38 slabs, and the pattern carries on in the same way. Work out how many slabs pattern 15 uses.","steps":["First differences 12 − 5 = 7, 23 − 12 = 11 and 38 − 23 = 15; second differences 11 − 7 = 4 and 15 − 11 = 4, so the slab counts form a quadratic sequence.","Half the second difference is 4 ÷ 2 = 2, so the nth term starts with 2n², whose values are 2, 8, 18, 32.","Subtracting gives 5 − 2 = 3, 12 − 8 = 4, 23 − 18 = 5 and 38 − 32 = 6, a linear sequence with nth term n + 2, so the nth term is 2n² + n + 2.","Pattern 15 is the term at n = 15, so substitute: 2 × 15² = 2 × 225 = 450.","450 + 15 + 2 = 467, so pattern 15 uses 467 slabs."],"cues":["What are the gaps between the slab counts, and do those gaps change steadily?","What does halving the constant second difference give you?","How do the slab counts compare with the values of 2n² in the same positions?","Which value of n stands for pattern 15?","Which two parts of the expression are still to be added on?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Deduce an expression for the nth term of the quadratic sequence 4, 10, 20, 34, 52.","a":"2n² + 2"},{"q":"The first three terms of a geometric progression are 5, 5√2, 10. Work out the 5th term.","a":"20"},{"q":"A sequence is defined by u₁ = 6 and uₙ₊₁ = uₙ/2 + 1. Work out u₃.","a":"3"},{"q":"Is 36 a term of the sequence with nth term n² + 5n? If it is, give its position.","a":"Yes — it is the 4th term (n = 4)"}]},"room":{"facts":["A quadratic sequence has a constant second difference, and that second difference is twice the coefficient of n² in the nth term: second difference = 2a.","nth term of a quadratic sequence = an² + bn + c: halve the second difference for a, subtract the an² values from the terms, then find the nth term of the linear sequence that is left.","nth term of a geometric progression = arⁿ⁻¹, where a is the first term and r the common ratio; r = any term ÷ the term immediately before it, and at Higher tier r may be a surd.","√a × √a = a, so two steps of a progression with ratio √2 double a term; a ratio such as 1/√2 is rationalised to √2/2.","A recursive sequence is defined by a rule such as uₙ₊₁ = 2uₙ − 3 together with its first term or terms; any unfamiliar recursive sequence will be defined in the question.","nth term of a linear sequence = dn + (a − d), where d is the common difference and a the first term — this is what is needed for the part left after the an² is subtracted.","A number is a term of a sequence only if setting the nth term expression equal to it gives a positive whole-number value of n; a negative or fractional root is rejected."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) The second differences of a quadratic sequence are all 8. Write down the n² term of the expression for its nth term.","a":"4n²","sol":"The constant second difference of a quadratic sequence is twice the coefficient of n², so the coefficient is 8 ÷ 2 = 4 and the n² term is 4n² (B1). Total 1 mark.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The second difference of a quadratic sequence depends only on the n² part of its nth term.","The constant second difference is twice the coefficient of n².","Write the coefficient as the second difference divided by 2."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The nth term of a quadratic sequence is 3n² − n. Work out the first three terms of the sequence.","a":"2, 10, 24","sol":"Substitute the position numbers in turn: n = 1 gives 3 × 1² − 1 = 3 − 1 = 2; n = 2 gives 3 × 2² − 2 = 12 − 2 = 10 (M1 for substituting n = 1, 2 and 3 with the squaring done first); n = 3 gives 3 × 3² − 3 = 27 − 3 = 24. The first three terms are 2, 10 and 24 (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The expression is a position-to-term rule, so the position number goes in place of n.","Substitute n = 1, then n = 2, then n = 3, squaring before multiplying by 3.","Write 3 × 1² − 1 for the first term and treat the other two positions the same way."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The first three terms of a geometric progression are 3, 3√2, 6. Work out the next term of the progression, giving your answer exactly.","a":"6√2","sol":"Divide a term by the one before it: 3√2 ÷ 3 = √2, so the common ratio is r = √2 (M1); the check is 3√2 × √2 = 3 × 2 = 6, the third term given. The next term is 6 × √2 = 6√2 (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["In a geometric progression each term comes from the one before it by multiplying.","Divide a term by the term immediately before it to find what is being multiplied by.","Work out 3√2 ÷ 3 first, then multiply the last term given by that ratio."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"A sequence is defined by u₁ = 1, u₂ = 4 and uₙ₊₂ = uₙ₊₁ + 2uₙ. Work out u₄. A calculator may be used.","a":"14","sol":"With n = 1: u₃ = u₂ + 2u₁ = 4 + 2 × 1 = 6 (M1). With n = 2: u₄ = u₃ + 2u₂ = 6 + 2 × 4 = 6 + 8 = 14 (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Each term here is built from the two terms before it, so work forwards one term at a time.","Use the rule with n = 1 to make u₃, then with n = 2 to make u₄.","Write u₃ = u₂ + 2u₁ and substitute the two terms you were given."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A geometric progression has first term 7 and common ratio √3. Work out the third term.","a":"21","sol":"Two steps of the ratio take the first term to the third, so the third term = 7 × √3 × √3 (M1). Since √3 × √3 = 3, the third term = 7 × 3 = 21 (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Two multiplications by the ratio take the first term to the third.","Multiplying by a surd twice is the same as multiplying by the number under the root sign.","Write the third term as 7 × √3 × √3 and simplify the surd part first."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The first five terms of a quadratic sequence are 2, 9, 20, 35, 54. Deduce an expression for the nth term.","a":"2n² + n − 1","sol":"First differences 7, 11, 15, 19; second differences 4, 4, 4, which are constant (M1). Half of 4 is 2, so the n² part is 2n², with values 2, 8, 18, 32, 50; subtracting these from the sequence leaves 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, a linear sequence with common difference 1 and nth term n − 1 (M1). So the nth term is 2n² + n − 1; check at n = 3: 18 + 3 − 1 = 20, the third term (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Look at the differences between the terms, and then at the differences of those differences.","Half the constant second difference is the coefficient of n²; take that part away from the sequence.","Write the first differences under the sequence, then the second differences under those."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The first four terms of a quadratic sequence are 1, 10, 25, 46. Work out the 10th term of the sequence.","a":"298","sol":"First differences 9, 15, 21; second differences 6, 6, which are constant (M1). Half of 6 is 3, so the n² part is 3n², with values 3, 12, 27, 48; subtracting these leaves −2, −2, −2, −2 (M1). The nth term is therefore 3n² − 2 (A1). At n = 10: 3 × 10² − 2 = 300 − 2 = 298 (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Find the rule for any position before going anywhere near the tenth term.","Halve the constant second difference for the n² part, subtract it, then deal with what is left over.","Write out the first and second differences, then compare the sequence with the values of the n² part."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"The nth term of a quadratic sequence is n² + 4n − 2. Show that 94 is a term of the sequence and state its position. A calculator may be used.","a":"n = 8, so 94 is the 8th term","sol":"Set the expression equal to the value: n² + 4n − 2 = 94, so n² + 4n − 96 = 0 (M1). Factorise: (n + 12)(n − 8) = 0, giving n = −12 or n = 8 (M1). A position must be a positive whole number, so n = 8; checking, 8² + 4 × 8 − 2 = 64 + 32 − 2 = 94, so 94 is the 8th term (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["If a number really is a term, some whole-number position must produce it.","Form an equation by setting the nth term expression equal to the number, then make one side zero.","Write n² + 4n − 2 = 94 and collect every term on one side."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The first three terms of a geometric progression are √5, 5, 5√5. Work out the 6th term.","a":"125","sol":"Divide a term by the one before it: 5 ÷ √5 = √5, because 5 = √5 × √5, so r = √5 (M1). Continuing: 4th term = 5√5 × √5 = 5 × 5 = 25 and 5th term = 25 × √5 = 25√5 (M1). 6th term = 25√5 × √5 = 25 × 5 = 125 (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["First find what each term is multiplied by to get the next one.","Once the ratio is known, keep multiplying term by term until the position asked for is reached.","Write r = 5 ÷ √5 and simplify it, then extend the progression one term at a time."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"A sequence is defined by u₁ = 4 and uₙ₊₁ = uₙ² − 10. Work out u₄. A calculator may be used.","a":"666","sol":"u₂ = 4² − 10 = 16 − 10 = 6 (M1). u₃ = 6² − 10 = 36 − 10 = 26 (M1). u₄ = 26² − 10 = 676 − 10 = 666 (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The rule squares the term you already have before anything is subtracted.","Work out u₂, then use that value for u₃, and use that result again for u₄.","Write u₂ = 4² − 10 and carry the result back into the rule."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The first five terms of a quadratic sequence are 8, 11, 10, 5, −4. Deduce an expression for the nth term.","a":"−2n² + 9n + 1","sol":"First differences 3, −1, −5, −9; second differences −4, −4, −4, which are constant (M1). Half of −4 is −2, so the n² part is −2n², with values −2, −8, −18, −32, −50 (M1). Subtracting these from the terms: 8 − (−2) = 10, 11 − (−8) = 19, 10 − (−18) = 28, 5 − (−32) = 37 and −4 − (−50) = 46, a linear sequence with common difference 9 and nth term 9n + 1 (M1). So the nth term is −2n² + 9n + 1; check at n = 4: −32 + 36 + 1 = 5, the fourth term (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The sequence rises and then falls, so do not assume the second difference is positive.","Half the constant second difference is still the coefficient of n², sign included.","Write the first differences, then their differences, and halve the constant value that appears."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The second term of a geometric progression is 6 and the fourth term is 12. The common ratio is positive. Work out the first term, giving your answer in the form a√b.","a":"3√2","sol":"Two steps of the ratio take the second term to the fourth, so r² = 12 ÷ 6 = 2 (M1). The ratio is positive, so r = √2 (A1). The first term is the second term divided by the ratio: 6 ÷ √2 (M1). Rationalising, 6/√2 = 6√2/2 = 3√2 (A1). Check: 3√2 × √2 = 3 × 2 = 6, the second term. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Two steps of the common ratio separate the two terms you are given.","Squaring the ratio takes the second term to the fourth, so find r² before r.","Write r² = 12 ÷ 6, then divide the second term by the ratio and rationalise."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Sequence A is a quadratic sequence with nth term n² + 3n. Sequence B is a linear sequence with nth term 9n − 5. Work out the two positions at which the two sequences have equal terms.","a":"n = 1 and n = 5","sol":"Equal terms means equal expressions at the same position: n² + 3n = 9n − 5 (M1). Rearrange to n² − 6n + 5 = 0 (M1). Factorise: (n − 1)(n − 5) = 0 (M1), so n = 1 or n = 5 (A1). Check at n = 5: sequence A gives 25 + 15 = 40 and sequence B gives 45 − 5 = 40. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Equal terms means the two expressions take the same value at the same position.","Set the two expressions equal to each other and rearrange into a quadratic equation equal to zero.","Write n² + 3n = 9n − 5 and bring every term to the left-hand side."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A sequence is defined by u₁ = 1 and uₙ₊₁ = 1/(1 + uₙ). Work out u₄, giving your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"3/5","sol":"u₂ = 1/(1 + 1) = 1/2 (M1). u₃ = 1/(1 + 1/2) = 1 ÷ 3/2 = 2/3 (M1). u₄ = 1/(1 + 2/3) = 1 ÷ 5/3 = 3/5 (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Each term is the reciprocal of one more than the term before it.","Add 1 to the term, then turn the resulting fraction upside down, and repeat.","Write u₂ = 1 ÷ (1 + 1) and feed each answer back into the rule."],"codes":["A24"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The nth term of a sequence is n² + n. Prove that every term of the sequence is an even number.","a":"n² + n = n(n + 1), and one of the consecutive integers n and n + 1 is always even, so every term is even","sol":"Factorise the expression: n² + n = n(n + 1) (M1). The position n is a whole number, so n and n + 1 are consecutive whole numbers and exactly one of them is even (M1). An even number multiplied by a whole number is even, so n(n + 1) is even for every n and every term of the sequence is even (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Both terms of n² + n share a factor of n, so the expression can be looked at as a product rather than a sum.","Factorise the expression and think about what kind of whole numbers the two factors are.","Write n² + n as a product of two factors and describe how those two factors are related."],"codes":["A25"]},{"q":"A square has sides of length 10 cm. A second square is drawn with its vertices at the midpoints of the sides of the first, a third with its vertices at the midpoints of the sides of the second, and so on. Each square's side is 1/√2 of the side before it, so the side lengths form a geometric progression. Work out the side length of the fifth square. A calculator may be used.","a":"2.5 cm","sol":"Second square: 10 ÷ √2 = 10√2/2 = 5√2 cm (M1). Third square: 5√2 ÷ √2 = 5 cm (M1). Fourth square: 5 ÷ √2 = 5√2/2 = 2.5√2 cm (M1). Fifth square: 2.5√2 ÷ √2 = 2.5 cm (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The side lengths form a geometric progression, so each one comes from the last by the same division.","Dividing by √2 twice halves a length, so work along the squares one at a time.","Write the second side as 10 ÷ √2 and rationalise before moving on to the next square."],"codes":["A24"]}],"exit":[{"q":"The second differences of a quadratic sequence are all 10. What is the coefficient of n² in its nth term?","a":"5"},{"q":"Deduce an expression for the nth term of the quadratic sequence 2, 8, 18, 32, 50.","a":"2n²"},{"q":"A geometric progression begins 4, 4√3, 12, … Write down the next term exactly.","a":"12√3"},{"q":"A sequence is defined by u₁ = 2 and uₙ₊₁ = 3uₙ − 1. Work out u₃.","a":"14"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Quadratic sequence","back":"A sequence whose first differences change by a constant amount, so its second differences are all equal. Its nth term has the form an² + bn + c with a not zero.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"Second difference of a quadratic sequence","back":"The difference between consecutive first differences. It is constant, and it equals 2a, twice the coefficient of n² in the nth term.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"Coefficient of n² from the second difference","back":"a = second difference ÷ 2. A constant second difference of 6 therefore gives 3n² as the n² part of the nth term.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"Method for the nth term of a quadratic sequence","back":"Find the constant second difference, halve it for an², subtract the an² values from the terms, find the nth term of the linear sequence left, then add the two parts.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"First differences of a quadratic sequence","back":"They form a linear sequence, rising or falling by the constant second difference each time, so continuing them continues the original sequence.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"Checking a quadratic nth term","back":"Substitute n = 1 and one later position into the expression; both must give the terms printed in the question.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"Testing whether a number is a term of a quadratic sequence","back":"Set the nth term expression equal to the number and solve the quadratic. It is a term only if n comes out as a positive whole number.","code":"A25","open":false},{"front":"Geometric progression","back":"A sequence in which each term is the one before it multiplied by a fixed common ratio r. Its nth term is arⁿ⁻¹, where a is the first term.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Common ratio of a geometric progression","back":"r = any term ÷ the term immediately before it. At Foundation r is a rational number greater than 0; at Higher tier r may also be a surd.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Multiplying a surd by itself","back":"√a × √a = a. So two steps of a progression with common ratio √a multiply a term by a, and the terms alternate between surds and whole numbers.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"The geometric progression 2, 2√2, 4, 4√2, 8, …","back":"Its common ratio is √2. Each single step multiplies by √2 and each pair of steps doubles the term, so whole-number terms and surd terms alternate.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Rationalising a common ratio","back":"Write 1/√2 as √2/2 by multiplying top and bottom by √2. A ratio or term is left in this exact simplified form, never as a decimal.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Recursive (term-to-term) definition","back":"A rule that makes each term from the one or two terms before it, together with the first term or terms. At Higher tier any unfamiliar recursive sequence is defined in the question.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"The notation uₙ and uₙ₊₁","back":"uₙ is the term in position n and uₙ₊₁ is the next term, so a recursive rule writes uₙ₊₁ in terms of uₙ, as in uₙ₊₁ = 2uₙ − 3.","code":"A24","open":false},{"front":"Give an example of a geometric progression whose common ratio is a surd","back":"3, 3√5, 15, 15√5, … with r = √5; 1, √3, 3, 3√3, … with r = √3; 10, 10√2, 20, 20√2, … with r = √2","code":"A24","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["What the Higher tier paper prints for this room: no sequence result at all. Section 4 of the course appendix records AQA's Higher insert for exams from 2026, which prints the trapezium and prism, the circumference and area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios, the quadratic formula, the sine rule, the cosine rule, the area of a triangle from two sides and the included angle, compound interest, and the two probability rules. Nothing about quadratic sequences, geometric progressions or recursive rules appears on it, and the specification's own Appendix lists no sequence formula in any of its three lists either — so every result below must be recalled on all three papers.","The one exception worth knowing: the quadratic formula is printed on the Higher tier sheet, even though the Appendix's list 1 says the formulae there 'will not be given in the exam' — where the two disagree the sheet governs what you are given on the day. It matters here because testing whether a number is a term of a quadratic sequence ends in a quadratic equation.","Second difference of a quadratic sequence (recall): second difference = 2a, where a is the coefficient of n², so a = second difference ÷ 2.","nth term of a quadratic sequence (recall): an² + bn + c — halve the constant second difference for a, subtract the an² values from the terms, and find the nth term of the linear sequence that is left.","nth term of a linear sequence (recall, from the Foundation room): dn + (a − d), where d is the common difference and a the first term.","nth term of a geometric progression (recall): arⁿ⁻¹, where a is the first term and r the common ratio.","Common ratio (recall): r = uₙ₊₁ ÷ uₙ, that is any term divided by the term immediately before it; at Higher tier r may be a surd.","Surd results used with a surd common ratio (recall): √a × √a = a, √a × √b = √(ab), and 1/√a = √a/a after rationalising.","Recursive definition (recall the notation, not the rule): a sequence given by uₙ₊₁ = f(uₙ), or uₙ₊₂ = f(uₙ₊₁, uₙ), with the first term or terms stated. The rule itself for any other recursive sequence is always defined in the question.","Membership test (recall): a value is a term only if solving 'nth term expression = value' gives n as a positive whole number; reject negative or fractional roots."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:29:34Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"All 16 bank questions, the 4 exit-ticket items, the 4 lesson check items and all 5 worked examples were re-solved independently in sympy with exact surd and fraction forms and every answer key, every solution line and every mark allocation is correct, including the Formulae sheet, whose claims match section 4 of appendix.md exactly (no sequence result on either list, quadratic formula printed on the Higher insert though the Appendix's list 1 says otherwise, and the full Higher insert list reproduced correctly); the remaining faults are presentational — idea ordering, one over-generous hint ladder on the proof question, and one closing cue that does not point at its step."}}},"ratio-higher":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"ratio-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"At Higher tier the ratio topic stops being arithmetic and becomes algebra. The paper no longer asks only what happens to y when x doubles: it asks you to construct the equation, y = kx, y = kx², y = k√x or y = k/x², find the constant of proportionality from one pair of values and then use the completed equation for any other value. It asks you to read a rate off a curve, telling the average rate between two points (the gradient of a chord) from the rate at one instant (the gradient of a tangent), with the right unit attached. And it asks you to work with a general iterative process, where one rule is applied over and over and each output becomes the next input, which is what growth, decay, depreciation and compound interest all are underneath. Ratio, proportion and rates of change carry about 20% of the Higher tier assessment, these statements are Higher content only, and they are exactly where AO2 and AO3 marks live: the interpreting sentence and the multi-step problem.","idea":[{"h":"Constructing and using equations of direct and inverse proportion","t":"Translate the words into an equation with a constant. 'y is directly proportional to x' is y = kx; 'directly proportional to the square of x' is y = kx²; 'directly proportional to the square root of x' is y = k√x. 'y is inversely proportional to x' means y is proportional to 1/x, so y = k/x, and 'inversely proportional to the square of x' is y = k/x². The method never changes: write the equation with k in it, substitute the one pair of values the question gives to find k, write the completed equation, then substitute the new value. That first line with k in it is where the method mark sits, so write it down even when you can see the answer. Working backwards is examined just as often — given y, rearrange to find x, remembering to square or square root at the end. A table can also be tested: if y = k/x² then yx² is the same for every pair, and showing that is how you prove the proportionality rather than assert it.","code":"R13"},{"h":"Average and instantaneous rates of change on a curve","t":"On a straight-line graph the gradient is one rate of change for the whole graph. On a curve the gradient changes from point to point, so there are two different questions. The average rate of change between two points is the gradient of the chord joining them: (change in y) ÷ (change in x). The instantaneous rate of change at one point is the gradient of the tangent drawn at that point, read from two points on the tangent line, and it is the rate at that single moment. On a distance-time curve these are the average speed over an interval and the speed at an instant; on a volume-time curve they are litres per second; on a temperature-time curve a negative gradient means cooling. A chord between two close points is also a good estimate of the tangent's gradient, and that is how 'estimate the gradient at x = 2' is set algebraically. Always give the unit — the vertical axis unit per horizontal axis unit — because the interpreting mark is for the rate in context, not the bare number.","code":"R15"},{"h":"General iterative processes, growth and decay","t":"An iterative process applies the same rule again and again, each answer becoming the next starting value. Write it as xₙ₊₁ = f(xₙ) with a starting value x₁, then turn the handle. When the rule is 'multiply by the same number every time', n steps multiply by that number to the power n: an increase of r% per step uses the multiplier 1 + r/100 and a decrease uses 1 − r/100, which gives compound interest, Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, and depreciation as the same calculation with a multiplier below 1. Going backwards, if a quantity is multiplied by M over n equal steps, each step's multiplier is the nth root of M. Not every iteration is a single multiplier: rules such as xₙ₊₁ = 0.7xₙ + 90, where some is lost and a fixed amount is added, are also examined, and there you must show each line of the recurrence. Write out every intermediate value, because those lines carry the method marks even when your calculator gives the final figure in one go.","code":"R16"}],"examples":[{"title":"Force between two magnets","setup":"The force F newtons between two magnets is inversely proportional to the square of the distance d centimetres between them. When the magnets are 5 cm apart the force is 12 N. Work out the force when the magnets are 2 cm apart. (non-calculator)","steps":["Inversely proportional to the square of d means F = k/d², where k is the constant of proportionality.","Substitute the pair given, d = 5 and F = 12: 12 = k/5² = k/25, so k = 12 × 25 = 300.","The completed equation is therefore F = 300/d².","Now substitute the new distance: F = 300/2² = 300/4 = 75 N."],"cues":["What does 'inversely proportional to the square of d' do to the letter d in the equation?","Which pair of values can be put into that equation to pin down the constant?","With the constant known, what connects F and d now?","What replaces d to answer the question asked?"]},{"title":"Average speed and speed at an instant","setup":"A cyclist's distance-time graph is a curve. Distance s is in metres and time t is in seconds, and the curve passes through (0, 0), (4, 24) and (10, 90). A tangent drawn to the curve at t = 4 passes through the points (2, 8) and (6, 40). Work out the cyclist's average speed over the first 10 seconds and her speed at the instant t = 4, and say which of the two is the greater. (non-calculator)","steps":["The average speed over the whole interval is the gradient of the chord from (0, 0) to (10, 90): (90 − 0)/(10 − 0) = 9 m/s.","The speed at the instant t = 4 is the gradient of the tangent there: (40 − 8)/(6 − 2) = 32/4 = 8 m/s.","8 m/s is the smaller, so at t = 4 the cyclist is travelling more slowly than her average over the ten seconds."],"cues":["Which two points on the curve span the whole of the first ten seconds?","Which line, the chord or the tangent, gives a rate at one moment?","Which of the two speeds you have found is the smaller?"]},{"title":"A general iterative process: a daily dose","setup":"A patient takes 80 mg of a drug each morning. During the 24 hours after a dose, 25% of the drug in the body is removed, and then the next morning's 80 mg dose is taken. Let xₙ be the amount of the drug in the body just after the nth dose, so x₁ = 80. Work out x₃, the amount just after the third dose. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Removing 25% leaves 75%, so each day multiplies by 0.75 and then adds the new dose: xₙ₊₁ = 0.75xₙ + 80.","Apply the rule once: x₂ = 0.75 × 80 + 80 = 60 + 80 = 140.","Apply the same rule to that output: x₃ = 0.75 × 140 + 80 = 105 + 80 = 185, so 185 mg is in the body just after the third dose."],"cues":["What single multiplier is left once a quarter of the drug has gone?","Which value goes into the rule first?","What is the input to the rule the second time it is used?"]},{"title":"Finding the yearly percentage from two years of growth","setup":"A colony of insects grows by the same percentage every year. There were 1500 insects at the start of one year and 2160 insects two years later. Work out the percentage increase each year. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Two years apply the same yearly multiplier twice, so (multiplier)² = 2160/1500 = 1.44.","Undo the squaring: the yearly multiplier is √1.44 = 1.2.","A multiplier of 1.2 is 1 + 20/100, so the colony grows by 20% each year."],"cues":["How many times has the yearly multiplier been applied between the two counts?","How do you get back from a squared multiplier to the multiplier itself?","How is a multiplier turned back into a percentage change?"]},{"title":"Testing a table for inverse proportion","setup":"A table gives three pairs of values of x and y: when x = 2, y = 50; when x = 4, y = 12.5; when x = 10, y = 2. Show that y is inversely proportional to the square of x, and use the equation you find to work out y when x = 5. A calculator may be used.","steps":["If y = k/x² then the product yx² must be the same for every pair; for the first pair, 50 × 2² = 200.","Test the other two pairs: 12.5 × 4² = 200 and 2 × 10² = 200, so yx² is constant and the proportionality holds.","The constant of proportionality is that common product, so the equation is y = 200/x².","Substituting the new value: y = 200/5² = 200/25 = 8."],"cues":["If y = k/x², what combination of x and y should stay the same down the table?","Do the remaining pairs give that same value?","What equation does that common value give you?","What goes in place of x in the equation?"]}],"check":[{"q":"P is inversely proportional to the square of v. When v = 2, P = 9. Write down the equation connecting P and v.","a":"P = 36/v² (since k = 9 × 2² = 36)"},{"q":"A curve passes through (2, 10) and (6, 34). Work out the average rate of change of y with respect to x between these points.","a":"6 (that is (34 − 10)/(6 − 2) = 24/4)"},{"q":"A town's population is 20 000 and falls by 4% each year. Work out the population after 2 years.","a":"18 432 (20 000 × 0.96²)"},{"q":"The tangent to a temperature-time curve at t = 3 minutes passes through (1, 70) and (5, 30), with temperature in °C. What is the instantaneous rate of change of temperature at t = 3?","a":"−10 °C per minute — the liquid is cooling at 10 °C per minute"},{"q":"T is directly proportional to the square root of x. When x = 16, T = 20. Work out T when x = 25.","a":"25 (k = 5, so T = 5√x)"}]},"room":{"facts":["Direct proportion: y ∝ x gives y = kx; y ∝ x² gives y = kx²; y ∝ √x gives y = k√x. Substitute the pair of values given to find k.","Inverse proportion: y ∝ 1/x gives y = k/x, so xy = k; y ∝ 1/x² gives y = k/x², so yx² = k. A constant product is the test for inverse proportion.","Average rate of change between two points = gradient of the chord = (change in y) ÷ (change in x).","Instantaneous rate of change at a point = gradient of the tangent drawn at that point, with the unit of the vertical axis per unit of the horizontal axis.","A general iterative process is xₙ₊₁ = f(xₙ) with a starting value: each output becomes the next input.","Growth and decay multipliers: increase of r% is × (1 + r/100), decrease of r% is × (1 − r/100); after n steps the quantity is multiplied by that number to the power n.","Compound interest: Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, where P is the principal, r the rate per period and n the number of periods.","If a quantity is multiplied by M over n equal steps, the multiplier for one step is the nth root of M."],"questions":[{"q":"y is directly proportional to x. When x = 8, y = 20. Write down an equation connecting y and x. (non-calculator)","a":"y = 2.5x","sol":"Direct proportion means y = kx. Substituting the pair given, 20 = k × 8, so k = 20 ÷ 8 = 2.5. M1 for 20 = k × 8 (or k = 20 ÷ 8). The equation is y = 2.5x, or equivalently y = 5x/2. A1 for y = 2.5x oe.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Direct proportion means one quantity is a fixed multiple of the other.","Put a constant in front of x, then use the pair of values you are given to work that constant out.","The first line is y = kx, with the pair of values substituted into it."],"codes":["R13"]},{"q":"y is inversely proportional to x. When x = 4, y = 6. Work out y when x = 3. (non-calculator)","a":"8","sol":"Inverse proportion means y = k/x. M1 for y = k/x. Substituting x = 4 and y = 6 gives 6 = k/4, so k = 24. A1 for k = 24 (or y = 24/x). When x = 3, y = 24/3 = 8. A1 for 8.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Under inverse proportion the product of the two quantities never changes.","Find the constant from the pair of values you are given, then use it with the new value of x.","The first line is y = k/x, with the known pair substituted to find k."],"codes":["R13"]},{"q":"d is directly proportional to the square of t. When t = 3, d = 45. Write down an equation connecting d and t. (non-calculator)","a":"d = 5t²","sol":"Proportional to the square of t means d = kt². Substituting t = 3 and d = 45 gives 45 = k × 3² = 9k. M1 for 45 = k × 3² (or k = 45 ÷ 9). So k = 5 and d = 5t². A1 for d = 5t².","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["It is t², not t, that d is a fixed multiple of.","Write the relationship with a constant in front of t², then substitute the pair of values given.","The first line is d = kt², with the given values put into it."],"codes":["R13"]},{"q":"A culture of bacteria multiplies by 1.2 every hour. At noon the culture contains 500 bacteria. Work out how many bacteria it contains three hours later. A calculator may be used.","a":"864","sol":"The same multiplier is applied each hour, an iterative process xₙ₊₁ = 1.2xₙ with x₁ = 500, so after three hours the count is 500 × 1.2³. M1 for 500 × 1.2³ (or for the chain 600, 720 seen). 1.2³ = 1.728 and 500 × 1.728 = 864. A1 for 864.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The same thing happens to the number of bacteria in every hour that passes.","Either apply the multiplier one hour at a time, or use a power of it for the whole three hours.","The first line is 500 multiplied by the hourly multiplier cubed."],"codes":["R16"]},{"q":"The graph of the depth of water d cm in a tank against time t seconds is a curve. A tangent drawn to the curve at t = 4 passes through (0, 2) and (8, 18). Work out the instantaneous rate of change of the depth at t = 4. Give the unit with your answer. (non-calculator)","a":"2 cm per second","sol":"The instantaneous rate of change is the gradient of the tangent at that point. M1 for (18 − 2)/(8 − 0). This is 16/8 = 2, and the axes give the unit as cm per second, so the depth is increasing at 2 cm/s at t = 4. A1 for 2 cm per second.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The rate at a single moment is read from the straight line that touches the curve there.","Use the two points given on that straight line to find its gradient.","The first line is the change in depth divided by the change in time between the two points on the tangent."],"codes":["R15"]},{"q":"The intensity of light I lux from a lamp is inversely proportional to the square of the distance d metres from the lamp. At a distance of 3 m the intensity is 40 lux. Work out the distance at which the intensity is 10 lux. (non-calculator)","a":"6 m","sol":"Inversely proportional to the square of d means I = k/d². M1 for I = k/d². Substituting d = 3 and I = 40 gives 40 = k/9, so k = 360 and I = 360/d². A1 for k = 360 (or I = 360/d²). Putting I = 10: 10 = 360/d², so d² = 360 ÷ 10 = 36. M1 for d² = 36. Taking the positive root, d = 6. A1 for 6 m.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The intensity is not inversely proportional to the distance itself, but to the square of the distance.","Find the constant from the pair of values you are given, then put the new intensity into the completed equation and unpick it to reach the distance.","The first line is I = k/d², with the pair of values given substituted to find the constant."],"codes":["R13"]},{"q":"T is directly proportional to the square root of L. When L = 9, T = 2.4. Work out L when T = 6. (non-calculator)","a":"56.25","sol":"T = k√L, so 2.4 = k × √9 = 3k, giving k = 0.8 and T = 0.8√L. M1 for k = 0.8 (or T = 0.8√L). Putting T = 6: 6 = 0.8√L, so √L = 6 ÷ 0.8 = 7.5. M1 for √L = 7.5. Squaring, L = 7.5² = 56.25. A1 for 56.25.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The square root of L, not L itself, is what T is a fixed multiple of.","Find the constant first, then rearrange the completed equation to get at L.","The first line is T = k√L, with the pair of values given substituted to find the constant."],"codes":["R13"]},{"q":"A curve shows the volume V litres of water in a tank against the time t seconds. The curve passes through (1, 8) and (5, 40). Work out the average rate of change of V between t = 1 and t = 5. Give the unit with your answer. (non-calculator)","a":"8 litres per second","sol":"The average rate of change between two points is the gradient of the chord joining them. M1 for (40 − 8)/(5 − 1). This is 32/4 = 8, and the axes give the unit as litres per second, so the tank is filling at an average of 8 litres/s over that interval. A1 for 8 litres per second.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["An average rate covers the whole interval, not one moment in it.","Join the two points on the curve with a straight line and find its gradient.","The first line is the change in volume divided by the change in time between the two points."],"codes":["R15"]},{"q":"A car is worth £18 000 when new. Its value falls by 12% each year. Work out its value after 4 years, to the nearest penny. A calculator may be used.","a":"£10 794.52","sol":"A fall of 12% multiplies by 1 − 0.12 = 0.88 each year, the same multiplier at every step. B1 for the multiplier 0.88. After 4 years the value is 18 000 × 0.88⁴. M1 for 18 000 × 0.88⁴. 0.88⁴ = 0.59969536, and 18 000 × 0.59969536 = 10 794.51648. A1 for £10 794.52.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each year the value is a percentage of the value at the start of that year, not of the price when new.","Turn the 12% fall into a multiplier, then apply it once for every year.","The first line is 18 000 multiplied by the yearly multiplier raised to the power of the number of years."],"codes":["R16"]},{"q":"A machine's cooling system is topped up every day. At the end of each day 20% of the coolant in the system is drained off, and then 600 ml of fresh coolant is added. At the end of the third day the system holds 4024 ml of coolant. Work out how much coolant was in the system at the start of the first day. A calculator may be used.","a":"5000 ml","sol":"Each day's rule is xₙ₊₁ = 0.8xₙ + 600, so going back a day means subtracting 600 and then dividing by 0.8. Undoing the third day: (4024 − 600) ÷ 0.8 = 3424 ÷ 0.8 = 4280. M1 for 4280. Undoing the second day: (4280 − 600) ÷ 0.8 = 3680 ÷ 0.8 = 4600. M1 for 4600. Undoing the first day: (4600 − 600) ÷ 0.8 = 4000 ÷ 0.8 = 5000. A1 for 5000 ml.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The same two things happen in the same order every day, so a day can be run backwards as well as forwards.","Undo the rule one day at a time: take off the amount that was added, then divide by the daily multiplier.","The first line is the amount at the end of the day with the added amount subtracted from it, then divided by the daily multiplier."],"codes":["R16"]},{"q":"y is inversely proportional to the square of x. The value of x is increased by 25%. Work out the percentage change in y. (non-calculator)","a":"A decrease of 36%","sol":"Write y = k/x². Increasing x by 25% replaces x by 1.25x = 5x/4. M1 for using 5x/4 in place of x. The new value of y is k/(5x/4)² = k/(25x²/16) = (16/25) × k/x² = 0.64y. M1 for the multiplier 16/25 (= 0.64). Since 0.64 = 1 − 0.36, y is multiplied by 0.64, a decrease of 36%. A1 for a decrease of 36%.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["No values are given, so choose to work with the equation itself rather than with numbers.","Replace x by the increased value in the equation and see what multiplies y.","The first line is y = k/x² with x replaced by the multiplier for a 25% increase times x."],"codes":["R13"]},{"q":"A managed forest covers 250 hectares and its area grows by 8% each year. A neighbouring forest covers 400 hectares and its area shrinks by 5% each year. Work out the number of complete years after which the managed forest is the larger of the two for the first time. A calculator may be used.","a":"4 years","sol":"Each forest is an iterative process with its own yearly multiplier, so after n years the areas are 250 × 1.08ⁿ and 400 × 0.95ⁿ. M1 for 250 × 1.08ⁿ. M1 for 400 × 0.95ⁿ. Testing whole numbers of years: after 3 years 250 × 1.08³ = 314.928 and 400 × 0.95³ = 342.95, so the managed forest is still smaller; after 4 years 250 × 1.08⁴ = 340.12... and 400 × 0.95⁴ = 325.80..., so it is now larger. M1 for both pairs of values compared. The first time is after 4 years. A1 for 4 years.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Each area changes by the same percentage every year, so each is its own repeated multiplication.","Write each area after n years as its starting area times its own multiplier to the power n, then try whole-number values of n until the order swaps.","The first line is each starting area multiplied by its own yearly multiplier raised to the power of the number of years."],"codes":["R16"]},{"q":"A ball is thrown upwards. Its height h metres above the ground t seconds after it is thrown is h = 20t − 5t². Use the chord between t = 1 and t = 1.01 to estimate the speed of the ball at t = 1. Give your answer in m/s to 2 decimal places. A calculator may be used.","a":"9.95 m/s","sol":"At t = 1, h = 20 × 1 − 5 × 1² = 15. At t = 1.01, h = 20 × 1.01 − 5 × 1.01² = 20.2 − 5.1005 = 15.0995. M1 for both heights, 15 and 15.0995. The gradient of the chord is (15.0995 − 15)/(1.01 − 1). M1 for that quotient. This is 0.0995/0.01 = 9.95, so the speed at t = 1 is about 9.95 m/s. A1 for 9.95 m/s.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Speed at an instant is the rate of change of height with time, and a short chord estimates it.","Substitute each of the two times into the formula, then find the gradient between those two points.","The first line is 20t − 5t² worked out at each end of the chord."],"codes":["R15"]},{"q":"£5000 is invested in an account paying compound interest at r% each year. After 6 years the account holds £5970.26. Work out the value of r. A calculator may be used.","a":"3","sol":"Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, so 5000(1 + r/100)⁶ = 5970.26. Dividing, (1 + r/100)⁶ = 5970.26/5000 = 1.194052. M1 for dividing to reach 1.194052 (oe). Taking the sixth root, 1 + r/100 = ⁶√1.194052 = 1.03. M1 for the sixth root. So r/100 = 0.03 and r = 3. A1 for r = 3.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Six years of the same percentage means the same multiplier used six times.","Find the overall multiplier first, then undo the power to get back to one year.","The first line is the final amount divided by the amount invested."],"codes":["R16"]},{"q":"A distance-time graph shows the distance travelled in kilometres against the time in hours, and it is a curve. The tangent to the curve at t = 1.5 passes through (0.5, 30) and (2.5, 150). Work out the speed of the vehicle at that instant in metres per second, to 3 significant figures. A calculator may be used.","a":"16.7 m/s","sol":"The speed at an instant is the gradient of the tangent: (150 − 30)/(2.5 − 0.5). M1 for that quotient. This is 120/2 = 60, and the axes make the unit km/h, so the speed is 60 km/h. A1 for 60 km/h. Converting, 60 km/h = 60 × 1000 metres in 3600 seconds. M1 for × 1000 ÷ 3600. This is 60 000/3600 = 16.666... = 16.7 m/s to 3 significant figures. A1 for 16.7 m/s.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Read the rate off the tangent first, then deal with the units it comes out in.","Find the gradient from the two points given, then change kilometres per hour into metres per second.","The first line is the change in distance divided by the change in time between the two points on the tangent."],"codes":["R15"]}],"exit":[{"q":"y is directly proportional to x². When x = 2, y = 20. Work out y when x = 5.","a":"125"},{"q":"A chord of a curve joins (1, 4) to (5, 24). Work out the average rate of change of y with respect to x.","a":"5"},{"q":"£4000 is invested at 5% compound interest a year. Work out the value after 3 years.","a":"£4630.50"},{"q":"y is inversely proportional to x. When x = 5, y = 12. Work out y when x = 2.","a":"30"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Direct proportion, y ∝ x","back":"y is a constant multiple of x: y = kx. The graph is a straight line through the origin. Find k by substituting the one pair of values given.","code":"R13","open":false},{"front":"Inverse proportion, y ∝ 1/x","back":"y = k/x, so the product xy = k never changes. The graph is a reciprocal curve that never meets either axis.","code":"R13","open":false},{"front":"Directly proportional to the square of x","back":"y = kx². Doubling x multiplies y by 4. Substitute one pair of values to find k, then use the completed equation for any other value.","code":"R13","open":false},{"front":"Inversely proportional to the square of x","back":"y = k/x², so yx² = k is constant. Doubling x divides y by 4; trebling x divides y by 9.","code":"R13","open":false},{"front":"Directly proportional to the square root of x","back":"y = k√x. To find x from y, divide by k to get √x and then square. Multiplying x by 4 doubles y.","code":"R13","open":false},{"front":"Constant of proportionality","back":"The number k in y = kx, y = kx², y = k√x, y = k/x or y = k/x². Substitute the pair of values the question gives, then write the completed equation.","code":"R13","open":false},{"front":"Average rate of change","back":"The change in y divided by the change in x between two points: the gradient of the chord joining them. On a distance-time curve it is the average speed over that interval.","code":"R15","open":false},{"front":"Instantaneous rate of change","back":"The rate at a single point: the gradient of the tangent drawn to the curve there. On a distance-time curve it is the speed at that instant.","code":"R15","open":false},{"front":"Chord and tangent on a curve","back":"A chord joins two points on the curve and gives an average rate; a tangent touches the curve at one point and gives the rate at that instant. A short chord estimates a tangent.","code":"R15","open":false},{"front":"Unit of a rate of change read from a graph","back":"The unit on the vertical axis per unit on the horizontal axis, such as metres per second or litres per minute. A rate given without its unit loses the accuracy mark.","code":"R15","open":false},{"front":"General iterative process","back":"One rule applied over and over, each output becoming the next input, written xₙ₊₁ = f(xₙ) with a starting value. Growth, decay and compound interest are all special cases.","code":"R16","open":false},{"front":"Growth and decay multiplier","back":"An increase of r% multiplies by 1 + r/100; a decrease of r% multiplies by 1 − r/100. After n steps the quantity has been multiplied by that number to the power n.","code":"R16","open":false},{"front":"Compound interest","back":"Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, where P is the principal, r the rate per period and n the number of periods. AQA's Higher tier formulae sheet prints it.","code":"R16","open":false},{"front":"Finding one step's rate from an overall multiplier","back":"If a quantity is multiplied by M over n equal steps, one step multiplies by the nth root of M; writing that root as 1 + r/100 gives the percentage change per step.","code":"R16","open":false},{"front":"Give one example of a quantity that is inversely proportional to another","back":"The speed for a journey and the time it takes; the pressure and the volume of a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature; the number of workers and the time to finish a fixed job.","code":"R13","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ — compound interest, where P is the principal, r the rate per period and n the number of periods. This one is given to you on the day: section 4 of the course appendix records that AQA's Higher tier formulae sheet, the insert for exams from 2026, prints compound interest (the total accrued). The specification's own Appendix places it in list 2, 'formulae students are expected to know or be able to derive; they will not be given in the exam', against content reference R16 — but the sheet is later than the Appendix, and where the two disagree the sheet governs what is printed in front of you. Still know what each letter means, and be ready to derive it as the same multiplier applied n times.","Direct proportion: y = kx; y = kx² for 'proportional to the square of x'; y = k√x for 'proportional to the square root of x'. Not in the Appendix at any of its three lists and not on the Higher sheet: recall them.","Inverse proportion: y = k/x, equivalently xy = k; y = k/x² for 'inversely proportional to the square of x', equivalently yx² = k. Not in the Appendix and not on the sheet: recall them.","Finding the constant of proportionality: substitute the one pair of values given into the equation and solve for k. No formula is provided for this: it is the method mark, so write the line down.","Average rate of change between two points = gradient of the chord = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁). Not in the Appendix and not on the sheet: recall it.","Instantaneous rate of change at a point = gradient of the tangent at that point, found from two points on the tangent line. Not in the Appendix and not on the sheet: recall it, and always give the unit as the vertical axis unit per horizontal axis unit.","General iterative process: xₙ₊₁ = f(xₙ), with a starting value x₁ (or x₀). Not in the Appendix and not on the sheet: recall it, and show every intermediate value.","Growth and decay multipliers: increase of r% per step is × (1 + r/100), decrease of r% per step is × (1 − r/100), and n steps multiply by the multiplier to the power n. Only the compound interest form of this is on the sheet: recall the decay form.","Reversing an iterative process: if a quantity is multiplied by M over n equal steps, one step's multiplier is the nth root of M, so r = 100(ⁿ√M − 1). Not in the Appendix and not on the sheet: recall it.","What the Higher tier sheet prints, from section 4 of the course appendix (the insert for exams from 2026): the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, the circumference of a circle, the area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios in a right-angled triangle, the quadratic formula, the sine rule, the cosine rule, the area of a triangle from two sides and the included angle, compound interest (the total accrued), the addition rule for probability, and the multiplication rule using a conditional probability. Of this room's results only compound interest is on it, so every 'recall it' above stands. The inserts are reissued each series, so check the sheet at the front of your own paper."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:33:26.813617Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":1,"notes":"Re-judged after the fix pass: the tangent in worked example 2 now passes through (4, 24) with gradient 8 m/s, correctly between the chord gradients 6 and 11 and still below the 9 m/s average, and the three replaced questions (inverse-square light intensity, answer 6 m; the coolant recurrence run backwards to 5000 ml; the 250 × 1.08ⁿ against 400 × 0.95ⁿ comparison, answer 4 years, first true at n = 4) all re-solve exactly, with mark tallies matching their tariffs of 4, 3 and 4 and codes R13, R16, R16 — publish as it is."}}},"geometry-properties-higher":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"geometry-properties-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Geometry is 20% of the Higher assessment, and this room holds the Higher-only part of shape and transformation: the eight standard circle theorems and the proofs behind them, enlargement by a negative scale factor, and the description of a combination of rotations, reflections and translations as one single transformation. It is where the reasoning marks live. AO2 is 30% of the Higher tier, and a circle-theorem question is marked on the chain of named reasons, not on the number at the end: an angle written down with no theorem quoted can score nothing, and a 'prove' question with the result already printed gives no credit for quoting it back. The transformation work is examined the same way — a description missing its centre, its sign or its column vector is an incomplete answer.","idea":[{"h":"Enlargement by a negative scale factor","code":"G7","t":"At Higher tier the scale factor of an enlargement may be negative. To enlarge point P by scale factor k about centre C, find the vector from C to P, multiply it by k, and add the result to C: image = C + k × (vector CP). When k is negative the vector reverses, so the image lands on the opposite side of the centre and the whole shape is turned through 180° as well as resized — object and image are still similar, with lengths multiplied by the size of k and areas by k². A full description needs three things: the word enlargement, the scale factor with its minus sign, and the centre as coordinates. To find the centre from a picture, join each vertex to its image and extend: the lines meet at the centre, and with a negative scale factor the centre lies between the object and its image."},{"h":"Combining transformations, and what stays the same","code":"G8","t":"A shape may be put through two transformations one after the other, and the question asks for the single transformation equivalent to the pair, described fully. The safe method is to follow one general point (x, y), or a single named vertex, through both moves and compare the finish with the start. Some results are worth knowing: two reflections in parallel mirror lines give a translation perpendicular to them of twice the distance between them, written as a column vector; two reflections in mirror lines that cross give a rotation about the crossing point through twice the angle between them; two rotations about the same centre add their angles. Invariance is the other half of the idea. Rotations, reflections and translations never change a length or an angle, so the final image is congruent to the original however many are combined; an invariant point is one that maps to itself, such as the centre of a rotation or any point on a mirror line."},{"h":"The circle theorems: applying them and proving them","code":"G10","t":"Eight standard results are assessed: the angle subtended by an arc at the centre is twice the angle subtended by the same arc at the circumference; the angle subtended at the circumference by a semicircle is 90°; angles in the same segment are equal; opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral add up to 180°; the tangent at any point is perpendicular to the radius at that point; tangents from an external point are equal in length; the perpendicular from the centre to a chord bisects the chord; and the alternate segment theorem, that the angle between a tangent and a chord equals the angle in the alternate segment. Every step of an answer must name the theorem it rests on. You must also be able to prove them and to use them to prove related results, and the tools for a proof are always the same: all radii are equal, so triangles with two radii are isosceles; the exterior angle of a triangle equals the sum of the two opposite interior angles; and the congruence criteria, especially RHS wherever a tangent meets a radius."}],"examples":[{"title":"Two circle theorems on one diagram","setup":"A, B, C and D all lie on a circle with centre O. B and D both lie on the major arc AC. The angle AOC at the centre, standing on the minor arc AC, is 118°. Work out the size of angle ABC and the size of angle ADC, naming the circle theorem that gives each one.","steps":["The theorem 'the angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference' links angle AOC to angle ABC, because both stand on the minor arc AC.","angle ABC = ½ × 118° = 59°.","Angle ADC stands on that same minor arc AC, and D lies in the same segment as B, so angle ADC and angle ABC are angles in the same segment.","Angles in the same segment are equal, so angle ADC = 59°."],"cues":["Which of the two angles named in the question has its point at the centre?","What does that theorem now ask you to do to 118°?","B and D are both on the major arc — what does that make angles ABC and ADC?","What does the same-segment theorem say about the sizes of such a pair?"]},{"title":"Enlarging by a negative scale factor about a point","setup":"Triangle PQR has vertices P(0, 1), Q(2, 1) and R(0, 4). It is enlarged by scale factor −2 about the point (1, 2), giving triangle P'Q'R'. Work out the coordinates of P', Q' and R'.","steps":["From the centre (1, 2) to P(0, 1) the vector is (0 − 1, 1 − 2) = (−1, −1).","Multiply that vector by the scale factor: −2 × (−1, −1) = (2, 2), then add it to the centre, so P' = (1 + 2, 2 + 2) = (3, 4).","For Q the vector from the centre is (1, −1), so −2 × (1, −1) = (−2, 2) and Q' = (1 − 2, 2 + 2) = (−1, 4).","For R the vector from the centre is (−1, 2), so −2 × (−1, 2) = (2, −4) and R' = (1 + 2, 2 − 4) = (3, −2)."],"cues":["What journey takes you from the centre of enlargement to P?","What does multiplying that journey by a negative number do to its direction and its length?","What is the journey from the centre to Q?","What is the journey from the centre to R?"]},{"title":"Two transformations that make one","setup":"Shape A is reflected in the line y = x to give shape B. Shape B is then rotated 90° clockwise about the origin to give shape C. One vertex of shape A is at the point (3, 5). Describe fully the single transformation that maps shape A directly onto shape C, and write down the coordinates of the image of (3, 5) on shape C.","steps":["Under a reflection in y = x every point (x, y) is sent to (y, x), so (3, 5) becomes (5, 3).","A rotation of 90° clockwise about the origin sends (x, y) to (y, −x), so (5, 3) becomes (3, −5).","Following a general point through both moves gives (x, y) → (y, x) → (x, −y), so only the sign of the second coordinate has changed.","Every point keeps its x-coordinate and reverses its y-coordinate, so shape A maps to shape C by a reflection in the x-axis, and the image of (3, 5) is (3, −5)."],"cues":["What does reflecting in the line y = x do to the coordinates of a point?","Where does a quarter turn clockwise about the origin send the point (5, 3)?","What happens to a general point (x, y) after both moves?","Which single transformation leaves the x-coordinate alone and changes the sign of the y-coordinate?"]},{"title":"The perpendicular from the centre to a chord","setup":"A circle has centre O and radius 17 cm. CD is a chord of the circle with length 30 cm. M is the point on CD for which OM is perpendicular to CD. Work out the length of OM.","steps":["The perpendicular from the centre of a circle to a chord bisects that chord, so CM = 30 ÷ 2 = 15 cm.","OC is a radius, so OC = 17 cm, and triangle OMC has a right angle at M.","By Pythagoras' theorem, OM² = 17² − 15² = 289 − 225 = 64.","OM = √64 = 8 cm."],"cues":["What does the perpendicular from the centre do to the chord it meets?","Which two side lengths of triangle OMC do you now know?","Which rule connects the three sides of a right-angled triangle?","What is left to do once you have OM²?"]},{"title":"Proving that two tangents from a point are equal","setup":"TA and TB are tangents from the external point T to a circle with centre O, touching the circle at A and at B. The radii OA and OB and the line OT are drawn. Prove that TA = TB.","steps":["A tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact, so angle OAT = 90° and angle OBT = 90°.","OA = OB, because both are radii of the same circle.","OT is the hypotenuse of triangle OAT and also the hypotenuse of triangle OBT, and it is common to the two triangles.","Triangles OAT and OBT therefore match under RHS: a right angle, an equal hypotenuse OT and an equal side OA = OB.","Corresponding sides of congruent triangles are equal, so TA = TB."],"cues":["What angle does a tangent make with the radius drawn to its point of contact?","Why must OA and OB be the same length?","Which side do the two triangles share?","Which congruence criterion fits a right angle, an equal hypotenuse and one equal side?","What follows about the remaining pair of sides once the triangles are congruent?"]}],"check":[{"q":"An arc AB subtends an angle of 140° at the centre of a circle. What angle does the same arc subtend at a point on the major arc, and which theorem gives it?","a":"70°, because the angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference."},{"q":"A shape is enlarged by scale factor −4. What is the area scale factor, and where does the image lie relative to the centre of enlargement?","a":"The area scale factor is (−4)² = 16, and the image lies on the opposite side of the centre from the object, turned through 180°."},{"q":"PT is a tangent to a circle at P and O is the centre. What is the size of angle OPT?","a":"90° — a tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact."},{"q":"A shape is reflected in the line y = 1 and the image is then reflected in the line y = 5. What single transformation is equivalent to the pair?","a":"A translation by the column vector (0, 8) — 0 across and 8 up, twice the distance between the mirror lines."}]},"room":{"facts":["The angle subtended by an arc at the centre of a circle is twice the angle subtended by the same arc at any point on the circumference.","The angle subtended at the circumference by a semicircle is 90°, and angles in the same segment, subtended by the same arc, are equal.","Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral add up to 180°, so an exterior angle of a cyclic quadrilateral equals the interior angle at the opposite vertex.","A tangent to a circle is perpendicular to the radius drawn to the point of contact.","The two tangents to a circle from the same external point are equal in length, so the triangle they make with the chord joining the points of contact is isosceles.","The perpendicular from the centre of a circle to a chord bisects the chord.","Alternate segment theorem: the angle between a tangent and a chord drawn from the point of contact equals the angle subtended by that chord in the alternate segment.","Under an enlargement of scale factor k about centre C, the image of P is C + k × (vector CP); a negative k puts the image on the opposite side of C, turned through 180°, with lengths × the size of k and areas × k².","A full description gives: an enlargement its scale factor (with its sign) and centre; a rotation its centre, angle and direction; a reflection its mirror line; a translation its column vector.","Two reflections in parallel mirror lines a distance d apart give a translation of 2d perpendicular to the mirrors; two reflections in mirror lines crossing at an angle θ give a rotation of 2θ about the crossing point.","Rotations, reflections and translations, and every combination of them, leave all lengths and angles unchanged, so the final image is congruent to the original.","In any circle proof: all radii are equal, so a triangle with two radii is isosceles, and the exterior angle of a triangle equals the sum of the two opposite interior angles."],"questions":[{"q":"A, B and C lie on a circle with centre O. C is on the major arc AB. Angle ACB = 37°. Work out the size of the smaller angle AOB, and name the circle theorem you use. (non-calculator)","a":"74° (the angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference)","sol":"Angle AOB at the centre and angle ACB at the circumference both stand on the minor arc AB, so the angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference. M1 for 2 × 37°. A1 for 74° with the theorem named.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Both angles stand on the same arc AB — one has its point at the centre, the other on the edge of the circle.","The theorem links the two angles by a factor of 2; decide which of the pair must be the larger.","Write the line 'angle AOB = 2 × angle ACB' before you put any number into it."],"codes":["G10"]},{"q":"ABCD is a cyclic quadrilateral, with the vertices in that order round the circle. Angle ABC = 103°. Work out the size of angle ADC, and name the property that gives it. (non-calculator)","a":"77° (opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral add up to 180°)","sol":"Angle ABC and angle ADC are at opposite vertices of the cyclic quadrilateral, so they add up to 180°. M1 for 180° − 103°. A1 for 77° with the property named.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["B and D sit at opposite corners of the quadrilateral drawn inside the circle.","Opposite angles of such a quadrilateral have a fixed total, so one of them is that total minus the other.","Write the line 'angle ABC + angle ADC = 180°' and rearrange it before substituting."],"codes":["G10"]},{"q":"Triangle ABC has vertices A(1, 1), B(3, 1) and C(1, 4). Triangle A'B'C' has vertices A'(−2, −2), B'(−6, −2) and C'(−2, −8). Describe fully the single transformation that maps triangle ABC onto triangle A'B'C'. (non-calculator)","a":"An enlargement of scale factor −2 about the origin (0, 0)","sol":"Each image point is the object point with both coordinates multiplied by −2, and the lines AA', BB' and CC' all pass through the origin with the centre between object and image. B1 for enlargement with scale factor −2. B1 for centre (0, 0).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The image is bigger than the object and turned the other way up, so only one of the four transformations fits.","Join each vertex to its own image and extend the lines; where they all meet is what the description needs first.","Write the line 'A(1, 1) → A'(−2, −2), so every coordinate has been multiplied by the same number' and find that number."],"codes":["G7"]},{"q":"Shape S is reflected in the x-axis, and the image is then translated by the column vector (0, 4) — 0 across and 4 up. Describe fully the single transformation that maps S to the final image. (non-calculator)","a":"A reflection in the line y = 2","sol":"Follow a general point: (x, y) → (x, −y) under the reflection, then (x, −y) → (x, −y + 4) = (x, 4 − y) under the translation. The x-coordinate never changes and a point and its final image have y-coordinates adding to 4, so the mirror is the horizontal line halfway between them. B1 for saying the single transformation is a reflection. B1 for the mirror line y = 2.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A reflection followed by a translation at right angles to the mirror is still one of the four basic transformations.","Track a general point (x, y) through both moves and compare where it ends with where it began.","Write the line '(x, y) → (x, −y) → (x, −y + 4)' and then ask which horizontal line sits midway between a point and its image."],"codes":["G8"]},{"q":"A, B and C lie on a circle and AB is a diameter of that circle. Angle CAB = 31°. Work out the size of angle ACB and the size of angle ABC, giving a reason for each. (non-calculator)","a":"angle ACB = 90° and angle ABC = 59°","sol":"B1 for angle ACB = 90°, because the angle subtended at the circumference by a semicircle is 90°. M1 for 180° − 90° − 31°, using the angle sum of triangle ABC. A1 for angle ABC = 59°.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["AB being a diameter cuts the circle into two semicircles, and C sits on one of them.","Once one angle of triangle ABC is known, the third comes from the angle sum of a triangle.","Write the line 'angle ACB = 90° (angle in a semicircle), so angle ABC = 180° − angle ACB − angle CAB' before substituting."],"codes":["G10"]},{"q":"A tangent touches a circle at the point A, and B and C are two further points on that circle. The chord AB makes an angle of 62° with the tangent on one side of AB, and C lies in the segment on the other side of AB from that 62° angle, which is the alternate segment for it. Angle ABC = 47°. Work out the size of angle BAC. (non-calculator)","a":"71°","sol":"B1 for angle ACB = 62°, by the alternate segment theorem: the angle between a tangent and a chord equals the angle subtended by that chord in the alternate segment. M1 for 180° − 62° − 47°, using the angle sum of triangle ABC. A1 for angle BAC = 71°.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The angle the chord makes with the tangent is copied somewhere else in the circle.","Once you have transferred that angle into triangle ABC, two of its three angles are known.","Write the line 'angle ACB = angle between the tangent and chord AB (alternate segment theorem)' and then use the angle sum of triangle ABC."],"codes":["G10"]},{"q":"Triangle ABC has vertices A(5, 7), B(9, 7) and C(5, 11). It is enlarged by scale factor −1/2 about the point (3, 1) to give triangle A'B'C'. Work out the coordinates of A', B' and C', and write down the scale factor of the single enlargement that maps A'B'C' back onto ABC. A calculator may be used.","a":"A'(2, −2), B'(0, −2), C'(2, −4), and the scale factor back is −2","sol":"The vector from the centre (3, 1) to A(5, 7) is (2, 6). M1 for finding the vector from the centre to at least one vertex. M1 for multiplying such a vector by −1/2 and adding the result to the centre, e.g. (3, 1) + (−1, −3) = (2, −2). A1 for A'(2, −2), B'(0, −2) and C'(2, −4). B1 for the reverse scale factor −2, the reciprocal of −1/2.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Work with the journey from the centre of enlargement to each vertex rather than with the coordinates themselves.","Multiplying that journey by a negative fraction reverses it and shortens it; the centre stays fixed throughout.","Write the line 'image = centre + (−1/2) × (vector from centre to vertex)' and apply it to A, then B, then C."],"codes":["G7"]},{"q":"TA and TB are tangents from the point T to a circle with centre O, touching the circle at A and at B. Angle ATB = 48°. Work out the size of angle AOB and the size of angle TAB. (non-calculator)","a":"angle AOB = 132° and angle TAB = 66°","sol":"B1 for angle OAT = angle OBT = 90°, because a tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact. M1 for 360° − 90° − 90° − 48°, using the angle sum of quadrilateral OATB. A1 for angle AOB = 132°. B1 for angle TAB = (180° − 48°) ÷ 2 = 66°, because tangents from an external point are equal in length, so triangle TAB is isosceles.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Draw in the radii OA and OB, and think about the angle each makes with its tangent.","OATB is a quadrilateral, so its four angles have a known total; triangle TAB has two equal sides.","Write the line 'angle OAT = angle OBT = 90°, so angle AOB = 360° − angle OAT − angle OBT − angle ATB' before substituting."],"codes":["G10"]},{"q":"A circle has centre O and radius 25 cm. AB is a chord of the circle, and the perpendicular distance from O to AB is 7 cm. Work out the length of AB. (non-calculator)","a":"48 cm","sol":"Let M be the foot of the perpendicular from O to AB. The perpendicular from the centre of a circle to a chord bisects the chord, so AM = MB, and triangle OMA has a right angle at M with OA = 25 cm a radius. M1 for AM² = 25² − 7² = 625 − 49 = 576 by Pythagoras' theorem. A1 for AM = 24 cm. A1 for AB = 2 × 24 = 48 cm.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The perpendicular from the centre does something helpful to the chord it meets.","Half the chord, the perpendicular distance and a radius make a right-angled triangle.","Write the line 'AM² = OA² − OM², where OA is a radius' and remember what AM is compared with AB."],"codes":["G10"]},{"q":"Shape S is reflected in the line x = 1, and the image is then reflected in the line x = 4. Describe fully the single transformation that maps S to the final image, using column vector notation, and state whether the final image has the same orientation as S. (non-calculator)","a":"A translation by the column vector (6, 0) — 6 across and 0 up; the orientation is the same as S","sol":"M1 for following one point through both reflections, e.g. (0, 0) → (2, 0) → (6, 0), or (3, 5) → (−1, 5) → (9, 5). A1 for a translation by the column vector (6, 0), that is 6 in the x-direction and 0 in the y-direction — twice the distance between the two mirror lines. B1 for stating the orientation is unchanged, because the second reflection restores the sense reversed by the first.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The two mirror lines are parallel, so nothing ends up turned at an angle.","Choose one convenient point of S and follow it through the first mirror and then the second.","Write the line '(0, 0) → (2, 0) after the first reflection' and carry that image through the second mirror."],"codes":["G8"]},{"q":"Shape S is rotated 90° clockwise about the origin, and the image is then rotated 90° clockwise about the origin again. Describe fully the single transformation that maps S to the final image, and write down the coordinates of the only invariant point. (non-calculator)","a":"A rotation of 180° about the origin; the invariant point is (0, 0)","sol":"Both turns are about the same centre, so the angles add. M1 for 90° + 90° = 180° about the origin. A1 for a rotation of 180° about the origin — no direction need be given for a half turn. B1 for the invariant point (0, 0), the centre of rotation, which maps to itself.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Both turns happen about the same fixed point, so the shape never wanders away from it.","Two turns about one centre can be replaced by a single turn — think about what happens to the angles.","Write the line 'total turn = 90° + 90° about the origin' and then ask which point of the plane has not moved at all."],"codes":["G8"]},{"q":"A, B and C lie on a circle with centre O, and C is on the major arc AB. The line CO is extended to meet the circle again at D, and in this figure CD lies between CA and CB, so that D is on the minor arc AB and O is inside angle ACB. By letting angle ACO = x and angle BCO = y, prove that angle AOB = 2 × angle ACB. (non-calculator)","a":"angle AOB = 2x + 2y = 2(x + y) = 2 × angle ACB","sol":"B1 for OA = OB = OC, because all three are radii of the circle, so triangles OAC and OBC are isosceles. M1 for angle OAC = angle OCA = x and angle OBC = angle OCB = y, the base angles of those isosceles triangles. M1 for using the exterior angle of a triangle: angle AOD = x + x = 2x and angle BOD = y + y = 2y. A1 for angle AOB = angle AOD + angle BOD = 2x + 2y = 2(x + y) = 2 × angle ACB.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Every radius of this circle is the same length — look for the triangles at O that this makes isosceles.","At D the line CD splits angle AOB into two parts, and each part is an exterior angle of one of those triangles.","Write the line 'OA = OC, so angle OAC = angle OCA = x' and mark both of those angles on the diagram."],"codes":["G10"]},{"q":"ABCD is a cyclic quadrilateral in a circle with centre O, with the vertices in that order round the circle. Using only the theorem that the angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference, prove that angle ABC + angle ADC = 180°. (non-calculator)","a":"2 × angle ABC + 2 × angle ADC = 360°, so angle ABC + angle ADC = 180°","sol":"Join OA and OC, giving two angles at O, one on each side of AC. M1 for the angle at the centre standing on the arc ADC, on the same side as D, being 2 × angle ABC. M1 for the angle at the centre standing on the arc ABC, on the same side as B, being 2 × angle ADC. B1 for those two angles at O adding up to 360°, because they are angles at a point. A1 for 2 × angle ABC + 2 × angle ADC = 360°, so angle ABC + angle ADC = 180°.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Drawing the two radii OA and OC creates two angles at the centre that between them fill the whole turn.","Each of those centre angles is linked to one of the two angles you are asked about by the theorem you are told to use.","Write the line 'obtuse or reflex angle AOC on D's side = 2 × angle ABC' and then write the matching line for the other side."],"codes":["G10"]},{"q":"Triangle T has vertices (1, 1), (4, 1) and (1, 3). T is enlarged by scale factor −3 about the point (2, 2) to give triangle T'. Work out the coordinates of the image of the vertex (4, 1), and work out the area of T'. A calculator may be used.","a":"(−4, 5) and an area of 27 square units","sol":"M1 for the vector from the centre (2, 2) to (4, 1), which is (2, −1). M1 for multiplying it by −3 to give (−6, 3) and adding that to the centre. A1 for the image (−4, 5). M1 for area of T = ½ × 3 × 2 = 3 square units together with an area scale factor of (−3)² = 9. A1 for area of T' = 3 × 9 = 27 square units.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Deal with the image point using journeys from the centre, and with the area using the fact that T and T' are similar.","T is right-angled, so its area comes from a base and a height; areas do not scale the same way lengths do.","Write the line 'vector from (2, 2) to (4, 1) = (2, −1)' for the first part, and 'area scale factor = (scale factor)²' for the second."],"codes":["G7"]},{"q":"A circle has centre O and radius 13 cm. AB is a chord of that circle of length 24 cm. The tangents to the circle at A and at B meet at the point T. Work out the length of OT, giving your answer in cm. A calculator may be used.","a":"33.8 cm","sol":"Let M be the midpoint of AB. The perpendicular from the centre to a chord bisects the chord, so AM = 12 cm and OM is perpendicular to AB; by symmetry O, M and T lie on one straight line. B1 for OM = √(13² − 12²) = √(169 − 144) = 5 cm. M1 for angle OAT = 90°, the tangent at A being perpendicular to the radius OA, so triangles OMA and OAT are similar — each has a right angle and they share the angle at O. M1 for OM/OA = OA/OT, that is 5/13 = 13/OT. A1 for OT = 169/5. A1 for 33.8 cm.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The figure is symmetrical about the line through O and T, and that line cuts the chord AB in a special way.","Find the distance from O to the chord first, then use the right angle a tangent makes with its radius to link two similar triangles.","Write the line 'OM² = OA² − AM², where AM is half of AB' and then compare triangle OMA with triangle OAT."],"codes":["G10"]}],"exit":[{"q":"A, B and C lie on a circle with centre O. Angle AOB = 96° and C is on the major arc AB. Write down the size of angle ACB.","a":"48°"},{"q":"PQRS is a cyclic quadrilateral with its vertices in that order. Angle PQR = 68°. Write down the size of angle PSR.","a":"112°"},{"q":"The point (3, 2) is enlarged by scale factor −2 about the origin. Write down the coordinates of its image.","a":"(−6, −4)"},{"q":"Shape A is reflected in the y-axis, and the image is then reflected in the x-axis. Describe fully the single transformation that maps A to the final image.","a":"A rotation of 180° about the origin (0, 0)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The angle at the centre theorem","back":"The angle subtended by an arc at the centre of a circle is twice the angle subtended by the same arc at any point on the circumference.","code":"G10","open":false},{"front":"The angle in a semicircle","back":"The angle subtended at the circumference by a semicircle is 90°. So if AB is a diameter and C is any other point on the circle, angle ACB = 90°.","code":"G10","open":false},{"front":"Angles in the same segment","back":"Angles subtended by the same arc at the circumference, on the same side of that arc, are equal, because they lie in the same segment of the circle.","code":"G10","open":false},{"front":"Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral","back":"A cyclic quadrilateral has all four vertices on the circle. Its opposite angles add up to 180°.","code":"G10","open":false},{"front":"The exterior angle of a cyclic quadrilateral","back":"An exterior angle of a cyclic quadrilateral equals the interior angle at the opposite vertex. It follows from opposite angles adding up to 180°.","code":"G10","open":false},{"front":"Tangent and radius","back":"A tangent touches a circle at one point only. The tangent at any point on a circle is perpendicular to the radius drawn to that point, so the angle between them is 90°.","code":"G10","open":false},{"front":"Tangents from an external point","back":"The two tangents from a point outside a circle to that circle are equal in length, so the triangle they form with the chord joining the points of contact is isosceles.","code":"G10","open":false},{"front":"The perpendicular from the centre to a chord","back":"The perpendicular from the centre of a circle to a chord bisects that chord, and the line from the centre to the midpoint of a chord is perpendicular to it.","code":"G10","open":false},{"front":"The alternate segment theorem","back":"The angle between a tangent and a chord drawn from the point of contact equals the angle subtended by that chord at the circumference in the alternate segment.","code":"G10","open":false},{"front":"Two reflections in mirror lines that cross","back":"The pair is equivalent to a rotation about the point where the two mirror lines cross, through twice the angle between them.","code":"G8","open":false},{"front":"Enlargement by a negative scale factor","back":"The image lies on the opposite side of the centre of enlargement, turned through 180°, with every length multiplied by the size of the scale factor. Object and image are similar.","code":"G7","open":false},{"front":"Describing an enlargement fully","back":"Write the word enlargement, the scale factor including its sign, and the centre of enlargement as coordinates. All three are needed, and one transformation only.","code":"G7","open":false},{"front":"Area scale factor of an enlargement","back":"Under an enlargement of scale factor k, lengths are multiplied by |k| and areas by k². A negative k still gives a positive area scale factor k².","code":"G7","open":false},{"front":"Two reflections in parallel mirror lines","back":"The pair is equivalent to a translation perpendicular to the mirrors, of twice the distance between them, in the direction from the first mirror to the second.","code":"G8","open":false},{"front":"Invariance under combined transformations","back":"Rotations, reflections and translations preserve all lengths and angles, so any combination of them gives an image congruent to the original. An invariant point is one that maps to itself.","code":"G8","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Angle at the centre: angle at the centre = 2 × angle at the circumference, standing on the same arc — recall it; no circle theorem appears in the specification's Appendix or on the Higher tier formulae sheet.","Angle in a semicircle = 90° — recall it; not printed for you.","Angles in the same segment are equal — recall it; not printed for you.","Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral add up to 180°, and an exterior angle equals the opposite interior angle — recall them; not printed for you.","A tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact (angle = 90°) — recall it; not printed for you.","Tangents from an external point are equal in length — recall it; not printed for you.","The perpendicular from the centre to a chord bisects the chord — recall it; not printed for you.","Alternate segment theorem: angle between tangent and chord = angle in the alternate segment — recall it; not printed for you.","Enlargement: image of P = C + k × (vector CP), for centre C and scale factor k; k negative puts the image on the opposite side of C, turned through 180° — recall it; no transformation result is in the Appendix or on either tier's sheet.","Scale factors of an enlargement of scale factor k: lengths × |k|, areas × k² (so a negative k gives a positive area factor) — recall it; not printed for you.","Two reflections in parallel mirror lines a distance d apart = a translation of 2d perpendicular to them; two reflections in mirror lines crossing at angle θ = a rotation of 2θ about the crossing point — recall or derive them by following one point; not printed for you.","Angle facts carried in from earlier work (G3) that this room's solutions quote without proof: angles on a straight line add up to 180°, angles at a point add up to 360°, the angle sum of a triangle is 180° (used in questions 5, 6 and 12) and the angle sum of a quadrilateral is 360° (question 8) — recall all four; none of them is in the Appendix's three lists or on the Higher tier sheet either.","Pythagoras' theorem, a² + b² = c² with c the hypotenuse — needed here for chord, tangent and radius lengths. The Appendix puts it in list 1, 'expected to know… will not be given in the exam', but section 4 of the appendix file records that AQA's Higher tier formulae sheet for exams from 2026 does print Pythagoras' theorem, and the sheet governs what you are given on the day, so it is provided.","What the Higher tier sheet prints (from outside this specification, as the post-2022 Ofqual requirement postdates this Version 1.0 document): area of a trapezium, volume of a prism, circumference and area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios, the quadratic formula, the sine rule, the cosine rule, Area = ½ab sin C, compound interest, and the two probability rules. Of the results this room uses, only Pythagoras' theorem is on that list.","Everything else on this page must be recalled or derived: not one circle theorem, enlargement rule or combination-of-transformations result appears in any of the Appendix's three lists or on either tier's formulae sheet. Read your own tier's insert on the day, and expect to quote every theorem above by name in your working."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:37:47Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"All six fixes verified against the kit JSON and nothing else in the kit changed (field-by-field diff against the version I first judged: only questions[0].q, questions[5].q, questions[9].q, questions[11].q, cards[12].back and four formulae items differ). Every answer re-solved again in Python and all still correct. The circle-theorem proof is now watertight: 49,958 randomly generated unit-circle configurations satisfying the new wording (C on the major arc, D on the minor arc AB, CD between CA and CB) all gave angle ACB = x + y and angle AOB = 2(x + y) with zero failures, and my original counter-example (A 80°, B 100°, C 0°, where angle ACB = x − y) is excluded by it. Question 0's \"smaller angle AOB\" is unambiguous (74° against the reflex 286°); question 5's tangent-chord angle is now pinned to one side of AB and still gives 71°; questions 5 and 9 are properly non-calculator, leaving 12 non-calculator questions; |k| is now consistent across cards[12], cards[10] and formulae item 10; and the Formulae sheet's tier claims still match section 4 of appendix.md item for item, with Pythagoras the only room result the Higher insert prints. Publishable as it stands."}}},"geometry-mensuration-higher":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"geometry-mensuration-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"This room carries the Higher tier additions to mensuration and calculation, and it is where a large part of the 20% Geometry weighting on the Higher papers is earned. Foundation work stops at right-angled triangles in two dimensions and at lengths in similar figures. Here the triangle need not have a right angle at all: the sine rule, the cosine rule and Area = ½ab sin C handle any triangle, and Pythagoras' theorem and the trigonometric ratios are taken into three dimensional solids by hunting out a right-angled triangle inside them. Similar figures gain their area and volume scale factors, so one length ratio now settles every area and every volume. Examiners set these as multi-step AO3 problems — bearings, fields, roofs, masts and scaled models — and Paper 1 asks the same work with no calculator, where sin 60° must become √3/2 and the answer left as a surd.","idea":[{"h":"Area and volume scale factors in similar figures","t":"Two figures are similar when one is an enlargement of the other, so every pair of corresponding lengths is in the same ratio. Call that ratio the linear scale factor k. At Foundation you used k on lengths only; at Higher, areas are multiplied by k² and volumes by k³. So lengths in the ratio 2 : 5 give areas in the ratio 4 : 25 and volumes in the ratio 8 : 125. The work also runs backwards: given an area ratio, k = √(area factor); given a volume ratio, k = ∛(volume factor). Because mass = density × volume, two solids of the same material also have masses in the ratio k³. Examiners test this with a small model and a full-size object, with two tins, or with a cone cut by a plane parallel to its base, and the common error is multiplying a volume by k instead of by k³.","code":"G19"},{"h":"Pythagoras' theorem and the trigonometric ratios in three dimensions","t":"Nothing new is needed beyond a² + b² = c² and sin, cos and tan — what is new is finding the right-angled triangle inside a solid. The routine is: name the triangle by its three vertices, check it really has a right angle, redraw it flat with the lengths you know marked on it, then work. In a cuboid the diagonal needs Pythagoras twice: first across the base, then up the vertical edge, giving d = √(l² + w² + h²). The angle between a line and a plane is the angle between that line and its projection on the plane, so drop a perpendicular from the top of the line to the plane and use the right-angled triangle it makes. The specification also allows general triangles in three dimensions 'where possible', which means the sine and cosine rules may be needed on a face that has no right angle. Always keep the full unrounded value from one stage to the next.","code":"G20"},{"h":"The sine rule and the cosine rule","t":"Label a triangle so that side a is opposite angle A, side b opposite B and side c opposite C. The sine rule, a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C, is used when a side and the angle opposite it are known together with one more side or angle; turn it upside down as sin A/a = sin B/b = sin C/c when the unknown is an angle. The cosine rule, a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A, is used in the two cases the sine rule cannot start: two sides and the included angle (find the third side), and three sides (find any angle, from the rearrangement cos A = (b² + c² − a²)/(2bc)). A negative cosine is not an error — it tells you the angle is obtuse, and the calculator returns the obtuse value directly. Both rules are printed on the Higher tier formulae sheet, but you must still choose the right one and label the triangle correctly.","code":"G22"},{"h":"Area = ½ab sin C","t":"The area of any triangle is half the product of two sides multiplied by the sine of the angle between them: Area = ½ab sin C. The angle must be the included angle, the one sitting between the two sides you use; if the given angle is somewhere else, find the included angle first or find a perpendicular height instead. The formula is worth as much in reverse: given the area and the two sides, sin C = 2 × Area/(ab) gives the angle, and given the area, one side and the included angle, the other side follows. On a non-calculator paper the angle is usually 30°, 45°, 60° or 150°, so the exact value goes in and the answer is left as a surd such as 21√3/2. It is printed on the Higher tier formulae sheet, so the marks are for choosing it, substituting correctly and rearranging.","code":"G23"}],"examples":[{"title":"Area and volume ratios of two similar cylinders","setup":"Two similar solid cylinders are made of the same material. Corresponding lengths on the smaller and on the larger are in the ratio 2 : 5. The smaller cylinder has a volume of 48 cm³. Write down the ratio of their surface areas and the ratio of their volumes, and work out the volume of the larger cylinder. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Divide the bigger number in the ratio by the smaller: going from the small cylinder to the large one, the linear scale factor is k = 5/2 = 2.5.","Areas are multiplied by k², and (5/2)² = 25/4, so the surface areas are in the ratio 4 : 25.","Volumes are multiplied by k³, and (5/2)³ = 125/8, so the volumes are in the ratio 8 : 125.","Volume of the larger = volume of the smaller × 125/8 = 48 × 125/8 — this is the line the method mark is awarded for.","48 × 125/8 = 6 × 125 = 750, so the larger cylinder has a volume of 750 cm³."],"cues":["How many times longer is the large cylinder than the small one?","What happens to an area when every length is multiplied by k?","What happens to a volume when every length is multiplied by k?","Which of the three ratios links the two volumes?","Which unit belongs on a volume measured in centimetres?"]},{"title":"Diagonal of a cuboid and the angle it makes with the base","setup":"A cuboid ABCDEFGH stands on the horizontal base ABCD, with E above A, F above B, G above C and H above D. AB = 8 cm, BC = 6 cm and the vertical edge CG = 5 cm. Work out the length of the diagonal AG, to 1 decimal place, and the size of the angle this diagonal makes with the base, to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Across the base first: triangle ABC has its right angle at B, so AC² = 8² + 6² = 64 + 36 = 100 and AC = 10 cm.","CG is vertical while AC lies flat, so triangle ACG has its right angle at C, with AC = 10 cm and CG = 5 cm as its shorter sides.","AG² = 10² + 5² = 100 + 25 = 125, so AG = √125 = 11.2 cm to 1 decimal place — this is the line the method mark is awarded for.","The diagonal meets the base along AC, so the angle wanted is GAC; in triangle ACG the side opposite GAC is CG = 5 cm and the side next to it is AC = 10 cm, giving tan GAC = 5/10.","GAC = tan⁻¹(0.5) = 26.6° to 1 decimal place."],"cues":["Which two edges form the right-angled triangle that contains AC?","Which triangle contains AG and has a right angle in it?","Which two lengths are the shorter sides of that triangle?","Which trigonometric ratio uses the opposite and the adjacent sides?","How is an angle recovered once its tangent is known?"]},{"title":"Cosine rule then sine rule in one triangle","setup":"In triangle ABC the side a = BC is 9 cm, the side b = AC is 7 cm and the angle C is 62°. Work out the length of the side c = AB, to 1 decimal place, and then the size of angle A, to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["The known angle sits between the two known sides, so the cosine rule applies in the form c² = a² + b² − 2ab cos C.","Substituting gives c² = 9² + 7² − 2 × 9 × 7 × cos 62° = 130 − 126 cos 62° — this is the line the method mark is awarded for.","126 cos 62° = 59.1534…, so c² = 130 − 59.1534… = 70.8466… and c = 8.4 cm to 1 decimal place.","Angle A is opposite the 9 cm side and the 62° angle is opposite c, so the sine rule with the full unrounded value of c gives sin A/9 = sin 62°/8.41704…","sin A = 9 × sin 62° ÷ 8.41704… = 0.94409…, so A = 70.8° to 1 decimal place."],"cues":["Which two sides does the 62° angle sit between?","Which letters do 9, 7 and 62° replace in the formula?","What must be done to c² to reach c?","Which side is opposite angle A, and which is opposite the 62° angle?","Why should the stored value of c be used rather than 8.4?"]},{"title":"Area = ½ab sin C forwards and backwards","setup":"Triangle ABC has AB = 8 cm, AC = 11 cm and angle BAC = 40°. Triangle DEF has DE = 9 cm, DF = 12 cm and an area of 27 cm², and its angle EDF is acute. Work out the area of triangle ABC, to 1 decimal place, and the size of angle EDF. A calculator may be used.","steps":["In triangle ABC the given angle lies between the two given sides, so Area = ½ × 8 × 11 × sin 40° — this is the line the method mark is awarded for.","½ × 8 × 11 = 44, so the area is 44 sin 40° = 28.3 cm² to 1 decimal place.","Triangle DEF uses the same formula with the area known instead: ½ × 9 × 12 × sin EDF = 27.","½ × 9 × 12 = 54, so sin EDF = 27 ÷ 54 = 0.5.","EDF = sin⁻¹(0.5) = 30°."],"cues":["Does the 40° angle lie between AB and AC?","What do the halved side lengths multiply to?","Which quantity in the formula is the unknown this time?","What is the area divided by to leave the sine on its own?","Which acute angle has this value as its sine?"]},{"title":"Cosine rule with no calculator","setup":"(non-calculator) In triangle PQR, PQ = 5 cm, PR = 8 cm and angle QPR = 60°. Work out the exact length of QR. No calculator may be used.","steps":["The given angle is between the two given sides, so the cosine rule gives QR² = PQ² + PR² − 2 × PQ × PR × cos QPR.","Substituting gives QR² = 5² + 8² − 2 × 5 × 8 × cos 60° — this is the line the method mark is awarded for.","cos 60° = 1/2 exactly, so the last term is 80 × 1/2 = 40.","QR² = 25 + 64 − 40 = 49.","QR = √49 = 7 cm exactly."],"cues":["Which rule suits two sides and the angle between them?","Which letters do 5, 8 and 60° replace?","Which exact value replaces the cosine here?","What do the three terms come to altogether?","What undoes the squaring?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A solid is enlarged by linear scale factor 3. By what number is its volume multiplied?","a":"27"},{"q":"In triangle ABC you know sides a and b and the angle C between them. Which rule finds c?","a":"The cosine rule, c² = a² + b² − 2ab cos C"},{"q":"A triangle has sides 6 cm and 10 cm with an included angle of 30°. Work out its area.","a":"15 cm²"},{"q":"Two similar cones have volumes in the ratio 8 : 27. What is the ratio of their heights?","a":"2 : 3"},{"q":"In a cuboid, which theorem is used twice to find the longest diagonal?","a":"Pythagoras' theorem"}]},"room":{"facts":["The sine rule: a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C, where side a is opposite angle A. Turned over as sin A/a = sin B/b = sin C/c when an angle is wanted.","The cosine rule: a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A, with A the angle between b and c; rearranged for an angle it is cos A = (b² + c² − a²)/(2bc).","Area of any triangle = ½ab sin C, where C is the angle included between the sides a and b.","For similar figures with linear scale factor k, areas are multiplied by k² and volumes (and, for the same material, masses) by k³.","The longest diagonal of a cuboid with edges l, w and h is √(l² + w² + h²), from Pythagoras' theorem used twice.","Exact values for non-calculator work: sin 30° = 1/2, sin 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = √3/2, cos 30° = √3/2, cos 45° = √2/2, cos 60° = 1/2, tan 45° = 1."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Triangle ABC has AB = 10 cm, AC = 7 cm and angle BAC = 30°. Work out the area of triangle ABC.","a":"17.5 cm²","sol":"The 30° angle is included between AB and AC, so Area = ½ab sin C = ½ × 10 × 7 × sin 30°. M1 for this substitution. sin 30° = 1/2 exactly, so Area = 35 × 1/2 = 17.5. A1 for 17.5 cm² with the unit.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The two lengths you are given meet at the angle you are given.","There is one area formula that works from two sides and the angle between them.","Write the area formula with the two sides and the included angle in place, leaving the sine to be replaced by its exact value."],"codes":["G23"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Two similar solids have corresponding lengths in the ratio 3 : 4. Work out the ratio of their volumes.","a":"27 : 64","sol":"The linear scale factor is 4/3, and volumes are multiplied by k³, so each number in the length ratio is cubed: the volume ratio is 3³ : 4³. M1 for cubing both parts of the ratio. 3³ = 27 and 4³ = 64, so the ratio is 27 : 64. A1.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A ratio of lengths does not carry straight across to volumes.","Each number in the length ratio is raised to a power, and the power depends on how many dimensions are involved.","Write the length ratio again with each part ready to be raised to that power."],"codes":["G19"]},{"q":"In triangle ABC, angle BAC = 42°, angle ABC = 73° and BC = 9 cm. Work out the length of AC, to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"12.9 cm","sol":"BC is opposite angle A and AC is opposite angle B, so the sine rule gives AC/sin 73° = 9/sin 42°. M1 for a correct sine rule statement or its rearrangement AC = 9 sin 73° ÷ sin 42°. AC = 9 × 0.95630… ÷ 0.66913… = 12.8625… A1 for 12.9 cm.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["One side and the angle facing it are both known here.","Pair each side with the angle opposite it before writing anything down.","Write the sine rule as one fraction for the unknown side and one for the known side, each over the sine of its opposite angle."],"codes":["G22"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A cuboid measures 3 cm by 4 cm by 12 cm. Work out the length of the longest diagonal of the cuboid.","a":"13 cm","sol":"Across the 3 cm by 4 cm base, the base diagonal d satisfies d² = 3² + 4² = 9 + 16 = 25. M1 for using Pythagoras' theorem on the base. The longest diagonal L then satisfies L² = 25 + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169, so L = √169 = 13. A1 for 13 cm.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The diagonal you want runs from one corner to the corner furthest away, through the inside of the solid.","Reach it in two stages, doing the flat base first and the vertical edge second.","Write Pythagoras' theorem for the right-angled triangle formed by the two edges of the base."],"codes":["G20"]},{"q":"In triangle ABC, AB = 11 cm, BC = 8 cm and angle ABC = 47°. Work out the length of AC, to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"8.1 cm","sol":"The known angle is between the two known sides, so the cosine rule gives AC² = 11² + 8² − 2 × 11 × 8 × cos 47°. M1 for this substitution. AC² = 185 − 176 × 0.68199… = 185 − 120.0317… = 64.9682… M1 (dep) for reaching AC². AC = √64.9682… = 8.0602… A1 for 8.1 cm.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["No angle here is opposite a known side, so the sine rule cannot get started.","Two sides with the angle between them is exactly the case one of the two rules is built for.","Write the cosine rule with the unknown side squared on the left and the two known sides and their included angle on the right."],"codes":["G22"]},{"q":"A triangle has sides of length 5 cm, 8 cm and 11 cm. Work out the size of the largest angle, to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"113.6°","sol":"The largest angle is opposite the longest side, so it is opposite the 11 cm side. Call it A, with b = 5 and c = 8: cos A = (5² + 8² − 11²)/(2 × 5 × 8). M1 for the rearranged cosine rule with 11 opposite A. cos A = (25 + 64 − 121)/80 = −32/80 = −0.4. A1 for −0.4. A = cos⁻¹(−0.4) = 113.578… A1 for 113.6°.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["In any triangle the biggest angle always faces the longest side.","With all three sides known and no angle known, only one of the two rules can start.","Write the cosine rule rearranged so that the cosine of the angle is on its own on the left."],"codes":["G22"]},{"q":"Triangle XYZ has XY = 14 cm, XZ = 9 cm and an area of 45 cm². Angle YXZ is acute. Work out the size of angle YXZ, to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"45.6°","sol":"Angle YXZ is included between XY and XZ, so ½ × 14 × 9 × sin YXZ = 45. M1 for this equation. ½ × 14 × 9 = 63, so sin YXZ = 45/63 = 0.71428… A1 for the sine. YXZ = sin⁻¹(0.71428…) = 45.584… A1 for 45.6°.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The area formula that uses two sides and the angle between them can be read in either direction.","Put what you know into that formula and treat the angle as the unknown in an equation.","Write the area formula with the two given sides in place and the area on the other side of the equals sign."],"codes":["G23"]},{"q":"A monument is a pyramid with a rectangular base 12 m by 16 m. Its apex is vertically above the centre of the base, and each of the four sloping edges is 15 m long. Work out the vertical height of the monument, to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"11.2 m","sol":"A sloping edge, the vertical height and the line from the centre of the base to a base corner form a right-angled triangle, so the base diagonal comes first: diagonal² = 12² + 16² = 144 + 256 = 400, giving a diagonal of 20 m. M1 for Pythagoras' theorem across the rectangular base. The centre is the midpoint of that diagonal, so the distance from the centre to a corner is 10 m. A1. The height h then satisfies h² = 15² − 10² = 225 − 100 = 125. M1 for Pythagoras' theorem in the vertical triangle. h = √125 = 11.180… so the height is 11.2 m to 1 decimal place. A1.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["A sloping edge, the vertical line through the apex and a line drawn across the base meet in one right-angled triangle.","The centre of a rectangle is the midpoint of each of its diagonals, so the whole diagonal is worth having before it is halved.","Write Pythagoras' theorem for the diagonal of the rectangular base using its two edge lengths."],"codes":["G20"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Two solid statues are mathematically similar and are cast from the same bronze. The larger statue is 45 cm tall and has a mass of 13.5 kg. The smaller statue is 30 cm tall. Work out the mass of the smaller statue.","a":"4 kg","sol":"The linear scale factor from the smaller statue to the larger is k = 45/30 = 3/2. B1. Both statues are the same bronze, so mass is multiplied by k³ = (3/2)³ = 27/8 going from small to large, and so divided by 27/8 going from large to small: mass = 13.5 × 8/27. M1. 13.5 ÷ 27 = 0.5 and 0.5 × 8 = 4, so the smaller statue has a mass of 4 kg. A1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Statues that are mathematically similar are enlargements of one another, so one number connects every pair of matching lengths.","Both are cast from the same bronze, so mass behaves under that number exactly as volume does — and here the working runs from the larger solid to the smaller.","Write the two heights as a single scale factor in its simplest fractional form, ready to be raised to a power."],"codes":["G19"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) In triangle ABC, angle BAC = 45°, angle ABC = 60° and BC = 6 cm. Work out the exact length of AC, giving your answer in the form k√6 where k is an integer.","a":"3√6 cm","sol":"BC is opposite A and AC is opposite B, so AC/sin 60° = 6/sin 45°. M1 for a correct sine rule statement. Using sin 60° = √3/2 and sin 45° = √2/2 gives AC = 6 × (√3/2) ÷ (√2/2) = 6√3/√2. M1 for the exact values substituted. Rationalising, 6√3/√2 = 6√6/2 = 3√6. A1 for 3√6 cm.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A side and the angle opposite it are both known, which is what one of the two rules needs.","Both of these angles have exact sines, so no decimals should ever appear.","Write the sine rule as the unknown side over the sine of its opposite angle, equal to the known side over the sine of its opposite angle."],"codes":["G22"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Triangle PQR has PQ = 6 cm, PR = 7 cm and angle QPR = 60°. Work out the exact area of triangle PQR, giving your answer in surd form.","a":"21√3/2 cm²","sol":"The angle is included between PQ and PR, so Area = ½ × 6 × 7 × sin 60°. M1 for this substitution. sin 60° = √3/2, so Area = 21 × √3/2. M1 for using the exact value. Area = 21√3/2 cm² (oe 10.5√3 cm²). A1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Two sides and the angle between them point at one particular area formula.","A surd answer is asked for, so the sine must be replaced by its exact value rather than a decimal.","Write the area formula with the two sides in place, leaving the sine of the included angle unevaluated."],"codes":["G23"]},{"q":"A triangular plot of land ABC has AB = 85 m, BC = 62 m and angle ABC = 108°. Turf costs £4.50 per square metre. Work out the cost of covering the whole plot with turf, to the nearest pound. A calculator may be used.","a":"£11 277","sol":"Angle ABC is included between AB and BC, so Area = ½ × 85 × 62 × sin 108°. M1. Area = 2635 × 0.95105… = 2506.03… m². A1 for the area (accept 2506 m²). Cost = 2506.03… × 4.50 = 11 277.15… M1 for multiplying the area by 4.50. To the nearest pound the cost is £11 277. A1.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The plot has no right angle, so no base-and-height pair is available.","Find the area from the two sides and the angle between them first, then deal with the money.","Write the area formula with the two given sides and the included angle in place."],"codes":["G23"]},{"q":"Two similar cuboids have surface areas of 150 cm² and 384 cm². The volume of the smaller cuboid is 125 cm³. Work out the volume of the larger cuboid. A calculator may be used.","a":"512 cm³","sol":"Areas are multiplied by k², so k² = 384/150 = 2.56. M1. k = √2.56 = 1.6 (the lengths are in the ratio 5 : 8). A1. Volumes are multiplied by k³ = 1.6³ = 4.096, so the larger volume is 125 × 4.096. M1. 125 × 4.096 = 512, so the volume is 512 cm³. A1.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The two areas fix how many times longer one cuboid is than the other, but not directly.","Work back from the area ratio to the length ratio before going forward to volume.","Write the ratio of the two surface areas as a single number and say which power of the scale factor it equals."],"codes":["G19"]},{"q":"A vertical mast AB stands with its foot B on horizontal ground. P and Q are points on the ground, in a straight line with B, with Q between P and B. From P the angle of elevation of the top A is 32°, and from Q it is 51°. PQ = 40 m. Work out the height of the mast, to the nearest metre. A calculator may be used.","a":"51 m","sol":"In triangle APQ, angle AQP = 180° − 51° = 129°, so angle PAQ = 180° − 32° − 129° = 19°. B1 for 129° or 19°. Sine rule: AQ/sin 32° = 40/sin 19°. M1. AQ = 40 × 0.52991… ÷ 0.32556… = 65.107… A1 for AQ (accept 65.1 m). In right-angled triangle ABQ, AB = AQ × sin 51°. M1. AB = 65.107… × 0.77714… = 50.597… so the mast is 51 m tall to the nearest metre. A1.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Neither of the two triangles on the ground line is right-angled on its own, but the one containing both sight lines can still be solved.","Use angles on a straight line to fill in the triangle formed by P, Q and the top of the mast, then find a slant distance before the height.","Write down the angle at Q inside triangle APQ using angles on a straight line."],"codes":["G22","G20"]},{"q":"A square-based pyramid has a base of edge 10 cm and four sloping edges each of length 13 cm. Work out the angle between a sloping edge and the base, to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"57.0°","sol":"The sloping edge meets the base along half a base diagonal. Base diagonal = √(10² + 10²) = √200 = 14.142… M1 for Pythagoras' theorem on the base. Half of it is 7.0710… cm. A1. The sloping edge, the half-diagonal and the vertical height form a right-angled triangle, so cos θ = 7.0710…/13. M1. θ = cos⁻¹(0.54392…) = 57.048… so the angle is 57.0° to 1 decimal place. A1.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The apex sits directly above the point where the two base diagonals cross.","The sloping edge, its shadow on the base and the vertical height make a right-angled triangle; find the shadow first.","Write Pythagoras' theorem for the diagonal of the square base."],"codes":["G20"]},{"q":"A ship sails from port P on a bearing of 040° for 18 km to a point Q. It then sails on a bearing of 155° for 25 km to a point R. Work out the distance PR, to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"23.8 km","sol":"The bearing of P from Q is 040° + 180° = 220°. B1. Angle PQR = 220° − 155° = 65°. B1. Two sides with the included angle, so PR² = 18² + 25² − 2 × 18 × 25 × cos 65°. M1. PR² = 949 − 900 × 0.42261… = 949 − 380.356… = 568.643… M1 (dep). PR = √568.643… = 23.846… so PR = 23.8 km. A1.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["A sketch with a north line drawn at Q turns the two bearings into one angle inside the triangle.","Find the angle at Q first, using the back bearing of the first leg, then treat PQR as an ordinary triangle.","Write down the bearing of P from Q by adding 180° to the bearing of the first leg."],"codes":["G22"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Two similar shapes have corresponding lengths in the ratio 2 : 3. Write the ratio of their areas.","a":"4 : 9"},{"q":"Write down the cosine rule for a² in triangle ABC.","a":"a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A"},{"q":"A triangle has sides 9 cm and 10 cm with an included angle of 30°. Work out its area.","a":"22.5 cm²"},{"q":"In triangle ABC, a = 8 cm, angle A = 40° and angle B = 65°. Work out b, to 1 decimal place.","a":"11.3 cm"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The sine rule","back":"a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C, where side a is opposite angle A. Use it when a side and the angle opposite it are both known.","code":"G22","open":false},{"front":"The cosine rule","back":"a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A, where angle A is the angle between sides b and c. Use it for two sides and the included angle.","code":"G22","open":false},{"front":"The cosine rule rearranged for an angle","back":"cos A = (b² + c² − a²)/(2bc). Use it when all three sides are known. A negative cosine means the angle is obtuse.","code":"G22","open":false},{"front":"The choice between the sine rule and the cosine rule","back":"Cosine rule for three sides, or for two sides and the included angle. Sine rule whenever a side and its opposite angle are known as a pair.","code":"G22","open":false},{"front":"Area of a triangle from two sides and the angle between them","back":"Area = ½ab sin C, where C is the angle between sides a and b. It works for any triangle, with no perpendicular height needed.","code":"G23","open":false},{"front":"Area = ½ab sin C rearranged for the angle","back":"sin C = 2 × Area ÷ (ab), then C = sin⁻¹ of that value. The two sides used must be the ones enclosing C.","code":"G23","open":false},{"front":"The included angle","back":"The angle lying between the two sides being used. Both the cosine rule and Area = ½ab sin C need it; if the given angle is elsewhere, find the included angle first.","code":"G23","open":false},{"front":"Area scale factor of similar figures","back":"If corresponding lengths are in the ratio 1 : k, corresponding areas are in the ratio 1 : k². Multiply an area by k² to enlarge it.","code":"G19","open":false},{"front":"Volume scale factor of similar figures","back":"If corresponding lengths are in the ratio 1 : k, corresponding volumes are in the ratio 1 : k³. Multiply a volume by k³ to enlarge it.","code":"G19","open":false},{"front":"Linear scale factor from an area ratio","back":"k = √(area factor). Areas in the ratio 25 : 64 give k = 8/5, and then volumes in the ratio 125 : 512.","code":"G19","open":false},{"front":"Linear scale factor from a volume ratio","back":"k = ∛(volume factor). Volumes in the ratio 8 : 125 give k = 5/2, and then areas in the ratio 4 : 25.","code":"G19","open":false},{"front":"Mass of similar solids made of the same material","back":"Mass is proportional to volume, so it scales by k³ too. Doubling every length multiplies the mass by 8.","code":"G19","open":false},{"front":"The longest diagonal of a cuboid","back":"d = √(l² + w² + h²). It comes from Pythagoras' theorem used twice: first across the base, then up the vertical edge.","code":"G20","open":false},{"front":"The angle between a line and a plane","back":"The angle between the line and its projection on the plane. Drop a perpendicular from the far end of the line to the plane and use the right-angled triangle formed.","code":"G20","open":false},{"front":"The method for trigonometry in a three dimensional figure","back":"Name a triangle inside the solid by its vertices, check it has a right angle, redraw it flat with the known lengths marked, then apply Pythagoras' theorem or a ratio.","code":"G20","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["The sine rule: a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C, where a, b and c are the sides opposite angles A, B and C — the Appendix places it in list 1 (expected to be known and not given in the exam), but the AQA Higher tier formulae sheet issued for every series since 2022, which is outside this specification, prints it. The sheet governs what you are given on the day, so it is provided.","The cosine rule: a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A — Appendix list 1 (expected to be known, not given in the exam); the AQA Higher tier formulae sheet prints it, so on the day it is provided. The sheet governs.","Area of a triangle = ½ab sin C, with C the angle between sides a and b — Appendix list 1; the AQA Higher tier formulae sheet prints it, so it is provided. The sheet governs.","The cosine rule rearranged to give an angle, cos A = (b² + c² − a²)/(2bc) — the sheet prints only the a² form, so this rearrangement must be made by you in the exam.","Area = ½ab sin C rearranged to give an angle, sin C = 2 × Area ÷ (ab) — again the sheet prints only the forward form, so the rearrangement is yours to make.","Pythagoras' theorem: a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse — Appendix list 1; also printed on the AQA Higher tier formulae sheet, so it is provided.","The right-angled trigonometric ratios: sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b with c the hypotenuse; in words, sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse, tan θ = opposite/adjacent — Appendix list 1; all three are printed on the AQA Higher tier formulae sheet.","Area scale factor of similar figures: multiply by k², where k is the linear scale factor — on none of the Appendix's lists and not on the formulae sheet, so it must be recalled.","Volume (and, for one material, mass) scale factor of similar figures: multiply by k³ — not in the Appendix and not on the formulae sheet: recall it.","Linear scale factor recovered from a ratio of areas or volumes: k = √(area factor) and k = ∛(volume factor) — not on the formulae sheet: recall it.","The longest diagonal of a cuboid: d = √(l² + w² + h²) — not on the formulae sheet, but you can derive it by applying Pythagoras' theorem across the base and then up the vertical edge.","Exact values needed for surd answers on Paper 1: sin 30° = 1/2, sin 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = √3/2, cos 30° = √3/2, cos 45° = √2/2, cos 60° = 1/2, tan 30° = √3/3, tan 45° = 1, tan 60° = √3 — on neither tier's formulae sheet: recall them.","The Higher tier sheet also prints every result on the Foundation tier sheet — the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, the circumference and area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem, the three right-angled ratios, compound interest and the addition rule for probability — plus the quadratic formula, the sine rule, the cosine rule, the area of a triangle from two sides and the angle between them (½ab sin C), and the multiplication rule for probability with a conditional probability. Those five additions are the whole of what the Higher sheet adds. None of them replace the results above that this room says you must recall.","The curved surface area of a cone, the surface area and volume of a sphere and the volume of a cone sit in Appendix list 3: they are not to be memorised and are not on the formulae sheet either — they are given inside the relevant question when a question needs them.","Areas and volumes of the everyday shapes used alongside this room — ½ × base × perpendicular height for a triangle, base × perpendicular height for a parallelogram, and the cuboid, cylinder, pyramid, arc and sector results — are on neither sheet, so recall them or derive them from the volume of a prism. The other area of a triangle, ½ab sin C, is a separate result and is printed on the Higher sheet, as item 3 above says."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:36:06Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.94,"notes":"Re-judged the whole kit from the fixed JSON: both changed questions re-solve in python to exactly their printed answers (11.2 m and 4 kg), all 16 bank answers, the 4 exit-ticket answers and the 5 check answers are still correct in the form and accuracy asked, every sol's mark tally still equals its m, six questions remain non-calculator, the difficulty spread is 4/7/5 with G19 3, G20 4, G22 6, G23 4, cards are 15 closed fact cards with backs at most 32 words, and every Formulae sheet claim now matches appendix.md section 4's Higher insert exactly — publishable as it stands."}}},"geometry-vectors-higher":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"geometry-vectors-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Vectors are the last idea in the Higher geometry list and one of the places where the reasoning marks sit. A Higher paper rarely asks you only to add two arrows: it gives a triangle or a parallelogram, names two vectors a and b, and then asks you to write a third vector in terms of them, to work out a ratio along a line, or to prove that two lines are parallel or that three points lie on one straight line. Those demands are AO2 and AO3, so the marks are for the route you choose, the scalar multiple you pull out and the conclusion you write in words — an answer with no working scores almost nothing. This room turns the Foundation work on adding, subtracting and scaling vectors into arguments an examiner will accept.","idea":[{"h":"Writing any vector in a figure in terms of the ones you are given","t":"Throughout this room the vector from A to B is written AB; on paper you write it with an arrow above the two letters, and single letters such as a and b are underlined or written in bold. Everything rests on one move: a vector between two points is the finish minus the start, AB = OB − OA, so if OA = a and OB = b then AB = b − a. When there is no arrow drawn straight from one point to another, build a route out of arrows that are drawn, adding them head to tail (AB + BC = AC) and reversing the sign of any you travel against, since BA = −AB. Points fixed by a ratio are handled the same way: AP:PB = m:n cuts AB into m + n equal parts, so AP = (m/(m + n))AB and OP = a + (m/(m + n))(b − a); the midpoint is the case m = n, giving OM = (1/2)(a + b). Examiners award the method mark for the route written down in terms of a and b before it is multiplied out, and the accuracy mark for the simplified form, so always collect the a terms and the b terms at the end.","code":"G25 (i)"},{"h":"Proving lines parallel, points collinear, and reading off the ratio","t":"Once a vector is written in terms of a and b, factorising it proves something geometric. If one vector is a scalar multiple of another, q = kp, the two are parallel and the length of q is |k| times the length of p — so MN = (1/2)AB says at once that MN is parallel to AB and half as long. That is why the last line of a vector proof always factorises: (1/2)b − (1/2)a is just algebra, but (1/2)(b − a) names AB and finishes the argument. Collinearity needs one more sentence. Parallel vectors can sit on two different lines, so scalar multiple alone never proves three points lie on a straight line; you must also say the two vectors share a common point, as in 'OT = 3OS, and OS and OT both pass through O, so O, S and T are collinear'. The same factorising gives ratios along a line: if AM = k AB with k between 0 and 1 then M lies on AB and AM:MB = k:(1 − k). Write the conclusion in words every time — on a 'prove' or 'show that' question the answer is already printed, so only the chain of reasoning can earn the marks.","code":"G25 (ii)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Routing a vector through a point given by a ratio","setup":"In triangle OAB, OA = a and OB = b. The point M lies on AB with AM:MB = 1:2. Write the vector OM in terms of a and b, in its simplest form.","steps":["Travel from A to B through O: AB = AO + OB = −a + b = b − a.","The ratio cuts AB into three equal parts and M is one part along from A, so AM = (1/3)AB = (1/3)(b − a).","Now route O to M through A: OM = OA + AM = a + (1/3)(b − a).","Multiply out and collect: OM = a + (1/3)b − (1/3)a = (2/3)a + (1/3)b."],"cues":["Which two labelled vectors make a route from A to B, and which of them do you travel backwards?","How many equal parts does the ratio cut AB into, and how many of them lie between A and M?","Which labelled vector takes you from O to A, and what carries you on from A to M?","How much of a is left once a third of a has been taken away from a whole a?"]},{"title":"The midpoint theorem proved with vectors","setup":"In triangle OAB, OA = a and OB = b. M is the midpoint of OA and N is the midpoint of OB. Prove that MN is parallel to AB and that MN is half the length of AB.","steps":["Halving each side gives OM = (1/2)a and ON = (1/2)b.","Route M to N through O: MN = MO + ON = −(1/2)a + (1/2)b = (1/2)(b − a).","The third side of the triangle is AB = AO + OB = b − a.","So MN = (1/2)AB. MN is a scalar multiple of AB, so MN is parallel to AB, and the scalar is one half, so MN is half the length of AB."],"cues":["What does the word midpoint do to the length of each of the two vectors from O?","Which way do you travel along OM to leave M, and what does that do to its sign?","Which two given vectors make a route from A to B?","What has to be true of two vectors before you may call the lines through them parallel?"]},{"title":"Proving the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other","setup":"OABC is a parallelogram with OA = a and OC = b, so the side AB is the same vector as OC and the diagonal OB = a + b. M is the midpoint of the diagonal OB. Prove that M is also the midpoint of the other diagonal, AC.","steps":["Halving the diagonal gives OM = (1/2)(a + b).","Route A to M through O: AM = AO + OM = −a + (1/2)(a + b) = (1/2)(b − a).","The whole of the other diagonal is AC = AO + OC = b − a.","So AM = (1/2)AC, which puts M on AC, half way along it — M is the midpoint of AC as well, so each diagonal cuts the other in half."],"cues":["What fraction of the journey from O to B does the journey from O to M cover?","What happens to a when you travel from A back to the origin?","Which route across the parallelogram gets you from A to C using only the two labelled vectors?","How does the vector you found for AM compare with the whole of AC?"]},{"title":"Proving three points lie on a straight line and finding the ratio","setup":"In triangle OAB, OA = a and OB = b. The point P lies on AB with AP:PB = 2:1. The point Q has position vector OQ = (1/2)a + b. Prove that O, P and Q lie on a straight line, and find the ratio OP:PQ.","steps":["Split the journey: AB = AO + OB = b − a, so AP = (2/3)(b − a).","OP = OA + AP = a + (2/3)(b − a) = (1/3)a + (2/3)b.","Compare with OQ: (3/2) × ((1/3)a + (2/3)b) = (1/2)a + b, so OQ = (3/2)OP.","OP and OQ are parallel and both pass through the point O, so O, P and Q lie on a straight line.","PQ = OQ − OP = (3/2)OP − OP = (1/2)OP, so OP:PQ = 2:1."],"cues":["How many equal parts does the ratio cut AB into, and how many of them does AP cover?","Which route from O reaches P using OA and the vector you have just written down?","What must you multiply a third of a by to turn it into a half of a?","Parallel on its own is not enough for a straight line — what else do OP and OQ have?","How much of OP is left over once one whole OP is taken from three halves of OP?"]},{"title":"Collinearity from coordinates","setup":"Relative to the origin, P has coordinates (1, 2), Q has coordinates (4, 8) and R has coordinates (6, 12). Show that P, Q and R lie on a straight line, and find the ratio PQ:QR.","steps":["Take each coordinate of P from the matching one of Q: PQ = (4 − 1, 8 − 2) = (3, 6).","In the same way QR = (6 − 4, 12 − 8) = (2, 4).","Each component of QR is two thirds of the matching component of PQ, so QR = (2/3)PQ.","QR is a scalar multiple of PQ and the two vectors share the point Q, so P, Q and R lie on a straight line, and PQ:QR = 3:2."],"cues":["How do you turn two pairs of coordinates into the vector that joins those points?","Which pair of points do you subtract to get the second leg of the journey?","What single number multiplies the top of PQ to give the top of QR, and does it work underneath as well?","Parallel vectors alone do not make one straight line — which point do PQ and QR have in common?"]}],"check":[{"q":"OA = a and OB = b. Write the vector BA in terms of a and b.","a":"BA = a − b"},{"q":"The vector CD = 4(a + 2b) and the vector EF = a + 2b. What can you say about CD and EF?","a":"CD = 4EF, so CD is parallel to EF and four times as long. They lie on one straight line only if they also share a point."},{"q":"Two vectors in a figure are scalar multiples of each other. What else must you write down before you may say three points are collinear?","a":"That the two vectors share a common point."},{"q":"P lies on AB with AP:PB = 3:1. Write AP in terms of AB.","a":"AP = (3/4)AB"}]},"room":{"facts":["A vector between two points is the finish minus the start: AB = OB − OA, so with OA = a and OB = b, AB = b − a.","Vectors add head to tail, AB + BC = AC, so any vector can be written by choosing a route made only of labelled vectors.","Reversing a vector reverses its sign: BA = −AB, and the sign changes on every term, so −(4a − 3b) = 3b − 4a.","If P lies on AB with AP:PB = m:n then AP = (m/(m + n))AB and OP = a + (m/(m + n))(b − a).","The midpoint M of AB has position vector OM = (1/2)(a + b).","Two vectors are parallel exactly when one is a scalar multiple of the other: if q = kp then p and q are parallel and the length of q is |k| times the length of p.","Three points are collinear when one vector joining a pair is a scalar multiple of another AND the two vectors share a common point; the scalar multiple on its own is not enough.","If AM = kAB with k between 0 and 1, then M lies on AB and AM:MB = k:(1 − k)."],"questions":[{"q":"In triangle OAB, OA = a and OB = b. (non-calculator) Write the vector AB in terms of a and b.","a":"AB = b − a","sol":"There is no labelled arrow from A to B, so travel through O: AB = AO + OB. AO is OA reversed, so AO = −a, and OB = b. B1 for b − a (accept −a + b). Total 1 mark.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["No arrow is drawn straight from A to B, so build one out of the two arrows that are drawn.","Go from A back to the origin first and then out to B, and remember what travelling against an arrow does to its sign.","First line: write AB as the journey AO followed by OB, then replace each leg by the letter that labels it, with its sign."],"codes":["G25 (i)"]},{"q":"The vector AB = 4a − 3b. (non-calculator) Write the vector BA in terms of a and b.","a":"BA = 3b − 4a","sol":"BA is the same journey travelled backwards, so BA = −AB = −(4a − 3b). B1 for 3b − 4a (accept −4a + 3b). Total 1 mark.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["B to A runs along the same line as A to B, but in the opposite direction.","Reversing a vector reverses the sign of the whole expression, not of one term only.","First line: write BA as the negative of the given vector, keeping the whole expression inside a bracket."],"codes":["G25 (i)"]},{"q":"OPQR is a trapezium with OP = p and OR = r. The side PQ is parallel to OR and three times as long, so PQ = 3r. A calculator may be used. Write the vector RQ in terms of p and r, in its simplest form.","a":"RQ = p + 2r","sol":"No arrow is drawn straight from R to Q, so travel round the trapezium: RQ = RO + OP + PQ. RO reverses r, and the stem gives PQ = 3r, so RQ = −r + p + 3r. M1 for the route −r + p + 3r oe. Collecting the r terms, −r + 3r = 2r, so RQ = p + 2r. A1. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["There is no arrow straight from R to Q, so build the journey out of the sides you do know.","Travelling from R to O reverses r, and the stem has already written PQ in terms of r.","First line: write RQ as the journey R to O, then O to P, then P to Q, each leg in terms of p and r."],"codes":["G25 (i)"]},{"q":"MN = 6a − 4b and PQ = 3a − 2b, where a and b are not parallel. (non-calculator) Explain why MN is parallel to PQ.","a":"MN = 2PQ, so MN is a scalar multiple of PQ","sol":"Take the common factor out of MN: 6a − 4b = 2(3a − 2b) = 2PQ. B1 for MN = 2PQ (or PQ = (1/2)MN) together with the statement that one vector is a scalar multiple of the other, so the two are parallel. Total 1 mark.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Compare the two expressions term by term — what has happened to each coefficient?","Parallel vectors are linked by one number that multiplies the whole vector.","First line: take the common factor out of MN so that what is left in the bracket matches PQ."],"codes":["G25 (ii)"]},{"q":"OABC is a parallelogram with OA = a and OC = b, so the side AB is the same vector as OC. The point P lies on the diagonal OB with OP:PB = 3:1. A calculator may be used. Write CP in terms of a and b, in its simplest form.","a":"CP = (3/4)a − (1/4)b","sol":"Opposite sides are equal vectors, so AB = OC = b and the diagonal OB = OA + AB = a + b. M1. The ratio cuts OB into four equal parts and P is three of them along from O, so OP = (3/4)(a + b), and routing from C through O gives CP = CO + OP = −b + (3/4)(a + b). M1 for the route with the correct fraction. Multiplying out: (3/4)a + (3/4)b − b = (3/4)a − (1/4)b. A1. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The diagonal OB carries no arrow of its own; build it from the two sides that do.","Turn the ratio into a fraction of the whole diagonal before you travel along it.","First line: write OB as the journey O to A and then A to B, then take the right fraction of it for OP."],"codes":["G25 (i)"]},{"q":"OABC is a parallelogram with OA = a and OC = b, so the side AB is the same vector as OC. M is the midpoint of BC. A calculator may be used. Write AM in terms of a and b, in its simplest form.","a":"AM = b − (1/2)a","sol":"Opposite sides are equal vectors, so AB = OC = b and BC is the side opposite OA travelled the other way, so BC = −a. M1 for AB = b or BC = −a. Route from A: AM = AB + BM = b + (1/2)(−a). M1. So AM = b − (1/2)a. A1. Equivalent route: OB = a + b, OM = a + b − (1/2)a = (1/2)a + b, AM = OM − OA = b − (1/2)a. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["In a parallelogram the opposite sides give you the same vector twice, so AB is already known.","Get from A to M in two legs: along the side AB first, then half of the side BC.","First line: write AB in terms of the side it copies, then write BC as the reverse of the side it copies."],"codes":["G25 (i)"]},{"q":"In triangle OAB, OA = a and OB = b. X lies on OB with OX:XB = 1:3, and Y is the midpoint of AB. A calculator may be used. Write XY in terms of a and b, in its simplest form.","a":"XY = (1/2)a + (1/4)b","sol":"The ratio splits OB into four equal parts, so OX = (1/4)b. B1. Y is the midpoint of AB, so OY = a + (1/2)(b − a) = (1/2)(a + b). M1. Routing through O, XY = XO + OY = −(1/4)b + (1/2)a + (1/2)b = (1/2)a + (1/4)b. A1. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["X and Y are each fixed by their own ratio, so find where each one is before joining them.","A journey from X to Y is allowed to go back through O.","First line: turn the ratio on OB into a fraction of b, then write Y as a route from O through A."],"codes":["G25 (i)"]},{"q":"In triangle OAB, OA = a and OB = b. The point C lies on the line through A and B, on the far side of B from A, with AC = 2AB. A calculator may be used. Write OC in terms of a and b, in its simplest form.","a":"OC = 2b − a","sol":"AB = AO + OB = b − a. M1. AC is twice as long and in the same direction, so AC = 2(b − a) and OC = OA + AC = a + 2(b − a). M1. Multiplying out: a + 2b − 2a = 2b − a. A1. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["C is reached by carrying on along the same line, so the direction of AC is the direction of AB.","Travel from O to A first, then along AC, which is a scalar multiple of AB.","First line: write AB as the journey from A through O to B, then double it for AC."],"codes":["G25 (i)"]},{"q":"Relative to the origin O, the point S has position vector 3a + 2b and the point T has position vector 9a + 6b, where a and b are not parallel. (non-calculator) Show that O, S and T lie on a straight line.","a":"OT = 3OS, so OS and OT are parallel and share the point O, hence O, S and T are collinear","sol":"Take the common factor out of OT: 9a + 6b = 3(3a + 2b) = 3OS. M1. OT is a scalar multiple of OS, so OS and OT are parallel. A1. OS and OT both pass through the point O, and parallel vectors through a common point lie on one straight line, so O, S and T are collinear. A1. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Look for a link between the two position vectors rather than at the points themselves.","If one vector is a whole number of times the other, what does that say about their directions?","First line: take the common factor out of OT so that the bracket matches OS, then look for what the two vectors share besides direction."],"codes":["G25 (ii)"]},{"q":"In triangle OAB, OA = a and OB = b. The point M lies on AB and OM = (1/4)a + (3/4)b. A calculator may be used. Work out the ratio AM:MB.","a":"AM:MB = 3:1","sol":"AM = OM − OA = (1/4)a + (3/4)b − a = −(3/4)a + (3/4)b. M1. Factorising, AM = (3/4)(b − a) = (3/4)AB, since AB = b − a. M1. AM is three quarters of AB, so MB is the remaining quarter and AM:MB = 3:1. A1. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A ratio compares two pieces of one line, so write AM as a multiple of the whole of AB.","Reach M from A by going back to O and out along OM, then compare the result with AB.","First line: write AM as OM with OA taken from it, then take a common factor out of what you get."],"codes":["G25 (i)","G25 (ii)"]},{"q":"ABCD is a quadrilateral with AB = p, BC = q and DC = r. P, Q, R and S are the midpoints of AB, BC, DC and AD. (non-calculator) Prove that PQ = SR.","a":"PQ = (1/2)(p + q) and SR = (1/2)(p + q), so PQ = SR","sol":"PQ = PB + BQ = (1/2)p + (1/2)q. M1 for both halves correct. Going round the quadrilateral, AD = AB + BC + CD = p + q − r, because CD = −DC = −r. M1. S is the midpoint of AD so AS = (1/2)(p + q − r), and R is the midpoint of DC so AR = AD + DR = (p + q − r) + (1/2)r. M1. SR = AR − AS = p + q − (1/2)r − (1/2)(p + q − r) = (1/2)p + (1/2)q. A1. Both PQ and SR equal (1/2)(p + q), so PQ = SR: the two are parallel and equal in length. A1. Total 5 marks.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Write every midpoint as a journey from A built only out of p, q and r.","Only three sides are labelled, so the fourth side has to be made from the other three.","First line: write PQ as half of one labelled side followed by half of the next."],"codes":["G25 (ii)"]},{"q":"A is the point (1, 1), B is (7, 3), C is (11, 9) and D is (2, 6). A calculator may be used. Show that AB is parallel to DC, and write the ratio AB:DC in its simplest form.","a":"AB:DC = 2:3","sol":"AB = (7 − 1, 3 − 1) = (6, 2). M1. DC = (11 − 2, 9 − 6) = (9, 3). M1. DC = (3/2)(6, 2) = (3/2)AB, so DC is a scalar multiple of AB and the two are parallel. A1. The scalar is three halves, so DC is one and a half times as long as AB, giving AB:DC = 2:3. A1. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Turn each pair of points into the vector that joins them before comparing anything.","Two vectors are parallel when one is a multiple of the other, so test the top and the bottom with the same number.","First line: take the coordinates of A from those of B, then do the same with D and C."],"codes":["G25 (i)","G25 (ii)"]},{"q":"In triangle OAB, OA = a and OB = b. M is the midpoint of OB, G lies on AM with AG:GM = 2:1, and N is the midpoint of AB. (non-calculator) Work out the ratio OG:GN.","a":"OG:GN = 2:1","sol":"OM = (1/2)b, so AM = AO + OM = −a + (1/2)b. M1. The ratio gives AG = (2/3)AM = −(2/3)a + (1/3)b. M1. OG = OA + AG = a − (2/3)a + (1/3)b = (1/3)(a + b). A1. N is the midpoint of AB so ON = (1/2)(a + b), and GN = ON − OG = (1/2)(a + b) − (1/3)(a + b) = (1/6)(a + b). M1. OG = (1/3)(a + b) = 2 × (1/6)(a + b) = 2GN, so OG:GN = 2:1. A1. Total 5 marks.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["G lies on AM, so reach it by setting out from A towards M; N is fixed by being half way along AB.","Write OG and ON both in terms of a and b — the difference between them is GN.","First line: write OM as a fraction of b, then write AM as the journey from A through O to M."],"codes":["G25 (i)","G25 (ii)"]},{"q":"A student is working with PQ = 2a − b and QR = 6a − 3b, where a and b are not parallel. The student writes: 'QR = 3PQ, so PQ is parallel to QR, so P, Q and R lie on a straight line.' A calculator may be used. Say whether the final conclusion is correct, and give the reason the middle line on its own does not justify it.","a":"The conclusion is correct, but only because PQ and QR share the point Q; parallel vectors on their own can lie on two different lines","sol":"QR = 6a − 3b = 3(2a − b) = 3PQ, so the student's first line is right. B1. Parallel is not enough on its own: two parallel vectors can be drawn on two different lines, so the middle line does not put four endpoints on one line. B1. Here PQ finishes at Q and QR starts at Q, so the two parallel vectors share a common point, and that is what makes P, Q and R collinear — the conclusion is correct. B1. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Check the student's arithmetic first, then ask what the word parallel does and does not guarantee.","Picture two parallel vectors drawn on two different lines of the page — are their endpoints on one straight line?","First line: take the common factor out of QR and compare it with PQ, then look at which letter the two vectors have in common."],"codes":["G25 (ii)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"OP = p and OQ = q. Write the vector QP in terms of p and q.","a":"QP = p − q"},{"q":"OA = a and R lies on OA with OR:RA = 1:2. Write OR in terms of a.","a":"OR = (1/3)a"},{"q":"CD = 3(2a − 5b) and EF = 2a − 5b. Write down the ratio of the length of CD to the length of EF.","a":"3:1"},{"q":"Two vectors in a figure are scalar multiples of each other and they share a common point. What does that prove about the points at their ends?","a":"They lie on one straight line (the points are collinear)."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The vector AB written from position vectors","back":"AB = OB − OA: the position vector of the finish minus the position vector of the start. With OA = a and OB = b this gives AB = b − a.","code":"G25 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Triangle law for adding vectors","back":"Vectors add head to tail: AB + BC = AC. Any chain of labelled steps that starts at A and finishes at C adds up to AC.","code":"G25 (i)","open":false},{"front":"The route rule for a figure","back":"To write an unknown vector, choose a path from its start to its finish that uses only labelled vectors, then add those vectors, reversing the sign of any travelled backwards.","code":"G25 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Reversing a vector","back":"BA = −AB. Travelling against a labelled arrow subtracts it, and the sign changes on every term: −(4a − 3b) = 3b − 4a.","code":"G25 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Position vector","back":"The vector from the origin O to a point, written OA = a. It fixes where the point is, and the vector joining two points is the difference of their position vectors.","code":"G25 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Position vector of the midpoint of AB","back":"OM = (1/2)(a + b), reached by OM = a + (1/2)(b − a). The midpoint of OA alone has position vector (1/2)a.","code":"G25 (i)","open":false},{"front":"The point dividing AB in the ratio m:n","back":"The ratio cuts AB into m + n equal parts, so AP = (m/(m + n))AB and OP = a + (m/(m + n))(b − a).","code":"G25 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Equal vectors","back":"Two vectors are equal when they have the same magnitude and the same direction, wherever they are drawn. The opposite sides of a parallelogram give equal vectors.","code":"G25 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Test for parallel vectors","back":"Two vectors are parallel exactly when one is a scalar multiple of the other, q = kp. Factorising the expression is what reveals the scalar k.","code":"G25 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"What the scalar k tells you","back":"If q = kp then the length of q is |k| times the length of p, and q points the opposite way to p whenever k is negative.","code":"G25 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Test for collinear points","back":"Two vectors joining the points are scalar multiples of each other AND the two vectors share a common point. Both parts must be written down.","code":"G25 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Parallel does not mean collinear","back":"Parallel vectors can sit on two different lines. Only a shared point pulls them onto one line, which is why a collinearity proof must name that point.","code":"G25 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"The conclusion line of a vector proof","back":"State the scalar multiple, say the vectors are therefore parallel, name the shared point if the claim is a straight line, and give the ratio of the lengths.","code":"G25 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Ratio along a line from a scalar multiple","back":"If AM = kAB with k between 0 and 1 then M lies on AB and AM:MB = k:(1 − k). So AM = (3/4)AB gives AM:MB = 3:1.","code":"G25 (ii)","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Preamble: the Appendix of the 8300 specification lists the prescribed formulae in three lists, and not one of the three contains a vector result. AQA also issues a formulae sheet with every paper; it is later than this specification document and is issued in addition to the Appendix. Section 4 of the course appendix, 'What AQA's formulae sheet prints (inserts for exams from 2026)', records everything the Higher tier insert carries, and no vector result is on it — so every vector result below must be written from memory. One result the Higher insert does print is Pythagoras' theorem, noted below for completeness even though no question here calls for it. See the last item. Where the sheet supplies a formula the Appendix says is to be memorised, the sheet governs on the day, so check the insert at the front of your own paper.","Remember: AB = OB − OA, so with OA = a and OB = b the vector AB = b − a — the finish minus the start.","Remember: BA = −AB, and the sign changes on every term of the expression.","Remember: AB + BC = AC — vectors add head to tail, so a route made of labelled vectors gives any vector in the figure.","Remember: the midpoint M of AB has OM = (1/2)(a + b).","Remember: if AP:PB = m:n then AP = (m/(m + n))AB and OP = a + (m/(m + n))(b − a).","Remember: q = kp means p and q are parallel, and the length of q is |k| times the length of p.","Remember: three points are collinear when one joining vector is a scalar multiple of another and the two vectors share a common point — both halves are needed for the marks.","Remember: if AM = kAB with k between 0 and 1 then AM:MB = k:(1 − k).","Given on the day: Pythagoras' theorem, a² + b² = c². The Appendix places it in list 1, formulae students are expected to know and which 'will not be given in the exam', but section 4 of the course appendix records that AQA's Higher tier formulae sheet (inserts for exams from 2026) prints Pythagoras' theorem, and where the sheet supplies a formula the Appendix says is to be memorised the sheet governs. It is recorded here only because it is printed on the sheet in front of you on the day; nothing in this room needs it. G24 and G25 go as far as adding, subtracting and scaling vectors and using them to argue geometrically, and the lengths compared in this room are read off the scalar multiple, never measured."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:39:45Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.93,"notes":"Re-judged after the fix pass: all 14 bank questions, 4 exit questions, 4 lesson checks and 5 worked examples re-solved symbolically with every answer, simplified form and mark tally (M/A/B summing to m) correct, the two rewritten questions and the relabelled example verified independently, the Formulae sheet now exactly matching section 4 of appendix.md for the Higher insert, and every figure answerable from words alone — publishable as it stands, with only the shared parallelogram context left as a presentational nicety."}}},"probability-higher":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"probability-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Higher tier papers reserve a whole content reference, P9, for conditional probability, and it is the probability idea that separates grades 7 to 9 from the rest. Examiners set it as a two-way table, a tree diagram or a Venn diagram filled with expected frequencies and then ask for a probability 'given that' something has already happened, or they hand you two probabilities and expect the multiplication rule rearranged. The marks are lost in one place above all others: writing the wrong denominator. A conditional probability counts only the cases in which the condition holds, so the total on the bottom is the size of that restricted group, not the size of the whole sample. This room teaches you to restrict first and divide second, on all three representations, and to interpret the answer in the words of the question as AO2 and AO3 demand.","idea":[{"h":"The condition restricts the cases, so it changes the denominator","t":"P(A given B) is the probability of A worked out on the assumption that B has already happened. The condition throws away every case in which B did not happen, so you count only inside B: P(A given B) = (number of cases with both A and B) ÷ (number of cases with B). In probabilities that same statement is P(A given B) = P(A and B) ÷ P(B), which is the Appendix's rule P(A and B) = P(A given B) × P(B) rearranged. Two warnings the examiner relies on. First, P(A given B) and P(B given A) are different numbers: they divide the same overlap by different totals. Second, A and B are independent exactly when P(A given B) = P(A) — if the condition changes nothing, the events do not affect each other; if it changes the probability, they are dependent, which is what makes drawing without replacement a conditional-probability question. Interpret the final value in context: 'of the people who are late, 60% came by bus' earns the communication mark that a bare fraction does not.","code":"P9 (i)"},{"h":"Expected frequencies in two-way tables, tree diagrams and Venn diagrams","t":"The specification asks for conditional probabilities 'through representation using expected frequencies', which means filling the picture with counts out of a total rather than with decimals. Given a total of 900 visitors to a museum and 2/5 of them children, write 360 and 540 into the table, not 0.4 and 0.6; then 30% of the 360 becomes 108 people. Counts are easier to condition on because the restricted group is a row, a column, a branch or a region you can point at. In a two-way table, the condition names a row or a column and its total is the denominator. On a tree diagram, the probabilities on the second set of branches are already conditional — after a red counter is kept out of the bag, the next branch shows 5 out of 11, not 5 out of 12 — so multiply along a path for 'and', and add the separate paths that fit the description. In a Venn diagram, fill the overlap first, work outwards by subtraction, and read P(A given B) as the overlap divided by the whole of region B. All three must give the same answer; choose the one the question has already half drawn for you.","code":"P9 (ii)"}],"examples":[{"title":"A conditional probability from a two-way table","setup":"A sports club has 120 members. 75 of the members are adults and the rest are juniors. 48 of the adults swim, and 66 members altogether swim. One member is chosen at random. Work out the probability that the member is a junior, given that the member swims. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","steps":["Missing totals first: 120 − 75 = 45 juniors, so the right-hand column of the table reads 75, 45, 120.","Missing cell in the swimming row: 66 − 48 = 18 juniors swim, so that row reads 48 adults, 18 juniors, 66 in total.","'Given that the member swims' throws away the 54 members who do not swim, leaving 66 cases to choose from.","P(junior given swims) = 18/66 = 3/11."],"cues":["How many of the members are juniors?","How many juniors are in the swimming row?","Which members are still being considered once you are told the member swims?","Which two of those numbers go on the top and the bottom of the fraction?"]},{"title":"Two beads without replacement","setup":"A bag contains 7 red beads and 5 blue beads. Two beads are taken at random, one after the other, without replacement. Work out the probability that the two beads are different colours. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","steps":["First pair of branches: P(red) = 7/12 and P(blue) = 5/12.","After a red bead is taken, 6 red and 5 blue remain, so the branch for blue shows P(blue given red) = 5/11.","After a blue bead is taken, 7 red and 4 blue remain, so the branch for red shows P(red given blue) = 7/11.","Multiply along each path: red then blue = 7/12 × 5/11 = 35/132, and blue then red = 5/12 × 7/11 = 35/132.","Different colours means either path, so add: 35/132 + 35/132 = 70/132 = 35/66."],"cues":["How many beads are in the bag for the first pick?","How many beads are left, and how many of them are blue, once a red one has gone?","Which two numbers change when the first bead taken is blue instead?","Do you multiply or add along a single path?","Which paths describe 'different colours'?"]},{"title":"Reading both conditional probabilities off a Venn diagram","setup":"In a class of 30 students, 18 have a dog, 12 have a cat and 7 have both a dog and a cat. One student is chosen at random. Work out P(dog given cat) and P(cat given dog), and explain why the two answers are not the same.","steps":["Start in the middle: 7 goes in the overlap of the dog circle and the cat circle.","Work outwards by subtracting: dog only = 18 − 7 = 11, cat only = 12 − 7 = 5, and neither = 30 − 11 − 7 − 5 = 7.","Conditioning on 'has a cat' leaves only the cat circle, which holds 7 + 5 = 12 students, so P(dog given cat) = 7/12.","Conditioning on 'has a dog' leaves only the dog circle, which holds 11 + 7 = 18 students, so P(cat given dog) = 7/18.","The overlap of 7 is the same both times, but the denominators are the 12 cat owners and the 18 dog owners, so the two conditional probabilities differ."],"cues":["Which region of the diagram can be filled in without any working?","How many students have a dog but not a cat?","Once you know the student has a cat, how many students are still being considered?","Which total replaces it when the condition is 'has a dog' instead?","What stays the same in the two fractions, and what changes?"]},{"title":"Expected frequencies from percentages","setup":"A factory makes items on two machines. 3/5 of the items are made on machine A and the rest on machine B. 2% of machine A's items are faulty and 5% of machine B's items are faulty. A batch of 2000 items is checked. Using expected frequencies for the 2000 items, work out the probability that an item was made on machine B, given that the item is faulty. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","steps":["Split the batch: machine A makes 3/5 × 2000 = 1200 items, so machine B makes 2000 − 1200 = 800 items.","Faulty items expected from each machine: 2% of 1200 = 24 from A, and 5% of 800 = 40 from B.","The faulty column of the table therefore totals 24 + 40 = 64 items.","The condition is 'faulty', so only those 64 items count: P(machine B given faulty) = 40/64 = 5/8."],"cues":["How many of the batch come off each machine?","How do you turn a percentage of a machine's output into a number of items?","How many faulty items are expected altogether?","Which of the faulty items were made on machine B?"]},{"title":"Rearranging the multiplication rule","setup":"For the commuters in a city, the probability that a commuter travels by bus is 0.4, the probability that a commuter is late is 0.2, and the probability that a commuter travels by bus and is late is 0.12. Work out P(late given bus) and P(bus given late), and say what each one means for these commuters.","steps":["The formulae sheet gives P(A and B) = P(A given B) × P(B); dividing both sides by P(B) gives P(A given B) = P(A and B) ÷ P(B).","Condition on travelling by bus, so divide by P(bus): P(late given bus) = 0.12 ÷ 0.4 = 0.3, which says 30% of bus users are late.","Now condition on being late, so the divisor becomes P(late) = 0.2 instead of P(bus) = 0.4.","P(bus given late) = 0.12 ÷ 0.2 = 0.6, which says 60% of the commuters who are late travelled by bus."],"cues":["Which printed formula has P(A given B) inside it, and how do you make it the subject?","Which of the three probabilities is the condition when you are told the commuter takes the bus?","What changes on the bottom of the fraction when the condition changes?","What does this second answer say about the late commuters?"]}],"check":[{"q":"In a two-way table, which total becomes the denominator of P(A given B)?","a":"The total of the row or column named by B — the number of cases in which the condition holds — not the grand total."},{"q":"50 people were asked two questions. 20 of them own a car, and 8 of those 20 also own a bicycle. Work out P(bicycle given car).","a":"8/20 = 2/5"},{"q":"A bag holds 6 green and 4 white counters. One counter is taken and not replaced, and it is green. What is the probability that the next counter taken at random is white?","a":"4/9"},{"q":"P(A) = 0.5 and P(A and B) = 0.2. Work out P(B given A).","a":"0.2 ÷ 0.5 = 0.4"},{"q":"P(A given B) = P(A). What does that tell you about A and B?","a":"They are independent: knowing that B has happened does not change the probability of A."}]},"room":{"facts":["P(A given B) = (number of cases with both A and B) ÷ (number of cases with B): the condition restricts which cases are counted, so it sets the denominator.","P(A and B) = P(A given B) × P(B), and rearranged, P(A given B) = P(A and B) ÷ P(B).","P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B), the addition rule, which subtracts the overlap so it is not counted twice — P8 prior knowledge, used in this room to read a Venn diagram of frequencies.","A and B are independent when P(A given B) = P(A), equivalently when P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B); otherwise they are dependent.","Expected frequency = probability × total number, which is how a two-way table, tree diagram or Venn diagram is filled with counts.","On a tree diagram the second set of branches is already conditional on the first: multiply along a path for 'and', add the paths for 'or'.","Without replacement, the number left in the bag and the number of that colour both fall by one, so the second probability depends on the first.","P(A given B) and P(B given A) are usually different, because the same overlap is divided by two different totals.","Inside the restricted group the probabilities still sum to 1, so P(not A given B) = 1 − P(A given B)."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) In a school year group, 25 students own a bike and 10 of those 25 students also own a skateboard. A student is chosen at random from the students who own a bike. Work out the probability that this student owns a skateboard. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"2/5","sol":"The condition restricts the choice to the 25 bike owners, so the denominator is 25 and the numerator is the 10 who own both: 10/25 (M1) = 2/5 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The words 'from the students who own a bike' tell you which group you are now working inside.","A conditional probability counts only the cases in which the condition already holds, so the total on the bottom is not the whole year group.","Write the number who own both over the number of bike owners, then cancel the fraction."],"codes":["P9 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) For two events A and B, P(B) = 0.8 and P(A and B) = 0.28. Work out P(A given B).","a":"0.35","sol":"P(A given B) = P(A and B) ÷ P(B) = 0.28 ÷ 0.8 (M1) = 0.35 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The formulae sheet prints a rule that links P(A and B), P(A given B) and P(B).","Make P(A given B) the subject of that rule by dividing both sides by the probability of the condition.","Divide the probability of both events happening by the probability of the event you are told has happened."],"codes":["P9 (i)"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A box contains 9 apples and 6 pears. One piece of fruit is taken at random and eaten, and it is an apple. A second piece of fruit is then taken at random. Work out the probability that it is a pear. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"3/7","sol":"After the apple has been eaten, 8 apples and 6 pears are left, so there are 14 pieces of fruit (M1). P(pear given the first was an apple) = 6/14 = 3/7 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Nothing is put back, so the box is not the same for the second pick.","Work out how many pieces of fruit are left, and how many of them are pears, before writing any fraction.","The number of pears over the new total, then cancel."],"codes":["P9 (i)","P9 (ii)"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. In a town, 60% of the households own a car. Of the households that own a car, 25% also own a bicycle. 400 households are surveyed. Work out how many of the 400 households would be expected to own both a car and a bicycle.","a":"60 households","sol":"Expected households owning a car = 0.6 × 400 = 240 (M1). Of those, the bicycle branch is conditional on owning a car: 0.25 × 240 = 60 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Turn each percentage into a number of households rather than working with decimals throughout.","The 25% is a percentage of the car-owning households only, not of the whole survey.","Find the expected number of car owners first, then take the stated percentage of that number."],"codes":["P9 (ii)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A Venn diagram for 40 people shows 13 people in set A only, 9 people in both A and B, 6 people in set B only and 12 people in neither set. One of the 40 people is chosen at random. Work out P(A given B). Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"3/5","sol":"Conditioning on B leaves only the B circle, which holds 9 + 6 = 15 people (M1). Of those, the 9 in the overlap are also in A, so P(A given B) = 9/15 = 3/5 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Only part of the diagram is still in play once you are told the person is in set B.","Add the regions that make up the whole of circle B to get the total you divide by.","Put the number in the overlap over the total in circle B and simplify."],"codes":["P9 (ii)","P9 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A hat contains 10 raffle tickets. 4 of the tickets are winning tickets and the other 6 are blank. Two tickets are taken out of the hat at random, one after the other, and neither is put back. Work out the probability that the second ticket taken is a winning ticket. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"2/5","sol":"Winning then winning: 4/10 × 3/9 = 12/90 (M1). Blank then winning: 6/10 × 4/9 = 24/90 (M1). The second ticket is a winner on either path, so 12/90 + 24/90 = 36/90 = 2/5 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["What the second ticket comes out of depends on which kind of ticket went first, so there are two ways the second one can be a winner.","Draw the two-stage tree: on both second branches 9 tickets are left, but the number of winners among them is not the same on the two paths.","Multiply along the path 'winning then winning' and along the path 'blank then winning', then add the two results."],"codes":["P9 (ii)","P9 (i)"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. 200 people were asked whether they had watched a film in the last week. 120 of the people were women, and 84 of those women had watched a film. Altogether 140 of the 200 people had watched a film. One of the people who had watched a film is chosen at random. Work out the probability that this person is a man. Give your answer as a decimal.","a":"0.4","sol":"Men who had watched a film = 140 − 84 = 56 (M1). The condition restricts the choice to the 140 people who had watched a film, so P(man given watched a film) = 56/140 (M1) = 0.4 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Draw the two-way table with rows for watched and not watched and columns for women and men.","The cell you need is not given, but the row total and the other cell in that row are.","Take the number of women who watched away from the total who watched, then divide by that same total."],"codes":["P9 (ii)","P9 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The probability that Maya practises the piano on any day is 3/4. On a day when she practises, the probability that she also reads a book is 2/3. On a day when she does not practise, the probability that she reads a book is 1/2. Work out the probability that Maya reads a book on a randomly chosen day. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"5/8","sol":"Practises and reads: 3/4 × 2/3 = 1/2 (M1). Does not practise and reads: 1/4 × 1/2 = 1/8 (M1). Reading can happen either way, so P(reads) = 1/2 + 1/8 = 5/8 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Reading can happen on a practice day or on a day with no practice, so both cases matter.","The two reading probabilities you are given are conditional, so they sit on the second set of branches of a tree diagram.","Multiply along each of the two reading paths, then add the results over a common denominator."],"codes":["P9 (ii)","P9 (i)"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A test for a plant disease is used on 500 plants. 40 of the plants have the disease, and the test is positive for 36 of those 40. Of the plants that do not have the disease, the test is positive for 24. One plant is chosen at random from the plants whose test was positive. Work out the probability that this plant has the disease. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"3/5","sol":"Plants with a positive test = 36 + 24 = 60 (M1). Of those 60, exactly 36 have the disease, so P(disease given positive) = 36/60 (M1) = 3/5 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A positive test happens to two different kinds of plant, and both are counted in the group you are choosing from.","Put the four expected frequencies into a two-way table with disease and no disease as the columns.","Add the two positive-test frequencies to get the denominator, then use the diseased one as the numerator."],"codes":["P9 (ii)","P9 (i)"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. In a survey of 250 shoppers, 150 bought bread, 90 bought milk and 60 bought both bread and milk. One shopper is chosen at random from the shoppers who bought milk. Work out the probability that this shopper also bought bread. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"2/3","sol":"The condition restricts the choice to the 90 shoppers who bought milk (M1). Of those, 60 also bought bread, so P(bread given milk) = 60/90 = 2/3 (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Two of the three figures given are not needed once you know which group the shopper comes from.","On a Venn diagram, the milk circle is the only region still in play.","Divide the number in the overlap of the two circles by the total in the milk circle."],"codes":["P9 (i)","P9 (ii)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A bag contains 6 red counters and n blue counters. Two counters are taken at random, one after the other, without replacement. The probability that both counters are red is 1/3. Work out the value of n.","a":"n = 4","sol":"P(both red) = 6/(6 + n) × 5/(5 + n) (M1). Setting this equal to 1/3 gives 30/((6 + n)(5 + n)) = 1/3, so (6 + n)(5 + n) = 90 (M1). Expanding: n² + 11n + 30 = 90, so n² + 11n − 60 = 0 (M1). Factorising: (n + 15)(n − 4) = 0, and n cannot be negative, so n = 4 (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Write the total number of counters in the bag in terms of n before you write any probability.","The second red counter comes from a bag with one fewer counter of each kind, and the two probabilities multiply.","Set the product of the two branch probabilities equal to the given probability and cross-multiply to clear the fractions."],"codes":["P9 (i)","P9 (ii)"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A box contains 12 pens: 5 are blue and 7 are black. Two pens are taken at random, one after the other, without replacement. Given that the two pens are the same colour, work out the probability that both pens are blue. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"10/31","sol":"Both blue: 5/12 × 4/11 = 20/132 (M1). Both black: 7/12 × 6/11 = 42/132 (M1). P(same colour) = 20/132 + 42/132 = 62/132 (M1). Conditioning on the same colour, P(both blue given same colour) = (20/132) ÷ (62/132) = 20/62 = 10/31 (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["You are told the outcome is one of two possible paths, and asked how likely it is to have been the first of them.","Work out the probability of each same-colour path, then use P(A given B) = P(A and B) ÷ P(B).","The condition is 'same colour', so its probability is the sum of the two paths and goes on the bottom."],"codes":["P9 (i)","P9 (ii)"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. At a college of 800 students, 3/8 of the students study Spanish. Of the students who study Spanish, 40% also study History. Of the students who do not study Spanish, 20% study History. One student is chosen at random from the students who study History. Work out the probability that this student studies Spanish. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"6/11","sol":"Expected frequencies: 3/8 × 800 = 300 study Spanish and 800 − 300 = 500 do not (M1). Spanish and History: 0.4 × 300 = 120; History without Spanish: 0.2 × 500 = 100 (M1). History altogether = 120 + 100 = 220 (M1). P(Spanish given History) = 120/220 = 6/11 (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Turn every fraction and percentage into a number of students before doing any probability.","The two percentages are taken from two different groups, so work them out separately and then combine.","Build the two-way table of expected frequencies, then divide the Spanish-and-History cell by the History total."],"codes":["P9 (ii)","P9 (i)"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A two-way table records 80 people. 32 of them own a tablet and the other 48 do not. Of the 48 people who do not own a tablet, 12 own a smartwatch. Altogether 20 of the 80 people own a smartwatch. Decide whether owning a smartwatch and owning a tablet are independent, showing your working.","a":"They are independent, because P(smartwatch given tablet) = 1/4 = P(smartwatch)","sol":"Smartwatch owners who also own a tablet = 20 − 12 = 8 (M1). P(smartwatch given tablet) = 8/32 = 1/4 (M1). P(smartwatch) = 20/80 = 1/4 (M1). The condition has not changed the probability, so owning a smartwatch and owning a tablet are independent (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Independence is a comparison between two probabilities, so you will need to work out both of them.","Complete the smartwatch row of the table first, using the row total and the cell you are given.","Compare the probability of owning a smartwatch among the tablet owners with the probability of owning one among all 80 people."],"codes":["P9 (i)","P9 (ii)"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A bag contains red beads and yellow beads in the ratio 4 : 5. There are 27 beads in the bag altogether. Two beads are taken at random, one after the other, without replacement. Work out the probability that at least one of the beads is red. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"82/117","sol":"The ratio has 4 + 5 = 9 parts and 27 ÷ 9 = 3 beads per part, so there are 12 red beads and 15 yellow beads (M1). P(no red) = 15/27 × 14/26 (M1) = 210/702 = 35/117 (M1). 'At least one red' is the complement, so P(at least one red) = 1 − 35/117 (M1) = 82/117 (A1).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Share the beads in the given ratio before any probability work begins.","Listing every way of getting at least one red is slow; there is only one way of getting no red at all.","Work out the probability of both beads being yellow along a single tree path, then take it from 1."],"codes":["P9 (ii)","P9 (i)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"P(B) = 0.25 and P(A and B) = 0.15. Work out P(A given B).","a":"0.6"},{"q":"A Venn diagram shows 14 people in set C, and 6 of them are also in set D. Work out P(D given C), as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"3/7"},{"q":"A box holds 8 sweets: 3 mints and 5 chews. One sweet is taken at random and eaten, and it is a mint. Work out the probability that the next sweet taken at random is also a mint.","a":"2/7"},{"q":"Of 300 drivers, 20% drive a van. Of the van drivers, 15% tow a trailer. How many of the 300 drivers would be expected to drive a van and tow a trailer?","a":"9 drivers"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Conditional probability","back":"The probability of an event worked out on the assumption that another event has already happened, written P(A given B). Only the cases in which B holds are counted.","code":"P9 (i)","open":false},{"front":"P(A given B) in terms of probabilities","back":"P(A given B) = P(A and B) ÷ P(B). It is the multiplication rule P(A and B) = P(A given B) × P(B) rearranged, so the divisor is always the probability of the condition.","code":"P9 (i)","open":false},{"front":"The multiplication rule for P(A and B)","back":"P(A and B) = P(A given B) × P(B). It is printed on the AQA Higher tier formulae sheet, so at Higher tier it is given on the day.","code":"P9 (i)","open":false},{"front":"The addition rule for P(A or B)","back":"P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). Subtracting the overlap stops it being counted twice, which is what a Venn diagram shows. P8 prior knowledge; printed on the AQA Higher tier formulae sheet.","code":"P9 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"P(A given B) counted from frequencies","back":"P(A given B) = number of cases with both A and B ÷ number of cases with B. The condition sets the denominator, which is never the grand total unless B is certain.","code":"P9 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Test for independent events","back":"A and B are independent when P(A given B) = P(A), equivalently when P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). If the condition changes the probability, the events are dependent.","code":"P9 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Dependent events","back":"Events for which one happening changes the probability of the other, so P(A given B) is not equal to P(A). Drawing without replacement always gives dependent events.","code":"P9 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Why P(A given B) and P(B given A) differ","back":"They divide the same overlap by different totals: P(A given B) divides by the number of cases in B, and P(B given A) divides by the number of cases in A.","code":"P9 (i)","open":false},{"front":"P(not A given B)","back":"P(not A given B) = 1 − P(A given B). Inside the restricted group the probabilities of an event and its complement still add up to 1.","code":"P9 (i)","open":false},{"front":"Expected frequency","back":"Expected frequency = probability × total number. It fills a two-way table, tree diagram or Venn diagram with counts instead of decimals, which makes conditioning easier.","code":"P9 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"The denominator when a conditional probability is read from a two-way table","back":"The total of the row or column named by the condition, because those are the only cases still being considered.","code":"P9 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"The second set of branches on a tree diagram","back":"Their probabilities are conditional on the first branch: each is the probability of the second outcome given that the first one has already happened.","code":"P9 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Multiplying along a tree path and adding paths","back":"Multiply the probabilities on the branches followed to get the probability of that sequence, then add the probabilities of all the paths that fit the description.","code":"P9 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Filling a Venn diagram of frequencies","back":"Write the overlap in first, subtract it from each set total to get the 'only' regions, then subtract all three from the grand total to get the region outside both.","code":"P9 (ii)","open":false},{"front":"Name one way of showing expected frequencies so that a conditional probability can be read off","back":"A two-way table, with the condition as a row or column; a tree diagram with frequencies on the branches; a Venn diagram with the frequency written in each region.","code":"P9 (ii)","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["P(A and B) = P(A given B) × P(B). GIVEN on the day at Higher tier. The specification's Appendix puts it in list 2 — formulae students are expected to know or be able to derive, which 'will not be given in the exam' — against content reference P9, but AQA's Higher tier formulae sheet for exams from 2026 prints the multiplication rule for the probability of one outcome and another using a conditional probability. Where the Appendix and the sheet disagree, the sheet governs what is in front of you. Know what it means anyway: the probability of both is the probability of the condition times the probability of the other event once the condition has happened. This rule and the addition rule below are the two results from this room that the Higher tier insert prints; everything else listed here must be recalled or derived. Since 2022 Ofqual has required a formulae sheet in every GCSE Mathematics paper and AQA issues one per tier per series; that sheet is later than the Version 1.0 specification, is issued in addition to its Appendix rather than in place of it, and is reissued each series, so read the sheet at the front of your own paper before relying on anything below being printed.","P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). GIVEN on the day at Higher tier. The Appendix places it in the same list 2, against content reference P8, and the Higher tier sheet prints the addition rule for the probability of one outcome or another. The subtraction removes the overlap that adding P(A) and P(B) counts twice.","P(A given B) = P(A and B) ÷ P(B). RECALL or derive it. Only the product form is printed on the sheet, so the rearrangement is yours to make; it is the form nearly every conditional-probability question needs.","P(A given B) = number of cases with both A and B ÷ number of cases with B. RECALL it. This counting form is in neither the Appendix nor on either tier's sheet, and it is what you use on a two-way table, a tree of frequencies or a Venn diagram.","Expected frequency = probability × total number. RECALL it. Not in the Appendix and on neither sheet; it is how P9's 'representation using expected frequencies' is built.","Independence: A and B are independent when P(A given B) = P(A), equivalently when P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). RECALL it. Neither line appears on the sheet, which prints the general multiplication rule only.","Without replacement, the second branch of a tree uses one fewer item of the chosen kind and one fewer item in total, so P(second | first) has a denominator one smaller than the first branch. RECALL it. Not in the Appendix and on neither sheet.","P(not A) = 1 − P(A), and inside a condition P(not A given B) = 1 − P(A given B). RECALL both. Neither is in the Appendix's three lists nor on either tier's sheet."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:29:29Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.87,"notes":"Every answer key and every sol line checks out exactly (all 15 questions, the 4 exit questions, the 5 lesson checks and all 5 worked examples re-solved with exact fractions), and the Formulae sheet's claims match section 4 of appendix.md for the Higher insert precisely — the addition rule and the conditional multiplication rule are the two it prints; fix the lesson illustration that gives away question 13's (index 12) opening figures before publishing."}}},"statistics-higher":{"id":"AQA-8300","topic":"statistics-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Probability and statistics are 15% of the Higher assessment, and this room holds the three diagrams that only Higher tier students are asked about: histograms with unequal class intervals, cumulative frequency graphs and box plots. They are worth learning properly because the marks are almost all method marks you can secure: frequency density is one division, the frequency a bar stands for is one multiplication, the median and quartiles are three readings off a curve, and the inter-quartile range is one subtraction. The examiner's favourite traps are plotting frequency instead of frequency density when the classes have different widths, plotting a cumulative frequency point at the middle of a class instead of at its upper end, and comparing two box plots with only the medians and no measure of spread. Learn the rule that the area of a bar is the frequency, the rule that n/4, n/2 and 3n/4 are where you read across, and the two-sentence comparison, and this room becomes some of the most reliable marks on the paper.","idea":[{"h":"Histograms: frequency density and the area of a bar","t":"A histogram displays grouped continuous data, so the bars touch with no gaps between them. When the classes all have the same width you could plot frequency, but as soon as the widths differ that is misleading: a class twice as wide would look twice as important. So the vertical axis is frequency density, where frequency density = frequency ÷ class width, and the frequency is then the area of the bar, not its height: frequency = frequency density × class width. Every histogram question is one of these two lines. Forwards, you are given a grouped frequency table with unequal classes and you divide each frequency by its class width to get the bar heights. Backwards, you are given the bars and you multiply each height by its width to recover the frequencies, then add them for a total. A third version asks for part of a class — 'how many were more than 30?' when the class runs 20 to 40 — and there you assume the values are spread evenly through the class and take that fraction of its frequency, so half the class width gives about half the frequency. Always write the units of a frequency density as 'per cm', 'per minute' and so on, and remember that the class width of 20 < t ≤ 30 is 10, the distance between its two boundaries.","code":"S3"},{"h":"Cumulative frequency graphs: building one and reading it","t":"Cumulative frequency is the running total of the frequencies: add each class's frequency to everything before it, so the last value is the total number of items. The point for each class is plotted at the upper end of that class — the upper class boundary — because by the time you reach the top of the class you have counted everything in it; plotting at the midpoint is the commonest lost mark. Join the points with a smooth curve, which usually gives the S shape, and start the curve at the lower boundary of the first class with a cumulative frequency of zero. What the graph is for is reading off: go up from a value on the horizontal axis to the curve and across to find how many are less than or equal to it, and subtract from the total to find how many are more than it; or go across from a cumulative frequency and down to find the value at that position. It is the right diagram when the question asks about a position in the data — the median, the quartiles, how many were under a limit — while a histogram is the right diagram for showing the shape of the distribution.","code":"S3"},{"h":"Quartiles and the inter-quartile range","t":"The quartiles cut an ordered set of data into four equal parts. For grouped data read from a cumulative frequency graph of n values, read across at n/2 for the median, at n/4 for the lower quartile and at 3n/4 for the upper quartile, then down to the horizontal axis; the answers are estimates because the graph, not the original list, is what you are using. So for 80 values you read at 40, 20 and 60. For a short ordered list you find the median first, then the lower quartile is the median of the values below it and the upper quartile is the median of the values above it. The inter-quartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile. It measures the spread of the middle half of the data, and because it throws away the bottom quarter and the top quarter it is not distorted by one unusually large or small value, which is exactly what the range cannot say for itself. A small inter-quartile range means the middle half of the data is tightly bunched, in other words the data are consistent.","code":"S4"},{"h":"Box plots and comparing two distributions","t":"A box plot shows five values: the minimum, the lower quartile, the median, the upper quartile and the maximum. The box runs from the lower quartile to the upper quartile with a line drawn inside it at the median, and a whisker runs from each end of the box out to the minimum and the maximum. The width of the box is the inter-quartile range and the full length from whisker to whisker is the range, so both measures of spread can be seen at a glance, and a median sitting off-centre in the box shows the distribution is not symmetrical. Box plots exist to be compared, and a comparison scores only when it is written in context and gives both an average and a measure of spread: one sentence comparing the medians ('the second town's daily rainfall was typically 2 mm higher') and one comparing the inter-quartile ranges ('and its inter-quartile range is smaller, so its daily totals are more consistent'). Comparing maximum with maximum, or quoting a number with no context, is what loses these marks. The terms the specification expects alongside this work are primary and secondary data, and discrete and continuous data.","code":"S4"}],"examples":[{"title":"Frequency densities for a histogram with unequal classes","setup":"The table shows the times, t seconds, that 60 people took to solve a puzzle. 0 < t ≤ 20: 12 people. 20 < t ≤ 30: 15 people. 30 < t ≤ 50: 24 people. 50 < t ≤ 80: 9 people. Work out the frequency density for each class and say what the bars of the histogram look like. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Subtract the two ends of each class to get widths of 20, 10, 20 and 30 seconds","Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width, so 12 ÷ 20 = 0.6 and 15 ÷ 10 = 1.5 people per second","For the last two classes, 24 ÷ 20 = 1.2 and 9 ÷ 30 = 0.3 people per second","So the bars have heights 0.6, 1.5, 1.2 and 0.3 people per second and are drawn edge to edge with no gaps, the widest bar being the shortest"],"cues":["How wide is each class in seconds?","What must the frequency be divided by before it can be plotted?","Which of the remaining two classes will give the smaller height, and why?","Which of the four bars is the tallest?"]},{"title":"Reading frequencies back out of a histogram","setup":"A histogram shows the ages, a years, of the 100 members of a swimming club. The bar for 0 < a ≤ 10 has frequency density 2.4 members per year, the bar for 10 < a ≤ 20 has frequency density 3.6, the bar for 20 < a ≤ 40 has frequency density 1.3 and the bar for 40 < a ≤ 80 has frequency density 0.35. Estimate how many members are older than 30. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Frequency = frequency density × class width, so the first two bars stand for 2.4 × 10 = 24 members and 3.6 × 10 = 36 members","The other two bars stand for 1.3 × 20 = 26 members and 0.35 × 40 = 14 members, and 24 + 36 + 26 + 14 = 100 as a check","Ages over 30 are the top half of the class 20 < a ≤ 40, so assume the ages are spread evenly and take 26 ÷ 2 = 13 members","Add the whole of the last bar: 13 + 14 = 27 members are older than 30"],"cues":["What do you multiply a bar's height by to get the number of members?","What should the four frequencies add up to?","What fraction of the 20 to 40 class lies above 30?","Which other bar is entirely above 30?"]},{"title":"A cumulative frequency graph, its median and its quartiles","setup":"The lengths, L cm, of 60 leaves are grouped as follows. 4 < L ≤ 6: 5 leaves. 6 < L ≤ 8: 13 leaves. 8 < L ≤ 10: 22 leaves. 10 < L ≤ 12: 15 leaves. 12 < L ≤ 14: 5 leaves. Work out the cumulative frequencies, say where the points are plotted, and use the graph to estimate the median, the quartiles and the inter-quartile range. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Keep a running total: the cumulative frequencies are 5, 18, 40, 55 and 60 leaves","Plot each total at the upper end of its class, giving (6, 5), (8, 18), (10, 40), (12, 55) and (14, 60), and join them with a smooth curve from (4, 0)","There are 60 values, so read across at 30 and down to the axis: the median is about 9.1 cm","Read across at 15 and at 45 for the quartiles: the lower quartile is about 7.5 cm and the upper quartile about 10.7 cm","Inter-quartile range = 10.7 − 7.5 = 3.2 cm — readings from a hand-drawn curve vary, and interpolating between the plotted points gives 7.54 cm and 10.67 cm and so 3.1 cm, so accept about 3.0 cm to 3.3 cm — and the middle half of the leaves lie within that spread"],"cues":["What do you add to each frequency to get the next total?","At which end of each class does its point belong?","Which cumulative frequency do you read across from for the median?","Which two cumulative frequencies give the quartiles?","Which two of the three readings does the inter-quartile range use?"]},{"title":"Turning graph readings into a box plot","setup":"A cumulative frequency graph has been drawn for the heights of 120 trees in a wood. The shortest tree is 2.4 m and the tallest is 9.6 m. Reading off the curve gives a height of 4.6 m at a cumulative frequency of 30, 5.8 m at 60 and 7.2 m at 90. Work out the five values needed for a box plot, describe the box plot, and find the inter-quartile range.","steps":["With 120 values, the median is read at 60, the lower quartile at 30 and the upper quartile at 90","So the median height is 5.8 m, the lower quartile is 4.6 m and the upper quartile is 7.2 m","The five values in order are 2.4 m, 4.6 m, 5.8 m, 7.2 m and 9.6 m: the box runs from the lower to the upper quartile with a line at the median, and a whisker reaches out to the shortest tree and to the tallest","Inter-quartile range = 7.2 − 4.6 = 2.6 m, which is the width of the box"],"cues":["Which cumulative frequencies do the three readings correspond to?","Which of the three readings is the median?","Which two of the five values are the ends of the whiskers?","Which measurement of the box gives the inter-quartile range?"]},{"title":"Comparing two box plots in context","setup":"Two box plots show how long a battery lasts, in hours, for two brands. Brand A: minimum 8, lower quartile 14, median 18, upper quartile 22, maximum 30. Brand B: minimum 12, lower quartile 16, median 19, upper quartile 21, maximum 26. Compare the two brands using an average and a measure of spread, and say which brand to buy if a steady lifetime matters. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Inter-quartile range for brand A = 22 − 14 = 8 hours","Inter-quartile range for brand B = 21 − 16 = 5 hours","Comparing the averages, brand B's median of 19 hours is 1 hour longer than brand A's, so a B battery typically lasts a little longer","Comparing the spreads, brand B's inter-quartile range of 5 hours is smaller than brand A's 8 hours, so B's lifetimes are more consistent — buy brand B"],"cues":["Which two of brand A's five values give its inter-quartile range?","What is the same calculation for brand B?","Which brand has the higher median, and by how much?","Which brand has the narrower box, and what does that say about consistency?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A class of width 25 contains 40 values. Work out the frequency density.","a":"1.6"},{"q":"On a cumulative frequency graph drawn for 160 values, at what cumulative frequency do you read across to estimate the upper quartile?","a":"120"},{"q":"A box plot has a lower quartile of 31 and an upper quartile of 47. Work out the inter-quartile range.","a":"16"},{"q":"Why is the inter-quartile range often a better measure of spread than the range?","a":"It uses only the middle half of the data, so an outlier at either end does not affect it."},{"q":"A cumulative frequency point for the class 30 < x ≤ 40 is plotted at which value of x?","a":"40, the upper end of the class"}]},"room":{"facts":["Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width. On a histogram it is the frequency density, never the frequency, that goes on the vertical axis when the classes have different widths.","Frequency = frequency density × class width, so the frequency is given by the area of the bar; the bars touch because the data are continuous.","To estimate the frequency in part of a class, assume the values are spread evenly through it and take that fraction of the class: half the class width holds about half the frequency.","Cumulative frequency is the running total of the frequencies, and each point is plotted at the upper end (the upper class boundary) of its class, then joined with a smooth curve.","From a cumulative frequency graph of n values: read across at n/2 for the median, at n/4 for the lower quartile and at 3n/4 for the upper quartile, then down to the horizontal axis.","Inter-quartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile. It measures the spread of the middle half of the data and is not distorted by an outlier.","A box plot shows five values: minimum, lower quartile, median, upper quartile, maximum. The box spans the quartiles with the median inside it and the whiskers reach the extremes.","Comparing two distributions needs an average and a measure of spread, both in context: compare the medians in one sentence and the inter-quartile ranges in the next."],"questions":[{"q":"The heights, h cm, of 50 sunflowers are grouped as 80 < h ≤ 100: 6 plants; 100 < h ≤ 120: 14 plants; 120 < h ≤ 150: 18 plants; 150 < h ≤ 200: 12 plants. Work out the frequency density for the class 100 < h ≤ 120 and for the class 120 < h ≤ 150. A calculator may be used.","a":"0.7 and 0.6 plants per cm","sol":"Class widths: 120 − 100 = 20 cm and 150 − 120 = 30 cm. Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width. 14 ÷ 20 = 0.7 plants per cm. 18 ÷ 30 = 0.6 plants per cm. M1 for dividing a frequency by its class width, A1 for both values correct (total 2 marks). Note that the class with more plants in it gives the shorter bar, because it is spread over a wider interval.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The vertical axis of a histogram is not frequency when the classes have different widths.","Each class has to be measured before it can be divided into: find the distance between its two ends first.","Write frequency ÷ class width for each of the two classes before putting any numbers in."],"codes":["S3"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) On a histogram showing continuous data, the bar for the class 0 < t ≤ 15 has a frequency density of 2.4. How many values are in that class?","a":"36","sol":"The class width is 15 − 0 = 15. Frequency = frequency density × class width = 2.4 × 15. Without a calculator, 2.4 × 15 = 2.4 × 10 + 2.4 × 5 = 24 + 12 = 36. M1 for 2.4 × 15, A1 for 36 (total 2 marks). The frequency is the area of the bar, not its height.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["On a histogram the frequency is shown by the area of the bar, not by how tall it is.","The rule for frequency density can be turned round to give the frequency.","Write frequency = frequency density × class width, and work out the class width first."],"codes":["S3"]},{"q":"A cumulative frequency graph is drawn for the masses of 200 letters. Which value on the cumulative frequency axis do you read across from to estimate (a) the median, (b) the lower quartile, (c) the upper quartile? A calculator may be used.","a":"(a) 100 (b) 50 (c) 150","sol":"With n = 200 grouped values read at n/2 = 200 ÷ 2 = 100 for the median; at n/4 = 200 ÷ 4 = 50 for the lower quartile; at 3n/4 = 3 × 200 ÷ 4 = 150 for the upper quartile. B1 for 100, B1 for both 50 and 150 (total 2 marks). Each reading is then taken down to the horizontal axis to give a mass.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The quartiles cut the data into four equal parts, and the median cuts it into two.","Every reading is a fraction of the total number of letters, not of the largest mass.","Write down the three positions as fractions of n before replacing n by its value."],"codes":["S4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A box plot for the lengths of some fish shows a minimum of 12 cm, a lower quartile of 19 cm, a median of 24 cm, an upper quartile of 31 cm and a maximum of 46 cm. Work out the inter-quartile range and the range.","a":"IQR 12 cm, range 34 cm","sol":"Inter-quartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile = 31 − 19 = 12 cm. Range = maximum − minimum = 46 − 12 = 34 cm. B1 for 12 cm, B1 for 34 cm (total 2 marks). The inter-quartile range is the width of the box and the range is the distance from whisker tip to whisker tip.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["One of these two measures uses the ends of the box and the other uses the ends of the whiskers.","Both are found by subtracting one of the five values from another.","Write inter-quartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile, and range = maximum − minimum."],"codes":["S4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Eleven people each recorded the number of books they read last year: 3, 5, 8, 8, 11, 15, 17, 18, 22, 25, 30. Work out the median, the lower quartile, the upper quartile and the inter-quartile range.","a":"Median 15, LQ 8, UQ 22, IQR 14","sol":"The list is already in order and n = 11, so the median is the (11 + 1)/2 = 6th value = 15 books. The five values below it are 3, 5, 8, 8, 11, whose middle value is the lower quartile, 8 books. The five values above it are 17, 18, 22, 25, 30, whose middle value is the upper quartile, 22 books. Inter-quartile range = 22 − 8 = 14 books. B1 for the median 15, B1 for both quartiles 8 and 22, B1 for 14 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The quartiles split the data into four equal parts, so find the halfway point first.","Once the middle value is found, each half of the list has its own middle value.","Write down the position of the median as (n + 1)/2 before you count along the list."],"codes":["S4"]},{"q":"The speeds, v mph, of 90 cars passing a checkpoint are grouped as 20 < v ≤ 30: 18 cars; 30 < v ≤ 35: 25 cars; 35 < v ≤ 40: 30 cars; 40 < v ≤ 60: 17 cars. Work out the frequency density for each class so that a histogram can be drawn. A calculator may be used.","a":"1.8, 5, 6 and 0.85 cars per mph","sol":"Class widths are 10, 5, 5 and 20 mph. Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width: 18 ÷ 10 = 1.8; 25 ÷ 5 = 5; 30 ÷ 5 = 6; 17 ÷ 20 = 0.85, all in cars per mph. M1 for frequency ÷ class width used for at least one class, A1 for any two correct, A1 for all four correct (total 3 marks). The tallest bar is the 35 < v ≤ 40 bar even though it is one of the narrowest.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The classes are not all the same width, so the frequencies cannot be plotted as they stand.","Work out how wide each class is first, then deal with each class in the same way.","Write frequency ÷ class width as the heading of a new column before filling it in."],"codes":["S3"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A histogram shows the lengths, t minutes, of some phone calls. The bar for 0 < t ≤ 4 has frequency density 5 calls per minute, the bar for 4 < t ≤ 6 has frequency density 12, the bar for 6 < t ≤ 10 has frequency density 7 and the bar for 10 < t ≤ 20 has frequency density 1.5. Estimate the number of calls that lasted more than 8 minutes.","a":"29 calls","sol":"Frequency = frequency density × class width. The class 6 < t ≤ 10 holds 7 × 4 = 28 calls and the class 10 < t ≤ 20 holds 1.5 × 10 = 15 calls. Calls over 8 minutes are the top half of the 6 < t ≤ 10 class, so assuming an even spread that is 28 ÷ 2 = 14 calls. Total = 14 + 15 = 29 calls. M1 for a frequency found as frequency density × class width, M1 for taking half of the 6 to 10 class and adding the whole of the last class, A1 for 29 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["8 minutes falls inside one of the classes, so part of that bar counts and part does not.","Turn the bar heights into numbers of calls first, then decide what share of the split class to keep.","Write frequency = frequency density × class width for the two bars that reach past 8 minutes."],"codes":["S3"]},{"q":"The times, t minutes, taken by 60 runners to finish a race are 20 < t ≤ 24: 6 runners; 24 < t ≤ 28: 9 runners; 28 < t ≤ 32: 21 runners; 32 < t ≤ 36: 18 runners; 36 < t ≤ 40: 6 runners. Work out the cumulative frequencies and use a cumulative frequency graph to estimate the median time and the inter-quartile range. A calculator may be used.","a":"Median about 30.9 minutes (accept 30.5 to 31.2), IQR 6 minutes","sol":"Cumulative frequencies: 6, 15, 36, 54, 60, plotted at 24, 28, 32, 36 and 40 minutes and joined with a smooth curve. n = 60, so read across at 30 for the median: it falls in the 28 < t ≤ 32 class, giving 28 + (30 − 15)/21 × 4 = 30.86, about 30.9 minutes (accept 30.5 to 31.2). Read across at 15 for the lower quartile: 28 minutes. Read across at 45 for the upper quartile: 32 + (45 − 36)/18 × 4 = 34 minutes. Inter-quartile range = 34 − 28 = 6 minutes. M1 for the correct cumulative frequencies, A1 for the median about 30.9 minutes, M1 for readings at 15 and 45, A1 for 6 minutes (total 4 marks).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The graph is built from running totals, not from the separate class frequencies.","Once the curve is drawn, three readings across from the cumulative frequency axis give everything you need.","Write the running totals of the frequencies, and plot each one at the top end of its class."],"codes":["S3","S4"]},{"q":"The masses, m grams, of 80 eggs are grouped as 40 < m ≤ 50: 6 eggs; 50 < m ≤ 55: 14 eggs; 55 < m ≤ 60: 26 eggs; 60 < m ≤ 65: 22 eggs; 65 < m ≤ 80: 12 eggs. A cumulative frequency graph is drawn. Use it to estimate how many of the eggs had a mass of more than 62 g. A calculator may be used.","a":"About 25 eggs (accept 23 to 27)","sol":"Cumulative frequencies: 6, 20, 46, 68, 80, plotted at 50, 55, 60, 65 and 80 g. Going up from 62 g to the curve and across gives a cumulative frequency of about 55 eggs (by interpolation 46 + (62 − 60)/5 × 22 = 54.8), which is the number of eggs with a mass of 62 g or less. Eggs heavier than 62 g = 80 − 55 = 25 eggs (accept 23 to 27). M1 for reading the cumulative frequency at 62 g, M1 for subtracting from 80, A1 for about 25 eggs (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The curve tells you how many are less than or equal to a value, which is the opposite of what is asked.","Read up from the mass on the horizontal axis first, then decide what to do with the number you get.","Write down the reading taken across from the curve at 62 g, and the total number of eggs, before combining them."],"codes":["S3","S4"]},{"q":"The masses, in kg, of 12 suitcases are 5, 8, 11, 12, 15, 16, 18, 21, 24, 26, 27 and 34. Write down the five values needed to draw a box plot of these masses. A calculator may be used.","a":"5, 11.5, 17, 25 and 34 kg","sol":"The list is in order and n = 12. Median = mean of the 6th and 7th values = (16 + 18)/2 = 17 kg. Lower quartile = middle of the first six values 5, 8, 11, 12, 15, 16 = (11 + 12)/2 = 11.5 kg. Upper quartile = middle of the last six values 18, 21, 24, 26, 27, 34 = (24 + 26)/2 = 25 kg. Minimum = 5 kg and maximum = 34 kg. B1 for the median 17 kg, B1 for both quartiles 11.5 kg and 25 kg, B1 for the minimum and maximum 5 kg and 34 kg (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A box plot needs the two ends of the whiskers and the three values that make the box.","With an even number of values, a middle value is the mean of the two either side of the gap.","Write down the position of the median for 12 values, then treat each half of the list the same way."],"codes":["S4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The times, t minutes, that 50 visitors spent in an exhibition are grouped as 0 < t ≤ 10: 12 visitors; 10 < t ≤ 25: 30 visitors; 25 < t ≤ 30: 8 visitors. (a) Name the diagram that should be used to show the shape of this distribution and explain why the frequencies cannot be plotted as bar heights. (b) Work out the frequency density of the class 10 < t ≤ 25.","a":"(a) Histogram, frequency density on the vertical axis; the reason must say that the classes have different widths, so the area of a bar and not its height represents the frequency (b) 2 visitors per minute","sol":"(a) The data are continuous and grouped into classes of different widths (10, 15 and 5 minutes), so a histogram is the appropriate diagram and the vertical axis must be frequency density: plotting the frequencies as heights would make the widest class look far bigger than it is, because on a histogram the frequency is the area of the bar. B1 for naming a histogram with frequency density, B1 for the reason that the area of the bar represents the frequency when the widths differ. (b) Frequency density = 30 ÷ 15 = 2 visitors per minute. B1 for 2 visitors per minute (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Look at how wide each class is before deciding what can go on the vertical axis.","On this diagram one quantity is represented by the size of the whole bar rather than by its height.","For part (b), write frequency ÷ class width and work out the width of that class first."],"codes":["S3"]},{"q":"Two box plots show the daily rainfall, in mm, recorded on 60 days in two towns. Town A: minimum 0, lower quartile 3, median 7, upper quartile 14, maximum 32. Town B: minimum 1, lower quartile 6, median 9, upper quartile 13, maximum 25. Work out the inter-quartile range for each town and make two comparisons of the daily rainfall in the two towns. A calculator may be used.","a":"Town A: IQR 11 mm; Town B: IQR 7 mm; the two comparisons must say that Town B has the higher median (9 mm against 7 mm) and that Town B has the smaller inter-quartile range (7 mm against 11 mm), each in terms of the daily rainfall","sol":"Inter-quartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile. Town A: 14 − 3 = 11 mm. Town B: 13 − 6 = 7 mm. Comparison of averages: Town B's median daily rainfall of 9 mm is 2 mm higher than Town A's 7 mm, so Town B is typically the wetter town. Comparison of spread: Town B's inter-quartile range of 7 mm is smaller than Town A's 11 mm, so the middle half of Town B's daily totals are more closely bunched — its rainfall is more consistent, while Town A has the more extreme wet days (maximum 32 mm). B1 for 11 mm, B1 for 7 mm, B1 for a comparison of the medians in context, B1 for a comparison of the spreads in context (total 4 marks).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A comparison scores only when it says something about a typical value and something about how spread out the data are.","Work the measure of spread out for both towns first, then write one sentence about each type of comparison.","Write inter-quartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile for each town before comparing anything."],"codes":["S4"]},{"q":"A histogram shows the distances, d km, driven in one day by 200 delivery drivers. The bar for 0 < d ≤ 10 has frequency density 4 drivers per km, the bar for 10 < d ≤ 20 has frequency density 6.5, the bar for 20 < d ≤ 40 has frequency density 2.75 and the bar for 40 < d ≤ 80 has frequency density 1. Estimate the median distance driven. A calculator may be used.","a":"19.2 km","sol":"Frequency = frequency density × class width: 4 × 10 = 40, 6.5 × 10 = 65, 2.75 × 20 = 55 and 1 × 40 = 40 drivers, and 40 + 65 + 55 + 40 = 200 as a check. Cumulative frequencies are 40, 105, 160, 200, so the 100th value (200 ÷ 2) lies in the class 10 < d ≤ 20, which is the 41st to the 105th driver. Assuming an even spread through that class, median = 10 + (100 − 40)/65 × 10 = 10 + 9.23 = 19.2 km (to 1 decimal place). M1 for frequency = frequency density × class width, A1 for all four frequencies 40, 65, 55 and 40, M1 for identifying the class holding the 100th value, M1 for the interpolation (100 − 40)/65 × 10, A1 for 19.2 km (total 5 marks).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The histogram hides the frequencies, so recover them before thinking about the middle value.","Running totals will show you which class the middle driver falls in.","Write frequency = frequency density × class width for every bar before any totalling."],"codes":["S3","S4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A histogram shows the times, t minutes, that members of a gym spent on a rowing machine. The bar for 0 < t ≤ 20 is 1.5 units high and represents 30 members. The bar for 20 < t ≤ 30 is 4.2 units high and the bar for 30 < t ≤ 60 is 0.6 units high. These are the only bars. How many members are shown by the histogram altogether?","a":"90 members","sol":"First find the scale: the first bar has frequency density 30 ÷ 20 = 1.5 members per minute and is 1.5 units high, so 1 unit of height is 1 member per minute. M1 for 30 ÷ 20 = 1.5 and the deduction that height = frequency density. Second bar: frequency = 4.2 × 10 = 42 members. A1 for 42. Third bar: frequency = 0.6 × 30 = 18 members. B1 for 18. Total = 30 + 42 + 18 = 90 members. A1 for 90 (total 4 marks).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The bar you know everything about is what tells you what one unit of height is worth.","Once the scale is known, each of the other bars is one multiplication.","Write frequency density = frequency ÷ class width for the first bar and compare it with that bar's height."],"codes":["S3"]},{"q":"The amounts, £x, spent by 50 shoppers are grouped as 0 < x ≤ 10: 8 shoppers; 10 < x ≤ 20: 14 shoppers; 20 < x ≤ 30: 16 shoppers; 30 < x ≤ 50: 9 shoppers; 50 < x ≤ 100: 3 shoppers. Work out an estimate of the mean amount spent, estimate the median from a cumulative frequency graph, and explain which of the two is the larger and why. A calculator may be used.","a":"Mean £24.70, median about £21.90 (accept £21 to £22.50); the mean is larger because a few shoppers spent a great deal more than the rest and the mean counts every value","sol":"Midpoints: 5, 15, 25, 40 and 75. M1 for using the midpoints. Σfx = 5 × 8 + 15 × 14 + 25 × 16 + 40 × 9 + 75 × 3 = 40 + 210 + 400 + 360 + 225 = 1235. M1 for Σfx ÷ 50. Mean = 1235 ÷ 50 = £24.70. A1. Cumulative frequencies: 8, 22, 38, 47, 50; with n = 50 the median is read at 25, which falls in the class 20 < x ≤ 30, giving 20 + (25 − 22)/16 × 10 = £21.88, about £21.90 (accept £21 to £22.50). M1 for the reading at a cumulative frequency of 25, A1 for the median. B1 for saying that the mean is the larger because the small number of large amounts in the 50 < x ≤ 100 class pull the mean up, while the median only depends on the position of the middle value (total 6 marks).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Two different averages are wanted, and each comes from a different way of using the table.","For the mean, each class needs a single value to stand for it; for the median, the running totals are what matter.","Write down the midpoint of each class, and separately the running total of the frequencies, before calculating either average."],"codes":["S3","S4"]}],"exit":[{"q":"A class 0 < x ≤ 8 contains 20 values. Work out the frequency density.","a":"2.5"},{"q":"On a histogram a bar of width 5 has a frequency density of 6. How many values does it represent?","a":"30"},{"q":"A cumulative frequency graph is drawn for 240 values. At what cumulative frequency do you read across to estimate the lower quartile?","a":"60"},{"q":"A box plot shows a lower quartile of 46 kg and an upper quartile of 58 kg. Work out the inter-quartile range.","a":"12 kg"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Frequency density","back":"Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width. It is what goes on the vertical axis of a histogram, and its units are 'per cm', 'per minute' and so on.","code":"S3","open":false},{"front":"What a bar of a histogram represents","back":"The area of the bar is the frequency: frequency = frequency density × class width. The height alone does not give the frequency unless every class has width 1.","code":"S3","open":false},{"front":"Class width of a continuous class","back":"The distance between the two class boundaries: the class 20 < t ≤ 35 has width 15. Bars are drawn from boundary to boundary and touch, because the data are continuous.","code":"S3","open":false},{"front":"Estimating the frequency in part of a class","back":"Assume the values are spread evenly through the class and take the same fraction of its frequency: the top half of a class holds about half of it. It is an estimate, not exact.","code":"S3","open":false},{"front":"Cumulative frequency","back":"The running total of the frequencies: each class's frequency added to all those before it. The final cumulative frequency is the total number of items.","code":"S3","open":false},{"front":"Plotting a cumulative frequency graph","back":"Plot each cumulative frequency against the upper end of its class, not the midpoint, starting at zero at the lower boundary of the first class, and join the points with a smooth curve.","code":"S3","open":false},{"front":"Reading how many are more than a value from a cumulative frequency graph","back":"Go up from the value to the curve and across: that gives how many are less than or equal to it. Subtract from the total to get how many are more than it.","code":"S3","open":false},{"front":"Median from a cumulative frequency graph of n values","back":"Read across at a cumulative frequency of n/2 and down to the horizontal axis. The answer is an estimate, because the graph is used in place of the original values.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"Lower and upper quartiles from a cumulative frequency graph of n values","back":"Read across at n/4 for the lower quartile and at 3n/4 for the upper quartile, then down to the horizontal axis. They cut the data into four equal parts.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"Quartiles of a short ordered list","back":"Find the median first. The lower quartile is the middle of the values below it and the upper quartile is the middle of the values above it; with an even number, average the two middle values.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"Inter-quartile range","back":"Inter-quartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile. It is the spread of the middle half of the data and, unlike the range, is not distorted by an outlier.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"The five values a box plot shows","back":"Minimum, lower quartile, median, upper quartile, maximum. The box runs from quartile to quartile with a line at the median; the whiskers reach the minimum and the maximum.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"Comparing two distributions from box plots","back":"Give an average and a measure of spread, both in context: compare the medians in one sentence, then the inter-quartile ranges. The smaller inter-quartile range means the more consistent data.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"Primary, secondary, discrete and continuous data","back":"Primary data: collected by you for your own purpose. Secondary data: collected by somebody else. Discrete data: can take only separate values, usually counted. Continuous data: measured, so it can take any value in a range.","code":"S4","open":false},{"front":"Give one reason for choosing a cumulative frequency graph rather than a histogram","back":"It gives the median and the quartiles; it answers 'how many were less than (or more than) this value'; it shows a position in the data rather than the shape of the distribution.","code":"S3","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width. Recall: the specification's Appendix does not list it and AQA's Higher tier formulae sheet does not print it.","Frequency from a histogram: frequency = frequency density × class width, which is the area of the bar. Recall: not in the Appendix, not on the Higher sheet.","Estimating a frequency inside a class: frequency ≈ (width of the part wanted ÷ class width) × frequency of the class, assuming the values are evenly spread. Recall: not in the Appendix, not on the Higher sheet.","Cumulative frequency = the running total of the frequencies, plotted against the upper end of each class. Recall: not in the Appendix, not on the Higher sheet.","Positions read off a cumulative frequency graph of n grouped values: median at n/2, lower quartile at n/4, upper quartile at 3n/4. Recall: not in the Appendix, not on the Higher sheet.","Median of an ordered list of n values: the ((n + 1)/2)th value, taking the mean of the two middle values when n is even. Recall: not in the Appendix, not on the Higher sheet.","Inter-quartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile. Recall: not in the Appendix, not on the Higher sheet.","Range = largest value − smallest value, which is the whisker-tip to whisker-tip length of a box plot. Recall: not in the Appendix, not on the Higher sheet.","The five values of a box plot: minimum, lower quartile, median, upper quartile, maximum. Recall: not in the Appendix, not on the Higher sheet.","What the Higher tier formulae sheet prints (AQA's insert for exams from 2026): area of a trapezium, volume of a prism, circumference of a circle, area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios in a right-angled triangle, the quadratic formula, the sine rule, the cosine rule, the area of a triangle from two sides and the angle between them, compound interest, the addition rule for probability and the multiplication rule using a conditional probability. Not one of them belongs to this room, so on the day the Higher sheet gives you nothing here.","What the specification's Appendix lists: the quadratic formula, the circle formulae, Pythagoras' theorem, the trigonometric ratios, the sine and cosine rules, Area = ½ab sin C, the trapezium and prism formulae, compound interest, the two probability formulae, the cone and sphere formulae and the kinematics formulae. None of them is a statistics result, so every result above must be written from memory — including on Papers 2 and 3, where the calculator does the arithmetic but not the choosing.","Not a formula but marked like one: when the classes have different widths it is the area of the bar, not its height, that represents the frequency.","Not a formula but marked like one: a cumulative frequency point is plotted at the upper class boundary, never at the midpoint of the class.","Not a formula but marked like one: any value read from a cumulative frequency graph, or found by interpolation, is an estimate, because it assumes the values are spread evenly within each class.","Not a formula but marked like one: a comparison of two distributions must give an average and a measure of spread, both in context."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-16T10:33:40Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"All 15 question answers, both check lists and all five worked examples re-solved independently in Python and every numerical answer, mark total and frequency-table total checks out, and the Formulae sheet's claims match the Higher tier insert list in section 4 of appendix.md item for item (nothing in this room is printed on the sheet, correctly stated); the only things to fix before publishing are the missing tolerances on the four graph-reading answers and the prose answer keys on the two comparison questions."}}}} };
